const DAYS = {"1":{"day":1,"week":"01","themeRu":"From recognising to using","themeEn":"Сно́ва вме́сте: чита́ющий челове́к","intro":"Welcome to the summit. B2 is where Russian stops being something you translate and becomes something you think in. And it opens with the form that defines this whole level: the <b>participle</b>. You've met «чита́ющий» in reading before — today you start <b>making</b> it. It's one word for a whole «кото́рый» clause: «the man who is reading» → <b>чита́ющий</b> man. Эмма's back, deep into her translator's life; let's describe the people all around her.","introAudio":"Welcome to the summit. B2 is where Russian stops being something you translate and becomes something you think in. And it opens with the form that defines this whole level: the participle. You've met chitayushchiy in reading before — today you start making it. It's one word for a whole 'kotory' clause: 'the man who is reading' becomes chitayushchiy man. Emma's back, deep into her translator's life; let's describe the people all around her.","goals":["Build an active present participle from any verb: <b>чита́ют → чита́ющий</b> (“the one reading”)","Fold a «кото́рый + present» clause into one word: <b>челове́к, кото́рый чита́ет → чита́ющий челове́к</b>","Decline it like an adjective — it agrees in gender, number and <b>case</b>","Know its home is <b>written</b> Russian — in speech you unfold it back to «кото́рый»"],"learned":["Built active present participles: <b>чита́ют → чита́ющий</b>","Folded «кото́рый + present» clauses into single participle words","Declined them like adjectives — agreeing in gender, number and case","Knew to write with participles and to speak with «кото́рый»"],"review":{"intro":"Before the new level opens, one minute on the B1 muscles — a plural case, an aspect, and a verb of motion. They'll all keep working under the participles this month.","introAudio":"Before the new level opens, one minute on the B1 muscles — a plural case, an aspect, and a verb of motion. They'll all keep working under the participles this month.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. This is B1 — an old friend.","q":"“I spoke with my new Russian friends.”","opts":["Я говори́л о мои́х но́вых ру́сских друзья́х.","Я говори́л с мои́ми но́выми ру́сскими друзья́ми.","Я говори́л с мои́ но́вые ру́сские друзья́."],"answer":1,"explain":"“With” → the instrumental plural: <b>с мои́ми но́выми ру́сскими друзья́ми</b> (-ыми/-ими on the adjectives, -ями on the noun). The first option is the prepositional (о ком)."},{"type":"choice","q":"“Yesterday I read the whole article (finished it).”","opts":["Вчера́ я чита́л всю статью́.","Вчера́ я бу́ду чита́ть всю статью́.","Вчера́ я прочита́л всю статью́."],"answer":2,"explain":"Finished, whole → the perfective <b>прочита́л</b>. чита́л is the process; бу́ду чита́ть is the future."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“I often travel to different cities.”</b> (a habit, by transport)","want":"Я ча́сто е́зжу по ра́зным города́м","answer":"Я ча́сто е́зжу по ра́зным города́м"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · A whole clause in one word","title":"<em>Челове́к, кото́рый чита́ет</em> → <em>чита́ющий челове́к</em>","paras":["All through B1 you described people with a «кото́рый» clause: <b>челове́к, кото́рый чита́ет</b> — “the man who reads / is reading”. That's still correct and still what you'll <b>say</b>. But written Russian loves to squeeze that whole clause into a single word — a <b>participle</b>: <b>чита́ющий челове́к</b>.","A participle is a verb wearing an adjective's clothes. <b>чита́ющий</b> means “the one who is reading” — it's <b>active</b> (he does the reading) and <b>present</b> (he's doing it now). English has this too: “the <b>reading</b> man”, “a <b>working</b> mother”. Russian just uses it far more, and — the B2 twist — it <b>bends for case</b>.","So today's move is simple to say and powerful to use: take a «кото́рый + present-tense verb» clause and turn it into one word that sits in front of the noun like any adjective."],"audio":"All through B1 you described people with a 'kotory' clause: chelovek, kotory chitayet — the man who reads or is reading. That's still correct and still what you'll say. But written Russian loves to squeeze that whole clause into a single word — a participle: chitayushchiy chelovek. A participle is a verb wearing an adjective's clothes. Chitayushchiy means 'the one who is reading' — it's active, he does the reading, and present, he's doing it now. English has this too: the reading man, a working mother. Russian just uses it far more, and — the B2 twist — it bends for case. So today's move: take a 'kotory' plus present-tense verb clause and turn it into one word that sits in front of the noun like an adjective.","table":{"rows":[["челове́к, кото́рый чита́ет → чита́ющий челове́к <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"читающий человек\">🔊</button>","the man who is reading → the reading man"],["же́нщина, кото́рая рабо́тает → рабо́тающая же́нщина <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"работающая женщина\">🔊</button>","the woman who works → the working woman"],["лю́ди, кото́рые живу́т здесь → живу́щие здесь лю́ди <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"живущие здесь люди\">🔊</button>","the people who live here → the people living here"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"The man reading the newspaper is my neighbour.","en":"<b>Чита́ющий</b> газе́ту челове́к — мой сосе́д.","say":"Читающий газету человек — мой сосед."},{"ru":"A woman working in the office called.","en":"Позвони́ла <b>рабо́тающая</b> в о́фисе же́нщина.","say":"Позвонила работающая в офисе женщина."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"челове́к, чита́ющий","right":"чита́ющий челове́к","why":"As a plain adjective the participle stands <b>before</b> the noun: <b>чита́ющий челове́к</b>. (A participle can follow the noun too — but then it takes commas, and that's day 6.)"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"“The woman who works” in one word is…","opts":["рабо́тавшая же́нщина","рабо́тающая же́нщина","рабо́танная же́нщина"],"answer":1,"explain":"Active, present → <b>рабо́тающая</b> (“the one working”). рабо́тавшая is the past (day 2)."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“the people living here”</b>","want":"живу́щие здесь лю́ди","answer":"живу́щие здесь лю́ди"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · How to build it — from the «они́» form","title":"<em>чита́ю(т) → чита́ющий</em> — drop the -т, add -щий","paras":["Here's the recipe, and it's wonderfully regular. Take the <b>они́</b> form (3rd person plural) of the present tense, drop the final <b>-т</b>, and add <b>-щий</b>: <b>чита́ют → чита́ю‑ → чита́ющий</b>. That's it.","The vowel before -щ- just echoes the conjugation you already know. First-conjugation verbs keep <b>-у/-ю-</b>: чита́ют → чита́<b>ю</b>щий, живу́т → жив<b>у́</b>щий, пи́шут → пи́ш<b>у</b>щий. Second-conjugation verbs keep <b>-а/-я-</b>: говоря́т → говор<b>я́</b>щий, лю́бят → лю́б<b>я</b>щий.","Reflexive verbs simply keep their <b>-ся</b> on the end: у́чатся → уча́щий<b>ся</b> (“studying / a student”). Learn the recipe once and every verb gives you its participle."],"audio":"Here's the recipe, and it's wonderfully regular. Take the 'oni' form, third person plural, of the present tense, drop the final -t, and add -shchiy: chitayut becomes chitayushchiy. That's it. The vowel before the -shch- just echoes the conjugation you know. First-conjugation verbs keep -u or -yu: chitayut to chitayushchiy, zhivut to zhivushchiy, pishut to pishushchiy. Second-conjugation verbs keep -a or -ya: govoryat to govoryashchiy, lyubyat to lyubyashchiy. Reflexive verbs keep their -sya on the end: uchatsya becomes uchashchiysya, a student. Learn the recipe once and every verb gives you its participle.","table":{"rows":[["чита́ют → чита́ющий <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"читают, читающий\">🔊</button>","read → reading (1st conj, -ющ)"],["живу́т → живу́щий <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"живут, живущий\">🔊</button>","live → living (1st conj, -ущ)"],["говоря́т → говоря́щий <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"говорят, говорящий\">🔊</button>","speak → speaking (2nd conj, -ящ)"],["лю́бят → лю́бящий <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"любят, любящий\">🔊</button>","love → loving (2nd conj, -ящ)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"a student living in Moscow","en":"студе́нт, <b>живу́щий</b> в Москве́","say":"студент, живущий в Москве"},{"ru":"people speaking Russian","en":"лю́ди, <b>говоря́щие</b> по-ру́сски","say":"люди, говорящие по-русски"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"чита́ащий","right":"чита́ющий","why":"Build it from the real они́ form: чита́<b>ю</b>т → чита́<b>ю</b>щий. The vowel comes straight from the verb you already say."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"From <b>говоря́т</b>, the participle “speaking” is…","opts":["говоря́щий","говору́щий","говори́вший"],"answer":0,"explain":"Second conjugation keeps -я-: говоря́т → <b>говоря́щий</b>."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Make the participle “living” from <b>живу́т</b>:","accept":["живущий","живущий."],"placeholder":"жив…щий","explain":"живу́т → drop -т, add -щий → <b>живу́щий</b>."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which is the active present participle of <b>рабо́тать</b>?","opts":["рабо́тавший","рабо́тающий","рабо́танный"],"answer":1,"explain":"рабо́тают → <b>рабо́тающий</b>. The others are past (рабо́тавший) and a passive form you'll meet later."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · It agrees like any adjective","title":"<em>-щий, -щая, -щее, -щие</em> — and every case","paras":["Once built, the participle behaves exactly like an ordinary adjective. It agrees with its noun in <b>gender and number</b>: чита́ю<b>щий</b> ма́льчик, чита́ю<b>щая</b> де́вочка, чита́ю<b>щее</b> дитя́, чита́ю<b>щие</b> лю́ди.","And — this is the B2 step B1's «кото́рый» never asked of you — it also agrees in <b>case</b>. Put the phrase in a preposition and the participle bends right along: <b>о рабо́тающем сосе́де</b> (about the working neighbour), <b>с говоря́щими по-ру́сски друзья́ми</b> (with Russian-speaking friends), <b>живу́щих здесь люде́й</b> (of the people living here).","You already know these endings — they're the adjective endings from A2–B1. The participle just wears them. Get the noun's case, and the participle copies it."],"audio":"Once built, the participle behaves exactly like an ordinary adjective. It agrees with its noun in gender and number: chitayushchiy malchik, chitayushchaya devochka, chitayushcheye ditya, chitayushchiye lyudi. And — this is the B2 step that B1's 'kotory' never asked of you — it also agrees in case. Put the phrase after a preposition and the participle bends along: o rabotayushchem sosede, about the working neighbour; s govoryashchimi po-russki druzyami, with Russian-speaking friends; zhivushchikh zdes lyudey, of the people living here. You already know these endings from A2 and B1. The participle just wears them. Get the noun's case, and the participle copies it.","table":{"rows":[["чита́ющий студе́нт <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"читающий студент\">🔊</button>","the reading student (nom.)"],["о чита́ющем студе́нте <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"о читающем студенте\">🔊</button>","about the reading student (prep.)"],["с рабо́тающими людьми́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"с работающими людьми\">🔊</button>","with the working people (instr. pl.)"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"I often think about the people living around me.","en":"Я ча́сто ду́маю о <b>живу́щих</b> вокру́г лю́дях.","say":"Я часто думаю о живущих вокруг людях."},{"ru":"She works with Russian-speaking clients.","en":"Она́ рабо́тает с <b>говоря́щими</b> по-ру́сски клие́нтами.","say":"Она работает с говорящими по-русски клиентами."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"о рабо́тающий сосе́де","right":"о рабо́тающем сосе́де","why":"The participle takes the case of its noun: prepositional сосе́де → <b>рабо́тающем</b>. It bends exactly like «но́вом», «ста́ром»."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“with the working people” (instrumental plural) —","opts":["с рабо́тающие людьми́","с рабо́тающими людьми́","с рабо́тающих людьми́"],"answer":1,"explain":"Instrumental plural adjective ending -ими: <b>рабо́тающими</b> людьми́."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“about the reading student”</b> (prepositional)","want":"о чита́ющем студе́нте","answer":"о чита́ющем студе́нте"},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Я зна́ю люде́й, живу́щих здесь.","sub":"“I know the people living here” — let the participle carry the clause","want":"я знаю людей живущих здесь"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Write it, speak «который» — and the week ahead","title":"<em>Пи́шущий</em> и́ли <em>кото́рый пи́шет</em>? Choose by register","paras":["One honest word about where participles live. They are the mark of <b>written and formal</b> Russian — articles, documents, literature. In everyday <b>speech</b>, Russians usually unfold them back into the «кото́рый» clause you already own: you'll <b>write</b> «чита́ющий студе́нт», but out loud you'll happily say «студе́нт, кото́рый чита́ет». Both are correct; register chooses.","So the skill has two directions: fold «кото́рый + present» into a participle when you write, and unfold a participle into «кото́рый» when you speak or when the sentence gets heavy. This flexibility is exactly what B2 reading and writing reward.","This week we build the whole participle family. Today: <b>active present</b> (чита́ющий). Tomorrow: <b>active past</b> (чита́вший / прочита́вший). Then the passive ones — <b>люби́мый</b>, <b>напи́санный</b> — the short form «напи́сано», and finally the participle phrase with its commas. One form a day, and by Sunday the whole system is yours to use."],"audio":"One honest word about where participles live. They are the mark of written and formal Russian — articles, documents, literature. In everyday speech, Russians usually unfold them back into the 'kotory' clause you already own: you'll write chitayushchiy student, but out loud you'll happily say student, kotory chitayet. Both are correct; register chooses. So the skill has two directions: fold 'kotory' plus present into a participle when you write, and unfold a participle into 'kotory' when you speak. This week we build the whole participle family. Today: active present, chitayushchiy. Tomorrow: active past, chitavshiy and prochitavshiy. Then the passive ones — lyubimy, napisanny — the short form napisano, and finally the participle phrase with its commas. One form a day.","examples":[{"ru":"In the article: “a growing interest in Russian.”","en":"В статье́: «<b>расту́щий</b> интере́с к ру́сскому языку́».","say":"В статье: растущий интерес к русскому языку."},{"ru":"Out loud: “an interest that is growing.”","en":"В ре́чи: «интере́с, <b>кото́рый растёт</b>».","say":"В речи: интерес, который растёт."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"You're writing a formal report. Which fits best?","opts":["лю́ди, кото́рые живу́т в го́роде","живу́щие в го́роде лю́ди","лю́ди, кото́рые как бы живу́т в го́роде"],"answer":1,"explain":"Formal/written → the participle: <b>живу́щие в го́роде лю́ди</b>. The «кото́рый» version is fine in speech."},{"type":"type","q":"Unfold this participle into speech: «пи́шущий студе́нт» → «студе́нт, кото́рый …» (one verb):","accept":["пишет","пишет.","студент который пишет"],"placeholder":"кото́рый …","explain":"пи́шущий = «кото́рый <b>пи́шет</b>». Same meaning, spoken register."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which two mean the same thing?","opts":["чита́ющий челове́к = челове́к, кото́рый чита́ет","чита́ющий челове́к = челове́к, кото́рый чита́л","чита́ющий челове́к = челове́к, кото́рый бу́дет чита́ть"],"answer":0,"explain":"Active <b>present</b> participle = «кото́рый» + <b>present</b> verb: кото́рый чита́ет."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Emma is back at Лена's café after a long translation job, watching the Saturday crowd with Иван. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Как хорошо́ сно́ва здесь сиде́ть. Ты ви́дишь челове́ка, чита́ющего газе́ту у окна́?","ru":"How nice to sit here again. Do you see the man reading the newspaper by the window?"},{"who":"A","en":"Ви́жу. А ря́дом — же́нщина, рабо́тающая за ноутбу́ком. Все чем-то за́няты.","ru":"I do. And next to him — a woman working at a laptop. Everyone's busy with something."},{"who":"B","en":"Тепе́рь я перевожу́ кни́гу. Ка́ждый день но́вые слова́ — иногда́ пи́шущие лю́ди лю́бят дли́нные фра́зы.","ru":"I'm translating a book now. New words every day — writers (writing people) sometimes love long phrases."},{"who":"A","en":"Ты уже́ ду́маешь как перево́дчик, живу́щий в двух язы́ках.","ru":"You already think like a translator living in two languages."},{"who":"B","en":"Мо́жет быть. Говоря́щие по-ру́сски друзья́ мне о́чень помога́ют.","ru":"Maybe. My Russian-speaking friends help me a lot."},{"who":"A","en":"Кста́ти, Макси́м — зна́ешь, мой брат, инжене́р — чита́ет ту же кни́гу.","ru":"By the way, Maxim — you know, my brother, the engineer — is reading the same book."},{"who":"B","en":"Пра́вда? Челове́к, лю́бящий те́хнику, и мой рома́н?","ru":"Really? A man who loves engineering (a tech-loving man) and my novel?"},{"who":"A","en":"Он мно́гое чита́ет. За́втра расскажу́ о чита́ющем инжене́ре подро́бнее.","ru":"He reads a lot. Tomorrow I'll tell you more about the reading engineer."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve active present participles and near-participles for a life full of busy people. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"чита́ющий","ru":"reading (the one reading)","ex":"Чита́ющий студе́нт не слы́шал меня́."},{"en":"рабо́тающий","ru":"working (the one working)","ex":"Рабо́тающая ма́ма — за́нятый челове́к."},{"en":"живу́щий","ru":"living (the one living)","ex":"Лю́ди, живу́щие здесь, о́чень до́брые."},{"en":"говоря́щий","ru":"speaking (the one speaking)","ex":"Мне ну́жен говоря́щий по-ру́сски гид."},{"en":"лю́бящий","ru":"loving (the one loving)","ex":"Она́ — лю́бящая мать."},{"en":"зна́ющий","ru":"knowing, knowledgeable","ex":"Он зна́ющий и о́пытный челове́к."},{"en":"пи́шущий","ru":"writing (the one writing)","ex":"Пи́шущий рома́н а́втор о́чень за́нят."},{"en":"иду́щий","ru":"going, walking (the one going)","ex":"Иду́щий по у́лице челове́к останови́лся."},{"en":"уча́щийся","ru":"a student (the one studying)","ex":"Ка́ждый уча́щийся сдаёт экза́мен."},{"en":"сле́дующий","ru":"next, following","ex":"На сле́дующий день пошёл дождь."},{"en":"настоя́щий","ru":"real, genuine, present","ex":"Э́то настоя́щий ру́сский чай."},{"en":"интере́с","ru":"interest","ex":"Расту́щий интере́с к языку́ ра́дует меня́."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the participle phrase to its meaning.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["чита́ющий студе́нт","the student who is reading"],["живу́щие в Москве́","(those) living in Moscow"],["говоря́щий по-ру́сски","Russian-speaking"],["лю́бящая мать","a loving mother"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"The active present participle of <b>чита́ть</b> (чита́ют) is…","opts":["чита́вший","чита́ющий","чита́емый"],"answer":1},{"q":"«кото́рый рабо́тает» in one word →","opts":["рабо́тающий","рабо́тавший","рабо́тавшийся"],"answer":0},{"q":"“about the reading student” —","opts":["о чита́ющий студе́нте","о чита́ющем студе́нте","о чита́ющего студе́нта"],"answer":1},{"q":"Second-conjugation <b>говоря́т</b> gives the participle…","opts":["говору́щий","говоря́щий","говори́щий"],"answer":1},{"q":"In everyday speech, «пи́шущий студе́нт» usually becomes…","opts":["студе́нт, кото́рый пи́шет","студе́нт, кото́рый писа́л","студе́нт, кото́рый напи́шет"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's note to herself after the café — read it aloud. A couple of these shapes are still to come this week; enjoy meeting them early.","title":"Лю́ди вокру́г","sentences":["Я сижу́ в кафе́ и смотрю́ на люде́й.","Челове́к, чита́ющий газе́ту, пьёт ко́фе.","Же́нщина, рабо́тающая за ноутбу́ком, что-то пи́шет.","Живу́щие здесь лю́ди ка́жутся мне бли́зкими.","Я — перево́дчик, лю́бящий свой го́род и свой язы́к."],"translation":"I'm sitting in a café and watching people. The man reading the newspaper is drinking coffee. The woman working at a laptop is writing something. The people living here feel close to me. I'm a translator who loves her city and her language."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — describe the people around you with participles.","prompt":"Look around you (or imagine a café) and describe 3–4 people in Russian using active present participles: the one reading, the one working, the one speaking, the one going somewhere. Put at least one into a case (e.g. «I'm looking at the man reading…»).","hint":"Recipe: они́-form minus -т plus -щий, then agree it like an adjective — чита́ющий челове́к, рабо́тающая же́нщина, о говоря́щем дру́ге 🙂","example":"Я смотрю́ на люде́й вокру́г. Челове́к, чита́ющий кни́гу, ти́хо сиди́т в углу́. Рабо́тающая же́нщина пьёт чай. Я ду́маю о друзья́х, живу́щих далеко́."}},"2":{"day":2,"week":"01","themeRu":"The one who did","themeEn":"Чита́вший и прочита́вший","intro":"Yesterday you built the participle for someone reading right now — <b>чита́ющий</b>. But what about the one who read <b>yesterday</b>, or the one who <b>finished</b> the book last week? For that Russian reaches into the past — and something neat happens: <b>aspect</b> steps in and picks the form for you. «who was reading» → <b>чита́вший</b>; «who read it through» → <b>прочита́вший</b>. Эмма's back at Лена's café, and today the talk is all about who wrote, who translated, who already finished.","introAudio":"Yesterday you built the participle for someone reading right now — chitayushchiy. But what about the one who read yesterday, or the one who finished the book last week? For that Russian reaches into the past — and something neat happens: aspect steps in and picks the form for you. Who was reading becomes chitavshiy; who read it through becomes prochitavshiy. Emma's back at Lena's café, and today the talk is all about who wrote, who translated, who already finished.","goals":["Build an active <b>past</b> participle: <b>чита́л → чита́вший</b> (“the one who was reading / read”)","Take the recipe from the masculine past: drop <b>-л</b>, add <b>-вший</b>","Let <b>aspect</b> choose the form: <b>чита́вший</b> (was reading) vs <b>прочита́вший</b> (read it through)","Decline it like an adjective and unfold it back to «кото́рый + past» in speech"],"learned":["Built active past participles: <b>чита́л → чита́вший</b>","Took the recipe from the masculine past: <b>-л → -вший</b>","Let aspect choose: <b>чита́вший</b> vs <b>прочита́вший</b>","Declined them and unfolded them into «кото́рый + past» for speech"],"review":{"intro":"One minute back on yesterday's <b>чита́ющий</b> — the <b>present</b> participle — before we send it into the past. And a plural case from B1 that the participles keep leaning on.","introAudio":"One minute back on yesterday's chitayushchiy — the present participle — before we send it into the past. And a plural case from B1 that the participles keep leaning on.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. Yesterday's form.","q":"“the woman who is working” in one word (present) —","opts":["рабо́тавшая же́нщина","рабо́тающая же́нщина","рабо́танная же́нщина"],"answer":1,"explain":"Active <b>present</b> → <b>рабо́тающая</b> (“the one working”). рабо́тавшая is the past you meet today."},{"type":"choice","q":"“I read a lot of interesting books.” (after мно́го)","opts":["Я прочита́л мно́го интере́сные кни́ги.","Я прочита́л мно́го интере́сных книг.","Я прочита́л мно́го интере́сными кни́гами."],"answer":1,"explain":"мно́го + genitive plural: <b>интере́сных книг</b> (adjective -ых, noun zero ending)."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Make the active <b>present</b> participle “living” from <b>живу́т</b>:","accept":["живущий","живущий."],"placeholder":"жив…щий","explain":"живу́т → drop -т, add -щий → <b>живу́щий</b> (present). Today's past would be жи́вший."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The past, folded into one word","title":"<em>Студе́нт, кото́рый чита́л</em> → <em>чита́вший студе́нт</em>","paras":["Yesterday's <b>чита́ющий</b> caught someone in the act — reading <b>now</b>. But most of what you read and translate happened in the <b>past</b>: the man who <b>read</b>, the author who <b>wrote</b>, the friend who <b>came</b>. For all of them Russian has the <b>active past participle</b>, and it folds a whole «кото́рый + past» clause into a single word: <b>студе́нт, кото́рый чита́л</b> → <b>чита́вший студе́нт</b>.","Like yesterday's participle it's <b>active</b> — the student does the reading — but now it points <b>backwards</b> in time. And like every participle, it dresses as an adjective and sits in front of its noun (or, with commas, just after it — that's day 6).","You already own the meaning; you've been saying «кото́рый чита́л» since B1. Today you just learn to compress it into one word."],"audio":"Yesterday's chitayushchiy caught someone in the act — reading now. But most of what you read and translate happened in the past: the man who read, the author who wrote, the friend who came. For all of them Russian has the active past participle, and it folds a whole 'kotory' plus past clause into a single word: student, kotory chital becomes chitavshiy student. Like yesterday's participle it's active — the student does the reading — but now it points backwards in time. And like every participle, it dresses as an adjective and sits in front of its noun, or, with commas, just after it — that's day 6. You already own the meaning; you've been saying kotory chital since B1. Today you just learn to compress it into one word.","table":{"rows":[["студе́нт, кото́рый чита́л → чита́вший студе́нт <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"читавший студент\">🔊</button>","the student who was reading → the student who read"],["а́втор, кото́рый написа́л → написа́вший а́втор <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"написавший автор\">🔊</button>","the author who wrote → the author who wrote (it)"],["друг, кото́рый пришёл → прише́дший друг <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"пришедший друг\">🔊</button>","the friend who came → the friend who came"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"The woman who called me is my colleague.","en":"Же́нщина, <b>звони́вшая</b> мне, — моя́ колле́га.","say":"Женщина, звонившая мне, — моя коллега."},{"ru":"The author who wrote this novel lives in Moscow.","en":"<b>Написа́вший</b> э́тот рома́н а́втор живёт в Москве́.","say":"Написавший этот роман автор живёт в Москве."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"чита́ющий студе́нт (about yesterday)","right":"чита́вший студе́нт","why":"For a <b>past</b> action use the past participle -вший: <b>чита́вший</b>. чита́ющий is happening now."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"“the friend who came” in one word —","opts":["приходя́щий друг","прише́дший друг","прихо́дящий друг"],"answer":1,"explain":"Past, active → <b>прише́дший</b> (from пришёл). приходя́щий is the present participle “the one who comes”."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“the author who wrote this novel”</b> (participle first)","want":"написа́вший э́тот рома́н а́втор","answer":"написа́вший э́тот рома́н а́втор"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Build it from the masculine past","title":"<em>чита́л → чита́вший</em> — swap -л for -вший","paras":["The recipe is even simpler than yesterday's. Take the <b>masculine past</b> — the -л form you've known since A1 — drop the <b>-л</b>, and add <b>-вший</b>: <b>чита́л → чита́‑ → чита́вший</b>. Do it with any verb: писа́л → писа́вший, ви́дел → ви́девший, жил → жи́вший.","The <b>perfective</b> works exactly the same way, keeping its prefix: прочита́л → <b>прочита́вший</b>, написа́л → <b>написа́вший</b>, купи́л → <b>купи́вший</b>. Reflexives keep <b>-ся</b>: учи́лся → учи́вший<b>ся</b>.","One small twist: when the masculine past has <b>no -л</b> (it ends in a consonant), you add just <b>-ший</b>: пришёл → <b>прише́дший</b>, нёс → <b>нёсший</b>, привёз → <b>привёзший</b>. A handful of verbs, quickly learned."],"audio":"The recipe is even simpler than yesterday's. Take the masculine past — the -l form you've known since A1 — drop the -l, and add -vshiy: chital becomes chitavshiy. Do it with any verb: pisal to pisavshiy, videl to videvshiy, zhil to zhivshiy. The perfective works exactly the same way, keeping its prefix: prochital to prochitavshiy, napisal to napisavshiy, kupil to kupivshiy. Reflexives keep -sya: uchilsya to uchivshiysya. One small twist: when the masculine past has no -l — it ends in a consonant — you add just -shiy: prishyol to prishedshiy, nyos to nyosshiy, privyoz to privyozshiy. A handful of verbs, quickly learned.","table":{"rows":[["чита́л → чита́вший <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"читал, читавший\">🔊</button>","was reading → who was reading (impf)"],["написа́л → написа́вший <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"написал, написавший\">🔊</button>","wrote → who wrote (pf, keeps на-)"],["ви́дел → ви́девший <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"видел, видевший\">🔊</button>","saw → who saw"],["пришёл → прише́дший <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"пришёл, пришедший\">🔊</button>","came → who came (consonant stem, just -ший)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"the friends who lived in St Petersburg","en":"друзья́, <b>жи́вшие</b> в Петербу́рге","say":"друзья, жившие в Петербурге"},{"ru":"a colleague who saw the letter","en":"колле́га, <b>ви́девший</b> письмо́","say":"коллега, видевший письмо"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"чита́ивший","right":"чита́вший","why":"Build straight off the past form чита́<b>л</b>: drop -л, add -вший. No extra vowels sneak in."}],"tasks":[{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks are just for you.","q":"Make the active past participle from <b>писа́л</b> (“who was writing”):","accept":["писавший","писавший."],"placeholder":"пис…ший","explain":"писа́л → drop -л, add -вший → <b>писа́вший</b>."},{"type":"choice","q":"The masculine past <b>пришёл</b> ends in a consonant, so its participle is…","opts":["прише́дший","пришёвший","пришёлший"],"answer":0,"explain":"No -л to drop → add just <b>-ший</b>: прише́дший."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Aspect chooses: was doing vs did","title":"<em>Чита́вший</em> и́ли <em>прочита́вший</em>? The aspect decides","paras":["Here is the heart of today, and it's a place English can't help you. English has one phrase — “the student who <b>read</b>” — for two different Russian pictures. Russian splits them by <b>aspect</b>, the same aspect you already choose for the plain verb.","From the <b>imperfective</b> чита́ть you get <b>чита́вший</b> — “the one who was reading / used to read”, the <b>process</b>, no finish line: <b>студе́нт, чита́вший всю ночь</b> (who read all night). From the <b>perfective</b> прочита́ть you get <b>прочита́вший</b> — “the one who read it through / finished it”, the <b>result</b>: <b>студе́нт, прочита́вший кни́гу</b> (who has read the book, done).","So the participle is decided <b>before</b> you build it: pick the aspect, then swap -л for -вший. «кото́рый чита́л» → чита́вший; «кото́рый прочита́л» → прочита́вший. Same split you've drilled all through B1, now wearing a participle's coat."],"audio":"Here is the heart of today, and it's a place English can't help you. English has one phrase — the student who read — for two different Russian pictures. Russian splits them by aspect, the same aspect you already choose for the plain verb. From the imperfective chitat you get chitavshiy — the one who was reading or used to read, the process, no finish line: student, chitavshiy vsyu noch, who read all night. From the perfective prochitat you get prochitavshiy — the one who read it through or finished it, the result: student, prochitavshiy knigu, who has read the book, done. So the participle is decided before you build it: pick the aspect, then swap -l for -vshiy. Kotory chital becomes chitavshiy; kotory prochital becomes prochitavshiy. Same split you've drilled all through B1, now wearing a participle's coat.","table":{"rows":[["студе́нт, чита́вший всю ночь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"студент, читавший всю ночь\">🔊</button>","the student who was reading all night (process, impf)"],["студе́нт, прочита́вший рома́н <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"студент, прочитавший роман\">🔊</button>","the student who has read the novel (result, pf)"],["перево́дчик, писа́вший перево́д <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"переводчик, писавший перевод\">🔊</button>","the translator who was writing the translation (process)"],["перево́дчик, написа́вший перево́д <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"переводчик, написавший перевод\">🔊</button>","the translator who finished the translation (result)"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"the girl who was reading a book","en":"де́вушка, <b>чита́вшая</b> кни́гу","say":"девушка, читавшая книгу"},{"ru":"the girl who read the book through","en":"де́вушка, <b>прочита́вшая</b> кни́гу","say":"девушка, прочитавшая книгу"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"челове́к, прочита́вший всю ночь","right":"челове́к, чита́вший всю ночь","why":"“all night” is an unfinished <b>process</b> → imperfective <b>чита́вший</b>. прочита́вший means he <b>finished</b> — it can't stretch across “all night”."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> the stem tells you finished or not.","q":"“the reader who <b>finished</b> the whole book” —","opts":["чита́тель, чита́вший всю кни́гу","чита́тель, прочита́вший всю кни́гу","чита́тель, чита́ющий всю кни́гу"],"answer":1,"explain":"Finished / whole → perfective <b>прочита́вший</b>. чита́вший is the process; чита́ющий is present."},{"type":"choice","q":"“the student who was reading (in the middle of it) when I came in” —","opts":["студе́нт, чита́вший","студе́нт, прочита́вший","студе́нт, чита́емый"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ongoing process → imperfective <b>чита́вший</b>. прочита́вший = already done; чита́емый is a passive form (day 3)."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · It bends, and it lives in writing","title":"<em>О чита́вшем студе́нте</em> — and speak it with «кото́рый»","paras":["Once built, the past participle behaves like any adjective: it agrees in gender, number and <b>case</b>. чита́вш<b>ий</b> студе́нт, чита́вш<b>ая</b> студе́нтка, о чита́вш<b>ем</b> студе́нте, о прочита́вш<b>их</b> кни́гу студе́нтах. Same endings you drilled at A2–B1; the participle just wears them.","And the same honest rule as yesterday: participles are the voice of <b>written</b> Russian. Out loud, Russians unfold them straight back — you'll <b>write</b> «прише́дший гость», but <b>say</b> «гость, кото́рый пришёл». Read and write with -вший; speak with «кото́рый + past».","Two participles down, two axes to go. You've done <b>active</b> present and past — “the one who does / did”. Next comes the flip: <b>passive</b> — “the one that is done <b>to</b>”: люби́мый, чита́емый tomorrow, then напи́санный."],"audio":"Once built, the past participle behaves like any adjective: it agrees in gender, number and case. Chitavshiy student, chitavshaya studentka, o chitavshem studente, o prochitavshikh knigu studentakh. Same endings you drilled at A2 and B1; the participle just wears them. And the same honest rule as yesterday: participles are the voice of written Russian. Out loud, Russians unfold them straight back — you'll write prishedshiy gost, but say gost, kotory prishyol. Read and write with -vshiy; speak with kotory plus past. Two participles down, two axes to go. You've done active present and past — the one who does or did. Next comes the flip: passive — the one that is done to: lyubimy, chitayemy tomorrow, then napisanny.","examples":[{"ru":"I'm thinking about the translator who worked here before me.","en":"Я ду́маю о перево́дчике, <b>рабо́тавшем</b> здесь до меня́.","say":"Я думаю о переводчике, работавшем здесь до меня."},{"ru":"Out loud: the guest who came.","en":"В ре́чи: «гость, <b>кото́рый пришёл</b>».","say":"В речи: гость, который пришёл."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"о чита́вший студе́нте","right":"о чита́вшем студе́нте","why":"The participle takes its noun's case: prepositional студе́нте → <b>чита́вшем</b>. It bends like «но́вом», «ста́ром»."}],"tasks":[{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“about the translator who worked here”</b> (prepositional)","want":"о рабо́тавшем здесь перево́дчике","answer":"о рабо́тавшем здесь перево́дчике"},{"type":"type","q":"Unfold into speech: «прише́дший гость» → «гость, кото́рый …» (one verb, past):","accept":["пришёл","пришел","гость который пришёл","гость который пришел"],"placeholder":"кото́рый …","explain":"прише́дший = «кото́рый <b>пришёл</b>». Same meaning, spoken register."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Я зна́ю а́втора, написа́вшего э́ту кни́гу.","sub":"“I know the author who wrote this book” — let the past participle carry the clause","want":"я знаю автора написавшего эту книгу"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Emma is back at Лена's café with Иван, and the conversation turns to the book she's translating — and the people behind it. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Вчера́ я нашла́ информа́цию об а́вторе, написа́вшем э́тот рома́н.","ru":"Yesterday I found information about the author who wrote this novel."},{"who":"A","en":"Интере́сно. Писа́тель, созда́вший таку́ю кни́гу, наве́рное, до́лго над ней рабо́тал.","ru":"Interesting. A writer who created such a book probably worked on it for a long time."},{"who":"B","en":"Да. А перево́дчик, рабо́тавший над ней до меня́, оста́вил мно́го оши́бок.","ru":"Yes. And the translator who worked on it before me left a lot of mistakes."},{"who":"A","en":"А ты уже́ прочита́ла его́? Челове́к, прочита́вший рома́н до конца́, зна́ет секре́т фина́ла.","ru":"And have you read it yet? A person who has read the novel to the end knows the secret of the ending."},{"who":"B","en":"Ещё нет. Но я — перево́дчик, чита́вший ты́сячи страни́ц, так что не бою́сь.","ru":"Not yet. But I'm a translator who has read thousands of pages, so I'm not afraid."},{"who":"A","en":"Кста́ти, Макси́м, купи́вший э́ту кни́гу в про́шлом ме́сяце, уже́ её прочита́л.","ru":"By the way, Maxim, who bought this book last month, has already read it."},{"who":"B","en":"Твой брат-инжене́р, прочита́вший рома́н ра́ньше перево́дчика? Мне на́до спеши́ть!","ru":"Your engineer brother, who read the novel before the translator? I need to hurry!"},{"who":"A","en":"Не волну́йся. Он с ра́достью расска́жет о прочи́танной кни́ге.","ru":"Don't worry. He'll gladly tell you about the book he's read."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve active past participles — who read, who wrote, who came, who finished. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"чита́вший","ru":"who was reading / read (impf)","ex":"Студе́нт, чита́вший всю ночь, о́чень уста́л."},{"en":"прочита́вший","ru":"who read (through), finished reading (pf)","ex":"Челове́к, прочита́вший рома́н, зна́ет фина́л."},{"en":"писа́вший","ru":"who was writing","ex":"Журнали́ст, писа́вший статью́, забы́л о вре́мени."},{"en":"написа́вший","ru":"who wrote (pf)","ex":"Написа́вший письмо́ студе́нт ти́хо ушёл."},{"en":"рабо́тавший","ru":"who worked / used to work","ex":"Инжене́р, рабо́тавший здесь, уе́хал в друго́й го́род."},{"en":"жи́вший","ru":"who lived","ex":"Лю́ди, жи́вшие в э́том до́ме, давно́ съе́хали."},{"en":"ви́девший","ru":"who saw","ex":"Челове́к, ви́девший ава́рию, позвони́л в поли́цию."},{"en":"сказа́вший","ru":"who said","ex":"Челове́к, сказа́вший э́то, был соверше́нно прав."},{"en":"купи́вший","ru":"who bought","ex":"Купи́вший маши́ну сосе́д о́чень рад."},{"en":"получи́вший","ru":"who received / got","ex":"Студе́нт, получи́вший пятёрку, улыба́лся."},{"en":"прише́дший","ru":"who came / arrived","ex":"Прише́дший по́здно гость извини́лся."},{"en":"сде́лавший","ru":"who did / made","ex":"Челове́к, сде́лавший оши́бку, испра́вил её."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each past participle to its meaning — watch the aspect.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["чита́вший студе́нт","the student who was reading"],["прочита́вший рома́н","the one who has read the novel"],["написа́вший письмо́","the one who wrote the letter"],["прише́дший гость","the guest who came"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"The active past participle of <b>чита́ть</b> (чита́л) is…","opts":["чита́ющий","чита́вший","чита́емый"],"answer":1},{"q":"«кото́рый написа́л» in one word →","opts":["написа́вший","пи́шущий","напи́санный"],"answer":0},{"q":"“the student who <b>finished</b> the book” —","opts":["чита́вший кни́гу студе́нт","прочита́вший кни́гу студе́нт","чита́ющий кни́гу студе́нт"],"answer":1},{"q":"The masculine past пришёл (consonant stem) gives the participle…","opts":["приходя́щий","прише́дший","пришёвший"],"answer":1},{"q":"“about the translator who worked here” (prepositional) —","opts":["о рабо́тавшем перево́дчике","о рабо́тавший перево́дчике","о рабо́тающем перево́дчике"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's note to herself after the café — read it aloud. Every describing word here is a past participle.","title":"Кто написа́л, кто перевёл","sentences":["Сего́дня я узна́ла бо́льше об а́вторе, написа́вшем мой рома́н.","Э́то челове́к, до́лго жи́вший за грани́цей и мно́го ви́девший.","Перево́дчик, рабо́тавший над кни́гой до меня́, сдал плохо́й перево́д.","Но чита́тель, прочита́вший рома́н внима́тельно, всё поймёт.","Я хочу́ стать перево́дчиком, сде́лавшим э́ту кни́гу живо́й по-ру́сски."],"translation":"Today I learned more about the author who wrote my novel. He's a person who lived abroad for a long time and saw a lot. The translator who worked on the book before me handed in a bad translation. But a reader who reads the novel carefully will understand everything. I want to become the translator who made this book alive in Russian."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — describe people by what they <b>did</b>.","prompt":"Describe 3–4 people using active <b>past</b> participles: the author who wrote, the friend who came, the colleague who worked here, the reader who finished the book. Let aspect do its job — <b>чита́вший</b> for the process, <b>прочита́вший</b> for the finished result — and put at least one into a case.","hint":"Recipe: masculine past minus -л plus -вший (пришёл → прише́дший with -ший). Then agree it like an adjective: написа́вший а́втор, о рабо́тавшем дру́ге 🙂","example":"Вчера́ я встре́тила челове́ка, написа́вшего интере́сную статью́. Друг, прише́дший по́здно, извини́лся. Я до́лго говори́ла с колле́гой, рабо́тавшей над э́тим перево́дом ра́ньше меня́."}},"3":{"day":3,"week":"01","themeRu":"The one being read","themeEn":"Люби́мый, чита́емый","intro":"Meet the participle you'll read a hundred times before you ever say it. So far every form described the <b>doer</b> — the one who reads. Now flip the lens: the book that <b>gets</b> read, the author who <b>is</b> loved, the professor who <b>is</b> respected. That's the <b>passive</b>, and today's form is the <b>present</b> one: <b>люби́мый</b>, <b>чита́емый</b>, <b>уважа́емый</b>. Эмма's just back from a lecture about the writer she's translating — her <b>люби́мый</b> author.","introAudio":"Meet the participle you'll read a hundred times before you ever say it. So far every form described the doer — the one who reads. Now flip the lens: the book that gets read, the author who is loved, the professor who is respected. That's the passive, and today's form is the present one: lyubimy, chitayemy, uvazhayemy. Emma's just back from a lecture about the writer she's translating — her lyubimy author.","goals":["Turn the lens from doer to <b>receiver</b>: <b>лю́бящий</b> (who loves) → <b>люби́мый</b> (who is loved)","Build a passive present participle from the «мы» form: <b>чита́ем → чита́емый</b>","Put the doer in the <b>instrumental</b>: <b>кни́га, чита́емая студе́нтами</b>","Know it's a <b>bookish</b> form — but own люби́мый and уважа́емый as everyday words"],"learned":["Turned the lens to the receiver: <b>лю́бящий → люби́мый</b>","Built passive present participles from the «мы» form: <b>чита́ем → чита́емый</b>","Named the doer in the instrumental: <b>чита́емая студе́нтами</b>","Used люби́мый and уважа́емый freely, and read the rest in print"],"review":{"intro":"A quick look back at both active participles — present and past — before the passive flips the picture. And a B1 case that today's passive leans on hard.","introAudio":"A quick look back at both active participles — present and past — before the passive flips the picture. And a B1 case that today's passive leans on hard.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> yesterday's aspect split.","q":"“the reader who <b>finished</b> the book” (one word) —","opts":["чита́вший","прочита́вший","чита́ющий"],"answer":1,"explain":"Finished → perfective <b>прочита́вший</b> (yesterday). чита́вший is the process; чита́ющий is present."},{"type":"choice","q":"“the people living here” (present, day 1) —","opts":["жи́вшие здесь лю́ди","живу́щие здесь лю́ди","жи́вущие здесь лю́ди"],"answer":1,"explain":"Active present → <b>живу́щие</b>. жи́вшие is the past participle (“who lived”)."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build (B1 instrumental — the “by/with” case today's doer uses): <b>“with my Russian friends”</b>","want":"с мои́ми ру́сскими друзья́ми","answer":"с мои́ми ру́сскими друзья́ми"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · From “who does” to “what is done to”","title":"<em>Лю́бящий</em> (who loves) → <em>люби́мый</em> (who is loved)","paras":["Everything so far described the <b>doer</b>: чита́ющий, чита́вший — “the one who reads / read”. Today the camera turns around to the <b>receiver</b> of the action — the one it's <b>done to</b>. That's the <b>passive</b> participle, and its present form ends in <b>-мый</b>.","Feel the flip in one pair: <b>лю́бящий</b> = “the one who <b>loves</b>” (active), but <b>люби́мый</b> = “the one who is <b>loved</b>” — beloved (passive). Same verb, opposite roles. A <b>чита́ющий</b> челове́к reads; a <b>чита́емый</b> рома́н <b>gets</b> read. It answers «кото́рого X-ают»: люби́мый = «кото́рого лю́бят».","You already know most of these as plain adjectives — <b>люби́мый</b> (favourite), <b>необходи́мый</b> (necessary), <b>ви́димый</b> (visible), <b>уважа́емый</b> (respected). Today you see the verb hiding inside them."],"audio":"Everything so far described the doer: chitayushchiy, chitavshiy — the one who reads or read. Today the camera turns around to the receiver of the action — the one it's done to. That's the passive participle, and its present form ends in -my. Feel the flip in one pair: lyubyashchiy means the one who loves, active, but lyubimy means the one who is loved — beloved, passive. Same verb, opposite roles. A chitayushchiy person reads; a chitayemy novel gets read. It answers 'kotorogo X-ayut': lyubimy equals kotorogo lyubyat. You already know most of these as plain adjectives — lyubimy, favourite; neobkhodimy, necessary; vidimy, visible; uvazhayemy, respected. Today you see the verb hiding inside them.","table":{"rows":[["лю́бящий → люби́мый <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"любящий, любимый\">🔊</button>","who loves → who is loved (beloved)"],["чита́ющий → чита́емый <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"читающий, читаемый\">🔊</button>","who reads → which is (being) read"],["уважа́ющий → уважа́емый <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"уважающий, уважаемый\">🔊</button>","who respects → (who is) respected"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"my favourite (beloved) writer","en":"мой <b>люби́мый</b> писа́тель","say":"мой любимый писатель"},{"ru":"a widely read (much-read) magazine","en":"широко́ <b>чита́емый</b> журна́л","say":"широко читаемый журнал"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"люби́мая мать (a mother who loves)","right":"лю́бящая мать","why":"“a mother who <b>loves</b>” is <b>active</b> → <b>лю́бящая</b>. люби́мая means <b>be</b>loved — she is loved by others."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"“a respected professor” (one whom people respect) —","opts":["уважа́ющий профе́ссор","уважа́емый профе́ссор","уважа́вший профе́ссор"],"answer":1,"explain":"Passive → <b>уважа́емый</b> (“the one who is respected”). уважа́ющий = who respects (active)."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which one means “the one who <b>loves</b>” (active)?","opts":["лю́бящий","люби́мый","люби́вший"],"answer":0,"explain":"Active present → <b>лю́бящий</b>. люби́мый is passive (beloved); люби́вший is active past (who loved)."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Build it from the «мы» form","title":"<em>чита́ем → чита́емый</em>, <em>лю́бим → люби́мый</em>","paras":["The recipe is short. Take the <b>мы</b> form (1st person plural) of an imperfective verb and add <b>-ый</b>: <b>чита́ем → чита́емый</b>. First-conjugation verbs give <b>-емый</b>: уважа́ем → уважа́<b>емый</b>, изуча́ем → изуча́<b>емый</b>, обсужда́ем → обсужда́<b>емый</b>.","Second-conjugation verbs give <b>-имый</b>: лю́бим → люб<b>и́мый</b>, ви́дим → ви́д<b>имый</b>, храни́м → храни́мый. (Note the stress can shift: лю́бим but люб<b>и́</b>мый.)","Only <b>transitive imperfective</b> verbs make this form — a verb that takes a direct object and describes an ongoing action. It's the rarest of the four participles in speech, but you'll meet it constantly in print."],"audio":"The recipe is short. Take the 'my' form, first person plural, of an imperfective verb and add -y: chitayem becomes chitayemy. First-conjugation verbs give -yemy: uvazhayem to uvazhayemy, izuchayem to izuchayemy, obsuzhdayem to obsuzhdayemy. Second-conjugation verbs give -imy: lyubim to lyubimy, vidim to vidimy, khranim to khranimy. Note the stress can shift: lyubim but lyubimy. Only transitive imperfective verbs make this form — a verb that takes a direct object and describes an ongoing action. It's the rarest of the four participles in speech, but you'll meet it constantly in print.","table":{"rows":[["чита́ем → чита́емый <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"читаем, читаемый\">🔊</button>","we read → (being) read (1st conj, -емый)"],["уважа́ем → уважа́емый <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"уважаем, уважаемый\">🔊</button>","we respect → respected (1st conj)"],["лю́бим → люби́мый <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"любим, любимый\">🔊</button>","we love → beloved (2nd conj, -имый, stress shifts)"],["ви́дим → ви́димый <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"видим, видимый\">🔊</button>","we see → visible (2nd conj)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"the language (being) studied","en":"<b>изуча́емый</b> язы́к","say":"изучаемый язык"},{"ru":"the question being discussed","en":"<b>обсужда́емый</b> вопро́с","say":"обсуждаемый вопрос"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"чита́мый","right":"чита́емый","why":"Keep the whole «мы» form and add -ый: чита́<b>ем</b> → чита́<b>ем</b>ый. Don't swallow the -е-."}],"tasks":[{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word.","q":"Make the passive present participle from <b>уважа́ем</b> (“respected”):","accept":["уважаемый","уважаемый."],"placeholder":"уважа́…мый","explain":"уважа́ем + ый → <b>уважа́емый</b>."},{"type":"choice","q":"Second-conjugation <b>лю́бим</b> gives…","opts":["люби́мый","люба́емый","лю́бемый"],"answer":0,"explain":"-им verbs give -имый: <b>люби́мый</b> (with the stress shifting onto -и-)."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · By whom? — instrumental, and it agrees","title":"<em>Кни́га, чита́емая студе́нтами</em>","paras":["If you want to name <b>who</b> does the action, put them in the <b>instrumental</b> — the “by” case you know from A2: <b>кни́га, чита́емая студе́нтами</b> (a book read <b>by students</b>), <b>рома́н, переводи́мый Э́ммой</b> (a novel being translated <b>by Emma</b>).","And like every participle, it agrees with its own noun in gender, number and <b>case</b>: люби́м<b>ый</b> геро́й, люби́м<b>ая</b> кни́га, о люби́м<b>ом</b> геро́е, с обсужда́ем<b>ыми</b> вопро́сами. Same adjective endings, once more.","So a whole «кото́рый» clause with a passive verb folds up neatly: «рома́н, кото́рый перево́дит Э́мма» → «рома́н, переводи́мый Э́ммой»."],"audio":"If you want to name who does the action, put them in the instrumental — the 'by' case you know from A2: kniga, chitayemaya studentami, a book read by students; roman, perevodimy Emmoy, a novel being translated by Emma. And like every participle, it agrees with its own noun in gender, number and case: lyubimy geroy, lyubimaya kniga, o lyubimom geroye, s obsuzhdayemymi voprosami. Same adjective endings, once more. So a whole 'kotory' clause with a passive verb folds up neatly: roman, kotory perevodit Emma, becomes roman, perevodimy Emmoy.","table":{"rows":[["кни́га, чита́емая студе́нтами <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"книга, читаемая студентами\">🔊</button>","a book read by students (doer = instr.)"],["рома́н, переводи́мый Э́ммой <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"роман, переводимый Эммой\">🔊</button>","a novel being translated by Emma"],["о люби́мом геро́е <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"о любимом герое\">🔊</button>","about the beloved hero (prep.)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"the article discussed by scholars","en":"статья́, <b>обсужда́емая</b> учёными","say":"статья, обсуждаемая учёными"},{"ru":"I'm thinking about my favourite book.","en":"Я ду́маю о <b>люби́мой</b> кни́ге.","say":"Я думаю о любимой книге."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"рома́н, переводи́мый Э́мму","right":"рома́н, переводи́мый Э́ммой","why":"The doer of a passive participle stands in the <b>instrumental</b>: Э́мм<b>ой</b> (“by Emma”), not the accusative Э́мму."}],"tasks":[{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“a book read by students”</b>","want":"кни́га, чита́емая студе́нтами","answer":"кни́га, чита́емая студе́нтами"},{"type":"choice","q":"“a novel being translated by Emma” —","opts":["рома́н, переводи́мый Э́ммой","рома́н, переводи́мый Э́мме","рома́н, переводя́щий Э́мму"],"answer":0,"explain":"Passive participle + doer in the <b>instrumental</b>: <b>переводи́мый Э́ммой</b>. переводя́щий would be active (“translating”)."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Rare in speech, everywhere in print","title":"<em>Уважа́емый чита́тель!</em> — where -мый lives","paras":["Be honest about this one: the passive present participle is the <b>bookish</b> star of the family. In speech Russians almost always unfold it into «кото́рый» + a verb, or flip to the active — for «широко́ чита́емая кни́га» you'd just say «кни́гу мно́го чита́ют». You'll mostly <b>read</b> it, not say it.","Two everyday exceptions worth owning. First, several -мый words have frozen into ordinary <b>adjectives</b> — <b>люби́мый</b> (favourite), <b>необходи́мый</b> (necessary), <b>ви́димый</b> (visible) — and those you use freely. Second, <b>уважа́емый</b> opens almost every formal letter and address: «<b>Уважа́емый</b> Ива́н Петро́вич!», «Уважа́емые колле́ги!» — literally “respected”, functionally “Dear”.","Tomorrow the passive turns to the <b>past</b>, and to the most useful participle in written Russian: <b>напи́санный</b>, <b>прочи́танный</b>, <b>откры́тый</b> — “the letter that <b>was written</b>”, “the door that <b>was opened</b>”."],"audio":"Be honest about this one: the passive present participle is the bookish star of the family. In speech Russians almost always unfold it into 'kotory' plus a verb, or flip to the active — for shiroko chitayemaya kniga you'd just say knigu mnogo chitayut. You'll mostly read it, not say it. Two everyday exceptions worth owning. First, several -my words have frozen into ordinary adjectives — lyubimy, favourite; neobkhodimy, necessary; vidimy, visible — and those you use freely. Second, uvazhayemy opens almost every formal letter and address: Uvazhayemy Ivan Petrovich, Uvazhayemye kollegi — literally 'respected', functionally 'Dear'. Tomorrow the passive turns to the past, and to the most useful participle in written Russian: napisanny, prochitanny, otkryty — the letter that was written, the door that was opened.","examples":[{"ru":"Dear colleagues! (opening a letter)","en":"<b>Уважа́емые</b> колле́ги!","say":"Уважаемые коллеги!"},{"ru":"Out loud, not «чита́емая»: “people read the book a lot.”","en":"В ре́чи: «кни́гу мно́го чита́ют».","say":"В речи: книгу много читают."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Приве́т, профе́ссор! (in a formal letter)","right":"Уважа́емый профе́ссор!","why":"A formal letter opens with <b>Уважа́емый</b> (“respected” = “Dear”), never a casual «Приве́т»."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> pick the register.","q":"You're opening a formal letter to a professor. Best:","opts":["Приве́т, профе́ссор!","Уважа́емый профе́ссор!","Лю́бящий профе́ссор!"],"answer":1,"explain":"Formal address → <b>Уважа́емый</b> (“Dear / respected”). Приве́т is casual; лю́бящий means “loving”."},{"type":"type","q":"Which -мый word is the everyday adjective “favourite / beloved”? Type it:","accept":["любимый","любимый.","любимая","любимое"],"placeholder":"люб…","explain":"<b>люби́мый</b> — a passive participle frozen into a plain adjective. люби́мая кни́га = favourite book."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Э́то мой люби́мый а́втор.","sub":"“This is my favourite author” — the participle that became an everyday word","want":"это мой любимый автор"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Emma went to a public lecture about the novelist she's translating. Afterwards she talks with Ка́тя, Ива́н's friend, who studies literature. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Э́то мой люби́мый писа́тель. Ле́кция была́ прекра́сной.","ru":"This is my favourite writer. The lecture was wonderful."},{"who":"A","en":"Профе́ссор, чита́вший ле́кцию, — о́чень уважа́емый учёный.","ru":"The professor who gave the lecture is a very respected scholar."},{"who":"B","en":"Да. Рома́н, переводи́мый мной сейча́с, тру́дный, но люби́мый.","ru":"Yes. The novel being translated by me now is difficult, but beloved."},{"who":"A","en":"Э́тот писа́тель — оди́н из са́мых чита́емых в стране́.","ru":"This writer is one of the most widely read in the country."},{"who":"B","en":"И са́мый обсужда́емый. Ка́ждый год выхо́дит но́вая статья́ о нём.","ru":"And the most discussed. Every year a new article about him comes out."},{"who":"A","en":"Так называ́емые «тру́дные» а́вторы ча́сто са́мые интере́сные.","ru":"So-called “difficult” authors are often the most interesting."},{"who":"B","en":"Согла́сна. Для перево́дчика ва́жен ка́ждый люби́мый геро́й, ка́ждое сло́во.","ru":"Agreed. For a translator every beloved character, every word matters."},{"who":"A","en":"Кста́ти, э́тот рома́н — изуча́емый в университе́те текст. Ива́н его́ сейча́с чита́ет.","ru":"By the way, this novel is a text studied at the university. Ivan is reading it right now."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve passive present participles — what is loved, read, respected, discussed. Several are everyday adjectives now. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"люби́мый","ru":"beloved, favourite (who/which is loved)","ex":"Э́то моя́ люби́мая кни́га."},{"en":"чита́емый","ru":"(widely) read, being read","ex":"Э́то широко́ чита́емый журна́л."},{"en":"уважа́емый","ru":"respected; “Dear …” (in letters)","ex":"Уважа́емый профе́ссор на́чал ле́кцию."},{"en":"изуча́емый","ru":"(being) studied","ex":"Изуча́емый на́ми язы́к — ру́сский."},{"en":"обсужда́емый","ru":"(being) discussed","ex":"Обсужда́емый вопро́с о́чень ва́жен."},{"en":"переводи́мый","ru":"(being) translated","ex":"Переводи́мый мной рома́н — о войне́."},{"en":"ви́димый","ru":"visible (which is seen)","ex":"Э́то едва́ ви́димая ли́ния."},{"en":"так называ́емый","ru":"so-called","ex":"Так называ́емый но́вый ме́тод не рабо́тает."},{"en":"испо́льзуемый","ru":"(being) used","ex":"Ча́сто испо́льзуемый спо́соб прост."},{"en":"необходи́мый","ru":"necessary, indispensable","ex":"Э́то необходи́мый докуме́нт."},{"en":"проводи́мый","ru":"(being) carried out, conducted","ex":"Проводи́мый о́пыт даёт результа́т."},{"en":"реша́емый","ru":"(being) solved, solvable","ex":"Э́то легко́ реша́емый вопро́с."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each passive participle to its meaning — the receiver, not the doer.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["люби́мый геро́й","a beloved hero (one who is loved)"],["чита́емый журна́л","a widely read magazine"],["уважа́емый учёный","a respected scholar"],["обсужда́емый вопро́с","the question being discussed"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"The passive present participle of <b>чита́ть</b> (чита́ем) is…","opts":["чита́ющий","чита́вший","чита́емый"],"answer":2},{"q":"Which means “beloved / who is loved” (passive)?","opts":["лю́бящий","люби́мый","люби́вший"],"answer":1},{"q":"“a respected scholar” —","opts":["уважа́емый учёный","уважа́ющий учёный","уважа́вший учёный"],"answer":0},{"q":"The doer of «кни́га, чита́емая …» stands in the…","opts":["accusative (кни́гу)","instrumental (студе́нтами)","dative (студе́нтам)"],"answer":1},{"q":"A formal letter usually opens with…","opts":["Уважа́емый…","Чита́емый…","Лю́бящий…"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's note after the lecture — read it aloud. Every describing word is a passive present participle.","title":"Люби́мый а́втор","sentences":["Мой люби́мый писа́тель — оди́н из са́мых чита́емых в стране́.","Ле́кцию чита́л о́чень уважа́емый профе́ссор.","Рома́н, переводи́мый мной, тру́ден, но прекра́сен.","Ка́ждое обсужда́емое сло́во для перево́дчика ва́жно.","Изуча́емый в университе́те текст стано́вится ещё бли́же."],"translation":"My favourite writer is one of the most widely read in the country. The lecture was given by a very respected professor. The novel being translated by me is difficult, but beautiful. Every discussed word matters to a translator. A text studied at university becomes even closer."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — describe things by what's <b>done to</b> them.","prompt":"Write 3–4 sentences using passive present participles: your <b>favourite</b> (люби́мый) book or author, a <b>respected</b> (уважа́емый) teacher, a widely <b>read</b> (чита́емый) magazine, a topic being <b>discussed</b> (обсужда́емый). Name a doer in the instrumental if you can («a book read <b>by students</b>»).","hint":"Recipe: the «мы» form + -ый — чита́ем → чита́емый, лю́бим → люби́мый. Register tip: люби́мый and уважа́емый are everyday words; the rest you'll mostly meet in print 🙂","example":"Мой люби́мый писа́тель — оди́н из са́мых чита́емых в стране́. Э́то кни́га, изуча́емая студе́нтами по всему́ ми́ру. Обсужда́емый сего́дня вопро́с ва́жен для ка́ждого."}},"4":{"day":4,"week":"01","themeRu":"The one that was written","themeEn":"Напи́санный, откры́тый","intro":"Here it is — the participle you'll use and read more than any other. Yesterday's «люби́мый» was passive but <b>present</b>; today the passive goes into the <b>past</b>: the letter that was <b>written</b>, the book that was <b>read</b>, the door that was <b>opened</b> — <b>напи́санный</b>, <b>прочи́танный</b>, <b>откры́тый</b>. Эмма's just finished her first big literary translation, and everything around her is a <b>done</b> thing now: a text written, a chapter solved, a letter received.","introAudio":"Here it is — the participle you'll use and read more than any other. Yesterday's lyubimy was passive but present; today the passive goes into the past: the letter that was written, the book that was read, the door that was opened — napisanny, prochitanny, otkryty. Emma's just finished her first big literary translation, and everything around her is a done thing now: a text written, a chapter solved, a letter received.","goals":["Build the long passive past participle: <b>написа́ть → напи́санный</b> (“that was written”)","Choose the suffix: <b>-нный</b> (напи́санный), <b>-енный/-ённый</b> (полу́ченный, решённый), <b>-тый</b> (откры́тый)","Hear the stress jump and the ё: <b>на́чатый</b>, <b>при́нятый</b>, <b>решённый</b>, <b>принесённый</b>","Agree it in case like an adjective: <b>в напи́санном письме́</b>"],"learned":["Built long passive past participles: <b>написа́ть → напи́санный</b>","Chose the suffix: <b>-нный / -енный / -тый</b>","Heard the stress jump and the ё: <b>на́чатый</b>, <b>решённый</b>","Agreed them in case: <b>в напи́санном письме́</b>"],"review":{"intro":"One more look at the passive present (люби́мый) and the active past (написа́вший) before today's big one — plus an aspect choice from B1 that decides the whole participle.","introAudio":"One more look at the passive present, lyubimy, and the active past, napisavshiy, before today's big one — plus an aspect choice from B1 that decides the whole participle.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> yesterday's passive-vs-active flip.","q":"“beloved (who is loved)” —","opts":["лю́бящий","люби́мый","люби́вший"],"answer":1,"explain":"Passive present → <b>люби́мый</b>. лю́бящий = who loves (active); люби́вший = who loved (active past)."},{"type":"choice","q":"“the author who wrote it (finished)” —","opts":["пи́шущий а́втор","написа́вший а́втор","писа́вший а́втор"],"answer":1,"explain":"Active past, perfective → <b>написа́вший</b>. писа́вший is the process; пи́шущий is present."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build (B1 aspect — finished, whole): <b>“I already read the whole book.”</b>","want":"Я уже́ прочита́л всю кни́гу","answer":"Я уже́ прочита́л всю кни́гу"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The thing that was done","title":"<em>Письмо́, кото́рое написа́ли</em> → <em>напи́санное письмо́</em>","paras":["This is the participle you'll use and read more than any other. It's the <b>passive past</b>: “the letter that <b>was written</b>”, “the book that <b>was read</b>”, “the door that <b>was opened</b>”. It folds a passive «кото́рый» clause into one word: <b>письмо́, кото́рое написа́ли</b> → <b>напи́санное письмо́</b>.","It's <b>passive</b> (the letter doesn't write — it <b>gets</b> written) and <b>past / finished</b>, so it comes from <b>perfective</b> verbs: написа́ть → напи́санный, прочита́ть → прочи́танный, откры́ть → откры́тый. The action is over and the result stands there in front of you.","This is the workhorse of every article, report and document you'll translate. Yesterday's -мый was rare; <b>-нный / -тый</b> is everywhere. Time to build it, not just recognise it."],"audio":"This is the participle you'll use and read more than any other. It's the passive past: the letter that was written, the book that was read, the door that was opened. It folds a passive 'kotory' clause into one word: pismo, kotoroye napisali becomes napisannoye pismo. It's passive — the letter doesn't write, it gets written — and past or finished, so it comes from perfective verbs: napisat to napisanny, prochitat to prochitanny, otkryt to otkryty. The action is over and the result stands there in front of you. This is the workhorse of every article, report and document you'll translate. Yesterday's -my was rare; -nny and -ty are everywhere. Time to build it, not just recognise it.","table":{"rows":[["письмо́, кото́рое написа́ли → напи́санное письмо́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"написанное письмо\">🔊</button>","the letter that was written → the written letter"],["кни́га, кото́рую прочита́ли → прочи́танная кни́га <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"прочитанная книга\">🔊</button>","the book that was read → the read (finished) book"],["дверь, кото́рую откры́ли → откры́тая дверь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"открытая дверь\">🔊</button>","the door that was opened → the opened door"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"a letter written in Russian","en":"письмо́, <b>напи́санное</b> по-ру́сски","say":"письмо, написанное по-русски"},{"ru":"the door opened by the wind","en":"дверь, <b>откры́тая</b> ве́тром","say":"дверь, открытая ветром"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"чита́емая кни́га (already finished)","right":"прочи́танная кни́га","why":"A <b>finished</b> result → passive <b>past</b> прочи́танная. чита́емая (yesterday) means it's <b>being</b> read right now."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"“the written letter” (one that was written) —","opts":["пи́шущее письмо́","напи́санное письмо́","писа́вшее письмо́"],"answer":1,"explain":"Passive, finished → <b>напи́санное</b> (“that was written”). The others are active forms."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“a letter written in Russian”</b>","want":"письмо́, напи́санное по-ру́сски","answer":"письмо́, напи́санное по-ру́сски"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Three endings: -нн-, -енн-, -т-","title":"<em>написа́ть → напи́санный</em>, <em>реши́ть → решённый</em>, <em>откры́ть → откры́тый</em>","paras":["Start from the <b>perfective infinitive</b>; the ending tells you the suffix. Verbs in <b>-ать / -ять</b> take <b>-нный</b>: написа́ть → напи́сан<b>ный</b>, прочита́ть → прочи́тан<b>ный</b>, сде́лать → сде́лан<b>ный</b>.","Verbs in <b>-ить / -еть</b> (and many in a consonant) take <b>-енный</b>, built off the «я» form: получи́ть (получу́) → полу́чен<b>ный</b>, купи́ть → ку́плен<b>ный</b>, встре́тить → встре́чен<b>ный</b>. When that -енн- lands <b>under stress</b> it becomes <b>-ённый</b> with ё: реши́ть → реш<b>ённый</b>, принести́ → принес<b>ённый</b>.","A third group takes just <b>-тый</b> — verbs in -ыть, -ть, -нуть, and the нача́ть / приня́ть / взять family: откры́ть → откры́<b>тый</b>, забы́ть → забы́<b>тый</b>, взять → взя́<b>тый</b>, нача́ть → на́ча<b>тый</b>."],"audio":"Start from the perfective infinitive; the ending tells you the suffix. Verbs in -at or -yat take -nny: napisat to napisanny, prochitat to prochitanny, sdelat to sdelanny. Verbs in -it or -et, and many in a consonant, take -enny, built off the 'ya' form: poluchit, poluchu, to poluchenny; kupit to kuplenny; vstretit to vstrechenny. When that -enn lands under stress it becomes -yonny with yo: reshit to reshyonny, prinesti to prinesyonny. A third group takes just -ty — verbs in -yt, -t, -nut, and the nachat, prinyat, vzyat family: otkryt to otkryty, zabyt to zabyty, vzyat to vzyaty, nachat to nachaty.","table":{"rows":[["написа́ть → напи́санный <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"написать, написанный\">🔊</button>","-ать → -нный (written)"],["получи́ть → полу́ченный <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"получить, полученный\">🔊</button>","-ить → -енный (received)"],["реши́ть → решённый <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"решить, решённый\">🔊</button>","-ить, stress on suffix → -ённый (solved)"],["откры́ть → откры́тый <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"открыть, открытый\">🔊</button>","-ыть → -тый (opened)"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"a decided (settled) question","en":"<b>решённый</b> вопро́с","say":"решённый вопрос"},{"ru":"the bought tickets","en":"<b>ку́пленные</b> биле́ты","say":"купленные билеты"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"напи́саный (one н)","right":"напи́санный","why":"The long passive past participle keeps <b>double -нн-</b>: напи́са<b>нн</b>ый, прочи́та<b>нн</b>ый. (The short form, tomorrow, drops one н.)"}],"tasks":[{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word.","q":"Build the passive past participle from <b>прочита́ть</b> (“read through”):","accept":["прочитанный","прочитанный.","прочитанная","прочитанное"],"placeholder":"прочи́та…ый","explain":"-ать → -нный: <b>прочи́танный</b> (stress hops back to the root)."},{"type":"choice","q":"From <b>реши́ть</b>, with the stress on the suffix, the participle is…","opts":["реши́нный","решённый","ре́шанный"],"answer":1,"explain":"-ить under stress → <b>-ённый</b> with ё: решённый."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The stress jumps — and ё appears","title":"<em>нача́ть → на́чатый</em>, <em>реши́ть → решённый</em>","paras":["Watch the stress: making this participle often <b>moves</b> it, and B2 wants you to hear that. In the -тый family the stress frequently jumps <b>back</b> onto the prefix: нача́ть → <b>на́</b>чатый, приня́ть → <b>при́</b>нятый, взять → <b>взя́</b>тый.","In the -ённый family the stress lands <b>on the suffix</b>, and a stressed «е» is always written <b>ё</b>: реши́ть → реш<b>ённый</b>, принести́ → принес<b>ённый</b>, привести́ → привед<b>ённый</b>. Say the ё out loud — it carries the stress.","This is exactly the B2 pronunciation focus: the same letters, a moved stress, a different word. In <b>на́чатый</b> (begun) the stress sits on the first syllable, на-, not the middle — the stress is the grammar."],"audio":"Watch the stress: making this participle often moves it, and B2 wants you to hear that. In the -ty family the stress frequently jumps back onto the prefix: nachat to nachaty, prinyat to prinyaty, vzyat to vzyaty. In the -yonny family the stress lands on the suffix, and a stressed 'e' is always written 'yo': reshit to reshyonny, prinesti to prinesyonny, privesti to privedyonny. Say the yo out loud — it carries the stress. This is exactly the B2 pronunciation focus: the same letters, a moved stress, a different word. In nachaty the stress sits on the first syllable, na-, not the middle — the stress is the grammar.","table":{"rows":[["нача́ть → на́чатый <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"начать, начатый\">🔊</button>","begun — stress jumps back to на́"],["приня́ть → при́нятый <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"принять, принятый\">🔊</button>","accepted / adopted — stress on при́"],["реши́ть → решённый <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"решить, решённый\">🔊</button>","solved — stress on ё"],["принести́ → принесённый <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"принести, принесённый\">🔊</button>","brought — stress on ё"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"an adopted (passed) law","en":"<b>при́нятый</b> зако́н","say":"принятый закон"},{"ru":"a problem brought from outside","en":"пробле́ма, <b>принесённая</b> извне́","say":"проблема, принесённая извне"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"нача́тый рома́н","right":"на́чатый рома́н","why":"The passive past participle of нача́ть pulls the stress <b>back</b>: <b>на́</b>чатый. нача́тый (stress on -ча-) isn't the standard form."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> mind where the stress lands.","q":"The participle of <b>приня́ть</b> (“accepted / adopted”) is…","opts":["приня́тый","при́нятый","приня́нный"],"answer":1,"explain":"Stress jumps back: <b>при́нятый</b>. (Its short forms shift again: при́нят, приня́та.)"},{"type":"type","q":"Type the participle of <b>реши́ть</b> (“solved”) — mind the ё:","accept":["решённый","решенный","решённая","решенная"],"placeholder":"реш…нный","explain":"Stress on the suffix → <b>решённый</b> with ё. (Writing «решенный» is a common ё-slip.)"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · It agrees in every case","title":"<em>В напи́санном письме́</em> — the participle bends too","paras":["Like every participle, the passive past agrees with its noun in gender, number and <b>case</b>: напи́санн<b>ое</b> письмо́, в напи́санн<b>ом</b> письме́, о при́нят<b>ом</b> реше́нии, с ку́пленн<b>ыми</b> биле́тами. Get the noun's case, copy it onto the participle.","In speech you can still unfold it: «прочи́танная кни́га» → «кни́га, кото́рую прочита́ли». But this one is common enough — especially the frozen adjectives откры́тый (open), закры́тый (closed), изве́стный (known) — that you'll happily say many of them out loud.","Tomorrow comes its <b>short</b> form — the real workhorse of officialese: <b>напи́сан, напи́сана, напи́сано</b>, and the быть-passive «<b>догово́р был подпи́сан</b>» (“the contract <b>was signed</b>”). One small step from today's напи́санный."],"audio":"Like every participle, the passive past agrees with its noun in gender, number and case: napisannoye pismo, v napisannom pisme, o prinyatom reshenii, s kuplennymi biletami. Get the noun's case, copy it onto the participle. In speech you can still unfold it: prochitannaya kniga becomes kniga, kotoruyu prochitali. But this one is common enough — especially the frozen adjectives otkryty, open; zakryty, closed; izvestny, known — that you'll happily say many of them out loud. Tomorrow comes its short form — the real workhorse of officialese: napisan, napisana, napisano, and the byt-passive dogovor byl podpisan, the contract was signed. One small step from today's napisanny.","examples":[{"ru":"I read about it in the letter I received.","en":"Я прочита́ла об э́том в <b>полу́ченном</b> письме́.","say":"Я прочитала об этом в полученном письме."},{"ru":"We talked about the decision that was made.","en":"Мы говори́ли о <b>при́нятом</b> реше́нии.","say":"Мы говорили о принятом решении."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"в напи́санное письме́","right":"в напи́санном письме́","why":"Prepositional case on both: письме́ → <b>напи́санном</b>. The participle copies its noun's case, just like «но́вом»."}],"tasks":[{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“in the written letter”</b> (prepositional)","want":"в напи́санном письме́","answer":"в напи́санном письме́"},{"type":"choice","q":"“about the decision that was made” (prepositional) —","opts":["о при́нятом реше́нии","о при́нятое реше́ние","о принима́ющем реше́нии"],"answer":0,"explain":"Prepositional + passive past: <b>о при́нятом реше́нии</b>. принима́ющем would be active present (“deciding”)."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Я держу́ в рука́х напи́санный мной перево́д.","sub":"“I'm holding the translation I wrote (written by me)” — one finished result","want":"я держу в руках написанный мной перевод"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Emma has just finished her first big literary translation. She shows Иван the pile of pages — and the letter from the publisher. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Я зако́нчила перево́д! Вот напи́санный мной те́кст.","ru":"I've finished the translation! Here's the text I wrote (written by me)."},{"who":"A","en":"Поздравля́ю! Прочи́танный до конца́ рома́н — э́то тру́дная рабо́та.","ru":"Congratulations! A novel read to the end is hard work."},{"who":"B","en":"Да. И ка́ждое при́нятое реше́ние ва́жно: одно́ неве́рное сло́во меня́ет весь смысл.","ru":"Yes. And every decision made matters: one wrong word changes the whole meaning."},{"who":"A","en":"Покажи́. О, здесь на́чатая глава́ ещё не гото́ва?","ru":"Show me. Oh, is this started chapter not ready yet?"},{"who":"B","en":"Нет, э́то уже́ решённая глава́. Все тру́дные места́ поза́ди.","ru":"No, that's an already-solved chapter. All the hard spots are behind me."},{"who":"A","en":"А э́то что? Полу́ченное из изда́тельства письмо́?","ru":"And what's this? A letter received from the publisher?"},{"who":"B","en":"Да, ку́пленный и́ми перево́д вы́йдет о́сенью. Мой пе́рвый напеча́танный рома́н!","ru":"Yes, the translation they bought will come out in the autumn. My first printed novel!"},{"who":"A","en":"Э́то настоя́щий успе́х. Забы́тый когда́-то писа́тель загово́рит по-ру́сски благодаря́ тебе́.","ru":"This is real success. A once-forgotten writer will speak Russian thanks to you."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve long passive past participles — written, read, opened, solved, received. Watch the double -нн- and the stress jumps. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"напи́санный","ru":"written","ex":"Напи́санное письмо́ лежа́ло на столе́."},{"en":"прочи́танный","ru":"read (through), finished","ex":"Прочи́танная кни́га верну́лась на по́лку."},{"en":"откры́тый","ru":"opened, open","ex":"Откры́тое окно́ впуска́ло све́жий во́здух."},{"en":"решённый","ru":"solved, decided","ex":"Решённый вопро́с бо́льше не беспоко́ил нас."},{"en":"полу́ченный","ru":"received, got","ex":"Полу́ченное письмо́ обра́довало Э́мму."},{"en":"ку́пленный","ru":"bought","ex":"Ку́пленный вчера́ хлеб уже́ ко́нчился."},{"en":"постро́енный","ru":"built","ex":"Постро́енный у реки́ дом о́чень краси́в."},{"en":"при́нятый","ru":"accepted, adopted, taken","ex":"При́нятый зако́н измени́л мно́гое."},{"en":"на́чатый","ru":"begun, started","ex":"На́чатый рома́н на́до дочита́ть."},{"en":"забы́тый","ru":"forgotten","ex":"Забы́тый зо́нтик оста́лся в кафе́."},{"en":"сде́ланный","ru":"done, made","ex":"Сде́ланная рабо́та ра́довала всех."},{"en":"принесённый","ru":"brought","ex":"Принесённый ко́фе бы́стро осты́л."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each finished result to its meaning.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["напи́санное письмо́","the written letter"],["откры́тая дверь","the opened / open door"],["решённый вопро́с","the solved question"],["при́нятый зако́н","the adopted (passed) law"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"The passive past participle of <b>написа́ть</b> is…","opts":["написа́вший","напи́санный","пи́шущий"],"answer":1},{"q":"Verbs in -ить with the stress on the suffix give…","opts":["реши́нный","решённый","ре́шенный"],"answer":1},{"q":"The participle of <b>откры́ть</b> (“opened”) is…","opts":["откры́нный","откры́тый","откры́енный"],"answer":1},{"q":"“in the written letter” (prepositional) —","opts":["в напи́санном письме́","в напи́санное письмо́","в напи́санный письме́"],"answer":0},{"q":"The stress in the participle of <b>нача́ть</b> (“begun”) falls on…","opts":["на́чатый (on на-)","нача́тый (on -ча-)","начаты́й (on -ты)"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's note the evening she finished — read it aloud. Every describing word is a long passive past participle.","title":"Зако́нченный перево́д","sentences":["Напи́санный мной перево́д наконе́ц лежи́т пе́редо мной.","Э́то прочи́танный сто раз, испра́вленный и переде́ланный текст.","Ка́ждое при́нятое реше́ние тепе́рь ка́жется ва́жным.","Полу́ченное из изда́тельства письмо́ обра́довало меня́.","Забы́тый когда́-то рома́н сно́ва найдёт свои́х чита́телей."],"translation":"The translation I wrote finally lies before me. This is a text read a hundred times, corrected and redone. Every decision made now seems important. The letter received from the publisher made me happy. A novel once forgotten will find its readers again."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — describe finished results with passive past participles.","prompt":"Write 3–4 sentences using long-form passive past participles: a letter that was <b>written</b> (напи́санный), a book that was <b>read</b> (прочи́танный), a door <b>opened</b> (откры́тый), a problem <b>solved</b> (решённый), a decision <b>made</b> (при́нятый). Put at least one into a case («in the written letter»).","hint":"Endings: -ать → -нный (напи́санный), -ить → -енный/-ённый (полу́ченный, решённый), -ыть → -тый (откры́тый). Mind the stress jump: на́чатый, при́нятый 🙂","example":"На столе́ лежи́т напи́санное мной письмо́. Прочи́танный рома́н тепе́рь на по́лке. В при́нятом реше́нии нет оши́бки."}},"5":{"day":5,"week":"01","themeRu":"It is written, it was built","themeEn":"Напи́сано, был постро́ен","intro":"«Догово́р подпи́сан.» «Рабо́та вы́полнена.» This is the sound of official Russian — and today it's finally yours. Yesterday you built the long напи́санный; today it slims to a <b>short</b> form that carries a whole sentence: <b>напи́сан, напи́сана, напи́сано</b>. Add <b>быть</b> and you can place it in any time: <b>дом постро́ен</b> (now), <b>дом был постро́ен</b> (past), <b>дом бу́дет постро́ен</b> (future). Эмма's translation contract has just been signed — a day full of documents.","introAudio":"Dogovor podpisan. Rabota vypolnena. This is the sound of official Russian — and today it's finally yours. Yesterday you built the long napisanny; today it slims to a short form that carries a whole sentence: napisan, napisana, napisano. Add byt and you can place it in any time: dom postroyen, now; dom byl postroyen, past; dom budet postroyen, future. Emma's translation contract has just been signed — a day full of documents.","goals":["Make the short passive participle: <b>напи́санный → напи́сан</b> (drops one н)","Agree it in gender and number: <b>напи́сан / напи́сана / напи́сано / напи́саны</b>","Place it in time with быть: <b>дом постро́ен · был постро́ен · бу́дет постро́ен</b>","Master the officialese and dodge the traps: keep был, agree the gender"],"learned":["Made short passive participles: <b>напи́санный → напи́сан</b>","Agreed them in gender and number: <b>напи́сан / напи́сана / напи́сано</b>","Placed them in time with быть: <b>был / — / бу́дет постро́ен</b>","Wrote officialese and kept был, agreed the gender"],"review":{"intro":"The last stop in the participle week — a quick sweep of all four before the short form lands. Passive past, passive present, active past: name each one.","introAudio":"The last stop in the participle week — a quick sweep of all four before the short form lands. Passive past, passive present, active past: name each one.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the passive past (day 4).","q":"“the written letter” (that was written) —","opts":["пи́шущее письмо́","напи́санное письмо́","чита́емое письмо́"],"answer":1,"explain":"Long passive past → <b>напи́санное</b> (yesterday). пи́шущее is active present; чита́емое is passive present."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which means “who is respected” (passive present, day 3)?","opts":["уважа́ющий","уважа́емый","уважа́вший"],"answer":1,"explain":"Passive present → <b>уважа́емый</b>. уважа́ющий is active (who respects); уважа́вший is active past."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build (active past, day 2): <b>“the author who wrote the novel”</b>","want":"написа́вший рома́н а́втор","answer":"написа́вший рома́н а́втор"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The short form — the predicate passive","title":"<em>Напи́санный</em> → <em>напи́сан</em>: “the book is written”","paras":["Yesterday's <b>напи́санный</b> was a full adjective sitting <b>before</b> its noun. Today it slims to a <b>short form</b> that works as a <b>predicate</b> — the “is / was done” at the heart of the sentence: <b>Кни́га напи́сана</b> (“The book is / was written”), <b>Догово́р подпи́сан</b> (“The contract is signed”).","The short form is easy to make and it drops one <b>н</b>: напи́сан<b>н</b>ый → напи́сан. Then it agrees, exactly like a short adjective, in <b>gender and number</b> only — no cases: <b>напи́сан</b> (m), <b>напи́сана</b> (f), <b>напи́сано</b> (n), <b>напи́саны</b> (pl).","This is the true face of the passive in everyday and official Russian. Where English says “the work <b>was done</b>”, “the house <b>is built</b>”, Russian reaches for this short participle: <b>Рабо́та вы́полнена</b>, <b>Дом постро́ен</b>."],"audio":"Yesterday's napisanny was a full adjective sitting before its noun. Today it slims to a short form that works as a predicate — the 'is or was done' at the heart of the sentence: kniga napisana, the book is or was written; dogovor podpisan, the contract is signed. The short form is easy to make and it drops one n: napisanny becomes napisan. Then it agrees, exactly like a short adjective, in gender and number only — no cases: napisan for masculine, napisana for feminine, napisano for neuter, napisany for plural. This is the true face of the passive in everyday and official Russian. Where English says the work was done, the house is built, Russian reaches for this short participle: rabota vypolnena, dom postroyen.","table":{"rows":[["Кни́га напи́сана. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"книга написана\">🔊</button>","The book is / was written. (f)"],["Догово́р подпи́сан. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"договор подписан\">🔊</button>","The contract is signed. (m)"],["Рабо́та вы́полнена. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"работа выполнена\">🔊</button>","The work is done. (f)"],["Все пи́сьма напи́саны. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"все письма написаны\">🔊</button>","All the letters are written. (pl)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"The novel is written in Russian.","en":"Рома́н <b>напи́сан</b> по-ру́сски.","say":"Роман написан по-русски."},{"ru":"The decision has been made.","en":"Реше́ние <b>при́нято</b>.","say":"Решение принято."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Кни́га напи́санна.","right":"Кни́га напи́сана.","why":"The short form drops one н: напи́са<b>н</b>а (one н), not напи́са<b>нн</b>а. The long form keeps -нн-, the short form has -н-."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> predicate = short form.","q":"“The contract is signed.” —","opts":["Догово́р подпи́санный.","Догово́р подпи́сан.","Догово́р подпи́сана."],"answer":1,"explain":"Predicate → short form, masculine: <b>подпи́сан</b>. подпи́санный is the long (attributive) form; подпи́сана is feminine."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word.","q":"Make the short form (fem.) of напи́санная: «Кни́га …»","accept":["написана","написана."],"placeholder":"напи́с…","explain":"Short feminine, one н: <b>напи́сана</b>. «Кни́га напи́сана»."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Present, past, future — with быть","title":"<em>Дом постро́ен · был постро́ен · бу́дет постро́ен</em>","paras":["The short participle names a result; <b>быть</b> puts it in time. In the <b>present</b>, Russian uses <b>no</b> verb (the zero copula you know): <b>Дом постро́ен</b> — “The house is built” (it stands built now).","For the <b>past</b>, add был / была́ / бы́ло / бы́ли — and this is the trap: you must <b>not drop it</b>. <b>Дом был постро́ен</b> год наза́д (“The house was built a year ago”), <b>Кни́га была́ напи́сана</b> в 1869 году́. Without был the sentence loses its past.","For the <b>future</b>, add бу́дет / бу́дут: <b>Дом бу́дет постро́ен</b> к ле́ту (“The house will be built by summer”), <b>Рабо́та бу́дет вы́полнена</b> за́втра. Same participle, three times."],"audio":"The short participle names a result; byt puts it in time. In the present, Russian uses no verb, the zero copula you know: dom postroyen, the house is built, it stands built now. For the past, add byl, byla, bylo, byli — and this is the trap: you must not drop it. Dom byl postroyen god nazad, the house was built a year ago; kniga byla napisana v 1869 godu. Without byl the sentence loses its past. For the future, add budet or budut: dom budet postroyen k letu, the house will be built by summer; rabota budet vypolnena zavtra. Same participle, three times.","table":{"rows":[["Дом постро́ен. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"дом построен\">🔊</button>","The house is built. (present, zero быть)"],["Дом был постро́ен. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"дом был построен\">🔊</button>","The house was built. (past — keep был!)"],["Дом бу́дет постро́ен. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"дом будет построен\">🔊</button>","The house will be built. (future)"],["Кни́га была́ напи́сана. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"книга была написана\">🔊</button>","The book was written. (past, fem)"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"The contract was signed last week.","en":"Догово́р <b>был подпи́сан</b> на про́шлой неде́ле.","say":"Договор был подписан на прошлой неделе."},{"ru":"The work will be done by tomorrow.","en":"Рабо́та <b>бу́дет вы́полнена</b> к за́втрашнему дню.","say":"Работа будет выполнена к завтрашнему дню."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Дом постро́ен год наза́д.","right":"Дом был постро́ен год наза́д.","why":"“a year ago” is <b>past</b> → you need <b>был</b>: Дом <b>был</b> постро́ен. Dropping был leaves it in the present."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> which tense does the time word want?","q":"“The house was built a hundred years ago.” —","opts":["Дом постро́ен сто лет наза́д.","Дом был постро́ен сто лет наза́д.","Дом бу́дет постро́ен сто лет наза́д."],"answer":1,"explain":"Past → keep <b>был</b>: Дом был постро́ен. Without был it reads as present; бу́дет is future."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“The work will be done tomorrow.”</b>","want":"Рабо́та бу́дет вы́полнена за́втра","answer":"Рабо́та бу́дет вы́полнена за́втра"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Agree the gender — and mind the stress","title":"<em>Кни́га напи́сана</em>, <em>вопро́с решён</em>","paras":["The short form must <b>agree in gender</b> with the subject — an English speaker's classic slip. It's <b>кни́га напи́сана</b> (fem), not «напи́сан»; <b>письмо́ напи́сано</b> (neut); <b>пи́сьма напи́саны</b> (pl). Match the subject or it grates.","Stress can jump between the forms, and B2 asks you to hear it: <b>при́нят</b> / принят<b>а́</b> / <b>при́нято</b> / <b>при́няты</b>; <b>на́чат</b> / начат<b>а́</b>. And the -ённый words shorten with a stressed <b>ё → о́</b>: реши́ть → <b>решён</b> / решен<b>а́</b> / решен<b>о́</b> / решен<b>ы́</b> (masculine <b>решён</b> with ё, the rest with -ен-).","Name the doer, as always, in the <b>instrumental</b>: <b>Рома́н напи́сан Толсты́м</b> (“The novel was written by Tolstoy”), <b>Дом постро́ен рабо́чими</b>."],"audio":"The short form must agree in gender with the subject — an English speaker's classic slip. It's kniga napisana, feminine, not napisan; pismo napisano, neuter; pisma napisany, plural. Match the subject or it grates. Stress can jump between the forms, and B2 asks you to hear it: prinyat, prinyata, prinyato, prinyaty; nachat, nachata. And the -yonny words shorten with a stressed yo turning to o: reshit gives reshyon, reshena, resheno, resheny — masculine reshyon with yo, the rest with -en-. Name the doer, as always, in the instrumental: roman napisan Tolstym, the novel was written by Tolstoy; dom postroyen rabochimi.","table":{"rows":[["кни́га напи́сана <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"книга написана\">🔊</button>","the book is written (fem — not напи́сан)"],["вопро́с решён <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"вопрос решён\">🔊</button>","the question is solved (masc, ё)"],["зада́ча решена́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"задача решена\">🔊</button>","the problem is solved (fem, stress shifts to end)"],["рома́н напи́сан Толсты́м <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"роман написан Толстым\">🔊</button>","the novel was written by Tolstoy (doer = instr.)"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"This problem is already solved.","en":"Э́та зада́ча уже́ <b>решена́</b>.","say":"Эта задача уже решена."},{"ru":"The tickets are already sold.","en":"Биле́ты уже́ <b>про́даны</b>.","say":"Билеты уже проданы."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Кни́га напи́сан.","right":"Кни́га напи́сана.","why":"кни́га is feminine, so the participle must be feminine too: <b>напи́сана</b>. напи́сан is masculine."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> agree with the subject's gender.","q":"“The problem is solved.” (зада́ча is feminine) —","opts":["Зада́ча решён.","Зада́ча решена́.","Зада́ча решено́."],"answer":1,"explain":"Feminine subject → <b>решена́</b> (stress on the end). решён is masculine; решено́ is neuter."},{"type":"type","q":"Give the masculine short form of решённый (“solved”) — mind the ё:","accept":["решён","решен"],"placeholder":"реш…н","explain":"Masculine short form → <b>решён</b>, with ё carrying the stress."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · The voice of documents","title":"<em>Догово́р подпи́сан. Рабо́та вы́полнена.</em>","paras":["This short passive is the <b>native tongue of officialese</b> — contracts, notices, reports, forms. It states a result without fuss about <b>who</b> did it: <b>Догово́р подпи́сан</b>, <b>Рабо́та вы́полнена</b>, <b>Реше́ние при́нято</b>, <b>Вход воспрещён</b>. Emma's bureaucracy is built from these.","Two traps to keep close. Don't drop <b>был</b> in the past (<b>Дом был постро́ен</b>, not «Дом постро́ен» for a past event); and always <b>agree the gender</b> (<b>кни́га напи́сана</b>, not напи́сан). Get those two right and you sound like the document itself.","That completes the passive core. Present люби́мый, past напи́санный, and now the short напи́сан / был постро́ен. Tomorrow we tie the bow: the full <b>participial phrase</b> with its commas, and how it swaps back and forth with «кото́рый» — «статья́, напи́санная профе́ссором, вы́шла вчера́»."],"audio":"This short passive is the native tongue of officialese — contracts, notices, reports, forms. It states a result without fuss about who did it: dogovor podpisan, rabota vypolnena, resheniye prinyato, vkhod vospreshchyon. Emma's bureaucracy is built from these. Two traps to keep close. Don't drop byl in the past — dom byl postroyen, not dom postroyen for a past event; and always agree the gender — kniga napisana, not napisan. Get those two right and you sound like the document itself. That completes the passive core. Present lyubimy, past napisanny, and now the short napisan and byl postroyen. Tomorrow we tie the bow: the full participial phrase with its commas, and how it swaps back and forth with 'kotory' — statya, napisannaya professorom, vyshla vchera.","examples":[{"ru":"Notice: The shop is closed.","en":"Объявле́ние: «Магази́н закры́т».","say":"Объявление: магазин закрыт."},{"ru":"Everything is done, the decision is made.","en":"Всё <b>сде́лано</b>, реше́ние <b>при́нято</b>.","say":"Всё сделано, решение принято."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Статья́ напи́сан.","right":"Статья́ напи́сана.","why":"Two reminders in one: agree the gender (статья́ is fem → напи́сана), and for a past event add была́: статья́ <b>была́</b> напи́сана."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> pick the official form.","q":"An official notice: “The museum is closed today.” —","opts":["Музе́й закры́т сего́дня.","Музе́й закры́л сего́дня.","Музе́й закрыва́ющий сего́дня."],"answer":0,"explain":"Short passive → <b>закры́т</b> (“is closed”). закры́л would mean “(someone) closed (something)”; закрыва́ющий is an active participle."},{"type":"type","q":"Complete the officialese: «Реше́ние …» (“The decision has been made”, neut.):","accept":["принято","принято."],"placeholder":"при́…","explain":"Neuter short form → <b>при́нято</b>. «Реше́ние при́нято»."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Догово́р подпи́сан, рабо́та вы́полнена.","sub":"“The contract is signed, the work is done” — pure officialese","want":"договор подписан работа выполнена"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"A day of documents. Emma's publishing contract is signed at last — and Иван has just signed his first work contract too. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Как твои́ дела́? Я слы́шала, что твой контра́кт уже́ подпи́сан.","ru":"How are you? I heard your contract is already signed."},{"who":"A","en":"Да! Всё сде́лано. Догово́р был подпи́сан вчера́ — я на́чал рабо́тать.","ru":"Yes! Everything's done. The contract was signed yesterday — I've started working."},{"who":"B","en":"Поздравля́ю! Мой контра́кт то́же был подпи́сан на про́шлой неде́ле. Рома́н бу́дет напеча́тан о́сенью.","ru":"Congratulations! My contract was also signed last week. The novel will be printed in autumn."},{"who":"A","en":"Как официа́льно всё зву́чит: «Догово́р подпи́сан», «рабо́та вы́полнена».","ru":"How official it all sounds: “The contract is signed”, “the work is done”."},{"who":"B","en":"Э́то язы́к докуме́нтов. В нём ва́жно, что де́йствие завершено́, а не кто его́ сде́лал.","ru":"That's the language of documents. In it what matters is that the action is completed, not who did it."},{"who":"A","en":"Кста́ти, но́вое кафе́ Ле́ны уже́ откры́то. Всё бы́ло постро́ено за ме́сяц.","ru":"By the way, Lena's new café is already open. Everything was built in a month."},{"who":"B","en":"Пра́вда? Зна́чит, реше́ние о второ́м кафе́ при́нято?","ru":"Really? So the decision about a second café has been made?"},{"who":"A","en":"При́нято и уже́ вы́полнено. Ле́на не лю́бит ждать.","ru":"Made — and already carried out. Lena doesn't like to wait."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve short passive participles — the language of contracts and notices. Watch the single -н- and the gender endings. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"напи́сан","ru":"(is / was) written (masc.)","ex":"Э́тот рома́н напи́сан по-ру́сски."},{"en":"напи́сана","ru":"written (fem.)","ex":"Кни́га напи́сана изве́стным а́втором."},{"en":"напи́сано","ru":"written (neut.)","ex":"Письмо́ напи́сано о́чень краси́во."},{"en":"напи́саны","ru":"written (plural)","ex":"Э́ти пи́сьма напи́саны давно́."},{"en":"постро́ен","ru":"(is / was) built","ex":"Дом был постро́ен сто лет наза́д."},{"en":"подпи́сан","ru":"signed","ex":"Догово́р уже́ подпи́сан."},{"en":"вы́полнена","ru":"carried out, fulfilled (fem.)","ex":"Рабо́та вы́полнена в срок."},{"en":"при́нято","ru":"accepted, adopted (neut.)","ex":"Реше́ние при́нято единогла́сно."},{"en":"откры́т","ru":"open(ed)","ex":"Магази́н откры́т до девяти́."},{"en":"закры́т","ru":"closed","ex":"Музе́й сего́дня закры́т."},{"en":"решён","ru":"solved (masc., ё)","ex":"Вопро́с уже́ решён."},{"en":"сде́лано","ru":"done, made (neut.)","ex":"Всё сде́лано во́время."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each short-passive statement to its meaning.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Догово́р подпи́сан.","The contract is signed."],["Кни́га напи́сана.","The book is written. (f)"],["Дом был постро́ен.","The house was built. (past)"],["Вопро́с бу́дет решён.","The question will be solved. (future)"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"The short (masculine) form of напи́санный is…","opts":["напи́сан","напи́сана","напи́санный"],"answer":0},{"q":"“The book was written in 1869.” Choose:","opts":["Кни́га напи́сана в 1869 году́.","Кни́га была́ напи́сана в 1869 году́.","Кни́га напи́санная в 1869 году́."],"answer":1},{"q":"“The problem is solved.” (зада́ча, feminine) —","opts":["Зада́ча решён.","Зада́ча решена́.","Зада́ча решено́."],"answer":1},{"q":"Correct officialese for “The contract is signed”:","opts":["Догово́р подпи́сан.","Догово́р подписа́л.","Догово́р подпи́санный."],"answer":0},{"q":"“The house will be built by summer.” —","opts":["Дом постро́ен к ле́ту.","Дом был постро́ен к ле́ту.","Дом бу́дет постро́ен к ле́ту."],"answer":2}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's note the day the contract came through — read it aloud. Almost every verb is a short passive.","title":"Всё подпи́сано","sentences":["Сего́дня был подпи́сан мой пе́рвый большо́й контра́кт.","Рома́н уже́ переведён и бу́дет напеча́тан о́сенью.","Вся рабо́та вы́полнена, и реше́ние при́нято.","Догово́ры подпи́саны, пи́сьма напи́саны.","За оди́н год сде́лано бо́льше, чем я ожида́ла."],"translation":"Today my first big contract was signed. The novel is already translated and will be printed in autumn. All the work is done, and the decision is made. The contracts are signed, the letters are written. In one year more has been done than I expected."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — write like a document.","prompt":"Write 3–4 sentences in the short passive: something <b>written</b> (напи́сан/напи́сана/напи́сано), <b>built</b> (постро́ен), <b>signed</b> (подпи́сан), <b>done</b> (сде́лано), <b>solved</b> (решён). Use all three tenses: present («Дом постро́ен»), past with был («Дом был постро́ен»), future («Дом бу́дет постро́ен»). Agree the gender!","hint":"The short form drops one н and agrees in gender/number only: напи́сан / напи́сана / напи́сано / напи́саны. Past keeps был/была́; future takes бу́дет. Officialese: «Догово́р подпи́сан» 🙂","example":"Мой перево́д зако́нчен. Догово́р был подпи́сан вчера́, и рома́н бу́дет напеча́тан о́сенью. Вся рабо́та вы́полнена, реше́ние при́нято."}},"6":{"day":6,"week":"01","themeRu":"The one which… in one word","themeEn":"Прича́стный оборо́т и кото́рый","intro":"«Догово́р подпи́сан» closed yesterday — the passive in two words. Now watch the participle bring friends along: «статья́, напи́санная изве́стным профе́ссором» — 'an article written by a famous professor.' That whole tail is a <b>participial phrase</b> (прича́стный оборо́т). Today you build it, punctuate it, and fold a «кото́рый» clause into it — then unfold it back when you speak.","introAudio":"'Dogovor podpisan' closed yesterday — the passive in two words. Now watch the participle bring friends along: 'statya, napisannaya izvestnym professorom' — an article written by a famous professor. That whole tail is a participial phrase, a prichastny oborot. Today you build it, punctuate it, and fold a 'kotory' clause into it — then unfold it back when you speak.","goals":["Grow a participle into a full <b>participial phrase</b>: <b>студе́нт, чита́ющий интере́сную кни́гу</b>","Punctuate it: a phrase <b>after</b> its noun takes commas on both sides — <b>статья́, напи́санная им, интере́сна</b>","Fold a «кото́рый» clause into a participle and <b>unfold</b> it back for speech","Match tense and voice when you convert: <b>кото́рый чита́л → чита́вший</b>, <b>кото́рую написа́ли → напи́санная</b>"],"learned":["Grew participles into full participial phrases: <b>студе́нт, чита́ющий интере́сную кни́гу</b>","Punctuated them: a phrase after its noun takes commas on both sides","Folded «кото́рый» clauses into participles and unfolded them back for speech","Matched tense and voice when converting: <b>кото́рый чита́л → чита́вший</b>"],"review":{"intro":"A minute on the week's participles before we join them into phrases. Build one, agree one, report one.","introAudio":"A minute on the week's participles before we join them into phrases. Build one, agree one, report one.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. All week-1 material.","q":"«кото́рый рабо́тает» in one word (active present) →","opts":["рабо́тавший","рабо́танный","рабо́тающий"],"answer":2,"explain":"Active, present → <b>рабо́тающий</b>. рабо́тавший is the past; рабо́танный isn't a real form here."},{"type":"choice","q":"«письмо́, кото́рое написа́ли» — the passive past participle (neuter) is…","opts":["напи́санный","напи́санное","напи́санная"],"answer":1,"explain":"письмо́ is neuter → <b>напи́санное</b>. The participle agrees in gender, just like an adjective."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build the short passive: <b>“The document is signed.”</b>","want":"Докуме́нт подпи́сан","answer":"Докуме́нт подпи́сан"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · A participle with its own words","title":"<em>Студе́нт, чита́ющий кни́гу</em> — the participial phrase","paras":["A participle isn't limited to standing alone in front of a noun. Like the verb inside it, it can carry its own objects and details — an <b>object</b>, a place, a manner. <b>чита́ющий</b> grows into <b>чита́ющий интере́сную кни́гу</b> (“reading an interesting book”). Participle + everything hanging off it = a <b>participial phrase</b>, in Russian a <b>прича́стный оборо́т</b>.","This is the workhorse of written Russian. One phrase replaces a whole «кото́рый» clause: instead of <b>студе́нт, кото́рый чита́ет интере́сную кни́гу</b>, you write <b>студе́нт, чита́ющий интере́сную кни́гу</b>. Same meaning, tighter and more bookish.","The participle still agrees with its noun in gender, number and case — the tail just travels along behind it. Get the head participle right, and the whole phrase falls into place."],"audio":"A participle isn't limited to standing alone in front of a noun. Like the verb inside it, it can carry its own objects and details — an object, a place, a manner. Chitayushchiy grows into chitayushchiy interesnuyu knigu, reading an interesting book. Participle plus everything hanging off it equals a participial phrase, in Russian a prichastny oborot. This is the workhorse of written Russian. One phrase replaces a whole 'kotory' clause: instead of student, kotory chitayet interesnuyu knigu, you write student, chitayushchiy interesnuyu knigu. Same meaning, tighter and more bookish. The participle still agrees with its noun in gender, number and case — the tail just travels along behind it.","table":{"rows":[["чита́ющий кни́гу студе́нт <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"читающий книгу студент\">🔊</button>","the student reading a book (before the noun)"],["студе́нт, чита́ющий кни́гу <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"студент, читающий книгу\">🔊</button>","the student, reading a book (after the noun)"],["статья́, напи́санная профе́ссором <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"статья, написанная профессором\">🔊</button>","an article written by a professor"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"The woman working in our office speaks three languages.","en":"Же́нщина, <b>рабо́тающая в на́шем о́фисе</b>, говори́т на трёх языка́х.","say":"Женщина, работающая в нашем офисе, говорит на трёх языках."},{"ru":"I'm reading an article written by a famous historian.","en":"Я чита́ю статью́, <b>напи́санную изве́стным исто́риком</b>.","say":"Я читаю статью, написанную известным историком."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"статья́, напи́санный профе́ссором","right":"статья́, напи́санная профе́ссором","why":"The participle agrees with its own noun: <b>статья́</b> is feminine → <b>напи́санная</b>, not напи́санный. However long the tail grows, the head still agrees."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"Which adds a proper participial <b>phrase</b> to «челове́к»?","opts":["челове́к, кото́рый газе́ту","челове́к, чита́ющий газе́ту","челове́к чита́я газе́ту"],"answer":1,"explain":"A participial phrase = participle + its words: <b>чита́ющий газе́ту</b> (reading a newspaper). The first has no verb; чита́я is a gerund (next week)."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“a woman working in the café”</b> (phrase after the noun)","want":"же́нщина, рабо́тающая в кафе́","answer":"же́нщина, рабо́тающая в кафе́"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The comma rule","title":"<em>Статья́, напи́санная им, интере́сна</em> — fence it after the noun","paras":["Position decides punctuation. When the participial phrase comes <b>before</b> its noun, there are <b>no commas</b>: <b>напи́санная им статья́</b> (“the written-by-him article”). It behaves like one long adjective.","When the phrase comes <b>after</b> its noun — the more common, more natural order — you fence it off with a comma on <b>each</b> side: <b>статья́, напи́санная им, интере́сна</b> (“the article, written by him, is interesting”). Both commas are obligatory; the phrase is a self-contained unit dropped into the sentence.","English does the same with “which” clauses: “the article, written by him, is interesting.” Russian just reaches for the participle where English often keeps the full clause."],"audio":"Position decides punctuation. When the participial phrase comes before its noun, there are no commas: napisannaya im statya. It behaves like one long adjective. When the phrase comes after its noun — the more common, more natural order — you fence it off with a comma on each side: statya, napisannaya im, interesna — the article, written by him, is interesting. Both commas are obligatory; the phrase is a self-contained unit dropped into the sentence. English does the same with 'which' clauses. Russian just reaches for the participle where English often keeps the full clause.","table":{"rows":[["напи́санная им статья́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"написанная им статья\">🔊</button>","before the noun — no commas"],["статья́, напи́санная им, интере́сна <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"статья, написанная им, интересна\">🔊</button>","after the noun — commas both sides"],["дом, постро́енный в про́шлом году́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"дом, построенный в прошлом году\">🔊</button>","the house built last year"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"The letter, written in an old-fashioned style, was hard to translate.","en":"Письмо́, <b>напи́санное ста́рым сти́лем</b>, бы́ло тру́дно перевести́.","say":"Письмо, написанное старым стилем, было трудно перевести."},{"ru":"A book read in childhood stays with you forever.","en":"Кни́га, <b>про́читанная в де́тстве</b>, остаётся с тобо́й навсегда́.","say":"Книга, прочитанная в детстве, остаётся с тобой навсегда."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"статья́ напи́санная им интере́сна","right":"статья́, напи́санная им, интере́сна","why":"After the noun the phrase needs a comma <b>before and after</b>. One comma isn't enough — both fences must stand."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Where do the commas go?","opts":["Дом постро́енный в 1950 году́ ещё стои́т.","Дом, постро́енный в 1950 году́, ещё стои́т.","Дом, постро́енный в 1950 году́ ещё стои́т."],"answer":1,"explain":"Phrase after the noun → a comma on each side: <b>Дом, постро́енный…, ещё стои́т.</b>"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the sentence. Stress marks are optional; commas welcome.","q":"Type “the article, written by him, is interesting”:","accept":["статья, написанная им, интересна","статья написанная им интересна","статья, написанная им, интересна."],"placeholder":"статья́, …","explain":"Commas both sides: <b>статья́, напи́санная им, интере́сна</b>."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Статья́, напи́санная им, о́чень интере́сна.","sub":"“The article, written by him, is very interesting” — feel the two commas as pauses","want":"Статья, написанная им, очень интересна."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Which way — «кото́рый» ⇄ participle","title":"<em>кото́рый чита́л → чита́вший</em>, <em>кото́рую написа́ли → напи́санная</em>","paras":["Everything from this week converges here: a participle is a folded «кото́рый» clause, and you convert <b>both ways</b>. To fold, match the clause's <b>tense</b>, <b>aspect</b> and <b>voice</b> onto the participle. Active present «кото́рый чита́ет» → <b>чита́ющий</b>; active past «кото́рый чита́л» → <b>чита́вший</b>; perfective «кото́рый прочита́л» → <b>прочита́вший</b>.","Passive folds the same way, but the clause has an unnamed “them”: «кни́га, кото́рую написа́ли» → <b>напи́санная кни́га</b> (“the written book”). The “who did it” turns instrumental: <b>напи́санная им</b>.","And unfold the other way when you <b>speak</b>. A participle sounds bookish out loud; drop it back to «кото́рый» + the matching verb: <b>чита́вший студе́нт</b> → «студе́нт, <b>кото́рый чита́л</b>». Fold to write, unfold to talk."],"audio":"Everything from this week converges here: a participle is a folded 'kotory' clause, and you convert both ways. To fold, match the clause's tense, aspect and voice onto the participle. Active present kotory chitayet becomes chitayushchiy; active past kotory chital becomes chitavshiy; perfective kotory prochital becomes prochitavshiy. Passive folds the same way, but the clause has an unnamed 'them': kniga, kotoruyu napisali becomes napisannaya kniga, the written book. The 'who did it' turns instrumental: napisannaya im. Unfold the other way when you speak: a participle sounds bookish out loud, so drop it back to 'kotory' plus the matching verb. Fold to write, unfold to talk.","table":{"rows":[["кото́рый чита́ет → чита́ющий <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"который читает, читающий\">🔊</button>","active present"],["кото́рый чита́л → чита́вший <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"который читал, читавший\">🔊</button>","active past"],["кото́рую написа́ли → напи́санная <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"которую написали, написанная\">🔊</button>","passive past"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"The professor who wrote this article teaches at the university.","en":"Профе́ссор, <b>написа́вший э́ту статью́</b>, преподаёт в университе́те.","say":"Профессор, написавший эту статью, преподаёт в университете."},{"ru":"Out loud: “the professor who wrote this article”.","en":"В ре́чи: «профе́ссор, <b>кото́рый написа́л</b> э́ту статью́».","say":"В речи: профессор, который написал эту статью."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"студе́нт, кото́рый чита́л → чита́ющий студе́нт","right":"студе́нт, кото́рый чита́л → чита́вший студе́нт","why":"Match the tense: «кото́рый чита́л» is <b>past</b> → <b>чита́вший</b>, not the present чита́ющий."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Fold it: «кни́га, кото́рую прочита́ли» →","opts":["чита́ющая кни́га","прочита́вшая кни́га","про́читанная кни́га"],"answer":2,"explain":"Passive, perfective, past → <b>про́читанная</b> (“the read book”). чита́ющая / прочита́вшая are <b>active</b> — the book doesn't read."},{"type":"choice","q":"Unfold for speech: «студе́нт, реши́вший зада́чу» → «студе́нт, кото́рый …»","opts":["реши́л зада́чу","реша́ет зада́чу","реша́л зада́чу"],"answer":0,"explain":"реши́вший is active <b>past perfective</b> → «кото́рый <b>реши́л</b> зада́чу»."},{"type":"type","q":"Fold «дом, кото́рый постро́или» into a phrase before «дом» (2 words):","accept":["построенный дом","построенный дом.","дом построенный"],"placeholder":"постро́енный …","explain":"Passive past → <b>постро́енный дом</b>."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Six days in one phrase","title":"<em>Пиши́ оборо́том, говори́ «кото́рым»</em> — the week, assembled","paras":["Step back and see the machine you've built this week. Active present <b>чита́ющий</b>, active past <b>чита́вший / прочита́вший</b>, passive present <b>чита́емый / люби́мый</b>, passive past <b>напи́санный / про́читанный</b>, the short form <b>напи́сан</b> — and now they all live inside the <b>прича́стный оборо́т</b>, fenced with commas when they follow their noun.","Two honest reminders. First, agreement never sleeps: the head participle takes the gender, number and <b>case</b> of its noun, however long the tail grows. Second, <b>register</b>: participial phrases are the voice of writing — articles, documents, literature. In conversation, unfold them into «кото́рый».","That's week one. Tomorrow is a review — no new grammar, just the whole participle family walked through one last time before the gerunds arrive. You've turned six days of forms into a single flexible tool."],"audio":"Step back and see the machine you've built this week. Active present chitayushchiy, active past chitavshiy and prochitavshiy, passive present chitayemy and lyubimy, passive past napisanny and prochitanny, the short form napisan — and now they all live inside the prichastny oborot, fenced with commas when they follow their noun. Two honest reminders. First, agreement never sleeps: the head participle takes the gender, number and case of its noun, however long the tail grows. Second, register: participial phrases are the voice of writing — articles, documents, literature. In conversation, unfold them into 'kotory'. That's week one. Tomorrow is a review — no new grammar, just the whole participle family one last time before the gerunds arrive.","examples":[{"ru":"In the document: “the decision taken at the meeting”.","en":"В докуме́нте: «<b>реше́ние, при́нятое на собра́нии</b>».","say":"В документе: решение, принятое на собрании."},{"ru":"Out loud: “the decision that was taken at the meeting”.","en":"В ре́чи: «реше́ние, <b>кото́рое при́няли</b> на собра́нии».","say":"В речи: решение, которое приняли на собрании."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"You're translating a legal document. Which register fits?","opts":["ме́ры, при́нятые прави́тельством","ме́ры, кото́рые как бы при́няли","ме́ры, что при́няли"],"answer":0,"explain":"Formal/written → the participial phrase: <b>ме́ры, при́нятые прави́тельством</b> (“measures taken by the government”)."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which pair means the same thing?","opts":["чита́вший студе́нт = студе́нт, кото́рый чита́ет","чита́вший студе́нт = студе́нт, кото́рый чита́л","чита́вший студе́нт = студе́нт, кото́рого чита́ют"],"answer":1,"explain":"Active past <b>чита́вший</b> = «кото́рый <b>чита́л</b>»."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Кни́га, напи́санная Ива́ном, уже́ переведена́.","sub":"“The book written by Ivan is already translated” — a phrase and a short passive together","want":"Книга, написанная Иваном, уже переведена."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Emma spreads a page of her translation on Ле́на's café table; Ива́н leans over to read the dense, participle-heavy Russian. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Смотри́, э́то абза́ц, кото́рый я перевела́ вчера́. Сли́шком мно́го прича́стий?","ru":"Look, this is the paragraph I translated yesterday. Too many participles?"},{"who":"A","en":"«Челове́к, вы́шедший из до́ма, уви́дел маши́ну, стоя́вшую у воро́т.» Краси́во, но о́чень кни́жно.","ru":"“The man who left the house saw a car standing at the gate.” Beautiful, but very bookish."},{"who":"B","en":"Зна́ю. В ли́тературе так пи́шут, но е́сли я чита́ю э́то вслух, звучи́т тяжело́.","ru":"I know. In literature they write like that, but if I read it aloud, it sounds heavy."},{"who":"A","en":"Тогда́ разверни́ оборо́т: «челове́к, кото́рый вы́шел из до́ма». В ре́чи так есте́ственнее.","ru":"Then unfold the phrase: “the man who left the house.” In speech that's more natural."},{"who":"B","en":"Да, для перево́да рома́на оста́влю прича́стия, а для диало́га — «кото́рый».","ru":"Yes, for the novel translation I'll keep the participles, and for dialogue — “который.”"},{"who":"A","en":"Кста́ти, статья́, напи́санная Макси́мом для журна́ла, то́же полна́ оборо́тов.","ru":"By the way, the article written by Maxim for the magazine is also full of phrases."},{"who":"B","en":"Твой брат-инжене́р пи́шет статьи́? Прочита́ю обяза́тельно.","ru":"Your engineer brother writes articles? I'll definitely read it."},{"who":"A","en":"Прочита́й. Текст, напи́санный инжене́ром, чита́ется на удивле́ние легко́.","ru":"Read it. A text written by an engineer reads surprisingly easily."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for turning clauses into phrases and back. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"прича́стный оборо́т","ru":"a participial phrase","ex":"Прича́стный оборо́т выделя́ется запяты́ми."},{"en":"напи́санный","ru":"written","ex":"Напи́санный им рома́н уже́ переведён."},{"en":"постро́енный","ru":"built","ex":"Дом, постро́енный здесь, о́чень ста́рый."},{"en":"вы́шедший","ru":"who came out / left","ex":"Челове́к, вы́шедший пе́рвым, ждал нас."},{"en":"стоя́вший","ru":"who was standing","ex":"Маши́на, стоя́вшая у воро́т, уе́хала."},{"en":"запята́я","ru":"a comma","ex":"Здесь ну́жно поста́вить запяту́ю."},{"en":"выделя́ть","ru":"to set off, mark out","ex":"Оборо́т выделя́ют запяты́ми."},{"en":"кни́жный","ru":"bookish, literary","ex":"Э́то о́чень кни́жный стиль."},{"en":"абза́ц","ru":"a paragraph","ex":"Пе́рвый абза́ц са́мый ва́жный."},{"en":"фра́за","ru":"a phrase, sentence","ex":"Дли́нная фра́за трудна́ для перево́да."},{"en":"перево́д","ru":"a translation","ex":"Мой перево́д почти́ гото́в."},{"en":"изве́стный","ru":"famous, well-known","ex":"Э́то статья́ изве́стного а́втора."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the participial phrase to its meaning.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["статья́, напи́санная им","the article written by him"],["дом, постро́енный в 1950 году́","the house built in 1950"],["кни́га, про́читанная за ночь","the book read in one night"],["студе́нт, реши́вший зада́чу","the student who solved the problem"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"Fold: «студе́нт, кото́рый чита́ет» →","opts":["чита́вший студе́нт","чита́ющий студе́нт","про́читанный студе́нт"],"answer":1},{"q":"Which is punctuated correctly?","opts":["Статья́, напи́санная им, интере́сна.","Статья́ напи́санная им интере́сна.","Статья́, напи́санная им интере́сна."],"answer":0},{"q":"Unfold for speech: «дом, постро́енный в про́шлом году́» → «дом, кото́рый …»","opts":["постро́или в про́шлом году́","стро́ят в про́шлом году́","постро́ят в про́шлом году́"],"answer":0},{"q":"«кни́га, кото́рую написа́ли» in one word →","opts":["написа́вшая кни́га","напи́санная кни́га","пи́шущая кни́га"],"answer":1},{"q":"In everyday <b>speech</b>, «чита́вший студе́нт» usually becomes…","opts":["студе́нт, кото́рый чита́л","студе́нт, кото́рый чита́ет","студе́нт, кото́рый прочита́ет"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's note to herself after a day of editing — read it aloud. Notice how each phrase could unfold into «кото́рый».","title":"Оди́н абза́ц, два го́лоса","sentences":["Сего́дня я редакти́ровала оди́н тру́дный абза́ц.","Текст, напи́санный ста́рым а́втором, по́лон прича́стных оборо́тов.","Фра́за «челове́к, стоя́вший у окна́» звучи́т краси́во, но кни́жно.","Когда́ я чита́ю вслух, я развора́чиваю оборо́т в «кото́рый».","Для рома́на я оставля́ю прича́стия, а геро́ям даю́ живу́ю речь."],"translation":"Today I edited one difficult paragraph. The text, written by an old author, is full of participial phrases. The phrase “a man standing by the window” sounds beautiful, but bookish. When I read aloud, I unfold the phrase into “который.” For the novel I keep the participles, but I give the characters living speech."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — turn a «кото́рый» clause into a phrase, then punctuate it.","prompt":"Write 3–4 sentences describing things or people, using at least two participial phrases <b>after</b> the noun (with commas): e.g. «кни́га, напи́санная …», «челове́к, живу́щий …». Then take one and unfold it into a «кото́рый» clause, as you would in speech.","hint":"After the noun, fence the phrase with commas on both sides: <b>статья́, напи́санная им, интере́сна</b>. Match tense and voice when you fold 🙂","example":"На столе́ лежи́т кни́га, напи́санная ру́сским писа́телем. Челове́к, чита́ющий её, — мой хоро́ший друг. В ре́чи звучи́т про́ще: «кни́га, кото́рую написа́л ру́сский писа́тель»."}},"7":{"day":7,"week":"01","themeRu":"Review 1","themeEn":"Повторе́ние 1","intro":"One week ago «чита́ющий» was a word you only recognised. Now you build the whole family — active and passive, present and past — and fold a «кото́рый» clause into a single word. Before the gerunds arrive, let's line all four participles up side by side and watch them work together. Nothing new today: just the quiet click of a system locking into place.","introAudio":"One week ago chitayushchiy was a word you only recognised. Now you build the whole family — active and passive, present and past — and fold a 'kotory' clause into a single word. Before the gerunds arrive, let's line all four participles up side by side and watch them work together. Nothing new today: just the quiet click of a system locking into place.","goals":["Line up all four participles: active <b>чита́ющий / чита́вший</b>, passive <b>чита́емый / про́читанный</b>","Choose the form by <b>voice</b> (who acts?) and <b>tense/aspect</b> (when? finished?)","Switch between the full participle and the short passive: <b>напи́санный → напи́сан</b>","Fold «кото́рый» into a participial phrase and unfold it back for speech"],"learned":["Lined up all four participles: active <b>чита́ющий / чита́вший</b>, passive <b>чита́емый / про́читанный</b>","Chose the form by voice and tense/aspect","Switched between the full participle and the short passive: <b>напи́санный → напи́сан</b>","Folded «кото́рый» into participial phrases and unfolded them back for speech"],"review":{"intro":"Straight into it — a pull from across the week. Build one participle, agree one, report one, and fold one into a phrase.","introAudio":"Straight into it — a pull from across the week. Build one participle, agree one, report one, and fold one into a phrase.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. All week-1 material.","q":"«кото́рый живёт здесь» in one word (active present) →","opts":["живу́щий","жи́вший","живо́й"],"answer":0,"explain":"Active, present → <b>живу́щий</b> (“the one living”). жи́вший is past; живо́й just means “alive”."},{"type":"choice","q":"«письмо́, кото́рое написа́ли» (passive past, neuter) →","opts":["написа́вшее письмо́","пи́шущее письмо́","напи́санное письмо́"],"answer":2,"explain":"Passive, past, neuter → <b>напи́санное</b>. The others are active (написа́вшее, пи́шущее)."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build the short passive: <b>“The work is done.”</b>","want":"Рабо́та вы́полнена","answer":"Рабо́та вы́полнена"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks are optional.","q":"Type the passive past participle “written” (masc.) from написа́ть:","accept":["написанный","написанный."],"placeholder":"напи́сан…","explain":"написа́ть → <b>напи́санный</b> (full form)."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Two questions pick the form","title":"<em>Кто де́йствует? Когда́?</em> — voice × tense","paras":["Every participle answers two questions. <b>Voice</b>: does the noun <b>do</b> the action (active) or <b>receive</b> it (passive)? <b>Tense/aspect</b>: is it happening now (present) or already done (past)? Two questions, two axes, four forms — the whole week on one grid.","Active present <b>чита́ющий</b> (“the one reading”), active past <b>чита́вший / прочита́вший</b> (“the one who read”). Passive present <b>чита́емый / люби́мый</b> (“the one being read / loved”), passive past <b>про́читанный / напи́санный</b> (“the one that was read / written”).","Read the noun first: if it acts, go active; if something is done to it, go passive. Then place it in time. That single reflex — voice, then time — chooses every participle you'll ever build."],"audio":"Every participle answers two questions. Voice: does the noun do the action, active, or receive it, passive? Tense and aspect: is it happening now, present, or already done, past? Two questions, two axes, four forms — the whole week on one grid. Active present chitayushchiy, the one reading; active past chitavshiy and prochitavshiy, the one who read. Passive present chitayemy and lyubimy, the one being read or loved; passive past prochitanny and napisanny, the one that was read or written. Read the noun first: if it acts, go active; if something is done to it, go passive. Then place it in time. Voice, then time, chooses every participle you'll ever build.","table":{"rows":[["Active, present — чита́ющий <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"читающий\">🔊</button>","the one reading (who reads)"],["Active, past — чита́вший / прочита́вший <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"читавший, прочитавший\">🔊</button>","the one who read"],["Passive, present — чита́емый, люби́мый <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"читаемый, любимый\">🔊</button>","the one being read / loved"],["Passive, past — про́читанный, напи́санный <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"прочитанный, написанный\">🔊</button>","the one that was read / written"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"A reading student (active) and a book being read (passive) — same root, opposite voice.","en":"<b>Чита́ющий</b> студе́нт и <b>чита́емая</b> кни́га — оди́н ко́рень, ра́зный зало́г.","say":"Читающий студент и читаемая книга — один корень, разный залог."},{"ru":"The author who wrote the novel; the novel written by the author.","en":"А́втор, <b>написа́вший</b> рома́н; рома́н, <b>напи́санный</b> а́втором.","say":"Автор, написавший роман; роман, написанный автором."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"люби́мый учи́тель = учи́тель, кото́рый лю́бит","right":"люби́мый учи́тель = учи́тель, кото́рого лю́бят","why":"<b>люби́мый</b> is <b>passive</b> — “the one who is loved”, «кото́рого лю́бят». The active “who loves” is <b>лю́бящий</b>. Voice flips the meaning."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"The novel <b>being translated</b> right now (passive present) is…","opts":["переводи́мый рома́н","переводя́щий рома́н","переведённый рома́н"],"answer":0,"explain":"Passive present → <b>переводи́мый</b> (“being translated”). переводя́щий is active; переведённый is done (past)."},{"type":"choice","q":"Active past, perfective — “the student who solved (and finished) the task”:","opts":["реша́вший студе́нт","реши́вший студе́нт","решённый студе́нт"],"answer":1,"explain":"Finished → perfective active past <b>реши́вший</b>. реша́вший is imperfective (was solving); решённый is passive (the solved task)."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Long, short, and tensed with быть","title":"<em>Напи́санный → напи́сан → был напи́сан</em>","paras":["The passive past participle has two shapes. The <b>full</b> form is an adjective naming a thing: <b>напи́санное письмо́</b> (“a written letter”). The <b>short</b> form is a predicate stating what happened: <b>письмо́ напи́сано</b> (“the letter is written”). Full describes; short reports.","The short form agrees only in gender and number: <b>напи́сан</b> (m), <b>напи́сана</b> (f), <b>напи́сано</b> (n), <b>напи́саны</b> (pl). Watch the shifting stress you met on days 4–5: <b>решён, решена́, решено́</b>.","Time rides on <b>быть</b>. Present drops it — <b>дом постро́ен</b> (“the house is built”); past adds <b>был / была́ / бы́ло</b> — <b>дом был постро́ен</b> (“was built”); future adds <b>бу́дет</b> — <b>дом бу́дет постро́ен</b>. Never drop <b>был</b> in the past."],"audio":"The passive past participle has two shapes. The full form is an adjective naming a thing: napisannoye pismo, a written letter. The short form is a predicate stating what happened: pismo napisano, the letter is written. Full describes; short reports. The short form agrees only in gender and number: napisan, napisana, napisano, napisany. Watch the shifting stress from days four and five: reshyon, reshena, resheno. Time rides on byt. Present drops it: dom postroyen, the house is built. Past adds byl, byla or bylo: dom byl postroyen, was built. Future adds budet: dom budet postroyen. Never drop byl in the past.","table":{"rows":[["напи́санное письмо́ → письмо́ напи́сано <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"написанное письмо, письмо написано\">🔊</button>","full (describes) → short (reports)"],["дом постро́ен / дом был постро́ен <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"дом построен, дом был построен\">🔊</button>","present (no быть) / past (был)"],["рабо́та бу́дет вы́полнена <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"работа будет выполнена\">🔊</button>","future (бу́дет)"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"The contract is signed, the work is done.","en":"Догово́р <b>подпи́сан</b>, рабо́та <b>вы́полнена</b>.","say":"Договор подписан, работа выполнена."},{"ru":"This house was built a hundred years ago.","en":"Э́тот дом <b>был постро́ен</b> сто лет наза́д.","say":"Этот дом был построен сто лет назад."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"дом постро́ен год наза́д","right":"дом был постро́ен год наза́д","why":"A past event needs <b>был</b>: <b>дом был постро́ен год наза́д</b>. Without был it reads as a present state (“the house is built”)."}],"tasks":[{"type":"type","q":"Type the short passive (fem.): «Кни́га …» (“the book is written”):","accept":["написана","написана."],"placeholder":"напи́с…","explain":"Feminine short form: <b>напи́сана</b>."},{"type":"choice","q":"“The letter will be translated tomorrow.” —","opts":["Письмо́ переведено́ за́втра.","Письмо́ бу́дет переведено́ за́втра.","Письмо́ был переведено́ за́втра."],"answer":1,"explain":"Future → <b>бу́дет переведено́</b>. Present has no быть; «был» is past (and the wrong gender here)."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Write it folded, speak it unfolded","title":"<em>Статья́, напи́санная им</em> ⇄ <em>статья́, кото́рую он написа́л</em>","paras":["The participle's real power is the <b>phrase</b> — прича́стный оборо́т. It folds a whole «кото́рый» clause into a compact unit and, when it follows its noun, wears a comma on each side: <b>статья́, напи́санная им, интере́сна</b>.","To convert, keep three things aligned: <b>voice</b> (active vs passive), <b>tense/aspect</b>, and <b>agreement</b> (the head participle takes its noun's gender, number and case). «кото́рый чита́ет» → чита́ющий; «кото́рую написа́ли» → напи́санная.","Register has the last word. Participial phrases are the sound of <b>writing</b>; in speech you unfold them into «кото́рый». Write «дом, постро́енный в 1950 году́»; say «дом, кото́рый постро́или в 1950 году́». One idea, two registers — and now both are yours."],"audio":"The participle's real power is the phrase, the prichastny oborot. It folds a whole 'kotory' clause into a compact unit and, when it follows its noun, wears a comma on each side: statya, napisannaya im, interesna. To convert, keep three things aligned: voice, active versus passive; tense and aspect; and agreement — the head participle takes its noun's gender, number and case. Kotory chitayet becomes chitayushchiy; kotoruyu napisali becomes napisannaya. Register has the last word. Participial phrases are the sound of writing; in speech you unfold them into 'kotory'. Write dom, postroyenny v 1950 godu; say dom, kotory postroili v 1950 godu. One idea, two registers — and now both are yours.","table":{"rows":[["студе́нт, кото́рый чита́ет → чита́ющий студе́нт <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"студент, который читает, читающий студент\">🔊</button>","fold (to write)"],["напи́санная им статья́ → статья́, кото́рую он написа́л <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"написанная им статья, статья, которую он написал\">🔊</button>","unfold (to speak)"],["дом, постро́енный в 1950 году́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"дом, построенный в 1950 году\">🔊</button>","phrase after the noun — commas"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"In writing: “the measures taken by the government”.","en":"На письме́: «<b>ме́ры, при́нятые прави́тельством</b>».","say":"На письме: меры, принятые правительством."},{"ru":"Out loud: “the measures that the government took”.","en":"Вслух: «ме́ры, <b>кото́рые при́няло прави́тельство</b>».","say":"Вслух: меры, которые приняло правительство."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"дом, постро́енный в 1950 году́ ещё стои́т","right":"дом, постро́енный в 1950 году́, ещё стои́т","why":"The phrase after the noun closes with a <b>second</b> comma too: …году́<b>,</b> ещё стои́т. Both fences, always."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Fold for a formal report: «лю́ди, кото́рые живу́т в го́роде» →","opts":["живу́щие в го́роде лю́ди","жи́вшие в го́роде лю́ди","живу́т в го́роде лю́ди"],"answer":0,"explain":"Present active → <b>живу́щие</b>. жи́вшие is past; the third isn't a participle at all."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Дом, постро́енный в про́шлом году́, уже́ прода́н.","sub":"“The house built last year is already sold” — a phrase plus a short passive","want":"Дом, построенный в прошлом году, уже продан."},{"type":"build","q":"Build (unfold for speech): «студе́нт, кото́рый прочита́л кни́гу»","want":"студе́нт, кото́рый прочита́л кни́гу","answer":"студе́нт, кото́рый прочита́л кни́гу"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Ка́тя runs into Эмма at the café, curious about the big literary translation. A week of participles, summed up over tea. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Э́мма, как твой большо́й перево́д? Всё ещё му́чаешься с прича́стиями?","ru":"Emma, how's your big translation? Still struggling with participles?"},{"who":"B","en":"Уже́ нет! Тепе́рь я их не про́сто узнаю́ — я их сама́ стро́ю.","ru":"Not any more! Now I don't just recognise them — I build them myself."},{"who":"A","en":"Пра́вда? Приведи́ приме́р.","ru":"Really? Give me an example."},{"who":"B","en":"«Челове́к, чита́ющий газе́ту» — э́то тот, кото́рый чита́ет. А «напи́санное письмо́» — то, кото́рое написа́ли.","ru":"“A man reading a newspaper” — that's the one who reads. And “a written letter” — the one they wrote."},{"who":"A","en":"А ра́зница ме́жду «лю́бящий» и «люби́мый»?","ru":"And the difference between “лю́бящий” and “люби́мый”?"},{"who":"B","en":"«Лю́бящий» — кто лю́бит, а «люби́мый» — кого́ лю́бят. Оди́н акти́вный, друго́й — пасси́вный.","ru":"“Лю́бящий” is who loves, and “люби́мый” is who is loved. One active, the other passive."},{"who":"A","en":"Ты за неде́лю ста́ла настоя́щим фило́логом.","ru":"In a week you've become a real philologist."},{"who":"B","en":"В письме́ — да. А вслух я всё равно́ говорю́ «кото́рый» — так есте́ственнее.","ru":"In writing, yes. But out loud I still say “который” — it's more natural."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"The whole participle family, plus the words that name it. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"чита́ющий","ru":"reading (active present)","ex":"Чита́ющий студе́нт не слы́шал звонка́."},{"en":"чита́вший","ru":"who was reading (active past)","ex":"Студе́нт, чита́вший всю ночь, уста́л."},{"en":"чита́емый","ru":"being read (passive present)","ex":"Э́то са́мая чита́емая кни́га го́да."},{"en":"люби́мый","ru":"beloved, favourite (passive present)","ex":"Э́то мой люби́мый а́втор."},{"en":"лю́бящий","ru":"loving (active present)","ex":"Она́ — лю́бящая мать."},{"en":"напи́санный","ru":"written (passive past)","ex":"Напи́санный текст ну́жно прове́рить."},{"en":"про́читанный","ru":"read (passive past)","ex":"Про́читанная кни́га лежи́т на по́лке."},{"en":"прочита́вший","ru":"who read (active past)","ex":"Челове́к, прочита́вший рома́н, всё по́нял."},{"en":"напи́сан","ru":"is written (short passive)","ex":"Догово́р уже́ напи́сан."},{"en":"постро́ен","ru":"is built (short passive)","ex":"Дом был постро́ен давно́."},{"en":"зало́г","ru":"voice (grammar)","ex":"Акти́вный и́ли пасси́вный зало́г?"},{"en":"прича́стие","ru":"a participle","ex":"Прича́стие — отглаго́льное прилага́тельное."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["чита́ющий студе́нт","the student who is reading"],["напи́санное письмо́","the written letter"],["люби́мый а́втор","a favourite author"],["дом постро́ен","the house is built"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Eight questions — one or two from every day this week. Pass this and the participle is yours.","items":[{"q":"«кото́рый чита́ет» in one word (active present) →","opts":["чита́вший","чита́ющий","чита́емый"],"answer":1},{"q":"«кото́рый прочита́л» (active past, finished) →","opts":["прочита́вший","чита́ющий","про́читанный"],"answer":0},{"q":"«кни́га, кото́рую чита́ют» (passive present) →","opts":["чита́ющая кни́га","чита́емая кни́га","чита́вшая кни́га"],"answer":1},{"q":"«письмо́, кото́рое написа́ли» (passive past) →","opts":["написа́вшее письмо́","напи́санное письмо́","пи́шущее письмо́"],"answer":1},{"q":"“The work is done.” (short passive) —","opts":["Рабо́та вы́полнена.","Рабо́та вы́полнен.","Рабо́та вы́полнено."],"answer":0},{"q":"“The house was built long ago.” —","opts":["Дом постро́ен давно́.","Дом был постро́ен давно́.","Дом бу́дет постро́ен давно́."],"answer":1},{"q":"Which is punctuated correctly?","opts":["статья́, напи́санная им, интере́сна","статья́ напи́санная им интере́сна","статья́, напи́санная им интере́сна"],"answer":0},{"q":"In speech, «дом, постро́енный в 1950 году́» becomes…","opts":["дом, кото́рый постро́или в 1950 году́","дом, кото́рый стро́ит в 1950 году́","дом, кото́рый постро́ят в 1950 году́"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Эмма looks back at her week in a short note — every participle from the week is hiding in it. Read it aloud.","title":"Что я поняла́ за неде́лю","sentences":["За э́ту неде́лю прича́стие ста́ло мои́м инструме́нтом.","Я ви́жу зало́г: кто де́йствует и над кем де́йствуют.","Чита́ющий челове́к де́йствует сам; чита́емая кни́га — нет.","Напи́санный текст я разбира́ю на «кото́рый», когда́ чита́ю вслух.","В докуме́нтах пишу́ «догово́р подпи́сан», а в разгово́ре — про́ще."],"translation":"This week the participle became my tool. I see voice: who acts and who is acted upon. A reading person acts himself; a book being read does not. A written text I break into “который” when I read aloud. In documents I write “the contract is signed,” and in conversation — more simply."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — put the whole participle family to work in one short paragraph.","prompt":"Write 4–5 sentences about a book, a project or a place, using at least one <b>active</b> participle (чита́ющий / чита́вший), one <b>passive</b> participle (напи́санный / про́читанный), and one <b>short</b> passive (напи́сан, постро́ен). Add one participial phrase with commas.","hint":"Ask two questions each time: who acts (active vs passive) and when (present vs past). Fence a phrase after its noun with commas 🙂","example":"Кни́га, напи́санная ру́сским писа́телем, лежи́т на моём столе́. Челове́к, прочита́вший её, сове́тует её всем. Перево́д уже́ сде́лан, и текст почти́ гото́в."}},"8":{"day":8,"week":"02","themeRu":"Doing while doing","themeEn":"Чита́я, ду́мая","intro":"Эмма translates with music on, a cup of tea going cold, one eye on the street — three things at once. Russian has a single elegant form for exactly that: the <b>gerund</b> (дееприча́стие). «Чита́я, она́ ду́мала» — 'Reading, she was thinking.' One subject, two actions, the same moment. Where the participle was an adjective, this one is an adverb — and today you start making them.","introAudio":"Emma translates with music on, a cup of tea going cold, one eye on the street — three things at once. Russian has a single elegant form for exactly that: the gerund, the deyeprichastie. Chitaya, ona dumala — reading, she was thinking. One subject, two actions, the same moment. Where the participle was an adjective, this one is an adverb — and today you start making them.","goals":["Build an imperfective gerund from the present stem: <b>чита́ют → чита́я</b>, <b>ду́мают → ду́мая</b>","Use it for a <b>simultaneous</b> action by the <b>same</b> subject: <b>чита́я, она́ ду́мала</b>","Keep it <b>invariable</b> — a gerund never changes its ending","Tell it apart from the participle: adverb-of-verb (how/when) vs adjective-of-verb (which)"],"learned":["Built imperfective gerunds from the present stem: <b>чита́ют → чита́я</b>","Used them for simultaneous actions by the same subject: <b>чита́я, она́ ду́мала</b>","Kept them invariable — a gerund never changes its ending","Told them apart from participles: adverb-of-verb vs adjective-of-verb"],"review":{"intro":"One more look at the participles before we meet their cousin, the gerund. Fold one, report one, agree one.","introAudio":"One more look at the participles before we meet their cousin, the gerund. Fold one, report one, agree one.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. Week-1 participles.","q":"«кни́га, кото́рую написа́ли» in one word →","opts":["напи́санная кни́га","написа́вшая кни́га","пи́шущая кни́га"],"answer":0,"explain":"Passive past → <b>напи́санная</b> (“the written book”). The others are active — the book doesn't write."},{"type":"choice","q":"The short passive: “the letter is written” —","opts":["письмо́ напи́сан","письмо́ напи́сано","письмо́ напи́сана"],"answer":1,"explain":"письмо́ is neuter → <b>напи́сано</b>. Agree the short form in gender: напи́сан (m), напи́сана (f), напи́сано (n)."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build (fold into one phrase): <b>“the student who is reading”</b>","want":"чита́ющий студе́нт","answer":"чита́ющий студе́нт"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The gerund — an action alongside an action","title":"<em>Чита́я, она́ ду́мала</em> — same person, same moment","paras":["Meet the <b>gerund</b> — in Russian the <b>дееприча́стие</b>. Where a participle turns a verb into an <b>adjective</b> (describing a noun), a gerund turns a verb into an <b>adverb</b> (describing another verb). It answers “doing <b>what</b> at the same time?” <b>Чита́я</b>, она́ ду́мала — “Reading, she was thinking.”","Two rules are baked in from the start. One <b>subject</b>: the same person does both the gerund's action and the main verb's — <b>она́</b> reads and <b>она́</b> thinks. One <b>time</b>: an imperfective gerund shows an action happening <b>simultaneously</b> with the main one, the background hum under the main event.","English hides this under a bare “-ing”: “Reading, she thought…”. Russian marks it cleanly with a special <b>invariable</b> form — and, always, a comma."],"audio":"Meet the gerund — in Russian the deyeprichastie. Where a participle turns a verb into an adjective describing a noun, a gerund turns a verb into an adverb describing another verb. It answers: doing what at the same time? Chitaya, ona dumala — reading, she was thinking. Two rules are baked in from the start. One subject: the same person does both the gerund's action and the main verb's — ona reads and ona thinks. One time: an imperfective gerund shows an action happening simultaneously with the main one, the background hum under the main event. English hides this under a bare -ing: reading, she thought. Russian marks it cleanly with a special invariable form — and, always, a comma.","table":{"rows":[["Чита́я, она́ ду́мала. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"читая, она думала\">🔊</button>","Reading, she was thinking. (both = she)"],["Он рабо́тал, слу́шая му́зыку. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"он работал, слушая музыку\">🔊</button>","He worked, listening to music."],["Улыба́ясь, она́ вошла́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"улыбаясь, она вошла\">🔊</button>","Smiling, she came in."]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Reading the newspaper, Ivan drank coffee.","en":"<b>Чита́я</b> газе́ту, Ива́н пил ко́фе.","say":"Читая газету, Иван пил кофе."},{"ru":"She translated the text, listening to Russian radio.","en":"Она́ переводи́ла текст, <b>слу́шая</b> ру́сское ра́дио.","say":"Она переводила текст, слушая русское радио."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Чита́я, она́ прочита́ла всё письмо́.","right":"Чита́я, она́ ду́мала о письме́.","why":"The imperfective gerund is for a <b>simultaneous</b>, ongoing action — «reading <b>while</b> thinking». For “having read, then…” you need the perfective gerund (tomorrow)."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"Which means “Reading, she smiled” (same person, same time)?","opts":["Чита́ющая, она́ улыба́лась.","Чита́я, она́ улыба́лась.","Она́ чита́ла и улыба́лась кни́гу."],"answer":1,"explain":"The gerund <b>чита́я</b> = “while reading”. чита́ющая is a participle (an adjective); the third is just clumsy."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“Working, he was singing.”</b>","want":"Рабо́тая, он пел","answer":"Рабо́тая, он пел"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The recipe — они́-stem plus -я","title":"<em>чита́ю(т) → чита́я</em>, <em>говоря́(т) → говоря́</em>","paras":["Building an imperfective gerund is a one-step move, and it starts from the same place as the participle: the <b>они́</b> form. Take the present-tense stem (drop the -ют/-ут/-ят/-ат) and add <b>-я</b>: чита́ют → <b>чита́я</b>, ду́мают → <b>ду́мая</b>, говоря́т → <b>говоря́</b>, иду́т → <b>идя́</b>.","After the hushers <b>ж, ш, ч, щ</b> the ending is spelled <b>-а</b> (same sound): спеша́т → <b>спеша́</b>, слы́шат → <b>слы́ша</b>. Reflexive verbs keep <b>-сь</b>: улыба́ются → <b>улыба́ясь</b>, встреча́ются → <b>встреча́ясь</b>.","Two you just memorise: <b>быть → бу́дучи</b> (“being”), and note that some verbs simply don't form one (пить, писа́ть, ждать) — there Russians reach for «когда́». One irregular worth having ready: <b>бу́дучи студе́нтом</b>, “being a student…”."],"audio":"Building an imperfective gerund is a one-step move, and it starts from the same place as the participle: the oni form. Take the present-tense stem, drop the -yut, -ut, -yat or -at, and add -ya: chitayut becomes chitaya, dumayut becomes dumaya, govoryat becomes govorya, idut becomes idya. After the hushers zh, sh, ch, shch the ending is spelled -a, same sound: speshat becomes spesha, slyshat becomes slysha. Reflexive verbs keep -s: ulybayutsya becomes ulybayas, vstrechayutsya becomes vstrechayas. Two you just memorise: byt becomes buduchi, being; and note that some verbs simply don't form one — pit, pisat, zhdat — there Russians reach for kogda.","table":{"rows":[["чита́ют → чита́я <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"читают, читая\">🔊</button>","read → reading"],["говоря́т → говоря́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"говорят, говоря\">🔊</button>","speak → speaking"],["спеша́т → спеша́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"спешат, спеша\">🔊</button>","hurry → hurrying (-а after ш)"],["улыба́ются → улыба́ясь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"улыбаются, улыбаясь\">🔊</button>","smile → smiling (reflexive -сь)"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"Hurrying to work, she forgot her phone.","en":"<b>Спеша́</b> на рабо́ту, она́ забы́ла телефо́н.","say":"Спеша на работу, она забыла телефон."},{"ru":"Being a student, Ivan read a great deal.","en":"<b>Бу́дучи</b> студе́нтом, Ива́н мно́го чита́л.","say":"Будучи студентом, Иван много читал."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"чита́ючи","right":"чита́я","why":"The living ending is <b>-я</b> (or -а after ж/ш/ч/щ): чита́ют → <b>чита́я</b>. Forms like «чита́ючи» are archaic."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"From <b>слу́шают</b>, the gerund “listening” is…","opts":["слу́шав","слу́шая","слу́шающи"],"answer":1,"explain":"Present stem слу́ша- + -я → <b>слу́шая</b>. слу́шав is a perfective-style ending (tomorrow)."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks are optional.","q":"Make the imperfective gerund from <b>ду́мают</b> (“thinking”):","accept":["думая","думая."],"placeholder":"ду́ма…","explain":"ду́мают → <b>ду́мая</b>."},{"type":"choice","q":"The gerund of <b>говори́ть</b> (говоря́т) is…","opts":["говоря́","говоря́щий","говори́вший"],"answer":0,"explain":"говоря́т → <b>говоря́</b>. говоря́щий / говори́вший are participles (adjectives)."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The subject rule and the comma","title":"<em>Одно́ подлежа́щее</em> — one doer for both actions","paras":["The gerund has no subject of its own — it <b>borrows</b> the subject of the main verb. So before you use one, check: does the same person do both actions? <b>Чита́я</b>, Ива́н <b>ду́мал</b> — Ivan reads, Ivan thinks. ✓ If the two doers differ, the gerund is wrong (that's tomorrow's big trap).","A gerund and its phrase (<b>дееприча́стный оборо́т</b>) are <b>always</b> set off with commas, wherever they sit: <b>Чита́я письмо́,</b> она́ пла́кала. Она́ пла́кала, <b>чита́я письмо́.</b> Both are fine; the comma travels with the gerund.","Because it's an adverb, the gerund never agrees with anything — <b>чита́я</b> stays чита́я whether the subject is он, она́, они́ or я. Nothing to decline: one form fits every subject."],"audio":"The gerund has no subject of its own — it borrows the subject of the main verb. So before you use one, check: does the same person do both actions? Chitaya, Ivan dumal — Ivan reads, Ivan thinks. If the two doers differ, the gerund is wrong — that's tomorrow's big trap. A gerund and its phrase, the deyeprichastny oborot, are always set off with commas, wherever they sit: chitaya pismo, ona plakala; ona plakala, chitaya pismo. Both are fine; the comma travels with the gerund. Because it's an adverb, the gerund never agrees with anything — chitaya stays chitaya whether the subject is on, ona, oni or ya. One form fits every subject.","table":{"rows":[["Чита́я письмо́, она́ пла́кала. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"читая письмо, она плакала\">🔊</button>","gerund first — comma after the phrase"],["Она́ пла́кала, чита́я письмо́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"она плакала, читая письмо\">🔊</button>","gerund last — comma before it"],["Мы шли домо́й, разгова́ривая. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"мы шли домой, разговаривая\">🔊</button>","one doer (мы), one form"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Returning home, she called her mother.","en":"<b>Возвраща́ясь</b> домо́й, она́ позвони́ла ма́ме.","say":"Возвращаясь домой, она позвонила маме."},{"ru":"The children ran, laughing loudly.","en":"Де́ти бежа́ли, гро́мко <b>смея́сь</b>.","say":"Дети бежали, громко смеясь."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Чита́я письмо́ она́ пла́кала.","right":"Чита́я письмо́, она́ пла́кала.","why":"The gerund phrase always takes a comma: <b>Чита́я письмо́,</b> она́ пла́кала. Never leave the оборо́т un-fenced."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which has one doer for both actions (a correct gerund)?","opts":["Гуля́я по па́рку, зазвони́л телефо́н.","Гуля́я по па́рку, ему́ бы́ло хо́лодно.","Гуля́я по па́рку, Ива́н слу́шал му́зыку."],"answer":2,"explain":"Ivan strolls and Ivan listens — one subject. In the others the doer changes (a phone; an impersonal state)."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Чита́я, я всегда́ де́лаю заме́тки.","sub":"“While reading, I always make notes” — one subject, one gerund","want":"Читая, я всегда делаю заметки."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“She was walking, smiling.”</b>","want":"Она́ шла, улыба́ясь","answer":"Она́ шла, улыба́ясь"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · The «-ing» trap: which -ing is it?","title":"<em>Чита́я</em> vs <em>чита́ющий</em> — how/when vs which","paras":["English “reading” hides two different Russian tools, and B2 asks you to pick the right one. If the “-ing” describes a <b>noun</b> (“the <b>reading</b> man” — which man?), it's a <b>participle</b> — an adjective that agrees and declines: <b>чита́ющий</b> челове́к, о <b>чита́ющем</b> челове́ке.","If the “-ing” describes <b>how or when</b> the main action happens (“he walked <b>reading</b>” — reading while walking), it's a <b>gerund</b> — an adverb that never changes: он шёл, <b>чита́я</b>. Same root чита́-, opposite job.","A quick test: can you replace it with «кото́рый»? Then it's a participle. Can you replace it with «когда́ / и при э́том»? Then it's a gerund. The adjective describes the <b>doer</b>; the adverb describes the <b>doing</b>."],"audio":"English 'reading' hides two different Russian tools, and B2 asks you to pick the right one. If the -ing describes a noun — the reading man, which man? — it's a participle, an adjective that agrees and declines: chitayushchiy chelovek, o chitayushchem cheloveke. If the -ing describes how or when the main action happens — he walked reading, reading while walking — it's a gerund, an adverb that never changes: on shyol, chitaya. Same root chita-, opposite job. A quick test: can you replace it with kotory? Then it's a participle. Can you replace it with kogda or i pri etom? Then it's a gerund. The adjective describes the doer; the adverb describes the doing.","examples":[{"ru":"the reading student (which student?) — a participle","en":"<b>чита́ющий</b> студе́нт — прича́стие","say":"читающий студент — причастие"},{"ru":"the student read, smiling (how did he read?) — a gerund","en":"студе́нт чита́л, <b>улыба́ясь</b> — дееприча́стие","say":"студент читал, улыбаясь — деепричастие"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Он вошёл, чита́ющий газе́ту.","right":"Он вошёл, чита́я газе́ту.","why":"Here “-ing” tells <b>how</b> he came in → a gerund, <b>чита́я</b>. чита́ющий (a participle) would need a noun to describe."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“A working mother is a busy person.” — which -ing?","opts":["Рабо́тающая мать — за́нятый челове́к.","Рабо́тая мать — за́нятый челове́к.","Рабо́тав мать — за́нятый челове́к."],"answer":0,"explain":"It describes the noun “mother” → participle <b>рабо́тающая</b> (agrees, feminine)."},{"type":"choice","q":"“He answered, smiling.” — which -ing?","opts":["Он отве́тил, улыба́ясь.","Он отве́тил, улыба́ющийся.","Он отве́тил, улыба́вшийся."],"answer":0,"explain":"“How did he answer?” → gerund <b>улыба́ясь</b>. улыба́ющийся / улыба́вшийся are participles (they need a noun)."},{"type":"type","q":"Type the gerund “walking” from <b>иду́т</b>:","accept":["идя","идя."],"placeholder":"ид…","explain":"иду́т → <b>идя́</b>. It answers “how” you moved, not “which” person."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Ле́на's café at lunchtime — Ле́на works the counter and talks at once, while Эмма quietly collects gerunds. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Извини́, я говорю́ с тобо́й, гото́вя ко́фе — сего́дня мно́го госте́й.","ru":"Sorry, I'm talking to you while making coffee — lots of guests today."},{"who":"B","en":"Ничего́! Я как раз собира́ю таки́е фра́зы. «Гото́вя ко́фе, ты говори́шь» — одно́ подлежа́щее.","ru":"It's fine! I'm actually collecting phrases like that. “Making coffee, you're talking” — one subject."},{"who":"A","en":"Ты слы́шишь? Я де́лаю два де́ла сра́зу, а ты де́лаешь грамма́тику.","ru":"You hear that? I'm doing two things at once, and you're doing grammar."},{"who":"B","en":"И́менно! Слу́шая тебя́, я учу́ дееприча́стия.","ru":"Exactly! Listening to you, I'm learning gerunds."},{"who":"A","en":"Смотри́: Макси́м вон там чита́ет, попива́я чай. То́же два де́ла.","ru":"Look: Maxim over there is reading, sipping tea. Two things as well."},{"who":"B","en":"Ви́жу. Улыба́ясь, он де́лает заме́тки в кни́ге.","ru":"I see. Smiling, he's making notes in the book."},{"who":"A","en":"Он всегда́ так — рабо́тая, отдыха́ет, отдыха́я, рабо́тает.","ru":"He's always like that — resting while working, working while resting."},{"who":"B","en":"Настоя́щий инжене́р. А я, переводя́ рома́н, забыва́ю про чай — он давно́ осты́л.","ru":"A real engineer. And I, translating a novel, forget about my tea — it went cold long ago."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve gerunds and words for doing two things at once. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"дееприча́стие","ru":"a gerund (verbal adverb)","ex":"Дееприча́стие не изменя́ется."},{"en":"чита́я","ru":"(while) reading","ex":"Чита́я, я де́лаю заме́тки."},{"en":"ду́мая","ru":"(while) thinking","ex":"Ду́мая о рабо́те, он молча́л."},{"en":"слу́шая","ru":"(while) listening","ex":"Слу́шая му́зыку, она́ рабо́тает."},{"en":"улыба́ясь","ru":"smiling","ex":"Улыба́ясь, он вошёл в ко́мнату."},{"en":"спеша́","ru":"hurrying","ex":"Спеша́, я забы́л ключи́."},{"en":"говоря́","ru":"(while) speaking","ex":"Говоря́ по-ру́сски, он ошиба́ется ре́дко."},{"en":"возвраща́ясь","ru":"returning","ex":"Возвраща́ясь домо́й, я встре́тил дру́га."},{"en":"бу́дучи","ru":"being","ex":"Бу́дучи ребёнком, я жил в дере́вне."},{"en":"одновреме́нно","ru":"simultaneously, at once","ex":"Он де́лает всё одновреме́нно."},{"en":"смея́сь","ru":"laughing","ex":"Смея́сь, де́ти вы́бежали во двор."},{"en":"заме́тка","ru":"a note","ex":"Я пишу́ заме́тки на поля́х."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the gerund phrase to its meaning.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["чита́я письмо́","(while) reading a letter"],["слу́шая му́зыку","(while) listening to music"],["улыба́ясь","smiling"],["бу́дучи студе́нтом","being a student"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"The imperfective gerund of <b>чита́ть</b> (чита́ют) is…","opts":["чита́я","чита́ющий","чита́вший"],"answer":0},{"q":"Which is a correct gerund sentence (one subject)?","opts":["Чита́я кни́гу, зазвони́л телефо́н.","Чита́я кни́гу, она́ пила́ чай.","Чита́я кни́гу, ей бы́ло гру́стно."],"answer":1},{"q":"With the subject «они́», the gerund «чита́я» becomes…","opts":["чита́ющие","чита́я","чита́вшие"],"answer":1},{"q":"“Smiling, she came in.” —","opts":["Улыба́ющаяся, она́ вошла́.","Улыба́ясь, она́ вошла́.","Улыба́вшись, она́ вошла́."],"answer":1},{"q":"Which “-ing” describes a <b>noun</b> (a participle, not a gerund)?","opts":["рабо́тающая мать","рабо́тая до́ма","спеша́ на рабо́ту"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Эмма's note on doing two things at once — read it aloud, and hear the gerunds hold the actions together.","title":"Два де́ла сра́зу","sentences":["Переводя́ рома́н, я ча́сто слу́шаю ру́сскую му́зыку.","Чита́я оригина́л, я сра́зу ду́маю о ру́сском сло́ве.","Улыба́ясь, Ле́на гото́вит ко́фе и разгова́ривает с гостя́ми.","Бу́дучи перево́дчицей, я живу́ в двух языка́х одновреме́нно.","Дееприча́стие — ма́ленькое сло́во, но оно́ де́ржит два де́йствия вме́сте."],"translation":"Translating a novel, I often listen to Russian music. Reading the original, I immediately think of the Russian word. Smiling, Lena makes coffee and chats with the guests. Being a translator, I live in two languages at once. A gerund is a small word, but it holds two actions together."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — describe two things you do at the same time.","prompt":"Write 3–4 sentences about doing two things at once, using at least two imperfective gerunds (чита́я, слу́шая, ду́мая, улыба́ясь…). Keep <b>one</b> subject for the gerund and the main verb, and remember the comma.","hint":"Recipe: они́-stem + -я (or -а after ж/ш/ч/щ); reflexive keeps -сь. Same doer for both actions 🙂","example":"Чита́я ру́сскую кни́гу, я слу́шаю му́зыку. Ду́мая о перево́де, я пью холо́дный чай. Улыба́ясь, я запи́сываю но́вое сло́во."}},"9":{"day":9,"week":"02","themeRu":"Having done, then…","themeEn":"Прочита́в, придя́","intro":"Yesterday's gerund ran <b>alongside</b> the main verb — reading <b>while</b> thinking. Today's does something different: it finishes <b>first</b>. «Прочита́в письмо́, она́ отве́тила» — 'Having read the letter, she replied.' First the reading, then the reply. And with it comes Russian's most notorious trap for English speakers — the one where the phone starts reading letters. Let's make sure yours never does.","introAudio":"Yesterday's gerund ran alongside the main verb — reading while thinking. Today's does something different: it finishes first. Prochitav pismo, ona otvetila — having read the letter, she replied. First the reading, then the reply. And with it comes Russian's most notorious trap for English speakers — the one where the phone starts reading letters. Let's make sure yours never does.","goals":["Build a perfective gerund for a <b>completed prior</b> action: <b>прочита́ть → прочита́в</b>, <b>узна́ть → узна́в</b>","Handle the -ти verbs: <b>прийти́ → придя́</b>, <b>войти́ → войдя́</b>; reflexive <b>верну́ться → верну́вшись</b>","Sequence two actions: <b>having done X, (then) do Y</b> — same subject, comma","Dodge the dangling gerund: the doer of the gerund <b>must</b> be the sentence's subject"],"learned":["Built perfective gerunds for completed prior actions: <b>прочита́ть → прочита́в</b>","Handled the -ти verbs and reflexives: <b>прийти́ → придя́</b>, <b>верну́ться → верну́вшись</b>","Sequenced two actions with one subject and a comma","Dodged the dangling gerund — matched the doer to the sentence's subject"],"review":{"intro":"Yesterday's gerund ran alongside the main verb; check it still works before today it learns to finish first.","introAudio":"Yesterday's gerund ran alongside the main verb; check it still works before today it learns to finish first.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. Yesterday's gerund.","q":"The imperfective gerund of <b>слу́шать</b> (слу́шают) is…","opts":["слу́шав","слу́шая","слу́шающий"],"answer":1,"explain":"Present stem слу́ша- + -я → <b>слу́шая</b> (“while listening”). слу́шающий is the participle."},{"type":"choice","q":"“Smiling, she answered.” —","opts":["Улыба́ясь, она́ отве́тила.","Улыба́ющаяся, она́ отве́тила.","Улыба́ется, она́ отве́тила."],"answer":0,"explain":"“How did she answer?” → gerund <b>улыба́ясь</b>. улыба́ющаяся is a participle; улыба́ется is a plain verb."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“Reading, he was thinking.”</b>","want":"Чита́я, он ду́мал","answer":"Чита́я, он ду́мал"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The perfective gerund — prior, completed","title":"<em>Прочита́в письмо́, она́ отве́тила</em> — first this, then that","paras":["Yesterday's imperfective gerund ran at the same time as the main verb. The <b>perfective</b> gerund is its partner in sequence: it names an action that <b>finished before</b> the main one began. <b>Прочита́в</b> письмо́, она́ отве́тила — “Having read the letter, she replied.” Read first; reply second.","The two aspects split the timeline. Imperfective <b>чита́я</b> = “while reading” (simultaneous). Perfective <b>прочита́в</b> = “having read” (before). Same subject rule as yesterday — one person does both — but now they're steps in a row, not a single blended moment.","This is how Russian chains completed actions elegantly: <b>Придя́ домо́й, поу́жинав и отдохну́в, он сел за рабо́ту</b> — “Having come home, eaten and rested, he sat down to work.” Three finished steps, then the main verb."],"audio":"Yesterday's imperfective gerund ran at the same time as the main verb. The perfective gerund is its partner in sequence: it names an action that finished before the main one began. Prochitav pismo, ona otvetila — having read the letter, she replied. Read first; reply second. The two aspects split the timeline. Imperfective chitaya means while reading, simultaneous. Perfective prochitav means having read, before. Same subject rule as yesterday — one person does both — but now they're steps in a row, not a single blended moment. This is how Russian chains completed actions elegantly: pridya domoy, pouzhinav i otdokhnuv, on sel za rabotu — having come home, eaten and rested, he sat down to work. Three finished steps, then the main verb.","table":{"rows":[["Прочита́в письмо́, она́ отве́тила. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"прочитав письмо, она ответила\">🔊</button>","Having read the letter, she replied."],["Придя́ домо́й, он лёг спать. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"придя домой, он лёг спать\">🔊</button>","Having come home, he went to bed."],["Узна́в но́вость, мы обра́довались. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"узнав новость, мы обрадовались\">🔊</button>","Having learned the news, we were glad."]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Having finished the translation, Emma sent it to the publisher.","en":"<b>Зако́нчив</b> перево́д, Э́мма отпра́вила его́ в изда́тельство.","say":"Закончив перевод, Эмма отправила его в издательство."},{"ru":"Having come to the café, they ordered coffee.","en":"<b>Придя́</b> в кафе́, они́ заказа́ли ко́фе.","say":"Придя в кафе, они заказали кофе."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Чита́я письмо́, она́ отве́тила.","right":"Прочита́в письмо́, она́ отве́тила.","why":"If she read <b>then</b> replied, the reading is finished first → perfective <b>прочита́в</b>. Imperfective чита́я would mean she replied <b>while still</b> reading."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"“Having eaten breakfast, she left.” —","opts":["За́втракая, она́ ушла́.","Поза́втракав, она́ ушла́.","За́втракав, она́ ушла́."],"answer":1,"explain":"Finished first → perfective <b>поза́втракав</b>. За́втракая (imperfective) = “while having breakfast”; <b>за́втракав</b> isn't a form — imperfective за́втракать can't take -в."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“Having read the book, he returned it.”</b>","want":"Прочита́в кни́гу, он верну́л её","answer":"Прочита́в кни́гу, он верну́л её"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The recipe — perfective stem plus -в","title":"<em>прочита́ть → прочита́в</em>, <em>прийти́ → придя́</em>","paras":["Most perfective gerunds are the easiest form in the language. Take the perfective verb, drop the <b>-ть</b> of the infinitive, and add <b>-в</b>: прочита́ть → <b>прочита́в</b>, написа́ть → <b>написа́в</b>, узна́ть → <b>узна́в</b>, сказа́ть → <b>сказа́в</b>. (You may also see <b>-вши</b> — прочита́вши — but plain -в is the norm.)","Reflexive verbs take <b>-вшись</b>: верну́ться → <b>верну́вшись</b>, просну́ться → <b>просну́вшись</b>, попроща́ться → <b>попроща́вшись</b>.","The <b>-ти / -йти</b> verbs of motion are the ones to memorise: they end in <b>-дя́ / -я́</b>, not -в: прийти́ → <b>придя́</b>, войти́ → <b>войдя́</b>, вы́йти → <b>вы́йдя</b>, принести́ → <b>принеся́</b>. A handful of high-frequency verbs — worth knowing cold."],"audio":"Most perfective gerunds are the easiest form in the language. Take the perfective verb, drop the -t of the infinitive, and add -v: prochitat becomes prochitav, napisat becomes napisav, uznat becomes uznav, skazat becomes skazav. You may also see -vshi, prochitavshi, but plain -v is the norm. Reflexive verbs take -vshis: vernutsya becomes vernuvshis, prosnutsya becomes prosnuvshis, poproshchatsya becomes poproshchavshis. The -ti and -yti motion verbs are the ones to memorise: they end in -dya or -ya, not -v: priyti becomes pridya, voyti becomes voydya, vyyti becomes vyydya, prinesti becomes prinesya. A handful of high-frequency verbs — worth knowing cold.","table":{"rows":[["прочита́ть → прочита́в <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"прочитать, прочитав\">🔊</button>","having read (drop -ть, add -в)"],["верну́ться → верну́вшись <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"вернуться, вернувшись\">🔊</button>","having returned (reflexive -вшись)"],["прийти́ → придя́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"прийти, придя\">🔊</button>","having arrived (-ти verb → -дя́)"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"Having written the letter, she went out.","en":"<b>Написа́в</b> письмо́, она́ вы́шла.","say":"Написав письмо, она вышла."},{"ru":"Having returned home, we made tea.","en":"<b>Верну́вшись</b> домо́й, мы сде́лали чай.","say":"Вернувшись домой, мы сделали чай."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Прише́в домо́й, он позвони́л.","right":"Придя́ домо́й, он позвони́л.","why":"прийти́ doesn't take -в. The motion verbs go to <b>-дя́</b>: <b>придя́</b>, войдя́, вы́йдя. Learn these few by heart."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"The perfective gerund of <b>написа́ть</b> is…","opts":["написа́вший","написа́я","написа́в"],"answer":2,"explain":"Drop -ть, add -в → <b>написа́в</b>. написа́вший is the participle; написа́я isn't a real form."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks are optional.","q":"Make the perfective gerund from <b>узна́ть</b> (“having found out”):","accept":["узнав","узнав."],"placeholder":"узна́…","explain":"узна́ть → <b>узна́в</b>."},{"type":"choice","q":"“Having returned, she called.” —","opts":["Возвраща́ясь, она́ позвони́ла.","Верну́вшись, она́ позвони́ла.","Верну́в, она́ позвони́ла."],"answer":1,"explain":"Reflexive perfective → <b>верну́вшись</b>. возвраща́ясь is imperfective (“while returning”); верну́в drops the needed -сь."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Дееприча́стный оборо́т","title":"<em>Зако́нчив рабо́ту, он пошёл домо́й</em> — fence it, share the subject","paras":["A perfective gerund usually travels with its own words — an object, a place — forming a <b>дееприча́стный оборо́т</b> (gerund phrase): <b>зако́нчив тру́дный перево́д</b>, <b>придя́ на рабо́ту по́здно</b>. Like its imperfective cousin, the whole phrase is <b>always</b> set off with commas.","And the subject rule is now iron: the person doing the gerund's action is the <b>same</b> person doing the main verb. <b>Зако́нчив</b> рабо́ту, <b>он</b> пошёл домо́й — he finishes, he goes. Before you write a gerund, name that shared subject in your head.","You can chain several: <b>Просну́вшись, умы́вшись и поза́втракав, она́ вы́шла из до́ма</b> — “Having woken, washed and breakfasted, she left the house.” Three completed steps, one subject (она́), one main verb."],"audio":"A perfective gerund usually travels with its own words — an object, a place — forming a deyeprichastny oborot, a gerund phrase: zakonchiv trudny perevod; pridya na rabotu pozdno. Like its imperfective cousin, the whole phrase is always set off with commas. And the subject rule is now iron: the person doing the gerund's action is the same person doing the main verb. Zakonchiv rabotu, on poshyol domoy — he finishes, he goes. Before you write a gerund, name that shared subject in your head. You can chain several: prosnuvshis, umyvshis i pozavtrakav, ona vyshla iz doma — having woken, washed and breakfasted, she left the house. Three completed steps, one subject, one main verb.","table":{"rows":[["Зако́нчив рабо́ту, он пошёл домо́й. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"закончив работу, он пошёл домой\">🔊</button>","Having finished work, he went home."],["Придя́ на рабо́ту, она́ включи́ла компью́тер. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"придя на работу, она включила компьютер\">🔊</button>","Having arrived at work, she turned on the computer."],["Написа́в письмо́, я отпра́вил его́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"написав письмо, я отправил его\">🔊</button>","Having written the letter, I sent it."]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Having translated the chapter, Emma took a break.","en":"<b>Переведя́</b> главу́, Э́мма сде́лала переры́в.","say":"Переведя главу, Эмма сделала перерыв."},{"ru":"Having said goodbye, the guests left.","en":"<b>Попроща́вшись</b>, го́сти ушли́.","say":"Попрощавшись, гости ушли."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Зако́нчив рабо́ту он пошёл домо́й.","right":"Зако́нчив рабо́ту, он пошёл домо́й.","why":"The gerund phrase closes with a comma before the main clause: …рабо́ту<b>,</b> он пошёл. Always fence the оборо́т."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which correctly chains completed actions (one subject)?","opts":["Поза́втракав и оде́вшись, она́ вы́шла.","Поза́втракав и оде́вшись, авто́бус уе́хал.","Поза́втракав и оде́вшись, ей пора́ идти́."],"answer":0,"explain":"She breakfasts, dresses and leaves — one subject. A bus can't have breakfast; an impersonal “ей” has no subject to share."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“Having finished the book, she went to bed.”</b>","want":"Зако́нчив кни́гу, она́ легла́ спать","answer":"Зако́нчив кни́гу, она́ легла́ спать"},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Придя́ домо́й, мы сра́зу се́ли рабо́тать.","sub":"“Having come home, we sat down to work at once” — first arrive, then work","want":"Придя домой, мы сразу сели работать."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · The trap: whose action is it?","title":"<em>Чита́я письмо́, зазвони́л телефо́н</em> ❌ — the phone can't read","paras":["Here is the single most famous mistake English speakers make in Russian, and the reason teachers watch gerunds like hawks. Because the gerund borrows the main clause's subject, that subject <b>must be able to do</b> the gerund's action. <b>Чита́я письмо́, зазвони́л телефо́н</b> literally says the <b>telephone</b> was reading the letter. Wrong — and to a Russian ear, absurd.","The fix is always one of two moves. Either give the gerund's action to the real subject — <b>Когда́ я чита́л письмо́, зазвони́л телефо́н</b> (“When I was reading…, the phone rang”) — or rebuild so the shared subject does both: <b>Чита́я письмо́, я услы́шал звоно́к</b> (“Reading the letter, I heard a ring”).","Watch the hidden version too: <b>impersonal</b> main clauses have no subject to share. <b>Прочита́в письмо́, мне ста́ло гру́стно</b> ❌ — “мне” isn't a subject. Say <b>Когда́ я прочита́л письмо́, мне ста́ло гру́стно</b>. The test never changes: can the subject of the sentence perform the gerund? If not, don't use one."],"audio":"Here is the single most famous mistake English speakers make in Russian, and the reason teachers watch gerunds like hawks. Because the gerund borrows the main clause's subject, that subject must be able to do the gerund's action. Chitaya pismo, zazvonil telefon literally says the telephone was reading the letter. Wrong — and to a Russian ear, absurd. The fix is always one of two moves. Either give the gerund's action to the real subject — kogda ya chital pismo, zazvonil telefon, when I was reading the letter, the phone rang — or rebuild so the shared subject does both: chitaya pismo, ya uslyshal zvonok, reading the letter, I heard a ring. Watch the hidden version too: impersonal main clauses have no subject to share. Prochitav pismo, mne stalo grustno is wrong — mne isn't a subject. Say kogda ya prochital pismo, mne stalo grustno. The test never changes: can the subject of the sentence perform the gerund? If not, don't use one.","examples":[{"ru":"✗ Approaching the house, the rain began. — the rain doesn't approach.","en":"✗ <b>Подходя́</b> к до́му, начался́ дождь.","say":"Подходя к дому, начался дождь."},{"ru":"✓ Approaching the house, I felt the rain. — one subject (I).","en":"✓ <b>Подходя́</b> к до́му, я почу́вствовал дождь.","say":"Подходя к дому, я почувствовал дождь."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Прочита́в статью́, у меня́ появи́лись вопро́сы.","right":"Прочита́в статью́, я за́дал вопро́сы.","why":"«у меня́» is not a subject — the questions didn't read the article. Give the reading to a real subject: <b>я</b> read, <b>я</b> asked."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which sentence is <b>correct</b> (no dangling gerund)?","opts":["Прочита́в письмо́, я всё по́нял.","Прочита́в письмо́, всё ста́ло я́сно.","Прочита́в письмо́, мне ста́ло гру́стно."],"answer":0,"explain":"Only the first has a subject (я) who can both read and understand. The others are subjectless/impersonal — dangling."},{"type":"choice","q":"Fix the dangling gerund: «Гуля́я по па́рку, пошёл дождь.»","opts":["Когда́ я гуля́л по па́рку, пошёл дождь.","Гуля́я по па́рку, дождь пошёл си́льно.","Гуля́я по па́рку, бы́ло хо́лодно."],"answer":0,"explain":"The rain doesn't stroll → drop the gerund: <b>Когда́ я гуля́л…, пошёл дождь</b>."},{"type":"type","q":"Type the perfective gerund of <b>прийти́</b> (“having arrived”):","accept":["придя","придя."],"placeholder":"прид…","explain":"прийти́ → <b>придя́</b> (a -ти verb — not прише́в)."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма tells Ива́н how she finally finished the first part of her big translation — a day told as a chain of completed steps. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Наконе́ц-то! Зако́нчив пе́рвую часть, я почу́вствовала облегче́ние.","ru":"Finally! Having finished the first part, I felt relief."},{"who":"A","en":"Поздравля́ю! И что пото́м?","ru":"Congratulations! And what then?"},{"who":"B","en":"Отпра́вив текст реда́ктору, я сра́зу пошла́ гуля́ть.","ru":"Having sent the text to the editor, I went straight out for a walk."},{"who":"A","en":"Пра́вильно. А я, сдав тру́дный экза́мен, то́же реши́л немно́го отдохну́ть.","ru":"Good for you. And I, having taken a hard exam, also decided to rest a little."},{"who":"B","en":"Ты сдал?! Поздравля́ю! Тру́дно бы́ло?","ru":"You passed?! Congratulations! Was it hard?"},{"who":"A","en":"Да, о́чень. Придя́ домо́й, я сра́зу сел за сле́дующую главу́.","ru":"Yes, very. Having come home, I sat straight down with the next chapter."},{"who":"B","en":"Ви́дишь, как мы говори́м? «Зако́нчив», «отпра́вив», «придя́» — оди́н за други́м.","ru":"See how we're talking? “Having finished,” “having sent,” “having arrived” — one after another."},{"who":"A","en":"Да, дееприча́стия стро́ят це́лый день из зако́нченных шаго́в.","ru":"Yes, gerunds build a whole day out of completed steps."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve perfective gerunds for a day made of finished steps. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"прочита́в","ru":"having read","ex":"Прочита́в письмо́, она́ улыбну́лась."},{"en":"написа́в","ru":"having written","ex":"Написа́в отчёт, он ушёл."},{"en":"узна́в","ru":"having found out","ex":"Узна́в пра́вду, мы удиви́лись."},{"en":"придя́","ru":"having arrived","ex":"Придя́ домо́й, я отдохну́л."},{"en":"войдя́","ru":"having entered","ex":"Войдя́ в ко́мнату, он поздоро́вался."},{"en":"верну́вшись","ru":"having returned","ex":"Верну́вшись, она́ позвони́ла ма́ме."},{"en":"зако́нчив","ru":"having finished","ex":"Зако́нчив рабо́ту, мы пошли́ в кафе́."},{"en":"попроща́вшись","ru":"having said goodbye","ex":"Попроща́вшись, го́сти ушли́."},{"en":"дееприча́стный оборо́т","ru":"a gerund phrase","ex":"Дееприча́стный оборо́т выделя́ется запяты́ми."},{"en":"сдав","ru":"having passed / handed in","ex":"Сдав экза́мены, Ива́н на́чал рабо́тать."},{"en":"отпра́вив","ru":"having sent","ex":"Отпра́вив письмо́, я вы́ключил компью́тер."},{"en":"отдохну́в","ru":"having rested","ex":"Отдохну́в, мы продо́лжили путь."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the gerund phrase to its meaning.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["прочита́в письмо́","having read the letter"],["придя́ домо́й","having come home"],["зако́нчив рабо́ту","having finished work"],["верну́вшись домо́й","having returned home"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"The perfective gerund of <b>прочита́ть</b> is…","opts":["чита́я","прочита́в","прочита́вший"],"answer":1},{"q":"“Having arrived home, he called.” —","opts":["Приходя́ домо́й, он позвони́л.","Придя́ домо́й, он позвони́л.","Прише́в домо́й, он позвони́л."],"answer":1},{"q":"Which sentence has a <b>dangling</b> gerund (wrong)?","opts":["Прочита́в письмо́, она́ отве́тила.","Прочита́в письмо́, зазвони́л телефо́н.","Прочита́в письмо́, я отве́тил."],"answer":1},{"q":"Which is <b>prior</b> (finished first): «чита́я» or «прочита́в»?","opts":["чита́я","прочита́в","о́ба одновреме́нны"],"answer":1},{"q":"The perfective gerund of <b>верну́ться</b> is…","opts":["возвраща́ясь","верну́в","верну́вшись"],"answer":2}]},"reading":{"intro":"Эмма's day, told as a chain of finished steps — read it aloud and feel each gerund click into the next.","title":"День, со́бранный из шаго́в","sentences":["Просну́вшись ра́но, я сра́зу се́ла за перево́д.","Зако́нчив пе́рвую главу́, я вы́пила ко́фе.","Отпра́вив текст реда́ктору, я почу́вствовала себя́ свобо́дной.","Придя́ ве́чером в кафе́, я рассказа́ла Ива́ну о своём дне.","Ка́ждое дееприча́стие здесь — зако́нченный шаг, за кото́рым идёт друго́й."],"translation":"Having woken early, I sat straight down to the translation. Having finished the first chapter, I drank a coffee. Having sent the text to the editor, I felt free. Having come to the café in the evening, I told Ivan about my day. Each gerund here is a completed step, followed by another."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — tell a small story as a chain of finished steps.","prompt":"Describe your morning or your day in 3–4 sentences, using at least two perfective gerunds (прочита́в, зако́нчив, придя́, верну́вшись…). Keep the <b>same subject</b> throughout — no dangling gerunds — and use commas.","hint":"Recipe: perfective verb minus -ть plus -в (реши́ть → реши́в); -ти verbs go to -дя́ (прийти́ → придя́); reflexive → -вшись. Ask: can my subject really do this action? 🙂","example":"Просну́вшись ра́но, мы вы́пили ко́фе и откры́ли ноутбу́ки. Зако́нчив дела́, мы вы́шли на у́лицу. Придя́ в парк, мы встре́тили друзе́й."}},"10":{"day":10,"week":"02","themeRu":"Done to, not doing","themeEn":"Стро́ится и постро́ен: зало́г","intro":"There's a contract on Эмма's desk, and every single line of it says something «is signed», «is carried out», «is being decided» — nobody actually <em>does</em> anything. Welcome to the <b>passive</b>. Russian has two of them, and it picks between them by aspect: a process still running (<b>дом стро́ится</b> — the house is being built) versus a result already finished (<b>дом постро́ен</b> — the house is built). Get that one split and the passive is yours.","introAudio":"There's a contract on Emma's desk, and every single line of it says something is signed, is carried out, is being decided — nobody actually does anything. Welcome to the passive. Russian has two of them, and it picks between them by aspect: a process still running, dom stroitsya, the house is being built, versus a result already finished, dom postroyen, the house is built. Get that one split and the passive is yours.","goals":["Split the passive by aspect: process → <b>-ся</b> (дом стро́ится), result → short participle (дом постро́ен)","Build a <b>-ся</b> passive from any imperfective verb, with the doer in the instrumental (статья́ пи́шется журнали́стом)","State a finished result with a short participle + быть: <b>догово́р подпи́сан</b>, <b>дом был постро́ен</b>","Read the passive everywhere — but <b>speak</b> it as active «они́»: <b>дом постро́или</b>"],"learned":["Split the passive by aspect — <b>-ся</b> for a process, short participle for a result","Built <b>-ся</b> passives with the doer in the instrumental","Stated finished results with a short participle + быть (был постро́ен)","Turned a written passive into spoken active «они́»"],"review":{"intro":"One minute back on last week's verbals — the gerunds from days 8–9 and a participle from week 1. They're the muscles today's passive is built on.","introAudio":"One minute back on last week's verbals — the gerunds from days 8 to 9 and a participle from week 1. They're the muscles today's passive is built on.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. One subject for the gerund!","q":"“Having read the letter, she called Ivan.” Which keeps one subject?","opts":["Прочита́в письмо́, она́ позвони́ла Ива́ну.","Прочита́в письмо́, зазвони́л телефо́н.","Прочита́в письмо́, Ива́ну позвони́ли."],"answer":0,"explain":"The gerund's doer must be the main subject: <b>она́</b> read and <b>она́</b> called → «Прочита́в письмо́, она́ позвони́ла». In option 2 the phone can't read."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks are optional.","q":"Make the imperfective gerund of <b>чита́ть</b> (“while reading”):","accept":["читая","читая."],"placeholder":"чита́…","explain":"Imperfective gerund: -я from the present stem → <b>чита́я</b>."},{"type":"choice","q":"“the article written by the professor” (a finished result) —","opts":["статья́, пи́шущая профе́ссором","статья́, напи́санная профе́ссором","статья́, чита́ющая профе́ссора"],"answer":1,"explain":"Passive past participle: <b>напи́санная</b> (written). пи́шущая = “writing” (active present); чита́ющая = “reading”."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Not one passive, but two","title":"<em>Дом стро́ится</em> vs <em>дом постро́ен</em> — process or result","paras":["English has one passive: “the house is built”, “is being built”, “was built” — one shape stretched across time. Russian splits the job in two, and the split is by <b>aspect</b>. An ongoing <b>process</b> uses a <b>-ся verb</b>: <b>дом стро́ится</b> — “the house is being built”, right now, in progress. A finished <b>result</b> uses the <b>short participle</b> you met on day 5: <b>дом постро́ен</b> — “the house is (stands) built”.","So before you build a passive, ask one question: <b>process or result?</b> Imperfective, still happening → <b>-ся</b>: вопро́с <b>реша́ется</b> (the question is being decided). Perfective, done and standing → <b>participle</b>: вопро́с <b>решён</b> (the question is settled).","Same English “is decided” — two different Russian sentences. That one choice is today's whole lesson."],"audio":"English has one passive: the house is built, is being built, was built — one shape stretched across time. Russian splits the job in two, and the split is by aspect. An ongoing process uses a -sya verb: dom stroitsya, the house is being built, in progress. A finished result uses the short participle from day five: dom postroyen, the house stands built. So before you build a passive, ask: process or result? Imperfective, still happening, takes -sya: vopros reshayetsya, the question is being decided. Perfective, done and standing, takes the participle: vopros reshon, the question is settled. Same English 'is decided', two different Russian sentences.","table":{"rows":[["дом стро́ится <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"дом строится\">🔊</button>","the house is being built (process)"],["дом постро́ен <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"дом построен\">🔊</button>","the house is built (result)"],["вопро́с реша́ется <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"вопрос решается\">🔊</button>","the question is being decided"],["вопро́с решён <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"вопрос решён\">🔊</button>","the question is (has been) decided"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"The new building is still being built.","en":"Но́вое зда́ние ещё <b>стро́ится</b>.","say":"Новое здание ещё строится."},{"ru":"The problem has already been solved.","en":"Пробле́ма уже́ <b>решена́</b>.","say":"Проблема уже решена."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Дом уже́ стро́ится. (for a finished house)","right":"Дом уже́ постро́ен.","why":"“Already built / finished” is a <b>result</b> → short participle <b>постро́ен</b>. <b>стро́ится</b> would mean it's still under construction."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"“The bridge is still being built” (in progress) —","opts":["Мост ещё стро́ится.","Мост ещё постро́ен.","Мост ещё стро́ил."],"answer":0,"explain":"Process, in progress → <b>-ся</b>: стро́ится. постро́ен = finished; стро́ил = “was building” (active, needs an object)."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The process passive: imperfective + -ся","title":"<em>Кни́ги продаю́тся, вопро́с обсужда́ется</em>","paras":["The process passive is easy to build: take an <b>imperfective</b> verb and add <b>-ся</b>. The thing that undergoes the action becomes the subject: <b>кни́ги продаю́т</b> (they sell books) → <b>кни́ги продаю́тся</b> (books are sold / being sold). It works in every tense: дом <b>стро́ился</b> (was being built), дом <b>стро́ится</b>, дом <b>бу́дет стро́иться</b>.","If you name the doer, it goes in the <b>instrumental</b>: <b>статья́ пи́шется журнали́стом</b> (the article is written by a journalist). But usually the doer is left out — that's the whole point of the passive.","This form is the home of signs and notices: <b>здесь продаётся хлеб</b>, <b>докуме́нты проверя́ются</b>, <b>вопро́с реша́ется</b>."],"audio":"The process passive is easy to build: take an imperfective verb and add -sya. The thing that undergoes the action becomes the subject: knigi prodayut, they sell books, becomes knigi prodayutsya, books are sold. It works in every tense: dom stroilsya, dom stroitsya, dom budet stroitsya. If you name the doer, it goes in the instrumental: statya pishetsya zhurnalistom, the article is written by a journalist. But usually the doer is left out — that's the whole point of the passive. This form is the home of signs and notices: here bread is sold, documents are checked, the question is being decided.","table":{"rows":[["кни́ги продаю́тся <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"книги продаются\">🔊</button>","books are (being) sold"],["дом стро́ится <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"дом строится\">🔊</button>","the house is being built"],["вопро́с обсужда́ется <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"вопрос обсуждается\">🔊</button>","the question is being discussed"],["статья́ пи́шется журнали́стом <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"статья пишется журналистом\">🔊</button>","the article is written by a journalist"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"Here, tickets are sold only online.","en":"Биле́ты <b>продаю́тся</b> то́лько онла́йн.","say":"Билеты продаются только онлайн."},{"ru":"This question is being decided by the director.","en":"Э́тот вопро́с <b>реша́ется</b> дире́ктором.","say":"Этот вопрос решается директором."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"статья́ пи́шется журнали́ста","right":"статья́ пи́шется журнали́стом","why":"The doer of a passive stands in the <b>instrumental</b> (кем): журнали́ст → <b>журнали́стом</b>."}],"tasks":[{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“Books are sold here.”</b>","want":"Здесь продаю́тся кни́ги","answer":"Здесь продаю́тся кни́ги"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the phrase. Stress marks optional.","q":"“The house is being built” (process, the -ся form of стро́ить):","accept":["дом строится","дом строится.","строится"],"placeholder":"дом стро́…","explain":"Imperfective + -ся: <b>дом стро́ится</b>."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The result passive: short participle + быть","title":"<em>Догово́р подпи́сан, дом был постро́ен</em>","paras":["For a finished result, reach back to day 5: the <b>short participle</b>. It states that the thing now stands done: <b>догово́р подпи́сан</b> (the contract is signed), <b>рабо́та вы́полнена</b> (the work is done), <b>пи́сьма напи́саны</b> (the letters are written). It agrees in gender and number: подпи́сан / подпи́сана / подпи́сано / подпи́саны.","Time comes from <b>быть</b>, exactly as on day 5. Present = nothing: <b>дом постро́ен</b>. Past = был / была́ / бы́ло / бы́ли: <b>дом был постро́ен</b> — don't drop был! Future = бу́дет / бу́дут: <b>дом бу́дет постро́ен</b>.","The doer, if named, is again <b>instrumental</b>: <b>рома́н напи́сан изве́стным писа́телем</b> (the novel was written by a famous writer)."],"audio":"For a finished result, reach back to day five: the short participle. It states that the thing now stands done: dogovor podpisan, the contract is signed; rabota vypolnena, the work is done; pisma napisany, the letters are written. It agrees in gender and number: podpisan, podpisana, podpisano, podpisany. Time comes from byt, exactly as on day five. Present is nothing: dom postroyen. Past is byl, byla, bylo, byli: dom byl postroyen — don't drop byl. Future is budet: dom budet postroyen. The doer, if named, is again instrumental: roman napisan izvestnym pisatelem, the novel was written by a famous writer.","table":{"rows":[["догово́р подпи́сан <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"договор подписан\">🔊</button>","the contract is signed"],["рабо́та была́ вы́полнена <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"работа была выполнена\">🔊</button>","the work was done"],["дом бу́дет постро́ен <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"дом будет построен\">🔊</button>","the house will be built"],["рома́н напи́сан писа́телем <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"роман написан писателем\">🔊</button>","the novel is written by a writer"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"The documents have already been signed.","en":"Докуме́нты уже́ <b>подпи́саны</b>.","say":"Документы уже подписаны."},{"ru":"The letter was written a week ago.","en":"Письмо́ <b>бы́ло напи́сано</b> неде́лю наза́д.","say":"Письмо было написано неделю назад."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Дом постро́ен год наза́д.","right":"Дом был постро́ен год наза́д.","why":"A past result still needs <b>был</b>: <b>дом был постро́ен год наза́д</b>. Dropping it strands the sentence in the present."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“The work was completed on time” (a past result) —","opts":["Рабо́та вы́полнена в срок.","Рабо́та была́ вы́полнена в срок.","Рабо́та выполня́ется в срок."],"answer":1,"explain":"Past result → был/была́ + participle: <b>была́ вы́полнена</b>. Option 1 is the present; option 3 is an ongoing process."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it.","target":"Догово́р уже́ подпи́сан.","sub":"“The contract is already signed” — a done result","want":"договор уже подписан"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · In speech, just use «они́»","title":"<em>Дом постро́или, кни́ги продаю́т</em> — the spoken passive","paras":["Here's the honest register note, the twin of the participle one. Both passives above are the language of <b>documents, notices and the press</b>. In everyday <b>speech</b>, Russians dodge the passive and use an <b>active</b> verb with an unstated “they” (<b>они́</b>): <b>дом постро́или</b> (“they built the house” = it was built), <b>кни́ги продаю́т</b> (“they sell books”), <b>здесь говоря́т по-ру́сски</b> (“Russian is spoken here”).","No subject is named — the 3rd-person plural verb does all the work, just like English “they say”, “they built”. It's the natural spoken equivalent of both <b>-ся</b> and the participle.","So the full picture: <b>read</b> the passive everywhere, <b>write</b> it in official texts, but <b>speak</b> it as active «они́». Три спо́соба — one meaning."],"audio":"Here's the honest register note, the twin of the participle one. Both passives above are the language of documents, notices and the press. In everyday speech, Russians dodge the passive and use an active verb with an unstated 'they', oni: dom postroili, they built the house; knigi prodayut, they sell books; zdes govoryat po-russki, Russian is spoken here. No subject is named — the third-person plural verb does all the work, just like English 'they say', 'they built'. So the full picture: read the passive everywhere, write it in official texts, but speak it as active oni. Three ways, one meaning.","examples":[{"ru":"Spoken: “They built this house last year.”","en":"В ре́чи: «Э́тот дом <b>постро́или</b> в про́шлом году́».","say":"Этот дом построили в прошлом году."},{"ru":"Spoken: “Russian is spoken here.”","en":"«Здесь <b>говоря́т</b> по-ру́сски».","say":"Здесь говорят по-русски."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Э́тот дом был постро́ен рабо́чими. (in casual chat)","right":"Э́тот дом постро́или.","why":"Grammatically fine, but in <b>conversation</b> it sounds like a report. Speech prefers active «они́»: <b>постро́или</b>."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Chatting with a friend, the most natural is —","opts":["Здесь стро́ится но́вый дом.","Здесь стро́ят но́вый дом.","Здесь но́вый дом стро́ящийся."],"answer":1,"explain":"Speech → active «они́»: <b>стро́ят</b>. The -ся passive fits a sign or report; option 3 isn't a sentence."},{"type":"type","q":"Turn the passive into spoken active: «дом постро́ен» → «дом …» (they built):","accept":["построили","построили.","дом построили"],"placeholder":"постро́…","explain":"Spoken «они́» active: <b>постро́или</b>."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Emma visits a construction site with Макси́м, who's showing her his engineering project — and she's carrying a contract she has to translate. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Смотри́, Э́мма: э́тот дом стро́ится уже́ год. Ско́ро он бу́дет постро́ен.","ru":"Look, Emma: this house has been under construction for a year already. Soon it'll be built."},{"who":"B","en":"Впечатля́ет. А сосе́днее зда́ние? Оно́ вы́глядит гото́вым.","ru":"Impressive. And the building next door? It looks finished."},{"who":"A","en":"Оно́ уже́ постро́ено. Догово́р был подпи́сан два го́да наза́д.","ru":"It's already built. The contract was signed two years ago."},{"who":"B","en":"Кста́ти о догово́рах — я как раз перевожу́ оди́н. Ка́ждая фра́за напи́сана таки́м официа́льным языко́м!","ru":"Speaking of contracts — I'm translating one right now. Every phrase is written in such official language!"},{"who":"A","en":"Да, в докуме́нтах всё «выполня́ется», «утвержда́ется», «подпи́сывается». Живо́й челове́к так не говори́т.","ru":"Yes, in documents everything “is carried out”, “is approved”, “is signed”. A living person doesn't talk like that."},{"who":"B","en":"И́менно. Вслух я бы сказа́ла про́ще: «э́тот дом постро́или».","ru":"Exactly. Out loud I'd say it more simply: “they built this house”."},{"who":"A","en":"Ви́дишь, ты уже́ чу́вствуешь ра́зницу ме́жду те́кстом и ре́чью.","ru":"See, you already feel the difference between text and speech."},{"who":"B","en":"За э́то и люблю́ свою́ рабо́ту. За́втра догово́р бу́дет переведён — обеща́ю!","ru":"That's exactly why I love my work. Tomorrow the contract will be translated — I promise!"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the passive — the language of processes, results and official documents. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"стро́иться","ru":"to be built, be under construction (process)","ex":"Здесь стро́ится но́вый дом."},{"en":"реша́ться","ru":"to be decided, be solved (in progress)","ex":"Э́тот вопро́с ещё реша́ется."},{"en":"продава́ться","ru":"to be sold, be on sale","ex":"Кни́ги продаю́тся в э́том магази́не."},{"en":"обсужда́ться","ru":"to be discussed","ex":"План обсужда́ется на собра́нии."},{"en":"постро́ен","ru":"built (finished result)","ex":"Дом уже́ постро́ен."},{"en":"решён","ru":"solved, decided","ex":"Вопро́с уже́ решён."},{"en":"подпи́сан","ru":"signed","ex":"Догово́р подпи́сан вчера́."},{"en":"вы́полнен","ru":"carried out, completed","ex":"Вся рабо́та вы́полнена."},{"en":"догово́р","ru":"contract, agreement","ex":"Я перевожу́ ва́жный догово́р."},{"en":"строи́тельство","ru":"construction, building (the process)","ex":"Строи́тельство до́ма продолжа́ется."},{"en":"зало́г","ru":"voice (grammatical); pledge","ex":"Пасси́вный зало́г — язы́к докуме́нтов."},{"en":"официа́льный","ru":"official, formal","ex":"Э́то официа́льный докуме́нт."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each passive phrase to its meaning.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["дом стро́ится","the house is being built"],["дом постро́ен","the house is built (finished)"],["кни́ги продаю́тся","the books are (being) sold"],["догово́р подпи́сан","the contract is signed"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"“The house is being built” (process, in progress) —","opts":["дом постро́ен","дом стро́ится","дом стро́ил"],"answer":1},{"q":"“The contract has been signed” (a finished result) —","opts":["догово́р подпи́сан","догово́р подпи́сывается","догово́р подпи́сывают"],"answer":0},{"q":"The doer of a passive stands in the…","opts":["nominative","instrumental","genitive"],"answer":1},{"q":"Most natural in speech: “Russian is spoken here.”","opts":["Здесь ру́сский говори́тся.","Здесь говоря́т по-ру́сски.","Здесь ру́сский язы́к постро́ен."],"answer":1},{"q":"“The question is still being decided” (process) —","opts":["Вопро́с ещё решён.","Вопро́с ещё реша́ется.","Вопро́с ещё реши́л."],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's note after a day over an official document — read it aloud and hear the two registers side by side.","title":"Официа́льный докуме́нт","sentences":["Сего́дня я перевожу́ ва́жный догово́р.","В нём всё напи́сано пасси́вным зало́гом.","«Рабо́та должна́ быть вы́полнена в срок», «докуме́нт подпи́сан сторона́ми».","В ре́чи мы сказа́ли бы про́ще: «сде́лайте в срок», «мы подписа́ли».","Но в докуме́нте всё стро́ится, реша́ется и утвержда́ется без люде́й."],"translation":"Today I'm translating an important contract. In it everything is written in the passive voice. “The work must be completed on time”, “the document is signed by the parties”. In speech we'd say it more simply: “get it done on time”, “we signed”. But in a document everything is built, decided and approved without any people."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — describe something being made or already done, in two registers.","prompt":"Describe a building, a project or a document in Russian using the passive. Show both sides: something still <b>in process</b> (a -ся verb: стро́ится, реша́ется) and something <b>finished</b> (a short participle: постро́ен, подпи́сан, решён). Then rewrite one sentence the way you'd <b>say</b> it out loud — with active «они́».","hint":"Process = imperfective + -ся (дом стро́ится). Result = short participle + был (дом был постро́ен). Speech = active они́ (дом постро́или). 🙂","example":"На на́шей у́лице стро́ится но́вый дом. Ста́рое зда́ние уже́ снесено́, а прое́кт давно́ утверждён. Догово́р был подпи́сан весно́й. А в разгово́ре я бы сказа́ла про́сто: «э́тот дом стро́ят уже́ год»."}},"11":{"day":11,"week":"02","themeRu":"The case the verb wants","themeEn":"Интересу́юсь чем, зави́сит от чего́","intro":"“Interested in what?” In English the preposition is “in”. In Russian there's no preposition at all — just a case: интересу́юсь <b>му́зыкой</b>. This, Эмма has discovered, is where the last of her “case errors” hide: not the logic of the case, but the <b>verb's demand</b>. Each verb quietly dictates the case that follows it — and the fix is to learn every verb glued to its question: интересова́ться <b>чем</b>, зави́сеть <b>от чего́</b>.","introAudio":"'Interested in what?' In English the preposition is 'in'. In Russian there's no preposition at all — just a case: interesuyus muzykoy. This, Emma has discovered, is where the last of her 'case errors' hide: not the logic of the case, but the verb's demand. Each verb quietly dictates the case that follows it — and the fix is to learn every verb glued to its question: interesovatsya chem, zaviset ot chego.","goals":["Learn a verb together with the <b>case it demands</b> — интересова́ться <b>чем</b>, not «что»","Handle the instrumental verbs: <b>интересу́юсь му́зыкой</b>, <b>по́льзуюсь словарём</b>, <b>занима́юсь спо́ртом</b>","Handle the genitive verbs: <b>зави́сит от пого́ды</b>, <b>дости́г це́ли</b>, <b>бою́сь темноты́</b>","Handle к + dative and в + prepositional: <b>отношу́сь к э́тому</b>, <b>уча́ствую в прое́кте</b>"],"learned":["Learned verbs together with the case they demand — verb + question","Used the instrumental verbs: интересова́ться, по́льзоваться, занима́ться","Used the genitive verbs: зави́сеть от, дости́чь, боя́ться","Used к + dative (относи́ться) and в + prepositional (уча́ствовать)"],"review":{"intro":"Yesterday's passive, one minute: process vs result. It'll keep coming back — like all of week 2 — under today's governed cases.","introAudio":"Yesterday's passive, one minute: process versus result. It'll keep coming back — like all of week 2 — under today's governed cases.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. Process or result?","q":"“The house is being built” (a process, in progress) —","opts":["дом постро́ен","дом стро́ится","дом стро́или"],"answer":1,"explain":"Process → <b>-ся</b>: стро́ится. постро́ен is the finished result; стро́или is the spoken active «они́»."},{"type":"choice","q":"“The contract has been signed” (a finished result) —","opts":["догово́р подпи́сан","догово́р подпи́сывается","догово́р подпи́сывают"],"answer":0,"explain":"Finished result → short participle: <b>подпи́сан</b>. The other two describe an ongoing process."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type it. Stress marks optional.","q":"Say it out loud as active «они́»: «дом постро́ен» → «дом …» (they built):","accept":["построили","построили."],"placeholder":"постро́…","explain":"Spoken active «они́»: <b>постро́или</b>."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The verb chooses the case","title":"<em>Интересу́юсь чем?</em> — learn the verb with its question","paras":["You've spent three levels learning what each case <em>means</em>. Here's the twist B2 saves for last: sometimes a case carries <b>no meaning at all</b> — it's simply the case a particular verb <b>demands</b>. English does this with prepositions: you “depend <b>on</b>”, “participate <b>in</b>”, “are interested <b>in</b>”. Nobody explains why “on”; you just memorise it with the verb.","Russian works the same way, but with <b>cases</b> instead of (or on top of) prepositions. <b>интересова́ться</b> demands the <b>instrumental</b>: интересу́юсь <b>му́зыкой</b>. <b>зави́сеть</b> demands <b>от + genitive</b>: зави́сит <b>от пого́ды</b>. There's no logic to reverse-engineer — the verb just governs that case.","So change how you file verbs. Don't store «интересова́ться = to be interested». Store <b>интересова́ться + чем</b> — the verb <b>and its question</b> together. Learn the pair, and the case comes free every time."],"audio":"You've spent three levels learning what each case means. Here's the twist B2 saves for last: sometimes a case carries no meaning at all — it's simply the case a particular verb demands. English does this with prepositions: you depend on, participate in, are interested in. Nobody explains why 'on'; you just memorise it with the verb. Russian works the same way, but with cases instead of, or on top of, prepositions. Interesovatsya demands the instrumental: interesuyus muzykoy. Zaviset demands ot plus genitive: zavisit ot pogody. There's no logic to reverse-engineer — the verb just governs that case. So change how you file verbs. Don't store 'interesovatsya equals to be interested'. Store interesovatsya plus chem — the verb and its question together.","table":{"rows":[["интересова́ться чем? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"интересоваться чем\">🔊</button>","to be interested in — + instr."],["зави́сеть от чего́? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"зависеть от чего\">🔊</button>","to depend on — от + gen."],["уча́ствовать в чём? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"участвовать в чём\">🔊</button>","to participate in — в + prep."],["относи́ться к чему́? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"относиться к чему\">🔊</button>","to relate to, treat — к + dat."]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"What are you interested in?","en":"Чем ты <b>интересу́ешься</b>?","say":"Чем ты интересуешься?"},{"ru":"Everything depends on the weather.","en":"Всё <b>зави́сит</b> от пого́ды.","say":"Всё зависит от погоды."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я интересу́юсь му́зыку.","right":"Я интересу́юсь му́зыкой.","why":"интересова́ться answers <b>чем</b> (instrumental), never «что»: <b>му́зыкой</b>."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"интересова́ться answers the question…","opts":["что?","чем?","о чём?"],"answer":1,"explain":"Instrumental — <b>чем</b>: интересу́юсь му́зыкой. Not «что» (accusative) and not «о чём» (prepositional)."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks optional.","q":"Fill the case: «Я интересу́юсь ру́сской …» (literature — интересова́ться + чем):","accept":["литературой","литературой."],"placeholder":"литерату́…","explain":"Instrumental (чем): <b>литерату́рой</b>."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Verbs that take «чем» — the instrumental","title":"<em>интересу́юсь, занима́юсь, по́льзуюсь</em> + твор.","paras":["A whole family of everyday verbs governs the <b>instrumental</b> (the «чем» case). Three you'll use constantly: <b>интересова́ться</b> (be interested in), <b>занима́ться</b> (be busy with / do / study), <b>по́льзоваться</b> (use). All three answer <b>чем</b>.","интересу́юсь <b>исто́рией</b> (interested in history), занима́юсь <b>перево́дом</b> (I do translation), по́льзуюсь <b>словарём</b> (I use a dictionary). No preposition — the bare instrumental does it all.","Notice how un-English this is: “use a dictionary” has a plain object in English, but Russian says по́льзуюсь <b>словарём</b> — instrumental, because the verb demands it."],"audio":"A whole family of everyday verbs governs the instrumental, the 'chem' case. Three you'll use constantly: interesovatsya, be interested in; zanimatsya, be busy with, do or study; and polzovatsya, use. All three answer chem. Interesuyus istoriey, interested in history; zanimayus perevodom, I do translation; polzuyus slovaryom, I use a dictionary. No preposition — the bare instrumental does it all. Notice how un-English this is: 'use a dictionary' has a plain object in English, but Russian says polzuyus slovaryom, instrumental, because the verb demands it.","table":{"rows":[["интересу́юсь исто́рией <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"интересуюсь историей\">🔊</button>","interested in history"],["занима́юсь спо́ртом <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"занимаюсь спортом\">🔊</button>","I do sport"],["по́льзуюсь словарём <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"пользуюсь словарём\">🔊</button>","I use a dictionary"],["она́ занима́ется му́зыкой <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"она занимается музыкой\">🔊</button>","she does music"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"Emma does translation and is interested in literature.","en":"Э́мма <b>занима́ется</b> перево́дом и <b>интересу́ется</b> литерату́рой.","say":"Эмма занимается переводом и интересуется литературой."},{"ru":"May I use your phone?","en":"Мо́жно, я <b>воспо́льзуюсь</b> ва́шим телефо́ном?","say":"Можно, я воспользуюсь вашим телефоном?"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я по́льзуюсь словарь.","right":"Я по́льзуюсь словарём.","why":"по́льзоваться + <b>чем</b> (instrumental): словарём, not the bare «словарь»."}],"tasks":[{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“I'm interested in history.”</b>","want":"Я интересу́юсь исто́рией","answer":"Я интересу́юсь исто́рией"},{"type":"choice","q":"«по́льзоваться» takes —","opts":["+ вин. (что)","+ твор. (чем)","+ род. (чего́)"],"answer":1,"explain":"Instrumental: по́льзуюсь <b>словарём</b> — «чем»."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Verbs that take the genitive","title":"<em>зави́сеть от, дости́чь, боя́ться</em> + род.","paras":["Another family governs the <b>genitive</b>. Some take it through the preposition <b>от</b>: <b>зави́сеть от</b> (depend on) — зави́сит <b>от пого́ды</b>. Others take the bare genitive: <b>дости́чь / достига́ть</b> (achieve, reach) — дости́г <b>це́ли</b>; <b>боя́ться</b> (be afraid of) — бою́сь <b>темноты́</b>.","English hides the split: “achieve a goal” looks like a plain object, but Russian demands genitive: дости́чь <b>це́ли</b>, not «цель». “Afraid of” at least warns you with “of”.","File them with their question: зави́сеть <b>от чего́</b>, дости́чь <b>чего́</b>, боя́ться <b>чего́</b>."],"audio":"Another family governs the genitive. Some take it through the preposition ot: zaviset ot, depend on — zavisit ot pogody. Others take the bare genitive: dostich or dostigat, achieve or reach — dostig tseli; and boyatsya, be afraid of — boyus temnoty. English hides the split: 'achieve a goal' looks like a plain object, but Russian demands the genitive: dostich tseli, not 'tsel'. 'Afraid of' at least warns you with 'of'. File them with their question: zaviset ot chego, dostich chego, boyatsya chego.","table":{"rows":[["зави́сит от пого́ды <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"зависит от погоды\">🔊</button>","depends on the weather"],["дости́г свое́й це́ли <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"достиг своей цели\">🔊</button>","reached his goal"],["бою́сь темноты́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"боюсь темноты\">🔊</button>","afraid of the dark"],["э́то зави́сит от тебя́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"это зависит от тебя\">🔊</button>","it depends on you"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Success depends on hard work.","en":"Успе́х <b>зави́сит</b> от труда́.","say":"Успех зависит от труда."},{"ru":"She achieved her goal.","en":"Она́ <b>дости́гла</b> свое́й це́ли.","say":"Она достигла своей цели."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Он дости́г свою́ цель.","right":"Он дости́г свое́й це́ли.","why":"дости́чь governs the <b>genitive</b> (чего́): свое́й це́ли, not the accusative «свою́ цель»."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“Everything depends on you” —","opts":["Всё зави́сит на тебя́.","Всё зави́сит от тебя́.","Всё зави́сит тебе́."],"answer":1,"explain":"зави́сеть <b>от</b> + genitive: от тебя́. Not «на» (the English “on”) and not the dative."},{"type":"type","q":"“I'm afraid of the dark” — «Я бою́сь …» (темнота́, genitive):","accept":["темноты","темноты."],"placeholder":"темнот…","explain":"боя́ться + genitive: <b>темноты́</b>."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · «к чему́» and «в чём» — dative & prepositional","title":"<em>относи́ться к, уча́ствовать в, стреми́ться к</em>","paras":["Two more governed cases round out today. <b>относи́ться к</b> (to relate to / treat / feel about) takes <b>к + dative</b>: как ты <b>отно́сишься к э́тому</b>? (how do you feel about this?). <b>стреми́ться к</b> (strive for) also takes <b>к + dative</b>: стремлю́сь <b>к це́ли</b>.","<b>уча́ствовать в</b> (participate in) takes <b>в + prepositional</b>: уча́ствую <b>в прое́кте</b> (in a project), <b>в конфере́нции</b> (in a conference).","Same trick as ever: learn the verb welded to its preposition-and-case. относи́ться <b>к чему́</b>, уча́ствовать <b>в чём</b>. Say the pair aloud until the case feels automatic."],"audio":"Two more governed cases round out today. Otnositsya k, to relate to, treat, or feel about, takes k plus dative: kak ty otnosishsya k etomu, how do you feel about this? Stremitsya k, strive for, also takes k plus dative: stremlyus k tseli. Uchastvovat v, participate in, takes v plus prepositional: uchastvuyu v proyekte, in a project; v konferentsii, in a conference. Same trick as ever: learn the verb welded to its preposition and case. Otnositsya k chemu, uchastvovat v chyom. Say the pair aloud until the case feels automatic.","table":{"rows":[["отношу́сь к нему́ хорошо́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"отношусь к нему хорошо\">🔊</button>","I feel good about him (к + dat.)"],["уча́ствую в прое́кте <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"участвую в проекте\">🔊</button>","I take part in a project (в + prep.)"],["стремлю́сь к це́ли <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"стремлюсь к цели\">🔊</button>","I strive for the goal (к + dat.)"],["как ты к э́тому отно́сишься? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"как ты к этому относишься\">🔊</button>","how do you feel about it?"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"How do you feel about modern art?","en":"Как ты <b>отно́сишься</b> к совреме́нному иску́сству?","say":"Как ты относишься к современному искусству?"},{"ru":"Emma is taking part in a translators' conference.","en":"Э́мма <b>уча́ствует</b> в конфере́нции перево́дчиков.","say":"Эмма участвует в конференции переводчиков."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я уча́ствую на конфере́нции.","right":"Я уча́ствую в конфере́нции.","why":"уча́ствовать governs <b>в + prepositional</b>: в конфере́нции, not «на»."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"относи́ться takes —","opts":["к + dative","в + prepositional","от + genitive"],"answer":0,"explain":"относи́ться <b>к</b> + dative: отношу́сь к э́тому."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it.","target":"Я уча́ствую в но́вом прое́кте.","sub":"“I'm taking part in a new project” — уча́ствовать в + prepositional","want":"я участвую в новом проекте"},{"type":"type","q":"“How do you feel about this?” — «Как ты … к э́тому?» (относи́ться, ты-form):","accept":["относишься","относишься."],"placeholder":"отно́с…","explain":"относи́ться к + dative: <b>отно́сишься</b> к э́тому."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Over coffee, Ка́тя asks Эмма about her work and her interests — and every answer needs the case the verb demands. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Э́мма, чем ты сейча́с занима́ешься? Ещё перево́дишь?","ru":"Emma, what are you working on now? Still translating?"},{"who":"B","en":"Да, занима́юсь перево́дом рома́на. И о́чень интересу́юсь ру́сской литерату́рой.","ru":"Yes, I'm working on translating a novel. And I'm very interested in Russian literature."},{"who":"A","en":"Здо́рово! А от чего́ зави́сит, ско́лько вре́мени займёт перево́д?","ru":"Great! And what does it depend on, how much time a translation takes?"},{"who":"B","en":"Всё зави́сит от те́кста. Иногда́ одна́ фра́за тре́бует це́лого дня.","ru":"It all depends on the text. Sometimes one phrase requires a whole day."},{"who":"A","en":"Понима́ю. Кста́ти, ты уча́ствуешь в на́шей конфере́нции перево́дчиков?","ru":"I understand. By the way, are you taking part in our translators' conference?"},{"who":"B","en":"Обяза́тельно! Я всегда́ стремлю́сь к но́вым зна́ниям. А как ты отно́сишься к таки́м встре́чам?","ru":"Definitely! I always strive for new knowledge. And how do you feel about such gatherings?"},{"who":"A","en":"Отношу́сь о́чень положи́тельно. Гла́вное — по́льзоваться ка́ждой возмо́жностью.","ru":"I feel very positive about them. The main thing is to use every opportunity."},{"who":"B","en":"Согла́сна. Успе́х зави́сит не от уда́чи, а от труда́.","ru":"Agreed. Success depends not on luck, but on hard work."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve verbs and nouns for interests, dependence and participation — each verb with the case it governs. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"интересова́ться","ru":"to be interested in (+ instr.)","ex":"Я интересу́юсь исто́рией."},{"en":"занима́ться","ru":"to do, be engaged in (+ instr.)","ex":"Она́ занима́ется му́зыкой."},{"en":"по́льзоваться","ru":"to use (+ instr.)","ex":"Я по́льзуюсь но́вым словарём."},{"en":"зави́сеть","ru":"to depend (от + gen.)","ex":"Всё зави́сит от пого́ды."},{"en":"уча́ствовать","ru":"to take part (в + prep.)","ex":"Он уча́ствует в прое́кте."},{"en":"относи́ться","ru":"to relate to, treat (к + dat.)","ex":"Я хорошо́ отношу́сь к нему́."},{"en":"дости́чь","ru":"to achieve, reach (+ gen.)","ex":"Она́ дости́гла свое́й це́ли."},{"en":"стреми́ться","ru":"to strive for (к + dat.)","ex":"Мы стреми́мся к успе́ху."},{"en":"боя́ться","ru":"to be afraid of (+ gen.)","ex":"Ребёнок бои́тся темноты́."},{"en":"цель","ru":"goal, aim","ex":"Кака́я у тебя́ цель?"},{"en":"успе́х","ru":"success","ex":"Успе́х зави́сит от труда́."},{"en":"настрое́ние","ru":"mood","ex":"Моё настрое́ние зави́сит от пого́ды."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each verb-plus-case phrase to its meaning.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["интересу́юсь му́зыкой","interested in music"],["зави́сит от пого́ды","depends on the weather"],["уча́ствую в прое́кте","taking part in a project"],["по́льзуюсь словарём","I use a dictionary"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"интересова́ться governs —","opts":["+ вин. (что)","+ твор. (чем)","+ предл. (о чём)"],"answer":1},{"q":"“Everything depends on the weather” —","opts":["Всё зави́сит на пого́ду.","Всё зави́сит от пого́ды.","Всё зави́сит пого́де."],"answer":1},{"q":"“I use a dictionary” —","opts":["Я по́льзуюсь словарём.","Я по́льзуюсь слова́рь.","Я по́льзуюсь словаря́."],"answer":0},{"q":"уча́ствовать governs —","opts":["на + предл.","в + предл.","к + дат."],"answer":1},{"q":"“She reached her goal (achieved it)” —","opts":["Она́ дости́гла свою́ цель.","Она́ дости́гла свое́й це́ли.","Она́ дости́гла свое́й цель."],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's note about her work — read it aloud and watch each verb pull its own case.","title":"Чем я занима́юсь","sentences":["Я занима́юсь перево́дом и интересу́юсь ру́сской культу́рой.","Ка́чество перево́да зави́сит от мно́гого: от те́кста, от вре́мени, от настрое́ния.","Я всегда́ стремлю́сь к то́чности и по́льзуюсь хоро́шими словаря́ми.","На э́той неде́ле я уча́ствую в конфере́нции.","К свое́й рабо́те я отношу́сь о́чень серьёзно."],"translation":"I do translation and I'm interested in Russian culture. The quality of a translation depends on many things: on the text, on time, on mood. I always strive for accuracy and use good dictionaries. This week I'm taking part in a conference. I treat my work very seriously."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — talk about yourself with governed cases.","prompt":"Write 4–5 sentences about your interests and work in Russian, using verbs that govern a case: интересова́ться <b>+ instr.</b> (interested in), занима́ться <b>+ instr.</b> (do / study), зави́сеть от <b>+ gen.</b> (depend on), уча́ствовать в <b>+ prep.</b> (take part in), относи́ться к <b>+ dat.</b> (feel about). Keep each verb glued to its case.","hint":"интересу́юсь <b>чем</b> (му́зыкой), занима́юсь <b>чем</b> (спо́ртом), зави́сит <b>от чего́</b> (от пого́ды), уча́ствую <b>в чём</b> (в прое́кте), отношу́сь <b>к чему́</b> (к рабо́те). 🙂","example":"Я интересу́юсь исто́рией и занима́юсь му́зыкой. Моё настрое́ние ча́сто зави́сит от пого́ды. Я уча́ствую в шко́льном прое́кте и отношу́сь к нему́ о́чень серьёзно. Успе́х, ду́маю, зави́сит не от та́ланта, а от труда́."}},"12":{"day":12,"week":"02","themeRu":"Married to, playing at","themeEn":"Управле́ние: на, в, по, к","intro":"Эмма almost said «жени́ться с» — “marry <b>with</b>”, straight out of English. Stop right there. Today we hunt down every place where the English preposition <b>lies to you</b>. You don't marry «with» anyone in Russian, you don't just «play football», and you don't miss «about» home. Each verb owns its own preposition-and-case, and it rarely matches English. Let's disarm the traps one by one.","introAudio":"Emma almost said 'zhenitsya s' — 'marry with', straight out of English. Stop right there. Today we hunt down every place where the English preposition lies to you. You don't marry 'with' anyone in Russian, you don't just 'play football', and you don't miss 'about' home. Each verb owns its own preposition and case, and it rarely matches English. Let's disarm the traps one by one.","goals":["Marry without «with»: <b>жени́ться на</b> + prep. (a man), <b>вы́йти за́муж за</b> + acc. (a woman)","Split «play»: <b>игра́ть в</b> + acc. for games (в футбо́л), <b>игра́ть на</b> + prep. for instruments (на гита́ре)","Miss with по: <b>скуча́ть по</b> + dative (по до́му) — and <b>ждать</b> + gen./acc.","Turn to someone with <b>обраща́ться к / в / с</b>, and prepare with <b>гото́виться к</b> + dative"],"learned":["Married without «with»: жени́ться на + prep., вы́йти за́муж за + acc.","Split «play»: игра́ть в (games) vs игра́ть на (instruments)","Missed with по + dative and waited with ждать + gen./acc.","Turned to people with обраща́ться and prepared with гото́виться к"],"review":{"intro":"Yesterday's governed cases, one minute — the verb still picks the case. It's always-on now, so let's keep it warm before today's preposition traps.","introAudio":"Yesterday's governed cases, one minute — the verb still picks the case. It's always-on now, so let's keep it warm before today's preposition traps.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. Which case does the verb want?","q":"интересова́ться takes —","opts":["+ вин. (что)","+ твор. (чем)","+ предл. (о чём)"],"answer":1,"explain":"Instrumental: интересу́юсь <b>му́зыкой</b> — «чем»."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the preposition.","q":"“It all depends on the weather” — «Всё зави́сит … пого́ды.» (which preposition?):","accept":["от","от."],"placeholder":"…","explain":"зави́сеть <b>от</b> + genitive: от пого́ды."},{"type":"choice","q":"“I take part in a project” —","opts":["Я уча́ствую в прое́кте.","Я уча́ствую на прое́кте.","Я уча́ствую прое́кту."],"answer":0,"explain":"уча́ствовать <b>в</b> + prepositional: в прое́кте."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · You don't marry «with» anyone","title":"<em>Жени́ться на</em> + предл., <em>вы́йти за́муж за</em> + вин.","paras":["English “marry” is one word for everyone. Russian splits it by <b>who's marrying</b>, and — the trap — <b>neither</b> version uses «с» (“with”). A <b>man</b> marries with <b>жени́ться на</b> + <b>prepositional</b>: Анто́н <b>жени́лся на О́льге</b> (literally “married on Olga”). A <b>woman</b> marries with <b>вы́йти за́муж за</b> + <b>accusative</b>: О́льга <b>вы́шла за́муж за Анто́на</b>.","When the couple marries together, use the reflexive <b>пожени́ться</b>: они́ <b>пожени́лись</b> (they got married).","The one thing to unlearn: never «жени́ться с». It's <b>на</b> (+ prep.) for a man, <b>за</b> (+ acc.) for a woman."],"audio":"English 'marry' is one word for everyone. Russian splits it by who's marrying, and — the trap — neither version uses 's', 'with'. A man marries with zhenitsya na plus prepositional: Anton zhenilsya na Olge, literally 'married on Olga'. A woman marries with vyyti zamuzh za plus accusative: Olga vyshla zamuzh za Antona. When the couple marries together, use the reflexive pozhenitsya: oni pozhenilis, they got married. The one thing to unlearn: never 'zhenitsya s'. It's na plus prepositional for a man, za plus accusative for a woman.","table":{"rows":[["он жени́лся на ней <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"он женился на ней\">🔊</button>","he married her (a man)"],["она́ вы́шла за́муж за него́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"она вышла замуж за него\">🔊</button>","she married him (a woman)"],["они́ пожени́лись <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"они поженились\">🔊</button>","they got married (together)"],["он же́нится на Ма́ше <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"он женится на Маше\">🔊</button>","he is marrying Masha"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"My brother married a doctor.","en":"Мой брат <b>жени́лся на</b> враче́.","say":"Мой брат женился на враче."},{"ru":"She married an engineer.","en":"Она́ <b>вы́шла за́муж за</b> инжене́ра.","say":"Она вышла замуж за инженера."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Он жени́лся с Ма́шей.","right":"Он жени́лся на Ма́ше.","why":"Never «жени́ться с». A man marries <b>на + prepositional</b>: на Ма́ше."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"“Anton married Olga” (a man marrying) —","opts":["Анто́н жени́лся с О́льгой.","Анто́н жени́лся на О́льге.","Анто́н вы́шел за́муж за О́льгу."],"answer":1,"explain":"A man → жени́ться <b>на</b> + prepositional: на О́льге. «с» is the English calque; «вы́йти за́муж» is for a woman."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“She married an engineer.”</b>","want":"Она́ вы́шла за́муж за инжене́ра","answer":"Она́ вы́шла за́муж за инжене́ра"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · «Play» splits in two","title":"<em>Игра́ть в</em> футбо́л, <em>игра́ть на</em> гита́ре","paras":["“Play” is another one-word-in-English trap. For <b>games and sports</b>, Russian uses <b>игра́ть в</b> + <b>accusative</b>: игра́ть <b>в футбо́л</b>, <b>в ша́хматы</b>, <b>в те́ннис</b>. For <b>musical instruments</b>, it's <b>игра́ть на</b> + <b>prepositional</b>: игра́ть <b>на гита́ре</b>, <b>на пиани́но</b>, <b>на скри́пке</b>.","And don't drop the preposition the way English does. English says “play football” with a bare noun; Russian <b>must</b> have the «в»: never «игра́ть футбо́л», always <b>игра́ть в футбо́л</b>.","Quick test: a game you <b>play in</b> (в), an instrument you <b>play on</b> (на). Two prepositions, one verb."],"audio":"'Play' is another one-word-in-English trap. For games and sports, Russian uses igrat v plus accusative: igrat v futbol, v shakhmaty, v tennis. For musical instruments, it's igrat na plus prepositional: igrat na gitare, na pianino, na skripke. And don't drop the preposition the way English does. English says 'play football' with a bare noun; Russian must have the 'v': never 'igrat futbol', always igrat v futbol. Quick test: a game you play in, v, an instrument you play on, na. Two prepositions, one verb.","table":{"rows":[["игра́ть в футбо́л <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"играть в футбол\">🔊</button>","to play football (game → в)"],["игра́ть в ша́хматы <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"играть в шахматы\">🔊</button>","to play chess"],["игра́ть на гита́ре <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"играть на гитаре\">🔊</button>","to play the guitar (instrument → на)"],["игра́ть на пиани́но <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"играть на пианино\">🔊</button>","to play the piano"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"The kids are playing football in the yard.","en":"Де́ти <b>игра́ют в футбо́л</b> во дворе́.","say":"Дети играют в футбол во дворе."},{"ru":"She plays the guitar beautifully.","en":"Она́ прекра́сно <b>игра́ет на гита́ре</b>.","say":"Она прекрасно играет на гитаре."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я игра́ю футбо́л.","right":"Я игра́ю в футбо́л.","why":"A game needs <b>в</b> + accusative: <b>в футбо́л</b>. Dropping «в» is a direct copy of English."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“I play chess” —","opts":["Я игра́ю в ша́хматы.","Я игра́ю на ша́хматы.","Я игра́ю ша́хматы."],"answer":0,"explain":"A game → игра́ть <b>в</b> + accusative: в ша́хматы."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the phrase. Stress marks optional.","q":"Type “on the guitar” (игра́ть …) — instrument:","accept":["на гитаре","на гитаре."],"placeholder":"на гита́…","explain":"An instrument → игра́ть <b>на</b> + prepositional: на гита́ре."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Miss «по», wait for «чего́»","title":"<em>Скуча́ть по</em> + дат., <em>ждать</em> + род./вин.","paras":["“I miss home” — English uses no preposition, or you might reach for «о» (“about”). Wrong both ways. <b>скуча́ть</b> takes <b>по</b> + <b>dative</b>: скуча́ю <b>по до́му</b> (I miss home), скуча́ю <b>по друзья́м</b> (I miss my friends), скуча́ю <b>по тебе́</b> (I miss you).","<b>ждать</b> (to wait for) has a subtler rule. For a <b>vague / indefinite</b> thing it takes the <b>genitive</b>: жду <b>авто́буса</b> (waiting for a bus, any bus), жду <b>по́мощи</b>. For a <b>specific person or thing</b> it takes the <b>accusative</b>: жду <b>Ма́шу</b> (waiting for Masha), жду <b>э́тот по́езд</b>.","English “wait for” hides all this behind one “for”. Russian makes you choose: definite → accusative, indefinite → genitive."],"audio":"'I miss home' — English uses no preposition, or you might reach for 'o', about. Wrong both ways. Skuchat takes po plus dative: skuchayu po domu, I miss home; skuchayu po druzyam, I miss my friends; skuchayu po tebe, I miss you. Zhdat, to wait for, has a subtler rule. For a vague or indefinite thing it takes the genitive: zhdu avtobusa, waiting for a bus, any bus; zhdu pomoshchi. For a specific person or thing it takes the accusative: zhdu Mashu, waiting for Masha; zhdu etot poyezd. English 'wait for' hides all this behind one 'for'. Russian makes you choose: definite is accusative, indefinite is genitive.","table":{"rows":[["скуча́ю по до́му <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"скучаю по дому\">🔊</button>","I miss home (по + dat.)"],["скуча́ю по друзья́м <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"скучаю по друзьям\">🔊</button>","I miss my friends"],["жду авто́буса <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"жду автобуса\">🔊</button>","waiting for a bus (indef. → gen.)"],["жду Ма́шу <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"жду Машу\">🔊</button>","waiting for Masha (def. → acc.)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Emma misses her family in England.","en":"Э́мма <b>скуча́ет по</b> семье́ в А́нглии.","say":"Эмма скучает по семье в Англии."},{"ru":"I've been waiting for this letter for a long time.","en":"Я давно́ <b>жду</b> э́то письмо́.","say":"Я давно жду это письмо."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я скуча́ю о до́ме.","right":"Я скуча́ю по до́му.","why":"скуча́ть takes <b>по</b> + dative, never «о»: по до́му. «о» is the English “about” sneaking in."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“I miss my friends” —","opts":["Я скуча́ю о друзья́х.","Я скуча́ю по друзья́м.","Я скуча́ю друзе́й."],"answer":1,"explain":"скуча́ть <b>по</b> + dative: по друзья́м."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“I miss home.”</b>","want":"Я скуча́ю по до́му","answer":"Я скуча́ю по до́му"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · «обраща́ться» — one verb, three prepositions","title":"<em>Обраща́ться к / в / с</em>, <em>гото́виться к</em> + дат.","paras":["<b>обраща́ться</b> (to turn to / address / apply) is a translator's and bureaucrat's workhorse, and it flexes across three prepositions. To a <b>person</b>: <b>к</b> + dative — обрати́ться <b>к врачу́</b> (see a doctor), <b>к юри́сту</b>. To an <b>institution</b>: <b>в</b> + accusative — обрати́ться <b>в поли́цию</b>, <b>в банк</b>. With a <b>request / matter</b>: <b>с</b> + instrumental — обрати́ться <b>с про́сьбой</b>, <b>с вопро́сом</b>.","And one more high-frequency verb: <b>гото́виться к</b> (prepare for) takes <b>к</b> + <b>dative</b>: гото́влюсь <b>к экза́мену</b> (preparing for the exam), <b>к встре́че</b>.","None of these match English's “to”. As always: store the verb with its preposition-and-case, and the calques disappear."],"audio":"Obrashchatsya, to turn to, address or apply, is a translator's and bureaucrat's workhorse, and it flexes across three prepositions. To a person: k plus dative — obratitsya k vrachu, see a doctor; k yuristu. To an institution: v plus accusative — obratitsya v politsiyu, v bank. With a request or matter: s plus instrumental — obratitsya s prosboy, s voprosom. And one more high-frequency verb: gotovitsya k, prepare for, takes k plus dative: gotovlyus k ekzamenu, preparing for the exam; k vstreche. None of these match English's 'to'. As always: store the verb with its preposition and case, and the calques disappear.","table":{"rows":[["обрати́ться к врачу́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"обратиться к врачу\">🔊</button>","to see a doctor (к + dat.)"],["обрати́ться в поли́цию <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"обратиться в полицию\">🔊</button>","to go to the police (в + acc.)"],["обрати́ться с про́сьбой <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"обратиться с просьбой\">🔊</button>","to make a request (с + instr.)"],["гото́виться к экза́мену <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"готовиться к экзамену\">🔊</button>","to prepare for the exam (к + dat.)"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"Ivan is preparing for his last exam.","en":"Ива́н <b>гото́вится к</b> после́днему экза́мену.","say":"Иван готовится к последнему экзамену."},{"ru":"Because of the document, Emma turned to a lawyer.","en":"Из-за докуме́нта Э́мма <b>обрати́лась к</b> юри́сту.","say":"Из-за документа Эмма обратилась к юристу."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я гото́влюсь для экза́мена.","right":"Я гото́влюсь к экза́мену.","why":"гото́виться takes <b>к</b> + dative: к экза́мену, not «для» + genitive."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“She turned to a doctor” —","opts":["Она́ обрати́лась в врача́.","Она́ обрати́лась с врачо́м.","Она́ обрати́лась к врачу́."],"answer":2,"explain":"To a person → <b>к</b> + dative: к врачу́. «в» is for institutions, «с» is for a request."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it.","target":"Я гото́влюсь к ва́жному экза́мену.","sub":"“I'm preparing for an important exam” — гото́виться к + dative","want":"я готовлюсь к важному экзамену"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"At the café, Ле́на and Эмма catch up — someone's playing guitar in the corner, Ива́н is buried in exam prep, and every verb wants its own preposition. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Слы́шишь? Кто-то игра́ет на гита́ре в углу́. Так ую́тно.","ru":"Do you hear? Someone's playing the guitar in the corner. So cozy."},{"who":"B","en":"Пре́лесть. А где Ива́н? Давно́ его́ не ви́дела.","ru":"Lovely. And where's Ivan? Haven't seen him in a while."},{"who":"A","en":"Гото́вится к после́днему экза́мену. Совсе́м не игра́ет ни в футбо́л, ни в ша́хматы.","ru":"He's preparing for his final exam. He doesn't play football or chess at all anymore."},{"who":"B","en":"Понима́ю его́. Я то́же иногда́ скуча́ю по споко́йным вечера́м.","ru":"I understand him. I sometimes miss quiet evenings too."},{"who":"A","en":"Кста́ти, моя́ подру́га на про́шлой неде́ле вы́шла за́муж за музыка́нта.","ru":"By the way, my friend got married to a musician last week."},{"who":"B","en":"Как романти́чно! А он то́же жени́лся по любви́?","ru":"How romantic! And did he also marry for love?"},{"who":"A","en":"Коне́чно. Они́ до́лго жда́ли э́того дня. Пожени́лись — и сра́зу уе́хали.","ru":"Of course. They waited for this day for a long time. Got married — and left right away."},{"who":"B","en":"За них ра́достно. Ла́дно, мне пора́ — обеща́ла обрати́ться в банк до обе́да.","ru":"I'm happy for them. Okay, I have to go — I promised to go to the bank before lunch."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve verbs and nouns where the Russian preposition refuses to match English. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"жени́ться","ru":"to marry (a man; на + prep.)","ex":"Он жени́лся на Ма́ше."},{"en":"вы́йти за́муж","ru":"to marry (a woman; за + acc.)","ex":"Она́ вы́шла за́муж за Анто́на."},{"en":"игра́ть","ru":"to play (в + acc. game / на + prep. instrument)","ex":"Де́ти игра́ют в футбо́л."},{"en":"скуча́ть","ru":"to miss (по + dat.)","ex":"Я скуча́ю по до́му."},{"en":"гото́виться","ru":"to prepare (к + dat.)","ex":"Ива́н гото́вится к экза́мену."},{"en":"обраща́ться","ru":"to turn to, apply (к / в / с)","ex":"Обрати́тесь к врачу́."},{"en":"ждать","ru":"to wait for (+ gen. / acc.)","ex":"Я жду авто́буса."},{"en":"футбо́л","ru":"football, soccer","ex":"Мы игра́ем в футбо́л."},{"en":"гита́ра","ru":"guitar","ex":"Она́ игра́ет на гита́ре."},{"en":"экза́мен","ru":"exam","ex":"За́втра ва́жный экза́мен."},{"en":"про́сьба","ru":"request","ex":"Я обраща́юсь к вам с про́сьбой."},{"en":"сва́дьба","ru":"wedding","ex":"Их сва́дьба была́ ле́том."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each verb-plus-preposition phrase to its meaning.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["жени́ться на Ма́ше","to marry Masha (a man)"],["игра́ть в футбо́л","to play football"],["игра́ть на гита́ре","to play the guitar"],["скуча́ть по до́му","to miss home"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"A man marrying: “Anton married Olga” —","opts":["Анто́н жени́лся с О́льгой.","Анто́н жени́лся на О́льге.","Анто́н жени́лся О́льге."],"answer":1},{"q":"“I play football” —","opts":["Я игра́ю футбо́л.","Я игра́ю в футбо́л.","Я игра́ю на футбо́л."],"answer":1},{"q":"“She plays the piano” —","opts":["Она́ игра́ет на пиани́но.","Она́ игра́ет в пиани́но.","Она́ игра́ет пиани́но."],"answer":0},{"q":"“I miss my friends” —","opts":["Я скуча́ю о друзья́х.","Я скуча́ю по друзья́м.","Я скуча́ю за друзья́ми."],"answer":1},{"q":"“to prepare for an exam” —","opts":["гото́виться для экза́мена","гото́виться к экза́мену","гото́виться на экза́мен"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's note on the little words that trip her up — read it aloud.","title":"Ма́ленькие слова́, больши́е оши́бки","sentences":["Ру́сские предло́ги — моё сла́бое ме́сто.","Мужчи́на же́нится на же́нщине, а же́нщина выхо́дит за́муж за мужчи́ну.","Игра́ют в футбо́л, но игра́ют на гита́ре.","Я скуча́ю по до́му и по друзья́м, а не «о до́ме».","Тепе́рь я учу́ ка́ждый глаго́л вме́сте с его́ предло́гом."],"translation":"Russian prepositions are my weak spot. A man marries a woman («на» + prep.), and a woman marries a man («за» + acc.). People play football (with «в»), but play the guitar (with «на»). I miss home and my friends (both with «по»), not “about home”. Now I learn each verb together with its preposition."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — catch the preposition traps in your own life.","prompt":"Write 4–5 sentences in Russian using these tricky-government verbs — and get the preposition right where English would mislead you: <b>жени́ться на</b> / <b>вы́йти за́муж за</b> (marry), <b>игра́ть в / на</b> (play a game / an instrument), <b>скуча́ть по</b> (miss), <b>гото́виться к</b> (prepare for), <b>обраща́ться к / в</b> (turn to).","hint":"жени́ться <b>на</b> (Ма́ше), игра́ть <b>в</b> футбо́л / <b>на</b> гита́ре, скуча́ть <b>по</b> до́му, гото́виться <b>к</b> экза́мену, обраща́ться <b>к</b> врачу́. 🙂","example":"В свобо́дное вре́мя я игра́ю в те́ннис и на пиани́но. Сейча́с я гото́влюсь к ва́жному экза́мену. Я ча́сто скуча́ю по свои́м роди́телям. Моя́ сестра́ неда́вно вы́шла за́муж за до́брого челове́ка, и я о́чень за неё ра́да."}},"13":{"day":13,"week":"02","themeRu":"Which aspect now","themeEn":"Вид: прика́з, отрица́ние, перено́сный смысл","intro":"«Не упади́!» Ле́на calls out as Эмма steps over a puddle — a perfective, a one-off <em>watch-you-don't</em>. But «Не смотри́!» (“don't watch”) would be imperfective. Same «не + imperative», opposite aspect, opposite meaning. Today aspect goes deep: it splits your commands, colours your negations, and even sends motion verbs off into metaphor. This is the finish of the aspect story you began back in B1.","introAudio":"'Ne upadi!' Lena calls out as Emma steps over a puddle — a perfective, a one-off watch-you-don't. But 'Ne smotri!', don't watch, would be imperfective. Same 'ne plus imperative', opposite aspect, opposite meaning. Today aspect goes deep: it splits your commands, colours your negations, and even sends motion verbs off into metaphor. This is the finish of the aspect story you began back in B1.","goals":["Choose imperative aspect: a general <b>ban</b> is imperfective (<b>Не смотри́!</b>), a <b>warning</b> against a slip is perfective (<b>Не упади́! Не забу́дь!</b>)","Invite with the imperfective (<b>Проходи́те! Сади́тесь!</b>), ask one concrete thing with the perfective (<b>Закро́йте дверь</b>)","Split negation by aspect: <b>не чита́л</b> (didn't read it at all) vs <b>не прочита́л</b> (didn't finish it)","Hear motion verbs go figurative: <b>перенести́ встре́чу</b>, <b>провести́ вре́мя</b>, <b>войти́ в исто́рию</b>"],"learned":["Chose imperative aspect — imperfective ban vs perfective warning","Invited with the imperfective, asked one concrete thing with the perfective","Split negation: не чита́л (didn't happen) vs не прочита́л (didn't finish)","Heard prefixed motion verbs turn figurative (перенести́, провести́, войти́ в исто́рию)"],"review":{"intro":"Government one more time — it's always-on now. The verb still names the case, and the preposition still isn't the English one. Then we finish the aspect story.","introAudio":"Government one more time — it's always-on now. The verb still names the case, and the preposition still isn't the English one. Then we finish the aspect story.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. Which case does the verb want?","q":"“I'm interested in music” —","opts":["Я интересу́юсь му́зыку.","Я интересу́юсь му́зыкой.","Я интересу́юсь о му́зыке."],"answer":1,"explain":"интересова́ться + instrumental: <b>му́зыкой</b> — «чем»."},{"type":"choice","q":"“He married Masha” (a man) —","opts":["Он жени́лся на Ма́ше.","Он жени́лся с Ма́шей.","Он жени́лся Ма́шу."],"answer":0,"explain":"A man → жени́ться <b>на</b> + prepositional: на Ма́ше. Never «с»."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the preposition.","q":"“I miss home” — «Я скуча́ю … до́му.» (which preposition?):","accept":["по","по."],"placeholder":"…","explain":"скуча́ть <b>по</b> + dative: по до́му."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · «Не смотри́!» vs «Не упади́!»","title":"Prohibition is imperfective, a warning is perfective","paras":["You can already form commands. Now aspect gives them meaning. A <b>negative</b> command splits cleanly. With the <b>imperfective</b>, «не + imperative» is a <b>general prohibition</b> — don't (ever, in general) do this: <b>Не смотри́!</b> (don't watch), <b>Не кури́!</b> (don't smoke), <b>Не говори́ так!</b> (don't talk like that).","With the <b>perfective</b>, «не + imperative» becomes a <b>warning</b> against a single accidental slip — “mind you don't…”: <b>Не упади́!</b> (don't fall!), <b>Не забу́дь!</b> (don't forget!), <b>Не опозда́й!</b> (don't be late!). You're not forbidding falling as a habit — you're flagging one possible accident.","So the aspect flips the whole tone: imperfective <b>Не смотри́</b> = “don't do this”; perfective <b>Не забу́дь</b> = “careful you don't let this slip”."],"audio":"You can already form commands. Now aspect gives them meaning. A negative command splits cleanly. With the imperfective, 'ne plus imperative' is a general prohibition — don't, ever, in general, do this: Ne smotri, don't watch; Ne kuri, don't smoke; Ne govori tak, don't talk like that. With the perfective, 'ne plus imperative' becomes a warning against a single accidental slip, 'mind you don't': Ne upadi, don't fall; Ne zabud, don't forget; Ne opozday, don't be late. You're not forbidding falling as a habit — you're flagging one possible accident. So the aspect flips the whole tone: imperfective Ne smotri, 'don't do this'; perfective Ne zabud, 'careful you don't let this slip'.","table":{"rows":[["Не смотри́! <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Не смотри\">🔊</button>","Don't watch! (ban, impf.)"],["Не забу́дь! <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Не забудь\">🔊</button>","Don't forget! (warning, pf.)"],["Не упади́! <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Не упади\">🔊</button>","Don't fall! (warning, pf.)"],["Не опа́здывай! <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Не опаздывай\">🔊</button>","Don't be late! (habitual ban, impf.)"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"Don't smoke here — it's a general rule.","en":"<b>Не кури́</b> здесь — э́то о́бщее пра́вило.","say":"Не кури здесь — это общее правило."},{"ru":"Careful, don't forget the documents!","en":"Осторо́жно, <b>не забу́дь</b> докуме́нты!","say":"Осторожно, не забудь документы!"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Не забыва́й ключи́! (for “don't forget this once”)","right":"Не забу́дь ключи́!","why":"For “don't forget (this once)”, use the <b>perfective</b>: <b>Не забу́дь</b>. The imperfective «не забыва́й» means “don't keep forgetting” (a habit)."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"A one-off warning: “Don't fall!” —","opts":["Не па́дай!","Не упади́!","Не упа́л!"],"answer":1,"explain":"A one-off warning → perfective: <b>Не упади́!</b> «Не па́дай» = “don't keep falling” (a habit)."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type it. Stress marks optional.","q":"“Don't forget!” (this once, perfective):","accept":["не забудь","не забудь!","не забудь."],"placeholder":"не заб…","explain":"A one-time warning → perfective: <b>Не забу́дь!</b>"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · «Проходи́те!» vs «Прочита́йте!»","title":"Invitation is imperfective, one concrete request is perfective","paras":["Positive commands split by aspect too. The <b>imperfective</b> imperative is warm and open — an <b>invitation</b> or a “go ahead, get on with it”: <b>Проходи́те!</b> (come in!), <b>Сади́тесь!</b> (have a seat!), <b>Бери́те!</b> (help yourself!). It welcomes the action generally, without focusing on its completion.","The <b>perfective</b> imperative asks for <b>one specific, completed</b> action: <b>Прочита́йте э́то письмо́</b> (read this letter — this one, all of it), <b>Закро́йте дверь</b> (close the door), <b>Скажи́те, пожа́луйста…</b> (tell me, please…).","That's why «Сади́тесь!» (impf.) feels hospitable but «Ся́дьте!» (pf.) sounds like a sharp order. Aspect carries the courtesy."],"audio":"Positive commands split by aspect too. The imperfective imperative is warm and open — an invitation or a 'go ahead, get on with it': Prokhodite, come in; Sadites, have a seat; Berite, help yourself. It welcomes the action generally, without focusing on its completion. The perfective imperative asks for one specific, completed action: Prochitayte eto pismo, read this letter, this one, all of it; Zakroyte dver, close the door; Skazhite, pozhaluysta, tell me, please. That's why 'Sadites', imperfective, feels hospitable but 'Syadte', perfective, sounds like a sharp order. Aspect carries the courtesy.","table":{"rows":[["Проходи́те! <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Проходите\">🔊</button>","Come in! (invitation, impf.)"],["Сади́тесь! <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Садитесь\">🔊</button>","Have a seat! (invitation, impf.)"],["Закро́йте дверь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Закройте дверь\">🔊</button>","Close the door (one action, pf.)"],["Прочита́йте письмо́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Прочитайте письмо\">🔊</button>","Read the letter (one action, pf.)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Come in, have a seat, help yourselves!","en":"<b>Проходи́те, сади́тесь, бери́те!</b>","say":"Проходите, садитесь, берите!"},{"ru":"Please read this document carefully.","en":"Пожа́луйста, внима́тельно <b>прочита́йте</b> э́тот докуме́нт.","say":"Пожалуйста, внимательно прочитайте этот документ."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Сядь, пожа́луйста, чу́вствуй себя́ как до́ма.","right":"Сади́сь, пожа́луйста, чу́вствуй себя́ как до́ма.","why":"For a warm invitation use the <b>imperfective</b>: <b>Сади́сь / Сади́тесь</b>. The perfective «Сядь!» sounds like a curt order."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Welcoming a guest: “Come in, have a seat!” —","opts":["Пройди́те, ся́дьте!","Проходи́те, сади́тесь!","Проходи́, сядь!"],"answer":1,"explain":"An invitation → imperfective: <b>Проходи́те, сади́тесь!</b> The perfective sounds like a command."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build the one-time request: <b>“Close the door, please.”</b>","want":"Закро́йте дверь, пожа́луйста","answer":"Закро́йте дверь, пожа́луйста"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · «Не чита́л» vs «не прочита́л»","title":"Didn't do it — or didn't finish it?","paras":["Negation and aspect together make a distinction English simply doesn't mark. <b>Не чита́л</b> (imperfective) = the action <b>didn't happen at all</b> — “I didn't read it / never read it”: Я <b>не чита́л</b> э́ту кни́гу (I haven't read this book). The focus is on the absence of the activity.","<b>Не прочита́л</b> (perfective) = the action <b>started but the result wasn't reached</b> — “I didn't finish it”: Я <b>не прочита́л</b> кни́гу (I didn't get through the book, though I began). The focus is on the missing result.","So «Он не сде́лал дома́шнее зада́ние» = he didn't get it done, whereas «Он не де́лал дома́шнее зада́ние» = he didn't do it at all. One little aspect, two different stories for the teacher."],"audio":"Negation and aspect together make a distinction English simply doesn't mark. Ne chital, imperfective, means the action didn't happen at all — 'I didn't read it, never read it': Ya ne chital etu knigu, I haven't read this book. The focus is on the absence of the activity. Ne prochital, perfective, means the action started but the result wasn't reached — 'I didn't finish it': Ya ne prochital knigu, I didn't get through the book, though I began. The focus is on the missing result. So 'On ne sdelal domashneye zadaniye' means he didn't get it done, whereas 'On ne delal domashneye zadaniye' means he didn't do it at all. One little aspect, two different stories for the teacher.","table":{"rows":[["я не чита́л э́ту кни́гу <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"я не читал эту книгу\">🔊</button>","I haven't read this book (didn't happen)"],["я не прочита́л кни́гу <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"я не прочитал книгу\">🔊</button>","I didn't finish the book"],["он не де́лал зада́ние <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"он не делал задание\">🔊</button>","he didn't do the task at all"],["он не сде́лал зада́ние <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"он не сделал задание\">🔊</button>","he didn't get the task done"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"I haven't read this article (didn't read it).","en":"Я <b>не чита́л</b> э́ту статью́.","say":"Я не читал эту статью."},{"ru":"I read the book, but didn't finish it.","en":"Я чита́л кни́гу, но <b>не прочита́л</b> её.","say":"Я читал книгу, но не прочитал её."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я не прочита́л но́вости, поэ́тому не зна́ю. (meaning “didn't read them at all”)","right":"Я не чита́л но́вости, поэ́тому не зна́ю.","why":"“Didn't read it at all” → <b>imperfective</b>: не чита́л. «не прочита́л» would mean you started but didn't finish."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“I haven't read this book” (didn't read it at all) —","opts":["Я не прочита́л э́ту кни́гу.","Я не чита́л э́ту кни́гу.","Я не чита́ю э́ту кни́гу."],"answer":1,"explain":"Action didn't happen → imperfective: <b>не чита́л</b>. «не прочита́л» = started but didn't finish."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the verb. Stress marks optional.","q":"“He didn't finish the book” (began, but no result): «Он не … кни́гу.» (прочита́ть):","accept":["прочитал","прочитал."],"placeholder":"прочит…","explain":"Missing result → perfective: <b>не прочита́л</b>."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · When motion goes figurative","title":"<em>Перенести́ встре́чу, провести́ вре́мя, войти́ в исто́рию</em>","paras":["Your verbs of motion have a second life: bolted to a prefix, they leave the road and turn <b>figurative</b>. <b>перенести́</b> (carry across) → <b>перенести́ встре́чу</b> (reschedule / postpone a meeting). <b>провести́</b> (lead through) → <b>провести́ уро́к</b> (give a lesson), <b>провести́ вре́мя</b> (spend time), <b>провести́ иссле́дование</b> (conduct research).","More of them: <b>вы́йти за́муж</b> (a woman marries — literally “go out into marriage”), <b>войти́ в исто́рию</b> (go down in history), <b>довести́ до конца́</b> (see through to the end), <b>вы́йти из себя́</b> (lose one's temper).","You don't translate these word by word — <b>провести́ вре́мя</b> isn't “lead time across”. Learn each as a fixed expression, and notice how the aspect pair still works: <b>переноси́ть / перенести́</b> встре́чу."],"audio":"Your verbs of motion have a second life: bolted to a prefix, they leave the road and turn figurative. Perenesti, carry across, becomes perenesti vstrechu, reschedule or postpone a meeting. Provesti, lead through, becomes provesti urok, give a lesson; provesti vremya, spend time; provesti issledovaniye, conduct research. More of them: vyyti zamuzh, a woman marries, literally 'go out into marriage'; voyti v istoriyu, go down in history; dovesti do kontsa, see through to the end; vyyti iz sebya, lose one's temper. You don't translate these word by word — provesti vremya isn't 'lead time across'. Learn each as a fixed expression, and notice how the aspect pair still works: perenosit, perenesti vstrechu.","table":{"rows":[["перенести́ встре́чу <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"перенести встречу\">🔊</button>","to reschedule a meeting"],["провести́ вре́мя <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"провести время\">🔊</button>","to spend time"],["войти́ в исто́рию <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"войти в историю\">🔊</button>","to go down in history"],["довести́ до конца́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"довести до конца\">🔊</button>","to see through to the end"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"We had to reschedule the meeting to Friday.","en":"Нам пришло́сь <b>перенести́</b> встре́чу на пя́тницу.","say":"Нам пришлось перенести встречу на пятницу."},{"ru":"We spent the evening wonderfully.","en":"Мы прекра́сно <b>провели́</b> ве́чер.","say":"Мы прекрасно провели вечер."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Мы хорошо́ провели́ на вечери́нке.","right":"Мы хорошо́ провели́ вре́мя на вечери́нке.","why":"<b>провести́ вре́мя</b> is a fixed phrase — “spend time”. You can't drop «вре́мя» the way English drops it in “we had a good time”."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“to reschedule the meeting” —","opts":["перенести́ встре́чу","принести́ встре́чу","унести́ встре́чу"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>перенести́</b> (carry across → move) встре́чу = reschedule. принести́ = bring; унести́ = carry away."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it.","target":"Дава́й перенесём встре́чу на за́втра.","sub":"“Let's move the meeting to tomorrow” — figurative перенести́","want":"давай перенесём встречу на завтра"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Ива́н has invited Эмма over between study sessions. Watch the aspects at work — invitations, a warning, a rescheduled plan. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Проходи́, Э́мма, сади́сь! Осторо́жно, не упади́ — здесь ступе́нька.","ru":"Come in, Emma, have a seat! Careful, don't fall — there's a step here."},{"who":"B","en":"Спаси́бо! Как твоя́ подгото́вка к экза́мену?","ru":"Thanks! How's your exam prep going?"},{"who":"A","en":"Тяжело́. Я чита́л уче́бник весь день, но так и не прочита́л после́днюю главу́.","ru":"Hard. I read the textbook all day, but never did finish the last chapter."},{"who":"B","en":"Не волну́йся. Гла́вное — не забу́дь отдохну́ть пе́ред экза́меном.","ru":"Don't worry. The main thing — don't forget to rest before the exam."},{"who":"A","en":"Постара́юсь. Кста́ти, дава́й перенесём наш киновы́ход на суббо́ту?","ru":"I'll try. By the way, let's move our movie outing to Saturday?"},{"who":"B","en":"Дава́й. За́втра я всё равно́ занята́ — сдаю́ ва́жный перево́д.","ru":"Let's. Tomorrow I'm busy anyway — I'm handing in an important translation."},{"who":"A","en":"Тогда́ в суббо́ту хорошо́ проведём вре́мя. Ты заслужи́ла о́тдых.","ru":"Then on Saturday we'll spend the time well. You've earned a rest."},{"who":"B","en":"Спаси́бо! Ла́дно, учи́сь, не отвлека́йся. Я побежа́ла.","ru":"Thanks! Okay, study, don't get distracted. I'm off."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve verbs and nouns for commands, negation and figurative motion — aspect at work. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"упа́сть","ru":"to fall (pf.) — «Не упади́!»","ex":"Осторо́жно, не упади́!"},{"en":"забы́ть","ru":"to forget (pf.) — «Не забу́дь!»","ex":"Не забу́дь ключи́!"},{"en":"опозда́ть","ru":"to be late (pf.) — «Не опозда́й!»","ex":"Не опозда́й на по́езд!"},{"en":"проходи́ть","ru":"to come in, pass (impf. invitation)","ex":"Проходи́те, сади́тесь!"},{"en":"перенести́","ru":"to reschedule, postpone","ex":"Дава́й перенесём встре́чу."},{"en":"провести́","ru":"to spend (time), conduct","ex":"Мы хорошо́ провели́ ве́чер."},{"en":"войти́","ru":"to enter; to go down (in history)","ex":"Э́тот день войдёт в исто́рию."},{"en":"довести́","ru":"to bring to, see through","ex":"На́до довести́ де́ло до конца́."},{"en":"запре́т","ru":"prohibition, ban","ex":"Э́то стро́гий запре́т."},{"en":"предупрежде́ние","ru":"warning","ex":"Спаси́бо за предупрежде́ние!"},{"en":"о́тдых","ru":"rest, break","ex":"Тебе́ ну́жен о́тдых."},{"en":"вид","ru":"aspect (grammatical); look, view","ex":"Ру́сский вид несёт смысл."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each command or phrase to its meaning.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Не упади́!","Don't fall! (one-off warning)"],["Не кури́!","Don't smoke! (general ban)"],["перенести́ встре́чу","to reschedule a meeting"],["провести́ вре́мя","to spend time"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"A general ban: “Don't smoke here!” —","opts":["Не покури́ здесь!","Не кури́ здесь!","Не курю́ здесь!"],"answer":1},{"q":"A one-off warning (this once): “Don't be late!” —","opts":["Не опа́здывай!","Не опозда́й!","Не опозда́ешь!"],"answer":1},{"q":"“I haven't read this book” (didn't read it at all) —","opts":["Я не прочита́л э́ту кни́гу.","Я не чита́л э́ту кни́гу.","Я не чита́ю э́ту кни́гу."],"answer":1},{"q":"A warm invitation: “Come in, have a seat!” —","opts":["Проходи́те, сади́тесь!","Пройди́те, ся́дьте!","Проходи́, сядь!"],"answer":0},{"q":"“to reschedule a meeting” —","opts":["перенести́ встре́чу","провести́ встре́чу","войти́ во встре́чу"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's note on how aspect changes everything — read it aloud.","title":"Оди́н глаго́л, две исто́рии","sentences":["«Не упади́!» — предупрежда́ют тебя́ оди́н раз.","«Не па́дай ду́хом!» — сове́туют не сдава́ться никогда́.","Я не прочита́л статью́ до конца́, но за́втра допишу́ отзы́в.","Мы перенесли́ встре́чу и прекра́сно провели́ ве́чер.","Ру́сский вид — э́то не про́сто вре́мя, э́то то́чность смы́сла."],"translation":"“Don't fall!” — they warn you once. “Don't lose heart!” — they advise you never to give up. I didn't finish reading the article, but tomorrow I'll write the review. We rescheduled the meeting and spent the evening wonderfully. Russian aspect isn't just about time — it's about precision of meaning."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — put aspect to work in commands, negation, and a figurative motion verb.","prompt":"Write 5–6 sentences in Russian that show aspect choosing meaning: one <b>ban</b> (imperfective «не + imperative»: <b>Не смотри́!</b>), one <b>warning</b> (perfective: <b>Не забу́дь! / Не упади́!</b>), one <b>invitation</b> (imperfective: <b>Проходи́те!</b>), one negated pair (<b>не чита́л</b> vs <b>не прочита́л</b>), and one figurative motion verb (<b>перенести́, провести́, войти́ в исто́рию</b>).","hint":"Ban = imperfective (Не кури́!). Warning = perfective (Не опозда́й!). Invitation = imperfective (Сади́тесь!). «не чита́л» = didn't read it at all; «не прочита́л» = didn't finish. провести́ вре́мя = spend time. 🙂","example":"Проходи́те, сади́тесь, чу́вствуйте себя́ как до́ма! То́лько не забу́дьте вы́ключить телефо́н. Я не чита́л сего́дняшнюю газе́ту — про́сто не́ было вре́мени. А вчера́шнюю на́чал, но так и не прочита́л. Дава́йте перенесём разгово́р на ве́чер и споко́йно проведём вре́мя вме́сте."}},"14":{"day":14,"week":"02","themeRu":"Review 2","themeEn":"Повторе́ние 2","intro":"A week ago a Russian sentence could really do one thing at a time. Now it walks and thinks at once — «Чита́я письмо́, она́ пла́кала» — it says what happens <b>to</b> things in the passive, and it bends to whatever case a verb quietly demands. Week two turned Russian from a row of separate clauses into one layered breath. Nothing new today: let's just watch the machine run.","introAudio":"A week ago a Russian sentence could really do one thing at a time. Now it walks and thinks at once — chitaya pismo, ona plakala — it says what happens to things in the passive, and it bends to whatever case a verb quietly demands. Week two turned Russian from a row of separate clauses into one layered breath. Nothing new today: let's just watch the machine run.","goals":["Chain two actions with a gerund, keeping <b>one</b> doer: <b>Прочита́в письмо́, она́ ушла́</b>","Say it passively two ways: process <b>дом стро́ится</b>, result <b>дом постро́ен</b> (and «они́» in speech)","Give a verb the case it demands: <b>интересу́юсь му́зыкой</b>, <b>зави́сит от пого́ды</b>","Let aspect carry the nuance: <b>Не смотри́!</b> (stop) vs <b>Не забу́дь!</b> (mind you don't)"],"learned":["Chained actions with a gerund, one doer throughout: <b>Прочита́в письмо́, она́ ушла́</b>","Said it passively two ways: process <b>стро́ится</b>, result <b>постро́ен</b>, active «они́» in speech","Gave each verb the case it demands: <b>интересу́юсь му́зыкой</b>, <b>зави́сит от пого́ды</b>","Let aspect carry meaning: <b>Не смотри́!</b> vs <b>Не забу́дь!</b>"],"review":{"intro":"Straight in — a quick pull from every corner of the week. You built all of this; no hints needed.","introAudio":"Straight in — a quick pull from every corner of the week. You built all of this; no hints needed.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. One doer must run through both halves.","q":"“Having finished the article, Emma called Ivan.” — which keeps one doer?","opts":["Зако́нчив статью́, зазвони́л телефо́н.","Зако́нчив статью́, Э́мма позвони́ла Ива́ну.","Зако́нчив статью́, Ива́ну позвони́ли."],"answer":1,"explain":"The one finishing the article must be the one calling: <b>Зако́нчив статью́, Э́мма позвони́ла</b>. In the first, a phone can't finish an article — that's the dangling-gerund trap."},{"type":"choice","q":"The house is still going up. “The house is being built.” —","opts":["Дом постро́ен.","Дом стро́ится.","Дом постро́ился."],"answer":1,"explain":"An ongoing process → the imperfective <b>-ся</b>-passive: <b>дом стро́ится</b>. постро́ен is the finished result (day 10)."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“I'm interested in Russian literature.”</b> (the verb wants the instrumental)","want":"Я интересу́юсь ру́сской литерату́рой","answer":"Я интересу́юсь ру́сской литерату́рой"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the phrase. Stress marks are optional.","q":"Finish it: “Everything depends on the weather.” — Всё зави́сит …","accept":["от погоды","от погоды.","зависит от погоды"],"placeholder":"от …","explain":"зави́сеть demands <b>от + genitive</b>: <b>зави́сит от пого́ды</b> — not «на» or a bare noun."},{"type":"choice","q":"You're leaving the house. Warn a friend not to forget the keys:","opts":["Не забу́дь ключи́!","Не забыва́й ключи́!","Не забыва́ешь ключи́!"],"answer":0,"explain":"A one-off warning → perfective <b>Не забу́дь!</b> The imperfective Не забыва́й! is a standing “don't keep forgetting”."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Two things at once, one doer","title":"<em>Чита́я</em> and <em>прочита́в</em> — the gerund and its golden rule","paras":["The gerund (деепричастие) is a verb doing an adverb's job — one word for “while doing” or “having done”. The <b>imperfective</b> gerund ends in <b>-я/-а</b> and marks a <b>simultaneous</b> action: <b>чита́я</b> — “while reading”, <b>ду́мая</b> — “while thinking”. The <b>perfective</b> gerund ends in <b>-в</b> and marks a <b>prior</b> one: <b>прочита́в</b> — “having read”, <b>придя́</b> — “having arrived”.","And here is the golden rule that trips every English speaker: the doer of the gerund must be <b>the same person</b> as the doer of the main verb. <b>Прочита́в письмо́, она́ ушла́</b> — she read, she left. If the two doers differ, you can't use a gerund — you unfold it back into «когда́…».","The gerund phrase always takes commas. Say it out loud as two beats: “having read the letter — comma — she left”."],"audio":"The gerund is a verb doing an adverb's job — one word for 'while doing' or 'having done'. The imperfective gerund ends in -ya or -a and marks a simultaneous action: chitaya, while reading; dumaya, while thinking. The perfective gerund ends in -v and marks a prior one: prochitav, having read; pridya, having arrived. And here is the golden rule that trips every English speaker: the doer of the gerund must be the same person as the doer of the main verb. Prochitav pismo, ona ushla — she read, she left. If the two doers differ, you can't use a gerund — you unfold it back into 'kogda'. The gerund phrase always takes commas.","table":{"rows":[["чита́я, ду́мая <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"читая, думая\">🔊</button>","while reading, while thinking (imperfective — same time)"],["прочита́в, придя́, узна́в <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"прочитав, придя, узнав\">🔊</button>","having read, arrived, found out (perfective — earlier)"],["Прочита́в письмо́, она́ ушла́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Прочитав письмо, она ушла\">🔊</button>","Having read the letter, she left. (one doer ✓)"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"Reading the article, Emma was taking notes.","en":"<b>Чита́я</b> статью́, Э́мма де́лала заме́тки.","say":"Читая статью, Эмма делала заметки."},{"ru":"Having translated the chapter, she poured herself some tea.","en":"<b>Переведя́</b> главу́, она́ нали́ла себе́ ча́ю.","say":"Переведя главу, она налила себе чаю."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Чита́я письмо́, зазвони́л телефо́н.","right":"Когда́ я чита́л письмо́, зазвони́л телефо́н.","why":"The phone isn't reading the letter — two different doers, so no gerund. Different subjects → unfold into <b>когда́</b>."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the sentence where one doer runs through both halves.","q":"Which is correct?","opts":["Гуля́я по па́рку, пошёл дождь.","Гуля́я по па́рку, мы встре́тили Ка́тю.","Гуля́я по па́рку, у нас была́ соба́ка."],"answer":1,"explain":"The strollers are the finders: <b>Гуля́я по па́рку, мы встре́тили Ка́тю</b>. Rain doesn't stroll; “we had a dog” has no matching doer."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the perfective gerund. Stress marks optional.","q":"From <b>прочита́ть</b>, the gerund “having read” is…","accept":["прочитав","прочитав,","прочитав."],"placeholder":"прочит…","explain":"Perfective stem + -в: прочита́ть → <b>прочита́в</b> (“having read”)."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“Having found out the news, he called his brother.”</b>","want":"Узна́в но́вость, он позвони́л бра́ту","answer":"Узна́в но́вость, он позвони́л бра́ту"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Being done — process, result, and speech","title":"<em>Стро́ится</em> vs <em>постро́ен</em> — and what you'd actually say","paras":["Russian has no single “passive”. It splits by aspect. An <b>ongoing process</b> takes the imperfective <b>-ся</b>-passive: <b>дом стро́ится</b> (the house is being built), <b>вопро́с реша́ется</b> (the question is being decided), <b>кни́ги продаю́тся</b> (the books are sold). A <b>finished result</b> takes the short perfective participle: <b>дом постро́ен</b> (the house is built), <b>рабо́та вы́полнена</b> (the work is done), <b>реше́ние при́нято</b> (the decision is made).","The short participle agrees like an adjective — <b>-Ø/-а/-о/-ы</b>: догово́р подпи́сан, статья́ напи́сан<b>а</b>, письмо́ напи́сан<b>о</b>, докуме́нты гото́вы. In the past you keep <b>был</b>: <b>дом был постро́ен</b> год наза́д.","One honest note: both live in <b>writing and officialese</b>. Out loud, Russians usually just go active with «они́»: <b>дом постро́или</b>, <b>кни́ги продаю́т</b>. Read the passive everywhere; write it in formal texts; speak it as active."],"audio":"Russian has no single passive. It splits by aspect. An ongoing process takes the imperfective -sya passive: dom stroitsya, the house is being built; vopros reshayetsya, the question is being decided; knigi prodayutsya, the books are sold. A finished result takes the short perfective participle: dom postroyen, the house is built; rabota vypolnena, the work is done; resheniye prinyato, the decision is made. The short participle agrees like an adjective: dogovor podpisan, statya napisana, pismo napisano. In the past you keep byl: dom byl postroyen god nazad. Both live in writing and officialese. Out loud, Russians usually just go active with 'oni': dom postroili, knigi prodayut. Read the passive everywhere, write it in formal texts, speak it as active.","table":{"rows":[["Дом стро́ится. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Дом строится\">🔊</button>","The house is being built. (process → -ся)"],["Дом постро́ен. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Дом построен\">🔊</button>","The house is built. (result → short participle)"],["Дом постро́или. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Дом построили\">🔊</button>","They built the house. (speech → active «они́»)"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"The contract is signed, the work is done.","en":"Догово́р <b>подпи́сан</b>, рабо́та <b>вы́полнена</b>.","say":"Договор подписан, работа выполнена."},{"ru":"This bridge was built a hundred years ago.","en":"Э́тот мост <b>был постро́ен</b> сто лет наза́д.","say":"Этот мост был построен сто лет назад."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Кни́га напи́сан.","right":"Кни́га напи́сана.","why":"The short participle agrees with its noun: feminine кни́га → <b>напи́сана</b>. Match gender exactly, just like an adjective."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"The report is finished and handed in. “The report is done.” —","opts":["Отчёт де́лается.","Отчёт вы́полнен.","Отчёт выполня́ется."],"answer":1,"explain":"Finished result → short perfective participle: <b>отчёт вы́полнен</b>. The other two describe an ongoing process."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the missing participle. Stress marks optional.","q":"Agree it: “The article is written.” — Статья́ …","accept":["написана","написана."],"placeholder":"напи́сан…","explain":"Feminine статья́ → <b>напи́сана</b> (-а)."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud, active-voice, as in speech.","target":"Э́тот дом постро́или в про́шлом году́.","sub":"“This house was built last year” — say it the spoken way, with «они́»-active","want":"этот дом построили в прошлом году"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The case a verb wants, the aspect it means","title":"<em>Интересу́юсь чем, зави́сит от чего́</em> — plus «Не смотри́!» vs «Не упади́!»","paras":["Two verbs of the week, two lessons. First, <b>government</b>: a verb (or its preposition) simply <b>demands</b> a case — not by logic, but by habit you memorise. <b>интересова́ться</b> + instrumental (интересу́юсь му́зыкой), <b>зави́сеть от</b> + genitive (зави́сит от пого́ды), <b>уча́ствовать в</b> + prepositional, <b>жени́ться на</b> + prepositional, <b>игра́ть в</b> футбо́л / <b>на</b> гита́ре, <b>скуча́ть по</b> + dative. Learn each as “verb + question word”.","Watch the English calques — they're the signature error: <b>жени́ться на</b> Ка́те (not «с»), <b>скуча́ть по</b> до́му (not «о»), <b>игра́ть в</b> ша́хматы (not a bare noun).","Second, <b>aspect carries nuance</b>. In a command, imperfective <b>Не смотри́!</b> forbids (“don't look”), while perfective <b>Не упади́!</b> warns of a one-off danger (“mind you don't fall”). Under negation, <b>не чита́л</b> means “never read it”, but <b>не прочита́л</b> means “didn't finish it”."],"audio":"Two verbs of the week, two lessons. First, government: a verb or its preposition simply demands a case — not by logic, but by habit you memorise. Interesovatsya plus instrumental: interesuyus muzykoy. Zaviset ot plus genitive: zavisit ot pogody. Uchastvovat v plus prepositional. Zhenitsya na plus prepositional. Igrat v futbol, igrat na gitare. Skuchat po plus dative. Learn each as verb plus question word. Watch the English calques — they're the signature error: zhenitsya na Kate, not 's'; skuchat po domu, not 'o'; igrat v shakhmaty. Second, aspect carries nuance. In a command, imperfective Ne smotri forbids, don't look, while perfective Ne upadi warns of a one-off danger, mind you don't fall. Under negation, ne chital means never read it, but ne prochital means didn't finish it.","table":{"rows":[["интересу́юсь му́зыкой <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"интересуюсь музыкой\">🔊</button>","interested in music (+ instrumental)"],["зави́сит от пого́ды <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"зависит от погоды\">🔊</button>","depends on the weather (от + genitive)"],["скуча́ю по до́му <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"скучаю по дому\">🔊</button>","I miss home (по + dative, not «о»)"],["Не смотри́! / Не упади́! <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Не смотри! Не упади!\">🔊</button>","Don't look! (ban) / Mind you don't fall! (warning)"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"Maxim is keen on engineering and takes part in a big project.","en":"Макси́м <b>интересу́ется</b> те́хникой и <b>уча́ствует в</b> большо́м прое́кте.","say":"Максим интересуется техникой и участвует в большом проекте."},{"ru":"Careful, don't fall — the steps are slippery!","en":"Осторо́жно, <b>не упади́</b> — ступе́ньки ско́льзкие!","say":"Осторожно, не упади — ступеньки скользкие!"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Он жени́лся с Ка́тей.","right":"Он жени́лся на Ка́те.","why":"жени́ться demands <b>на + prepositional</b>, never «с». The English “married <em>to</em>” calque is exactly the trap."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“The whole trip depends on money.” — Вся пое́здка …","opts":["зави́сит от де́нег","зави́сит на де́ньги","зави́сит де́ньгами"],"answer":0,"explain":"зави́сеть → <b>от + genitive</b>: <b>зави́сит от де́нег</b>. The verb dictates the case."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I really miss my old friends.”</b> (по + dative)","want":"Я о́чень скуча́ю по ста́рым друзья́м","answer":"Я о́чень скуча́ю по ста́рым друзья́м"},{"type":"choice","q":"A friend keeps checking their phone at dinner. Tell them to stop looking at it:","opts":["Не посмотри́ на телефо́н!","Не смотри́ на телефо́н!","Не смо́тришь на телефо́н!"],"answer":1,"explain":"A standing prohibition → imperfective <b>Не смотри́!</b> The perfective Не посмотри́! would oddly warn of a single glance."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Sunday at Ле́на's café. Emma, Иван and Макси́м trade news — a translation handed in, a bridge going up, studies nearly done. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Зако́нчив большо́й перево́д, я наконе́ц свобо́дна. Рома́н переведён и о́тдан в изда́тельство.","ru":"Having finished the big translation, I'm finally free. The novel is translated and handed to the publisher."},{"who":"A","en":"Поздравля́ю! А я как раз расска́зывал о своём мосте́.","ru":"Congratulations! And I was just telling you about my bridge."},{"who":"B","en":"Како́м мосте́? Ты им сейча́с занима́ешься?","ru":"Which bridge? Are you working on it right now?"},{"who":"A","en":"Да. Он ещё стро́ится — бу́дет постро́ен к зиме́. Всё зави́сит от пого́ды.","ru":"Yes. It's still being built — it'll be finished by winter. Everything depends on the weather."},{"who":"B","en":"Я то́же интересу́юсь таки́ми прое́ктами. Мо́жно как-нибу́дь посмотре́ть?","ru":"I'm interested in projects like that too. Could I take a look sometime?"},{"who":"A","en":"Коне́чно. А Ива́н, сдав после́дний экза́мен, начина́ет рабо́ту в понеде́льник.","ru":"Of course. And Ivan, having passed his last exam, starts work on Monday."},{"who":"B","en":"Вот э́то неде́ля! Осторо́жно, не упади́ — Ле́на несёт горя́чий ко́фе.","ru":"What a week! Careful, don't fall — Lena's carrying hot coffee."},{"who":"A","en":"Спаси́бо, что предупреди́л. За нас — за всё, что уже́ сде́лано!","ru":"Thanks for the warning. To us — to everything that's already been done!"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve carriers of week two — gerunds, the passive pair, and the verbs with a case of their own. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"чита́я","ru":"while reading (imperfective gerund)","ex":"Чита́я, она́ де́лала заме́тки."},{"en":"прочита́в","ru":"having read (perfective gerund)","ex":"Прочита́в письмо́, он ушёл."},{"en":"придя́","ru":"having arrived","ex":"Придя́ домо́й, я сра́зу усну́л."},{"en":"узна́в","ru":"having found out","ex":"Узна́в но́вость, она́ обра́довалась."},{"en":"стро́иться","ru":"to be built / be under construction","ex":"Но́вый дом стро́ится бы́стро."},{"en":"постро́ен","ru":"built (short participle, result)","ex":"Мост уже́ постро́ен."},{"en":"вы́полнен","ru":"done, carried out (short participle)","ex":"План вы́полнен в срок."},{"en":"реша́ться","ru":"to be decided / being resolved","ex":"Э́тот вопро́с ещё реша́ется."},{"en":"интересова́ться","ru":"to be interested (in) (+ instrumental)","ex":"Я интересу́юсь исто́рией."},{"en":"зави́сеть","ru":"to depend (on) (от + genitive)","ex":"Всё зави́сит от тебя́."},{"en":"уча́ствовать","ru":"to take part (in) (в + prepositional)","ex":"Он уча́ствует в конфере́нции."},{"en":"скуча́ть","ru":"to miss (по + dative)","ex":"Я скуча́ю по друзья́м."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the week-two form to what it does.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Прочита́в письмо́","having read the letter (prior action, one doer)"],["Дом стро́ится","the house is being built (process)"],["Рабо́та вы́полнена","the work is done (result)"],["зави́сит от пого́ды","depends on the weather (от + genitive)"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Eight questions — one or two from every day this week. Pass this and week two is yours.","items":[{"q":"“Reading the letter, she was crying.” (same doer) —","opts":["Чита́я письмо́, она́ пла́кала.","Чита́я письмо́, письмо́ пла́кало.","Чита́я письмо́, ей бы́ло гру́стно."],"answer":0},{"q":"“Having arrived home, he called.” — the gerund of прийти́ is…","opts":["приходя́","придя́","пришёл"],"answer":1},{"q":"The building is still going up. “It is being built.” —","opts":["Оно́ постро́ено.","Оно́ стро́ится.","Оно́ постро́илось."],"answer":1},{"q":"“The letter is written.” — agree the participle:","opts":["Письмо́ напи́сан.","Письмо́ напи́сана.","Письмо́ напи́сано."],"answer":2},{"q":"“I'm interested in Russian literature.” —","opts":["интересу́юсь ру́сскую литерату́ру","интересу́юсь ру́сской литерату́рой","интересу́юсь о ру́сской литерату́ре"],"answer":1},{"q":"“He got married to Katya.” —","opts":["Он жени́лся на Ка́те.","Он жени́лся с Ка́тей.","Он жени́лся Ка́те."],"answer":0},{"q":"“I miss my home.” —","opts":["скуча́ю о до́ме","скуча́ю по до́му","скуча́ю за до́мом"],"answer":1},{"q":"Warn a friend once: “Don't be late!” —","opts":["Не опа́здывай!","Не опозда́й!","Не опа́здываешь!"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's Sunday note to herself — the whole week hiding in five lines. Read it aloud.","title":"Неде́ля сде́лана","sentences":["Зако́нчив большо́й перево́д, я почу́вствовала себя́ свобо́дной.","Рома́н переведён, прове́рен и о́тдан в изда́тельство.","Но́вый мост Макси́ма ещё стро́ится и бу́дет постро́ен к зиме́.","Я всё бо́льше интересу́юсь его́ рабо́той, хотя́ она́ зави́сит от пого́ды.","Придя́ домо́й, я поняла́: за э́ту неде́лю сде́лано о́чень мно́го."],"translation":"Having finished the big translation, I felt free. The novel is translated, checked and handed to the publisher. Maxim's new bridge is still being built and will be finished by winter. I'm more and more interested in his work, although it depends on the weather. Arriving home, I realised: an awful lot got done this week."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — sum up a busy stretch using the week's machinery.","prompt":"In 3–4 sentences, describe something you finished and something still in progress. Use at least one gerund (чита́я / прочита́в), one passive (стро́ится / сде́лано / напи́сано), and one verb with its own case (интересова́ться + instr., зави́сеть от + gen., скуча́ть по + dat.).","hint":"Chain with one doer: «Зако́нчив …, я …». Passive by aspect: process → стро́ится, result → сде́лано. Verb + question word: интересу́юсь чем, зави́сит от чего́ 🙂","example":"Зако́нчив о́чень тру́дный перево́д, я реши́ла отдохну́ть. Рабо́та сде́лана, но оди́н докуме́нт ещё гото́вится. Я всё бо́льше интересу́юсь литерату́рой и, чита́я по-ру́сски, ме́ньше скуча́ю по до́му."}},"15":{"day":15,"week":"03","themeRu":"Someone, anyone","themeEn":"Кто-то и́ли кто-нибу́дь","intro":"Emma gets home and the little light on her phone is blinking: someone called while she was out. Someone — a real, flesh-and-blood person; she just doesn't know who yet. That «someone» is <b>кто-то</b>. But when Ка́тя says «call someone at the office», <em>that</em> «someone» is anyone at all — <b>кто-нибу́дь</b>. English shrugs both into “some/any”; Russian keeps them apart, and today you'll feel exactly where the line falls.","introAudio":"Emma gets home and the little light on her phone is blinking: someone called while she was out. Someone — a real, flesh-and-blood person; she just doesn't know who yet. That 'someone' is kto-to. But when Katya says 'call someone at the office', that 'someone' is anyone at all — kto-nibud. English shrugs both into 'some' or 'any'; Russian keeps them apart, and today you'll feel exactly where the line falls.","goals":["Use <b>-то</b> for a real but unknown someone/something (past or present fact): <b>Кто-то звони́л</b>","Use <b>-нибу́дь</b> for any-one-will-do — future, question, request: <b>Позвони́ кому́-нибу́дь</b>","Decline the pronoun while the particle rides along: <b>кому́-то</b>, <b>о ком-нибу́дь</b>; adverbs <b>где-то</b>, <b>когда́-нибу́дь</b>","Reach for <b>ко́е-</b> when you know but won't say: <b>Я ко́е-что зна́ю</b>"],"learned":["Used <b>-то</b> for a real but unknown someone/something: <b>Кто-то звони́л</b>","Used <b>-нибу́дь</b> for any-one-will-do — future, question, request: <b>Позвони́ кому́-нибу́дь</b>","Declined the pronoun while the particle rode along: <b>кому́-то</b>, <b>о ком-нибу́дь</b>; adverbs too","Reached for <b>ко́е-</b> to mean “I know, but I'm not saying”: <b>Я ко́е-что зна́ю</b>"],"review":{"intro":"One minute back down the mountain — a participle, a gerund, a verb with its own case — before we open the pronouns.","introAudio":"One minute back down the mountain — a participle, a gerund, a verb with its own case — before we open the pronouns.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. Week-one and week-two muscles.","q":"“the woman working at the laptop” in one word —","opts":["рабо́тающая же́нщина","рабо́тавшая же́нщина","рабо́танная же́нщина"],"answer":0,"explain":"Active, present → <b>рабо́тающая</b>. рабо́тавшая is past; рабо́танная isn't a real form here."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which keeps one doer? “Having read the letter, …”","opts":["Прочита́в письмо́, пришёл отве́т.","Прочита́в письмо́, она́ улыбну́лась.","Прочита́в письмо́, ста́ло ти́хо."],"answer":1,"explain":"The reader must be the smiler: <b>Прочита́в письмо́, она́ улыбну́лась</b>. A reply and “it went quiet” can't read a letter."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the missing word. Stress marks optional.","q":"Give the verb its case: “She's interested in art.” — Она́ интересу́ется …","accept":["искусством","искусством."],"placeholder":"иску́сством","explain":"интересова́ться demands the <b>instrumental</b>: <b>иску́сством</b>."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Real, but unnamed","title":"<em>Кто-то звони́л</em> — «-то» for a definite someone you can't name","paras":["English has one lazy word for two very different ideas. When Emma comes home to a blinking phone, «someone called» means a <b>real, specific person</b> — she simply doesn't know who. That's <b>-то</b>: <b>Кто-то звони́л</b>. The «-то» whispers “a definite one, unknown to me”.","So <b>-то</b> lives among <b>facts that already happened or are happening</b>: <b>Она́ что-то сказа́ла</b> (she said something — real words were spoken), <b>Он живёт где-то ря́дом</b> (he lives somewhere nearby — a real place). There is a thing out there; you just can't put a name on it.","Hold «-то» in your ear as: “some particular one — I'd tell you if I knew.”"],"audio":"English has one lazy word for two very different ideas. When Emma comes home to a blinking phone, 'someone called' means a real, specific person — she simply doesn't know who. That's -to: kto-to zvonil. The -to whispers 'a definite one, unknown to me'. So -to lives among facts that already happened or are happening: ona chto-to skazala, she said something, real words were spoken; on zhivyot gde-to ryadom, he lives somewhere nearby, a real place. There is a thing out there; you just can't put a name on it. Hold -to in your ear as: some particular one — I'd tell you if I knew.","table":{"rows":[["Кто-то звони́л, пока́ тебя́ не́ было. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Кто-то звонил, пока тебя не было\">🔊</button>","Someone called while you were out. (a real caller)"],["Она́ что-то сказа́ла, но я не расслы́шал. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Она что-то сказала, но я не расслышал\">🔊</button>","She said something, but I didn't catch it."],["Он живёт где-то в це́нтре. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Он живёт где-то в центре\">🔊</button>","He lives somewhere in the centre. (a real place, unknown to me)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Someone left an umbrella at the café.","en":"<b>Кто-то</b> оста́вил зонт в кафе́.","say":"Кто-то оставил зонт в кафе."},{"ru":"I've already seen this face somewhere.","en":"Я уже́ <b>где-то</b> ви́дела э́то лицо́.","say":"Я уже где-то видела это лицо."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Кто-нибу́дь звони́л, пока́ тебя́ не́ было.","right":"Кто-то звони́л, пока́ тебя́ не́ было.","why":"A call that <b>really happened</b> has a real (if unknown) caller → <b>-то</b>. «-нибу́дь» would turn it into a hypothetical “did anyone at all call?”."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> a real, finished event — which particle?","q":"“Someone has already taken this seat.” —","opts":["Кто-нибу́дь уже́ за́нял э́то ме́сто.","Кто-то уже́ за́нял э́то ме́сто.","Ко́е-кто уже́ за́нял э́то ме́сто."],"answer":1,"explain":"A real person already sitting there → <b>кто-то</b>. Ко́е-кто would hint you know exactly who."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks optional.","q":"Fill the gap: “She whispered something.” — Она́ … прошепта́ла.","accept":["что-то","что-то."],"placeholder":"что…","explain":"Real words were spoken → <b>что-то</b>."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Any one will do","title":"<em>Позвони́ кому́-нибу́дь</em> — «-нибу́дь» for the open and the unreal","paras":["Now flip it. When the someone <b>isn't chosen yet</b> — any one will do — Russian switches to <b>-нибу́дь</b>. Its home is the <b>unreal</b>: the future, questions, requests, conditions. <b>Позвони́ кому́-нибу́дь</b> — “call someone, anyone”. <b>Хо́чешь чего́-нибу́дь?</b> — “do you want anything?”","The test is quick: is there a real referent, or are you reaching into open possibility? <b>Когда́-нибу́дь</b> я напишу́ рома́н (someday — no fixed day). <b>Возьми́ каку́ю-нибу́дь кни́гу</b> (grab any book). <b>Е́сли кто-нибу́дь позвони́т…</b> (if anyone calls…).","So the line runs: <b>-то</b> = it happened, it's real; <b>-нибу́дь</b> = it might, if at all. Future, question, request, “if” → reach for <b>-нибу́дь</b>."],"audio":"Now flip it. When the someone isn't chosen yet — any one will do — Russian switches to -nibud. Its home is the unreal: the future, questions, requests, conditions. Pozvoni komu-nibud, call someone, anyone. Khochesh chego-nibud, do you want anything? The test is quick: is there a real referent, or are you reaching into open possibility? Kogda-nibud ya napishu roman, someday, no fixed day. Vozmi kakuyu-nibud knigu, grab any book. Yesli kto-nibud pozvonit, if anyone calls. So the line runs: -to means it happened, it's real; -nibud means it might, if at all. Future, question, request, 'if' — reach for -nibud.","table":{"rows":[["Позвони́ кому́-нибу́дь из друзе́й. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Позвони кому-нибудь из друзей\">🔊</button>","Call someone (anyone) among your friends. (request)"],["Хо́чешь чего́-нибу́дь вы́пить? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Хочешь чего-нибудь выпить?\">🔊</button>","Do you want anything to drink? (offer / question)"],["Когда́-нибу́дь я напишу́ кни́гу. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Когда-нибудь я напишу книгу\">🔊</button>","Someday I'll write a book. (indefinite future)"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"Buy something for tea on the way.","en":"Купи́ по доро́ге <b>что-нибу́дь</b> к ча́ю.","say":"Купи по дороге что-нибудь к чаю."},{"ru":"Have you ever been to Siberia?","en":"Ты <b>когда́-нибу́дь</b> был в Сиби́ри?","say":"Ты когда-нибудь был в Сибири?"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Хо́чешь что-то вы́пить?","right":"Хо́чешь чего́-нибу́дь вы́пить?","why":"An open offer isn't about one specific drink → <b>-нибу́дь</b>. English “something / anything” hides the split; the question form is your cue."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> a request into the open — which particle?","q":"“Ask someone the way.” (you don't care who) —","opts":["Спроси́ доро́гу у кого́-то.","Спроси́ доро́гу у кого́-нибу́дь.","Спроси́ доро́гу у кто-нибу́дь."],"answer":1,"explain":"Any passer-by will do → <b>у кого́-нибу́дь</b> (genitive after «у»). «-то» would mean a specific person you have in mind; the last option leaves the pronoun in the wrong case."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“Let's go somewhere on Saturday.”</b> (anywhere, a plan)","want":"Дава́й пое́дем куда́-нибу́дь в суббо́ту","answer":"Дава́й пое́дем куда́-нибу́дь в суббо́ту"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The pronoun bends, the tail rides","title":"<em>Кому́-то, о ком-нибу́дь</em> — declension, and the adverb family","paras":["Two mechanical notes that save you. First, the particle never changes — the <b>pronoun in front</b> takes the case. “Someone” as subject is <b>кто-то</b>; “to someone” is <b>кому́-то</b>; “about someone” is <b>о ком-то</b>. The «-то» (or «-нибу́дь») just clings to the back and rides along.","Second, the same two particles build a whole family of <b>adverbs</b>: place <b>где-то / куда́-нибу́дь</b>, time <b>когда́-то / когда́-нибу́дь</b>, reason <b>почему́-то</b>, manner <b>как-то</b>. Same rule of meaning — <b>-то</b> real, <b>-нибу́дь</b> open.","<b>Где-то</b> means “in a real place I can't name”; <b>куда́-нибу́дь</b> means “to anywhere at all”. You can already feel the gap between them."],"audio":"Two mechanical notes that save you. First, the particle never changes — the pronoun in front takes the case. Someone as subject is kto-to; to someone is komu-to; about someone is o kom-to. The -to, or -nibud, just clings to the back and rides along. Second, the same two particles build a whole family of adverbs: place, gde-to and kuda-nibud; time, kogda-to and kogda-nibud; reason, pochemu-to; manner, kak-to. Same rule of meaning — -to real, -nibud open. Gde-to means in a real place I can't name; kuda-nibud means to anywhere at all. You can already feel the gap between them.","table":{"rows":[["кто-то → кому́-то → о ком-то <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"кто-то, кому-то, о ком-то\">🔊</button>","the pronoun bends; «-то» just rides along"],["где-то, куда́-нибу́дь, когда́-то <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"где-то, куда-нибудь, когда-то\">🔊</button>","somewhere, (to) anywhere, once / long ago"],["почему́-то, как-то, чей-то <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"почему-то, как-то, чей-то\">🔊</button>","for some reason, somehow, someone's"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"For some reason I feel a little sad today.","en":"Мне <b>почему́-то</b> сего́дня немно́го гру́стно.","say":"Мне почему-то сегодня немного грустно."},{"ru":"I need to talk to someone about this.","en":"Мне на́до <b>с кем-нибу́дь</b> поговори́ть об э́том.","say":"Мне надо с кем-нибудь поговорить об этом."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я говори́л с кто-то.","right":"Я говори́л с кем-то.","why":"After «с» the pronoun goes instrumental — <b>кем-то</b> — and «-то» simply rides along. Decline the front, never the tail."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“I gave the book to someone (a real person) and forgot who.” —","opts":["Я отда́л кни́гу кто-то.","Я отда́л кни́гу кому́-то.","Я отда́л кни́гу кому́-нибу́дь."],"answer":1,"explain":"A real, finished giving “to someone” → dative <b>кому́-то</b>. The pronoun declines; «-то» rides. кому́-нибу́дь would make it hypothetical."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Я его́ уже́ где-то ви́дела.","sub":"“I've seen him somewhere before” — a real place, just unnamed","want":"я его уже где-то видела"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · I know — but I'm not saying","title":"<em>Ко́е-что</em> — the knowing wink, and how «some/any» misleads","paras":["One quieter member remains: <b>ко́е-</b>. It means the speaker <b>knows perfectly well</b> and simply chooses not to say — a knowing wink. <b>Я ко́е-что зна́ю</b> (I know a thing or two — and I'm not telling). <b>Ко́е-кто</b> тебя́ ждёт (a certain someone is waiting for you).","When a preposition meets «ко́е-», it slips <b>inside</b>: “with a certain someone” is <b>ко́е с кем</b>, “about a certain something” is <b>ко́е о чём</b>. The «ко́е» steps aside and lets the preposition through.","So keep the three apart — because English melts them all into “some/any”: <b>-то</b> = real but unnamed, <b>-нибу́дь</b> = any-one-will-do, <b>ко́е-</b> = I know but won't say. Choose by what <em>you</em> know, not by the English word."],"audio":"One quieter member remains: koye-. It means the speaker knows perfectly well and simply chooses not to say — a knowing wink. Ya koye-chto znayu, I know a thing or two, and I'm not telling. Koye-kto tebya zhdyot, a certain someone is waiting for you. When a preposition meets koye-, it slips inside: with a certain someone is koye s kem; about a certain something is koye o chyom. The koye steps aside and lets the preposition through. So keep the three apart — because English melts them all into 'some' or 'any': -to is real but unnamed, -nibud is any-one-will-do, koye- is I know but won't say. Choose by what you know, not by the English word.","table":{"rows":[["Я ко́е-что зна́ю об э́том. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я кое-что знаю об этом\">🔊</button>","I know a thing or two about this. (I know, won't say)"],["Мне на́до ко́е с кем поговори́ть. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Мне надо кое с кем поговорить\">🔊</button>","I need to talk to a certain someone. (preposition splits «ко́е-»)"],["ко́е-кто, ко́е-где, ко́е-как <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"кое-кто, кое-где, кое-как\">🔊</button>","a certain someone, here and there, any old how"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"I've brought you a little something.","en":"Я <b>ко́е-что</b> тебе́ принесла́.","say":"Я кое-что тебе принесла."},{"ru":"A certain someone was asking about you today.","en":"<b>Ко́е-кто</b> сего́дня о тебе́ спра́шивал.","say":"Кое-кто сегодня о тебе спрашивал."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Мне на́до поговори́ть с ко́е-кем.","right":"Мне на́до ко́е с кем поговори́ть.","why":"A preposition breaks «ко́е-» open and stands in the middle: <b>ко́е с кем</b>. Never «с ко́е-кем»."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> the speaker knows exactly who — which one?","q":"“A certain someone missed you.” (I know who, I won't say) —","opts":["Кто-то по тебе́ скуча́л.","Кто-нибу́дь по тебе́ скуча́л.","Ко́е-кто по тебе́ скуча́л."],"answer":2,"explain":"The knowing wink → <b>ко́е-кто</b>. кто-то would mean you don't know who; кто-нибу́дь isn't even about a real person."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the two words. Stress marks optional.","q":"“I know something (and I'm keeping it to myself).” — Я … зна́ю.","accept":["кое-что","кое что","кое-что."],"placeholder":"ко́е-…","explain":"A deliberate secret → <b>ко́е-что</b>."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Emma is back home; Ка́тя has dropped by. A blinking phone, a missing notebook, and a little teasing. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Ты чего́ така́я заду́мчивая?","ru":"Why so pensive?"},{"who":"B","en":"Кто-то звони́л, пока́ меня́ не́ было, а но́мер незнако́мый.","ru":"Someone called while I was out, and the number's unknown."},{"who":"A","en":"Мо́жет, по рабо́те? Перезвони́ кому́-нибу́дь из изда́тельства.","ru":"Maybe it's work? Call someone at the publisher's."},{"who":"B","en":"Наве́рное. Хо́чешь чего́-нибу́дь, пока́ я ищу́ но́мер?","ru":"Probably. Want anything while I look for the number?"},{"who":"A","en":"Ко́е-что зна́ю: у тебя́ в холоди́льнике есть торт. Я ви́дела.","ru":"I know a certain thing: you've got cake in the fridge. I saw it."},{"who":"B","en":"Пойма́ла! Ла́дно, доста́ну. Кста́ти, ты не ви́дела где-то тут мой блокно́т?","ru":"Caught me! Fine, I'll get it. By the way, did you see my notebook somewhere around here?"},{"who":"A","en":"Он на по́лке, за како́й-то кни́гой.","ru":"It's on the shelf, behind some book."},{"who":"B","en":"Нашла́! И но́мер то́же. Е́сли кто-нибу́дь спро́сит — я на свя́зи.","ru":"Found it! And the number too. If anyone asks — I'm reachable."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve indefinite words for a day with a little mystery. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"кто-то","ru":"someone (real, unknown to me)","ex":"Кто-то оста́вил зонт."},{"en":"что-то","ru":"something (real)","ex":"Он что-то забы́л сказа́ть."},{"en":"кто-нибу́дь","ru":"someone / anyone (open, hypothetical)","ex":"Спроси́ кого́-нибу́дь."},{"en":"что-нибу́дь","ru":"something / anything (open)","ex":"Купи́ что-нибу́дь к ча́ю."},{"en":"где-то","ru":"somewhere (a real place)","ex":"Ключи́ где-то тут."},{"en":"где-нибу́дь","ru":"anywhere (open)","ex":"Посиди́м где-нибу́дь в па́рке."},{"en":"когда́-то","ru":"once, at some past time","ex":"Когда́-то он жил в Пари́же."},{"en":"когда́-нибу́дь","ru":"someday; ever","ex":"Ты был когда́-нибу́дь в Сиби́ри?"},{"en":"куда́-нибу́дь","ru":"(to) somewhere / anywhere","ex":"Дава́й пое́дем куда́-нибу́дь."},{"en":"почему́-то","ru":"for some reason","ex":"Мне почему́-то гру́стно."},{"en":"ко́е-что","ru":"a certain thing (I know, won't say)","ex":"Я ко́е-что тебе́ принесла́."},{"en":"ко́е-кто","ru":"a certain someone","ex":"Ко́е-кто тебя́ вспомина́л."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each phrase to the shade it carries.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Кто-то звони́л","someone called (real, past)"],["Позвони́ кому́-нибу́дь","call someone (any, request)"],["Я ко́е-что зна́ю","I know a thing or two (won't say)"],["Он живёт где-то ря́дом","he lives somewhere nearby (real place)"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five questions on the some/any split. Choose by what the speaker knows, not by the English word.","items":[{"q":"A missed call from an unknown number. “Someone called.” —","opts":["Кто-то звони́л.","Кто-нибу́дь звони́л.","Ко́е-кто звони́л."],"answer":0},{"q":"Offer a guest a drink: “Do you want anything?” —","opts":["Хо́чешь что-то?","Хо́чешь чего́-нибу́дь?","Хо́чешь ко́е-что?"],"answer":1},{"q":"“Call someone from the office.” (a request — anyone) —","opts":["Позвони́ кому́-то из о́фиса.","Позвони́ кому́-нибу́дь из о́фиса.","Позвони́ кто-нибу́дь из о́фиса."],"answer":1},{"q":"“I need to talk to a certain someone.” (I know who) —","opts":["Мне на́до ко́е с кем поговори́ть.","Мне на́до с кем-нибу́дь поговори́ть.","Мне на́до с кем-то поговори́ть."],"answer":0},{"q":"“Someday I'll write a novel.” —","opts":["Когда́-то я напишу́ рома́н.","Где-то я напишу́ рома́н.","Когда́-нибу́дь я напишу́ рома́н."],"answer":2}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's note about the mysterious call — read it aloud. Watch «-то», «-нибу́дь» and «ко́е-» each do their own job.","title":"Про́пущенный звоно́к","sentences":["Придя́ домо́й, я уви́дела, что кто-то звони́л.","Но́мер незнако́мый, и я почему́-то заволнова́лась.","Ка́тя сказа́ла: «Перезвони́ кому́-нибу́дь из изда́тельства».","Пото́м она́ призна́лась, что ко́е-что зна́ет про торт в холоди́льнике.","Когда́-нибу́дь я привы́кну, что здесь у меня́ есть лю́ди, кото́рым не всё равно́."],"translation":"Arriving home, I saw that someone had called. The number was unknown, and for some reason I got worried. Katya said: “Call someone at the publisher's.” Then she admitted she knew a certain thing about the cake in the fridge. Someday I'll get used to the fact that here I have people who care about me."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — tell a small story with a little mystery in it.","prompt":"In 3–4 sentences, describe a day with an unknown in it. Use «-то» for a real-but-unnamed someone/something that happened, «-нибу́дь» for something still open (a plan, a question, an offer), and one «ко́е-» for a thing you know but won't spell out.","hint":"«-то» = it's real, I just can't name it (Кто-то звони́л). «-нибу́дь» = future / question / request (Позвони́ кому́-нибу́дь). «ко́е-» = a knowing wink (Я ко́е-что зна́ю) 🙂","example":"Сего́дня кто-то оста́вил мне запи́ску, но по́дпись я почему́-то не разобра́ла. Наве́рное, за́втра спрошу́ кого́-нибу́дь в о́фисе. А ещё я ко́е-что заду́мала — но пока́ никому́ не скажу́."}},"16":{"day":16,"week":"03","themeRu":"No one, nothing to","themeEn":"Никто́ и не́кого","intro":"«Мне <b>не́чего</b> наде́ть!» — Emma groans in front of a wardrobe that is visibly full. It's the eternal joke, and it's today's grammar: Russian's two ways of saying <b>nothing</b>. One piles negatives on top of each other — <b>Никто́ ничего́ не зна́ет</b> (“nobody knows nothing”, and that double “no” is compulsory). The other, stressed <b>не́-</b>, stands alone — <b>не́чего де́лать</b>, “there's nothing to do”. Let's learn to say no like a Russian.","introAudio":"'Mne nechego nadet!' — Emma groans in front of a wardrobe that is visibly full. It's the eternal joke, and it's today's grammar: Russian's two ways of saying nothing. One piles negatives on top of each other — Nikto nichego ne znayet, nobody knows nothing, and that double 'no' is compulsory. The other, stressed ne-, stands alone — nechego delat, there's nothing to do. Let's learn to say no like a Russian.","goals":["Pile up the negatives — the verb still needs «не»: <b>Я никого́ не ви́жу</b>","Split «ни-» around a preposition: <b>ни с кем</b>, <b>ни о чём</b>","Use the stressed <b>не́-</b> series with dative + infinitive — and <b>no</b> second «не»: <b>Мне не́чего де́лать</b>","Say “there isn't / wasn't any” with the genitive: <b>нет вре́мени</b>, <b>не́ было де́нег</b>"],"learned":["Piled up the negatives, keeping «не» on the verb: <b>Я никого́ не ви́жу</b>","Split «ни-» around a preposition: <b>ни с кем</b>, <b>ни о чём</b>","Used the stressed <b>не́-</b> series with dative + infinitive, no second «не»: <b>Мне не́чего де́лать</b>","Said “there isn't / wasn't any” with the genitive: <b>нет вре́мени</b>, <b>не́ было де́нег</b>"],"review":{"intro":"Yesterday's «someone», one passive, one demanding verb — a quick warm-up before we learn to say no.","introAudio":"Yesterday's 'someone', one passive, one demanding verb — a quick warm-up before we learn to say no.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer.","q":"“Buy anything for tea.” (an open request) —","opts":["Купи́ что-то к ча́ю.","Купи́ что-нибу́дь к ча́ю.","Купи́ ко́е-что к ча́ю."],"answer":1,"explain":"A request into the open → <b>что-нибу́дь</b>. что-то would mean one particular thing you have in mind."},{"type":"choice","q":"The bridge is finished. “The bridge is built.” —","opts":["Мост стро́ится.","Мост постро́ится.","Мост постро́ен."],"answer":2,"explain":"A finished result → short participle <b>постро́ен</b>. стро́ится is the ongoing process; постро́ится is the future."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the verb. Stress marks optional.","q":"Give the verb its case: “Everything depends on you.” — Всё … от тебя́.","accept":["зависит","зависит."],"placeholder":"зави́сит","explain":"зави́сеть от + genitive: <b>зави́сит от тебя́</b>."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Say “no” twice, on purpose","title":"<em>Никто́ ничего́ не зна́ет</em> — the compulsory double negative","paras":["English says “nobody knows <b>anything</b>” — one negative, then a positive-looking “anything”. Russian refuses that. If you start a sentence with a <b>ни-</b> word, the verb <b>still needs «не»</b>: <b>Никто́ не зна́ет</b> (nobody knows), <b>Я ничего́ не ви́жу</b> (I see nothing), <b>Он никогда́ не опа́здывает</b> (he's never late).","You can even pile several ни-words in one clause, and there's still exactly one «не» on the verb: <b>Никто́ никогда́ ничего́ не говори́т</b> — “nobody ever says anything”. To an English ear it's a triple no; to a Russian it's simply agreement.","The one thing you must not do is drop that «не». <b>Никто́ зна́ет</b> is broken. The ни-word and the «не» travel together, always."],"audio":"English says 'nobody knows anything' — one negative, then a positive-looking 'anything'. Russian refuses that. If you start a sentence with a ni- word, the verb still needs 'ne': Nikto ne znayet, nobody knows; ya nichego ne vizhu, I see nothing; on nikogda ne opazdyvayet, he's never late. You can even pile several ni-words in one clause, and there's still exactly one 'ne' on the verb: Nikto nikogda nichego ne govorit, nobody ever says anything. To an English ear it's a triple no; to a Russian it's simply agreement. The one thing you must not do is drop that 'ne'. Nikto znayet is broken. The ni-word and the 'ne' travel together, always.","table":{"rows":[["Никто́ не зна́ет. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Никто не знает\">🔊</button>","Nobody knows. (ни- word + «не»)"],["Я ничего́ не понима́ю. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я ничего не понимаю\">🔊</button>","I don't understand anything."],["Никто́ никогда́ ничего́ не говори́т. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Никто никогда ничего не говорит\">🔊</button>","Nobody ever says anything. (pile them up, one «не»)"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"I'm not waiting for anyone today.","en":"Я сего́дня <b>никого́ не</b> жду.","say":"Я сегодня никого не жду."},{"ru":"He never complains about anything.","en":"Он <b>никогда́ ни</b> на что <b>не</b> жа́луется.","say":"Он никогда ни на что не жалуется."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Никто́ зна́ет об э́том.","right":"Никто́ не зна́ет об э́том.","why":"The ни-word never travels alone — the verb keeps its <b>«не»</b>: <b>Никто́ не зна́ет</b>. Dropping «не» is the number-one trap."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the fully-negated sentence.","q":"“Nobody said anything.” —","opts":["Кто-то ничего́ не сказа́л.","Никто́ ничего́ сказа́л.","Никто́ ничего́ не сказа́л."],"answer":2,"explain":"Both the ни-words and «не» are needed: <b>Никто́ ничего́ не сказа́л</b>. One option drops «не»; «Кто-то…» isn't even negative in the subject."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I never watch anything in the evenings.”</b>","want":"Я никогда́ ничего́ не смотрю́ по вечера́м","answer":"Я никогда́ ничего́ не смотрю́ по вечера́м"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The preposition splits «ни-»","title":"<em>Ни с кем, ни о чём</em> — negation in the oblique cases","paras":["The ни-pronoun declines like any pronoun: <b>никого́</b> (no one, acc/gen), <b>никому́</b> (to no one), <b>нике́м</b> (by no one), <b>ни о ком</b> (about no one). And when a preposition turns up, it wedges <b>into the middle</b>: <b>ни с кем</b> (with no one), <b>ни о чём</b> (about nothing), <b>ни у кого́</b> (nobody has).","The verb still carries «не»: <b>Я ни с кем не говори́л</b> (I spoke with no one), <b>Она́ ни о чём не жале́ет</b> (she regrets nothing), <b>Ни у кого́ нет отве́та</b> (nobody has an answer).","So the English calque «с никем» is impossible. The preposition always splits the pair: <b>ни + preposition + case</b>, and «не» waits on the verb."],"audio":"The ni-pronoun declines like any pronoun: nikogo, no one; nikomu, to no one; nikem, by no one; ni o kom, about no one. And when a preposition turns up, it wedges into the middle: ni s kem, with no one; ni o chyom, about nothing; ni u kogo, nobody has. The verb still carries 'ne': ya ni s kem ne govoril, I spoke with no one; ona ni o chyom ne zhaleyet, she regrets nothing; ni u kogo net otveta, nobody has an answer. So the English calque 's nikem' is impossible. The preposition always splits the pair: ni, plus preposition, plus case, and 'ne' waits on the verb.","table":{"rows":[["Я ни с кем не говори́л. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я ни с кем не говорил\">🔊</button>","I spoke with no one. (ни + с + кем)"],["Она́ ни о чём не жале́ет. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Она ни о чём не жалеет\">🔊</button>","She regrets nothing. (ни + о + чём)"],["Ни у кого́ нет отве́та. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Ни у кого нет ответа\">🔊</button>","Nobody has an answer. (ни + у + кого́)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"I'm not afraid of anything and I don't depend on anyone.","en":"Я <b>ничего́ не</b> бою́сь и <b>ни от кого́ не</b> зави́шу.","say":"Я ничего не боюсь и ни от кого не завишу."},{"ru":"She didn't tell anyone about her plans.","en":"Она́ <b>никому́ не</b> рассказа́ла о свои́х пла́нах.","say":"Она никому не рассказала о своих планах."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я не говорю́ с никем.","right":"Я ни с кем не говорю́.","why":"The preposition breaks «ни-» open — <b>ни с кем</b>, never «с никем» — and «не» stays on the verb."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“I'm not arguing with anyone.” —","opts":["Я не спо́рю с нике́м.","Я ни с кем не спо́рю.","Я спо́рю ни с кем."],"answer":1,"explain":"Preposition inside, «не» on the verb: <b>Я ни с кем не спо́рю</b>."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the three words. Stress marks optional.","q":"Fill the gap: “She regrets nothing.” — Она́ … не жале́ет.","accept":["ни о чём","ни о чем","ни о чём."],"placeholder":"ни … чём","explain":"about nothing → <b>ни о чём</b> (preposition wedged in the middle)."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The stressed «не́-», with a job to do","title":"<em>Не́чего де́лать</em> — «не́-» + dative + infinitive, and no second «не»","paras":["There's a second, quite different negative series — <b>stressed</b> «не́-». It doesn't just deny; it says “there is <b>no one / nothing / nowhere</b> to do the action”, and it always comes with a <b>dative</b> person and an <b>infinitive</b>: <b>Мне не́чего де́лать</b> (I have nothing to do), <b>Ему́ не́где жить</b> (he has nowhere to live), <b>Нам не́когда отдыха́ть</b> (we have no time to rest).","Two things make it different from «ни-». First, «не́-» is <b>stressed</b> — you hear the stress jump onto the «не́». Second, it takes <b>no second «не»</b>: just <b>Мне не́чего де́лать</b>, never «не́чего не де́лать».","And a preposition splits it too — the stress stays on «не́»: <b>не́ с кем говори́ть</b> (no one to talk to), <b>не́ о чем спо́рить</b> (nothing to argue about), <b>не́ на что жа́ловаться</b> (nothing to complain about)."],"audio":"There's a second, quite different negative series — stressed ne-. It doesn't just deny; it says there is no one, nothing or nowhere to do the action, and it always comes with a dative person and an infinitive: Mne nechego delat, I have nothing to do; yemu negde zhit, he has nowhere to live; nam nekogda otdykhat, we have no time to rest. Two things make it different from ni-. First, ne- is stressed — you hear the stress jump onto the ne. Second, it takes no second 'ne': just Mne nechego delat, never 'nechego ne delat'. And a preposition splits it too, the stress staying on ne: ne s kem govorit, no one to talk to; ne o chem sporit, nothing to argue about; ne na chto zhalovatsya, nothing to complain about.","table":{"rows":[["Мне не́чего де́лать. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Мне нечего делать\">🔊</button>","I have nothing to do. (dative + не́- + infinitive)"],["Ему́ не́где жить. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Ему негде жить\">🔊</button>","He has nowhere to live."],["Ей не́ с кем пойти́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Ей не с кем пойти\">🔊</button>","She has no one to go with. (preposition splits «не́-»)"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"There's nothing to talk about — everything's already decided.","en":"<b>Не́ о чем</b> говори́ть — всё уже́ решено́.","say":"Не о чем говорить — всё уже решено."},{"ru":"I've got no time to rest this week.","en":"На э́той неде́ле мне <b>не́когда</b> отдыха́ть.","say":"На этой неделе мне некогда отдыхать."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Мне не́чего не де́лать.","right":"Мне не́чего де́лать.","why":"Stressed «не́-» carries the whole negation itself — <b>no second «не»</b>. Compare «ни-», which always needs «не»; «не́-» never does."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> “there's no one to …” — pick the stressed «не́-» form.","q":"“He has no one to talk to.” —","opts":["Ему́ ни с кем не говори́ть.","Ему́ не́ с кем не говори́ть.","Ему́ не́ с кем говори́ть."],"answer":2,"explain":"Stressed «не́-» + infinitive, no second «не»: <b>Ему́ не́ с кем говори́ть</b>. One option uses the wrong «ни-» series; another adds a forbidden «не»."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“We have nowhere to go tonight.”</b> (нам … идти́)","want":"Нам не́куда идти́ сего́дня ве́чером","answer":"Нам не́куда идти́ сего́дня ве́чером"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · There isn't any — the genitive of absence","title":"<em>Нет вре́мени, не́ было де́нег</em> — “no X” takes the genitive","paras":["To say something simply <b>isn't there</b>, Russian has no “is not” — it uses <b>нет / не́ было / не бу́дет</b> plus the <b>genitive</b>, with no nominative subject at all. Present: <b>У меня́ нет вре́мени</b> (I have no time). Past: <b>Не́ было де́нег</b> (there was no money — stress on «не́», «было» leans on it). Future: <b>За́втра не бу́дет вре́мени</b>.","Whatever is missing goes into the genitive: вре́мя → нет <b>вре́мени</b>, де́ньги → нет <b>де́нег</b>, вопро́сы → нет <b>вопро́сов</b>.","One last contrast that catches everyone, because English uses “nothing” for both: <b>Я ничего́ не де́лаю</b> = “I'm not doing anything” (I could, I'm just not). <b>Мне не́чего де́лать</b> = “there's nothing for me to do” (nothing exists to do). Same English word, two different Russian worlds."],"audio":"To say something simply isn't there, Russian has no 'is not' — it uses net, ne bylo, or ne budet plus the genitive, with no nominative subject at all. Present: u menya net vremeni, I have no time. Past: ne bylo deneg, there was no money, stress on ne, bylo leans on it. Future: zavtra ne budet vremeni. Whatever is missing goes into the genitive: vremya becomes net vremeni, dengi becomes net deneg, voprosy becomes net voprosov. One last contrast that catches everyone, because English uses 'nothing' for both: ya nichego ne delayu means I'm not doing anything, I could, I'm just not. Mne nechego delat means there's nothing for me to do, nothing exists to do. Same English word, two different Russian worlds.","table":{"rows":[["У меня́ нет вре́мени. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"У меня нет времени\">🔊</button>","I have no time. (нет + genitive)"],["Не́ было де́нег. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Не было денег\">🔊</button>","There was no money. (past → не́ было + gen.)"],["Ничего́ не де́лаю ≠ Не́чего де́лать <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Ничего не делаю, нечего делать\">🔊</button>","not doing anything ≠ nothing to do"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"There won't be time for this tomorrow.","en":"За́втра на э́то <b>не бу́дет вре́мени</b>.","say":"Завтра на это не будет времени."},{"ru":"We had no money then, but plenty of dreams.","en":"Тогда́ у нас <b>не́ было де́нег</b>, но бы́ло мно́го мечта́ний.","say":"Тогда у нас не было денег, но было много мечтаний."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"У меня́ нет вре́мя.","right":"У меня́ нет вре́мени.","why":"«нет» demands the <b>genitive</b>: вре́мя → <b>вре́мени</b>. The thing that's missing is never in the nominative."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“There was no money for a taxi.” —","opts":["Не́ было де́ньги на такси́.","Не был де́нег на такси́.","Не́ было де́нег на такси́."],"answer":2,"explain":"Absence in the past → <b>не́ было</b> + genitive: <b>не́ было де́нег</b>."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the two words. Stress marks optional.","q":"Fill the gap: “I have no time today.” — Сего́дня у меня́ …","accept":["нет времени","нет времени.","нету времени"],"placeholder":"нет …","explain":"нет + genitive: <b>нет вре́мени</b>."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"At Ле́на's café, Emma frets about a reception she's been invited to. Ле́на talks her into going. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Ты идёшь за́втра на приём в изда́тельстве?","ru":"Are you going to the reception at the publisher's tomorrow?"},{"who":"B","en":"Не зна́ю. Мне не́чего наде́ть — и во́все нет вре́мени купи́ть.","ru":"I don't know. I've nothing to wear — and no time at all to buy anything."},{"who":"A","en":"Да у тебя́ по́лный шкаф! Никто́ не заме́тит, что пла́тье ста́рое.","ru":"But your wardrobe's full! Nobody will notice the dress is old."},{"who":"B","en":"И мне не́ с кем туда́ пойти́. Ива́н рабо́тает, ты в кафе́…","ru":"And I've no one to go there with. Ivan's working, you're at the café…"},{"who":"A","en":"Возьми́ Ка́тю! Она́ за́втра ниче́м не занята́.","ru":"Take Katya! She's not busy with anything tomorrow."},{"who":"B","en":"Пра́вда? Тогда́ мне не́чего боя́ться. Позвоню́ ей.","ru":"Really? Then I've nothing to be afraid of. I'll call her."},{"who":"A","en":"Вот и хорошо́. И переста́нь говори́ть, что тебе́ не́чего наде́ть!","ru":"There you go. And stop saying you've nothing to wear!"},{"who":"B","en":"Ла́дно, ла́дно. Никогда́ бо́льше не пожа́луюсь. Ну… почти́.","ru":"Fine, fine. I'll never complain again. Well… almost."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve ways to say no — the «ни-» row, the stressed «не́-» row, and the genitive of absence. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"никто́","ru":"no one (+ «не» on the verb)","ex":"Никто́ не отве́тил."},{"en":"ничего́","ru":"nothing (object; + «не»)","ex":"Я ничего́ не слы́шу."},{"en":"никого́","ru":"no one (acc./gen.; + «не»)","ex":"Я никого́ не жду."},{"en":"никогда́","ru":"never (+ «не»)","ex":"Он никогда́ не лжёт."},{"en":"нигде́","ru":"nowhere (+ «не»)","ex":"Её нигде́ нет."},{"en":"никуда́","ru":"(to) nowhere (+ «не»)","ex":"Я сего́дня никуда́ не иду́."},{"en":"не́кого","ru":"no one to (+ dative + infinitive)","ex":"Мне не́кого спроси́ть."},{"en":"не́чего","ru":"nothing to (+ dative + infinitive)","ex":"Ему́ не́чего сказа́ть."},{"en":"не́где","ru":"nowhere to (+ infinitive)","ex":"Здесь не́где сесть."},{"en":"не́когда","ru":"no time to (+ infinitive)","ex":"Мне сейча́с не́когда."},{"en":"нет","ru":"there is no (+ genitive)","ex":"У меня́ нет вре́мени."},{"en":"жа́ловаться","ru":"to complain (на + acc.)","ex":"Ему́ не́ на что жа́ловаться."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each negative to what it does.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Никто́ ничего́ не зна́ет","no one knows anything (double negation)"],["Мне не́чего де́лать","I have nothing to do (не́- + infinitive)"],["Ему́ не́ с кем говори́ть","he has no one to talk to (preposition splits не́-)"],["У меня́ нет вре́мени","I have no time (genitive of absence)"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five questions. Watch the split: «ни-» always keeps «не»; stressed «не́-» never does.","items":[{"q":"“Nobody said anything.” —","opts":["Никто́ ничего́ сказа́л.","Никто́ ничего́ не сказа́л.","Кто-то ничего́ не сказа́л."],"answer":1},{"q":"“I'm not talking to anyone.” —","opts":["Я ни с кем не говорю́.","Я не говорю́ с нике́м.","Я говорю́ ни с кем."],"answer":0},{"q":"“I have nothing to do.” (nothing exists to do) —","opts":["Мне не́чего де́лать.","Мне ничего́ не де́лать.","Мне не́чего не де́лать."],"answer":0},{"q":"“He has no one to go with.” —","opts":["Ему́ не́ с кем пойти́.","Ему́ не с кем не пойти́.","Ему́ ни с кем пойти́."],"answer":0},{"q":"“There was no money.” —","opts":["Не́ было де́ньги.","Не́ был де́нег.","Не́ было де́нег."],"answer":2}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's wry little note about the wardrobe crisis — read it aloud. Both negative rows are hiding in it.","title":"Не́чего наде́ть","sentences":["Э́мма стоя́ла пе́ред по́лным шка́фом и вздыха́ла.","«Мне соверше́нно не́чего наде́ть», — ду́мала она́.","На са́мом де́ле никто́ никогда́ не смотре́л на её пла́тья.","Про́сто ей не́ с кем пойти́ на приём.","Но Ка́тя свобо́дна — и тепе́рь боя́ться не́чего."],"translation":"Emma stood in front of the full wardrobe and sighed. “I have absolutely nothing to wear,” she thought. In truth nobody ever looked at her dresses. It was simply that she had no one to go to the reception with. But Katya is free — and now there's nothing to be afraid of."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — describe a moment of “no one, nothing, nowhere”.","prompt":"In 3–4 sentences, describe a quiet or empty moment. Use one «ни-…не…» double negative, one stressed «не́-» with an infinitive (не́чего / не́ с кем / не́куда), and one genitive of absence (нет / не́ было + genitive).","hint":"Two “no”s together: Никто́ ничего́ не зна́ет. Stressed «не́-», no second «не»: Мне не́чего де́лать, ей не́ с кем пойти́. Absence: нет вре́мени, не́ было де́нег 🙂","example":"Ве́чером мне ча́сто не́чем себя́ заня́ть: никто́ не звони́т, и идти́ не́куда. Но я не жа́луюсь — нет ничего́ прия́тнее ти́хого ве́чера до́ма."}},"17":{"day":17,"week":"03","themeRu":"Although, provided that","themeEn":"Хотя́, несмотря́ на то что","intro":"Emma and Ива́н are arguing — warmly — about a film. He loved it; she didn't. To hold her ground she needs the connectives that let you <b>concede a point and still disagree</b>: <b>хотя́</b> (although), <b>несмотря́ на то что</b> (despite the fact that), <b>как ни</b> (however hard…). These are the joints of a real Russian argument — and, good news, in Russian “despite” is a clause, not the bare little word English lets you get away with.","introAudio":"Emma and Ivan are arguing — warmly — about a film. He loved it; she didn't. To hold her ground she needs the connectives that let you concede a point and still disagree: khotya, although; nesmotrya na to chto, despite the fact that; kak ni, however hard. These are the joints of a real Russian argument — and, good news, in Russian 'despite' is a clause, not the bare little word English lets you get away with.","goals":["Concede with a clause: <b>хотя́</b> + full clause — <b>Хотя́ бы́ло по́здно, она́ рабо́тала</b>","Say “despite” properly: <b>несмотря́ на то что</b> (+ clause), <b>несмотря́ на</b> (+ noun)","Push through with <b>как ни</b>: <b>как ни стара́лся</b> — however hard he tried","Set conditions and pivot: <b>при усло́вии что</b>, <b>в слу́чае е́сли</b>, and <b>Де́ло в том, что…</b>"],"learned":["Conceded with a clause: <b>хотя́</b> + full clause — <b>Хотя́ бы́ло по́здно, она́ рабо́тала</b>","Said “despite” properly: <b>несмотря́ на то что</b> (+ clause), <b>несмотря́ на</b> (+ noun)","Pushed through with <b>как ни</b>: <b>как ни стара́лся</b> — however hard he tried","Set conditions and used the pivot: <b>при усло́вии что</b>, <b>в слу́чае е́сли</b>, <b>Де́ло в том, что…</b>"],"review":{"intro":"Yesterday's “no one”, the day-before's “someone”, and one gerund — a quick pull before we learn to argue.","introAudio":"Yesterday's 'no one', the day-before's 'someone', and one gerund — a quick pull before we learn to argue.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the fully-negated sentence.","q":"“Nobody knows anything.” —","opts":["Никто́ ничего́ зна́ет.","Никто́ ничего́ не зна́ет.","Кто-то ничего́ не зна́ет."],"answer":1,"explain":"The ни-words keep «не» on the verb: <b>Никто́ ничего́ не зна́ет</b>."},{"type":"choice","q":"“I've seen him somewhere before.” (a real place) —","opts":["Я его́ где-нибу́дь ви́дела.","Я его́ где-то ви́дела.","Я его́ ко́е-где ви́дела."],"answer":1,"explain":"A real but unnamed place → <b>где-то</b>. где-нибу́дь would be hypothetical “anywhere”."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“Having finished work, she went home.”</b>","want":"Зако́нчив рабо́ту, она́ пошла́ домо́й","answer":"Зако́нчив рабо́ту, она́ пошла́ домо́й"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Concede with a whole clause","title":"<em>Хотя́ бы́ло по́здно, она́ рабо́тала</em> — «хотя́» = although","paras":["<b>Хотя́</b> means “although”, and it opens a <b>full clause</b> — subject and verb — that grants a point you're about to push against: <b>Хотя́ бы́ло по́здно, она́ продолжа́ла рабо́тать</b> (although it was late, she kept working). The main clause often answers with «(но) всё равно́» or «тем не ме́нее».","«хотя́» can also sit in the middle: <b>Она́ рабо́тала, хотя́ уста́ла</b> (she worked, although she was tired). Either way, a whole clause follows it — never just a lone word.","Hold it as the honest opener of a disagreement: “yes, that's true, <em>but</em>…”. You concede, then you counter."],"audio":"Khotya means 'although', and it opens a full clause — subject and verb — that grants a point you're about to push against: Khotya bylo pozdno, ona prodolzhala rabotat, although it was late, she kept working. The main clause often answers with 'no vsyo ravno' or 'tem ne meneye'. Khotya can also sit in the middle: ona rabotala, khotya ustala, she worked, although she was tired. Either way, a whole clause follows it — never just a lone word. Hold it as the honest opener of a disagreement: yes, that's true, but. You concede, then you counter.","table":{"rows":[["Хотя́ бы́ло по́здно, она́ рабо́тала. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Хотя было поздно, она работала\">🔊</button>","Although it was late, she was working."],["Она́ рабо́тала, хотя́ уста́ла. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Она работала, хотя устала\">🔊</button>","She worked, although she was tired. (хотя́ mid-sentence)"],["Хотя́ он согла́сен, он молчи́т. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Хотя он согласен, он молчит\">🔊</button>","Although he agrees, he stays silent."]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Although she was tired, she finished the chapter.","en":"<b>Хотя́</b> она́ уста́ла, она́ дочита́ла главу́.","say":"Хотя она устала, она дочитала главу."},{"ru":"He came, although nobody had invited him.","en":"Он пришёл, <b>хотя́</b> его́ никто́ не приглаша́л.","say":"Он пришёл, хотя его никто не приглашал."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Хотя́ по́здно.","right":"Хотя́ бы́ло по́здно, она́ продолжа́ла рабо́тать.","why":"«хотя́» needs a <b>full clause</b>, not a fragment. Give it a subject and a verb: <b>Хотя́ бы́ло по́здно, …</b>."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the correct concession.","q":"Which correctly concedes with «хотя́»?","opts":["Хотя́ уста́ла, она́ дочита́ла главу́.","Хотя́ уста́ла.","Несмотря́ уста́ла, она́ дочита́ла главу́."],"answer":0,"explain":"«хотя́» + full clause + main clause: <b>Хотя́ уста́ла, она́ дочита́ла главу́</b>. The second is a fragment; the third misuses «несмотря́»."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“Although it was raining, we went for a walk.”</b>","want":"Хотя́ шёл дождь, мы пошли́ гуля́ть","answer":"Хотя́ шёл дождь, мы пошли́ гуля́ть"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · “Despite” is a clause, not a bare word","title":"<em>Несмотря́ на то что</em> — and «несмотря́ на» + noun, «как ни» + verb","paras":["Here is the signature English trap. “Despite” looks like one small word, so learners write «несмотря́ он опозда́л» — impossible. If a <b>clause</b> follows, you need the full connective <b>несмотря́ на то что</b>: <b>Несмотря́ на то что он опозда́л, его́ при́няли</b> (despite the fact that he was late, they took him on).","If only a <b>noun</b> follows, use <b>несмотря́ на</b> + accusative: <b>несмотря́ на дождь</b> (despite the rain), <b>несмотря́ на уста́лость</b> (despite the tiredness). So «хотя́» + clause (although) and «несмотря́ на» + noun (despite) are <b>not</b> interchangeable — and «несмотря́ на то что» is the clause-form of “despite”.","One more concessive, very Russian: <b>как ни</b> + verb = “however much / however hard…”. <b>Как ни стара́лся, он не смог</b> (however hard he tried, he couldn't). The «ни» here isn't a negative — it's part of the “however” frame."],"audio":"Here is the signature English trap. 'Despite' looks like one small word, so learners write 'nesmotrya on opozdal' — impossible. If a clause follows, you need the full connective nesmotrya na to chto: Nesmotrya na to chto on opozdal, yego prinyali, despite the fact that he was late, they took him on. If only a noun follows, use nesmotrya na plus accusative: nesmotrya na dozhd, despite the rain; nesmotrya na ustalost, despite the tiredness. So khotya plus clause, although, and nesmotrya na plus noun, despite, are not interchangeable — and nesmotrya na to chto is the clause-form of despite. One more concessive, very Russian: kak ni plus verb equals however much or however hard. Kak ni staralsya, on ne smog, however hard he tried, he couldn't. The 'ni' here isn't a negative — it's part of the 'however' frame.","table":{"rows":[["Несмотря́ на то что по́здно, мы вы́шли. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Несмотря на то что поздно, мы вышли\">🔊</button>","Despite the fact that it's late, we went out. (+ clause)"],["Мы шли несмотря́ на дождь. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Мы шли несмотря на дождь\">🔊</button>","We walked despite the rain. (+ noun, accusative)"],["Как ни стара́лся, он не успе́л. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Как ни старался, он не успел\">🔊</button>","However hard he tried, he was too late."]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Despite the fact that the ending is sad, I love this film.","en":"<b>Несмотря́ на то что</b> коне́ц гру́стный, я люблю́ э́тот фильм.","say":"Несмотря на то что конец грустный, я люблю этот фильм."},{"ru":"However much I tried, I couldn't fall asleep.","en":"<b>Как ни</b> стара́лась, я не могла́ засну́ть.","say":"Как ни старалась, я не могла заснуть."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Несмотря́ он опозда́л, его́ при́няли.","right":"Несмотря́ на то что он опозда́л, его́ при́няли.","why":"Before a clause, “despite” must be the full connective <b>несмотря́ на то что</b>. Bare «несмотря́» can't head a clause on its own."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“Despite the fact that it was hard, he finished.” —","opts":["Несмотря́ бы́ло тру́дно, он зако́нчил.","Несмотря́ на то что бы́ло тру́дно, он зако́нчил.","Хотя́ на то что бы́ло тру́дно, он зако́нчил."],"answer":1,"explain":"Clause → <b>несмотря́ на то что</b>. The first drops «на то что»; the third bolts «на то что» onto «хотя́», which doesn't take it."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> complete with a noun phrase. Stress marks optional.","q":"Finish it: “We walked despite the rain.” — Мы шли …","accept":["несмотря на дождь","несмотря на дождь."],"placeholder":"несмотря́ …","explain":"Before a noun → <b>несмотря́ на</b> + accusative: <b>несмотря́ на дождь</b>."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Conditions past plain «е́сли»","title":"<em>При усло́вии что, в слу́чае е́сли</em> — finer “if”s","paras":["Beyond plain «е́сли» you have sharper conditions, the language of plans and agreements. <b>при усло́вии что</b> = “provided that / on condition that”: <b>Я помогу́ при усло́вии что ты попро́сишь</b> (I'll help provided you ask). <b>в слу́чае е́сли</b> = “in case / in the event that”: <b>В слу́чае е́сли пойдёт дождь, встре́тимся в кафе́</b>.","<b>е́сли то́лько</b> = “as long as / provided … doesn't”: <b>Пойдём гуля́ть, е́сли то́лько не бу́дет дождя́</b> (we'll go for a walk, as long as it doesn't rain).","Each one heads a <b>full clause</b>, exactly like «е́сли». Keep the little «что» after «при усло́вии» — dropping it is the usual slip."],"audio":"Beyond plain 'yesli' you have sharper conditions, the language of plans and agreements. Pri uslovii chto equals 'provided that' or 'on condition that': Ya pomogu pri uslovii chto ty poprosish, I'll help provided you ask. V sluchaye yesli equals 'in case' or 'in the event that': V sluchaye yesli poydyot dozhd, vstretimsya v kafe. Yesli tolko equals 'as long as' or 'provided it doesn't': Poydyom gulyat, yesli tolko ne budet dozhdya, we'll go for a walk, as long as it doesn't rain. Each one heads a full clause, exactly like 'yesli'. Keep the little 'chto' after 'pri uslovii' — dropping it is the usual slip.","table":{"rows":[["Помогу́ при усло́вии что ты попро́сишь. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Помогу при условии что ты попросишь\">🔊</button>","I'll help provided that you ask."],["В слу́чае е́сли опозда́ю, начина́йте. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"В случае если опоздаю, начинайте\">🔊</button>","In case I'm late, start without me."],["Пойдём, е́сли то́лько не бу́дет дождя́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Пойдём, если только не будет дождя\">🔊</button>","We'll go, as long as it doesn't rain."]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"I'll sign the contract, provided that the date is changed.","en":"Я подпишу́ догово́р, <b>при усло́вии что</b> да́ту изме́нят.","say":"Я подпишу договор, при условии что дату изменят."},{"ru":"In case something happens, call me.","en":"<b>В слу́чае е́сли</b> что-нибу́дь случи́тся, позвони́ мне.","say":"В случае если что-нибудь случится, позвони мне."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я помогу́ при усло́вии ты попро́сишь.","right":"Я помогу́ при усло́вии что ты попро́сишь.","why":"The connective is <b>при усло́вии что</b> — the «что» links the clause. Don't drop it."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“I'll sign, provided that you change the date.” —","opts":["Подпишу́ при усло́вии что вы измени́те да́ту.","Подпишу́ при усло́вии вы измени́те да́ту.","Подпишу́ в слу́чае что вы измени́те да́ту."],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>при усло́вии что</b> + clause. The second drops «что»; «в слу́чае что» isn't a connective (it's «в слу́чае е́сли»)."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“In case it rains, we'll meet at the café.”</b>","want":"В слу́чае е́сли пойдёт дождь, встре́тимся в кафе́","answer":"В слу́чае е́сли пойдёт дождь, встре́тимся в кафе́"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · The «то + что / что́бы» pivot","title":"<em>Де́ло в том, что…</em> — hang a clause off a pivot word","paras":["Russian usually can't hang a «что»-clause straight off a preposition — it slips in a pivot word <b>то</b>, in whatever case the preposition or verb wants, and then adds «что» or «что́бы». <b>Де́ло в том, что…</b> (the thing is that…), <b>Я ду́маю о том, что…</b> (I'm thinking about the fact that…), <b>Он наста́ивал на том, что́бы…</b> (he insisted that…).","The choice of tail is the old fact-vs-goal split you know from «что́бы». Use <b>что</b> to report a <b>fact</b>: <b>Де́ло в том, что он опозда́л</b>. Use <b>что́бы</b> to aim at an <b>action or wish</b>: <b>Я слежу́ за тем, что́бы все бы́ли гото́вы</b>.","So when a «что»-clause won't attach, reach for the pivot: preposition + <b>том</b> (or the case you need) + что / что́бы."],"audio":"Russian usually can't hang a 'chto'-clause straight off a preposition — it slips in a pivot word 'to', in whatever case the preposition or verb wants, and then adds 'chto' or 'chtoby'. Delo v tom, chto, the thing is that; ya dumayu o tom, chto, I'm thinking about the fact that; on nastaival na tom, chtoby, he insisted that. The choice of tail is the old fact-versus-goal split you know from 'chtoby'. Use 'chto' to report a fact: Delo v tom, chto on opozdal. Use 'chtoby' to aim at an action or wish: ya slezhu za tem, chtoby vse byli gotovy. So when a 'chto'-clause won't attach, reach for the pivot: preposition, plus 'tom' or the case you need, plus chto or chtoby.","table":{"rows":[["Де́ло в том, что он опозда́л. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Дело в том, что он опоздал\">🔊</button>","The thing is (that) he was late. (fact → что)"],["Я ду́маю о том, что ты сказа́л. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я думаю о том, что ты сказал\">🔊</button>","I'm thinking about what you said."],["Он наста́ивал на том, что́бы мы оста́лись. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Он настаивал на том, чтобы мы остались\">🔊</button>","He insisted that we stay. (goal → что́бы)"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"The thing is, I don't agree with you.","en":"<b>Де́ло в том, что</b> я с тобо́й не согла́сна.","say":"Дело в том, что я с тобой не согласна."},{"ru":"I'm making sure that everyone is ready.","en":"Я слежу́ <b>за тем, что́бы</b> все бы́ли гото́вы.","say":"Я слежу за тем, чтобы все были готовы."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Де́ло, что он опозда́л.","right":"Де́ло в том, что он опозда́л.","why":"The clause needs a pivot: <b>в том, что</b>. The «то» takes the case the phrase requires (here «в том»), then «что» opens the clause."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“The thing is that I forgot the keys.” —","opts":["Де́ло, что я забы́л ключи́.","Де́ло в том, что я забы́л ключи́.","Де́ло в то, что я забы́л ключи́."],"answer":1,"explain":"Pivot in the prepositional: <b>в том, что</b>. The first drops the pivot; the third uses the wrong case («в то» instead of «в том»)."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the pivot's tail. Stress marks optional.","q":"Fill the gap: “I insist that you rest.” — Я наста́иваю на том, … ты отдохну́л.","accept":["чтобы","чтобы."],"placeholder":"что́бы","explain":"A demand aims at an action → <b>что́бы</b> (+ past-form verb)."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Emma and Ива́н spar over a film after the credits roll — nobody quite wins. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Ну как тебе́ фильм? По-мо́ему, гениа́льно!","ru":"So how'd you like the film? Brilliant, I think!"},{"who":"B","en":"Хотя́ карти́нка краси́вая, сюже́т меня́ не тро́нул.","ru":"Although the picture's beautiful, the plot didn't move me."},{"who":"A","en":"Несмотря́ на то что коне́ц гру́стный, э́то лу́чший фильм го́да!","ru":"Despite the fact that the ending's sad, it's the best film of the year!"},{"who":"B","en":"Как ни стара́лся режиссёр, геро́и по́лучились ску́чными.","ru":"However hard the director tried, the characters came out dull."},{"who":"A","en":"Ла́дно. Пересмо́трим его́ вме́сте — при усло́вии что ты досмо́тришь до конца́.","ru":"Fine. Let's rewatch it together — provided you watch to the end."},{"who":"B","en":"Де́ло в том, что я уже́ засну́ла на середи́не.","ru":"The thing is, I already fell asleep halfway through."},{"who":"A","en":"Тогда́ спо́рить не́ о чем! Смо́трим за́ново, в слу́чае е́сли ты не уста́ла.","ru":"Then there's nothing to argue about! We watch again, in case you're not tired."},{"who":"B","en":"Договори́лись. Хотя́, че́стно, я всё равно́ оста́нусь при своём мне́нии.","ru":"Deal. Although, honestly, I'll still stick to my opinion."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve joints for a real argument — concession, condition, and the pivot. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"хотя́","ru":"although (+ clause)","ex":"Хотя́ уста́л, он помо́г."},{"en":"несмотря́ на то что","ru":"despite the fact that (+ clause)","ex":"Несмотря́ на то что по́здно, мы пошли́."},{"en":"несмотря́ на","ru":"despite (+ noun, accusative)","ex":"Мы шли несмотря́ на дождь."},{"en":"как ни","ru":"however (much / hard) (+ verb)","ex":"Как ни стара́йся, не успе́ешь."},{"en":"при усло́вии что","ru":"provided that (+ clause)","ex":"Помогу́ при усло́вии что попро́сишь."},{"en":"в слу́чае е́сли","ru":"in case, in the event that","ex":"В слу́чае е́сли опозда́ю, начина́йте."},{"en":"тем не ме́нее","ru":"nevertheless, all the same","ex":"Бы́ло тру́дно, тем не ме́нее мы спра́вились."},{"en":"всё равно́","ru":"anyway, all the same","ex":"Он всё равно́ пришёл."},{"en":"де́ло в том, что","ru":"the thing is that (pivot)","ex":"Де́ло в том, что я забы́л."},{"en":"что́бы","ru":"so that, in order to (goal)","ex":"Я пришёл, что́бы помо́чь."},{"en":"усло́вие","ru":"condition","ex":"Гла́вное усло́вие — че́стность."},{"en":"мне́ние","ru":"opinion","ex":"У ка́ждого своё мне́ние."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each connective to its shape.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Хотя́ бы́ло по́здно","although it was late (concession + clause)"],["Несмотря́ на дождь","despite the rain (+ noun)"],["Как ни стара́лся","however hard he tried"],["При усло́вии что ты придёшь","provided that you come"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five questions on concession and condition. Remember: “despite” before a clause is «несмотря́ на то что».","items":[{"q":"“Although it was late, she kept working.” —","opts":["Хотя́ бы́ло по́здно, она́ рабо́тала.","Несмотря́ бы́ло по́здно, она́ рабо́тала.","Хотя́ на по́здно, она́ рабо́тала."],"answer":0},{"q":"“Despite the fact that he was late, they accepted him.” —","opts":["Несмотря́ он опозда́л, его́ при́няли.","Несмотря́ на то что он опозда́л, его́ при́няли.","Хотя́ на то что он опозда́л, его́ при́няли."],"answer":1},{"q":"“However hard he tried, he couldn't.” —","opts":["Как ни стара́лся, он не смог.","Хотя́ ни стара́лся, он не смог.","Несмотря́ ни стара́лся, он не смог."],"answer":0},{"q":"“I'll come, provided that there's time.” —","opts":["Приду́, при усло́вии что бу́дет вре́мя.","Приду́, при усло́вии бу́дет вре́мя.","Приду́, в слу́чае что бу́дет вре́мя."],"answer":0},{"q":"“The thing is that I forgot.” —","opts":["Де́ло, что я забы́л.","Де́ло в том, что я забы́л.","Де́ло в то, что я забы́л."],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's note after the film argument — read it aloud. Every connective from today is in it.","title":"Спор о фи́льме","sentences":["Мы с Ива́ном по́сле фи́льма немно́го поспо́рили.","Хотя́ карти́нка была́ краси́вой, сюже́т меня́ не тро́нул.","Несмотря́ на то что коне́ц гру́стный, Ива́н счита́ет фильм лу́чшим.","Как ни стара́лся режиссёр, геро́и вы́шли ску́чными.","Де́ло в том, что мы про́сто лю́бим ра́зные фи́льмы — и спо́рить об э́том прия́тно."],"translation":"Ivan and I argued a little after the film. Although the picture was beautiful, the plot didn't move me. Despite the fact that the ending is sad, Ivan considers the film the best. However hard the director tried, the characters came out dull. The thing is, we simply like different films — and it's nice to argue about it."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — defend an opinion, conceding as you go.","prompt":"In 3–4 sentences, defend an opinion about a film, book or place. Concede a point with «хотя́» or «несмотря́ на то что», push back with «как ни …», and set one condition with «при усло́вии что» or «в слу́чае е́сли». Try opening with «Де́ло в том, что…».","hint":"«хотя́» + clause (although). «несмотря́ на то что» + clause = despite; «несмотря́ на» + noun. «как ни стара́лся» = however hard. Pivot: «Де́ло в том, что…» 🙂","example":"Де́ло в том, что мне бо́льше нра́вится кни́га, чем фильм. Хотя́ актёры игра́ли отли́чно, глубины́ рома́на они́ не переда́ли. Как ни стара́лся режиссёр, кни́га всё равно́ бога́че — по кра́йней ме́ре при усло́вии что ты внима́тельно её чита́л."}},"18":{"day":18,"week":"03","themeRu":"Because, so that, therefore","themeEn":"Поско́льку, для того́ что́бы, так что","intro":"Emma just turned down a comfortable job and took a brutal one instead. Ива́н wants to know <em>why</em> — and “because” alone won't carry the answer. Written Russian argues: it front-loads a reason with <b>поско́льку</b>, weighs a cause as welcome (<b>благодаря́</b>) or unwelcome (<b>из-за</b>), aims at a goal with <b>для того́ что́бы</b>, and lands a result with <b>сле́довательно</b>. Today you pick up the whole toolkit of cause, purpose and consequence.","introAudio":"Emma just turned down a comfortable job and took a brutal one instead. Ivan wants to know why — and 'because' alone won't carry the answer. Written Russian argues: it front-loads a reason with poskolku, weighs a cause as welcome, blagodarya, or unwelcome, iz-za, aims at a goal with dlya togo chtoby, and lands a result with sledovatelno. Today you pick up the whole toolkit of cause, purpose and consequence.","goals":["Front-load a reason with <b>поско́льку</b> / <b>так как</b> — “since, because” at the head of the sentence","Weigh the cause: <b>благодаря́</b> (+dat., welcome) vs <b>из-за</b> (+gen., unwelcome); official <b>ввиду́ того́ что</b>","Aim at a goal with <b>что́бы</b> and its formal head <b>для того́ что́бы</b> (+ infinitive / past)","Land a result with <b>так что</b>, <b>поэ́тому</b>, <b>сле́довательно</b> — never confusing result «так что» with purpose «что́бы»"],"learned":["Front-loaded reasons with <b>поско́льку</b> / <b>так как</b>","Weighed causes: <b>благодаря́</b> (+dat.) vs <b>из-за</b> (+gen.), plus official <b>ввиду́ того́ что</b>","Aimed at goals with <b>что́бы</b> and <b>для того́ что́бы</b> (+ infinitive / past)","Landed results with <b>так что</b>, <b>поэ́тому</b>, <b>сле́довательно</b> — keeping result apart from purpose"],"review":{"intro":"A minute on this week so far — a concession, an indefinite, and a “nothing to” — before we widen the argument.","introAudio":"A minute on this week so far — a concession, an indefinite, and a 'nothing to' — before we widen the argument.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. This is yesterday's grammar.","q":"“Although it was raining, we went for a walk.” (a whole clause)","opts":["Несмотря́ на дождь, мы гуля́ли.","Хотя́ шёл дождь, мы гуля́ли.","Поско́льку шёл дождь, мы гуля́ли."],"answer":1,"explain":"“Although + clause” → <b>хотя́</b> + clause. «несмотря́ на» takes a noun (несмотря́ на дождь); поско́льку means “since”, not “although”."},{"type":"choice","q":"“Call someone (anyone), please.” — an open request","opts":["Позвони́ кому́-то.","Позвони́ кому́-нибу́дь.","Позвони́ никому́."],"answer":1,"explain":"An open “anyone” in a request → <b>-нибу́дь</b>: кому́-нибу́дь. кому́-то would be one specific person; никому́ needs a negated verb."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the missing word. Stress marks optional.","q":"“I have nothing to do.” — «Мне ___ де́лать.» (не́-word + infinitive)","accept":["нечего","нечего."],"placeholder":"Мне … де́лать.","explain":"«не́чего + infinitive» = “nothing to do”: <b>Мне не́чего де́лать</b> — stress on не́-, and no second «не»."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Because — beyond «потому́ что»","title":"<em>Поско́льку</em> и <em>так как</em> — “since, because” up front","paras":["At B1 you gave a reason with <b>потому́ что</b> — “because” — and it sits <b>after</b> the main clause: «Я оста́лся до́ма, <b>потому́ что</b> шёл дождь.» Written Russian argues with a wider set. The most useful pair: <b>поско́льку</b> and <b>так как</b> — both mean “since / because / as”, and both love the <b>front</b> of the sentence.","«<b>Поско́льку</b> перево́д сро́чный, я рабо́таю всю ночь» — “Since the translation is urgent, I'm working all night.” Reason first, result second, a comma between. <b>потому́ что</b> can't lead a sentence like this; <b>поско́льку</b> and <b>так как</b> can.","Register: <b>поско́льку</b> is the bookish, argue-a-point word — it belongs in Emma's reports and essays. <b>так как</b> is neutral, fine anywhere. <b>потому́ что</b> stays your everyday spoken “because”."],"audio":"At B1 you gave a reason with potomu chto — 'because' — and it sits after the main clause: ya ostalsya doma, potomu chto shyol dozhd. Written Russian argues with a wider set. The most useful pair: poskolku and tak kak — both mean 'since, because, as', and both love the front of the sentence. Poskolku perevod srochny, ya rabotayu vsyu noch — 'Since the translation is urgent, I'm working all night.' Reason first, result second, a comma between. Potomu chto can't lead a sentence like this; poskolku and tak kak can. Register: poskolku is the bookish, argue-a-point word — it belongs in Emma's reports and essays. Tak kak is neutral, fine anywhere. Potomu chto stays your everyday spoken 'because'.","table":{"rows":[["Поско́льку бы́ло по́здно, мы оста́лись. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Поскольку было поздно, мы остались\">🔊</button>","Since it was late, we stayed. (bookish, front)"],["Так как я за́нят, позвоню́ по́зже. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Так как я занят, позвоню позже\">🔊</button>","As I'm busy, I'll call later. (neutral)"],["Я рабо́таю, потому́ что э́то ва́жно. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я работаю, потому что это важно\">🔊</button>","I'm working because it's important. (spoken, follows)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Since the text is difficult, I read it twice.","en":"<b>Поско́льку</b> текст сло́жный, я чита́ю его́ два́жды.","say":"Поскольку текст сложный, я читаю его дважды."},{"ru":"As the office was closed, she came back the next day.","en":"<b>Так как</b> о́фис был закры́т, она́ верну́лась на сле́дующий день.","say":"Так как офис был закрыт, она вернулась на следующий день."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Поско́льку, что текст сло́жный…","right":"Поско́льку текст сло́жный…","why":"<b>поско́льку</b> is complete on its own — no «что» after it. Only the «то́, что»-type pivots pull a «что»."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"“Since the translation is urgent, I'm working all night.” — reason at the front:","opts":["Поско́льку перево́д сро́чный, я рабо́таю всю ночь.","Потому́ что перево́д сро́чный, я рабо́таю всю ночь.","Для того́ что́бы перево́д сро́чный, я рабо́таю всю ночь."],"answer":0,"explain":"A reason at the head → <b>поско́льку</b> (or так как). <b>потому́ что</b> can't lead the sentence; для того́ что́бы is purpose, not cause."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the connector. Stress marks optional.","q":"Fill the front-loaded “since”: «___ бы́ло по́здно, мы оста́лись до́ма.»","accept":["поскольку","так как"],"placeholder":"Поско́…","explain":"A reason that leads → <b>поско́льку</b> (or <b>так как</b>): the reason first, the result after a comma."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Thanks to vs because of — the cause has a mood","title":"<em>Благодаря́</em> (+dat., good) vs <em>из-за</em> (+gen., bad)","paras":["Russian marks whether a cause is <b>welcome</b> or <b>unwelcome</b>. <b>благодаря́</b> — “thanks to” — is for a cause you're glad of, and it takes the <b>dative</b>: <b>благодаря́ дру́гу</b> (thanks to a friend), <b>благодаря́ по́мощи</b> (thanks to the help). <b>из-за</b> — “because of / due to” — is for a cause you'd rather avoid, and it takes the <b>genitive</b>: <b>из-за дождя́</b> (because of the rain), <b>из-за оши́бки</b> (because of a mistake).","To attach a whole clause, add <b>тому́ что</b>: <b>благодаря́ тому́ что</b> мне помогли́… (thanks to the fact that they helped me…), <b>из-за того́ что</b> шёл дождь… (because of the fact that it was raining…).","One more, straight from Emma's bureaucracy: <b>ввиду́</b> (+gen.) — “in view of / owing to”, coldly official: <b>ввиду́ того́ что</b> докуме́нты не гото́вы… (in view of the fact that the documents aren't ready…). Save it for formal writing."],"audio":"Russian marks whether a cause is welcome or unwelcome. Blagodarya — 'thanks to' — is for a cause you're glad of, and it takes the dative: blagodarya drugu, thanks to a friend; blagodarya pomoshchi, thanks to the help. Iz-za — 'because of, due to' — is for a cause you'd rather avoid, and it takes the genitive: iz-za dozhdya, because of the rain; iz-za oshibki, because of a mistake. To attach a whole clause, add tomu chto: blagodarya tomu chto mne pomogli, thanks to the fact that they helped me; iz-za togo chto shyol dozhd, because of the fact that it was raining. One more, straight from Emma's bureaucracy: vvidu, plus genitive — 'in view of, owing to', coldly official: vvidu togo chto dokumenty ne gotovy, in view of the fact that the documents aren't ready. Save it for formal writing.","table":{"rows":[["благодаря́ дру́гу (dat.) <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"благодаря другу\">🔊</button>","thanks to a friend (welcome cause)"],["из-за дождя́ (gen.) <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"из-за дождя\">🔊</button>","because of the rain (unwelcome cause)"],["благодаря́ тому́ что мне помогли́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"благодаря тому что мне помогли\">🔊</button>","thanks to the fact that they helped me"],["ввиду́ того́ что докуме́нты не гото́вы <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ввиду того что документы не готовы\">🔊</button>","in view of the fact that the documents aren't ready (official)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Thanks to my friends, I learned the language quickly.","en":"<b>Благодаря́</b> друзья́м я бы́стро вы́учила язы́к.","say":"Благодаря друзьям я быстро выучила язык."},{"ru":"Because of a mistake in the document, the deal fell through.","en":"<b>Из-за</b> оши́бки в докуме́нте сде́лка сорвала́сь.","say":"Из-за ошибки в документе сделка сорвалась."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"благодаря́ дождя́","right":"из-за дождя́","why":"Rain is an unwelcome cause → <b>из-за</b> + genitive. Keep <b>благодаря́</b> for causes you're grateful for — and it takes the dative."},{"wrong":"из-за друзья́м","right":"из-за друзе́й","why":"<b>из-за</b> takes the <b>genitive</b> (друзе́й). It's <b>благодаря́</b> that takes the dative (благодаря́ друзья́м)."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“___ heavy rain, the flight was delayed.” (an unwelcome cause):","opts":["Благодаря́ си́льному дождю́","Из-за си́льного дождя́","Для си́льного дождя́"],"answer":1,"explain":"A delay is bad news → <b>из-за</b> + genitive: <b>из-за си́льного дождя́</b>. благодаря́ (+dat.) would praise the rain."},{"type":"choice","q":"“I passed the exam ___ my teacher.” (a welcome cause):","opts":["из-за моего́ преподава́теля","благодаря́ моему́ преподава́телю","благодаря́ моего́ преподава́теля"],"answer":1,"explain":"A welcome cause → <b>благодаря́</b> + <b>dative</b>: благодаря́ моему́ преподава́телю. Option 3 wrongly uses the genitive; из-за would blame the teacher."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the connector. Stress marks optional.","q":"Attach a clause: “___ they helped me, everything worked out.” (thanks to the fact that)","accept":["благодаря тому что","благодаря тому, что"],"placeholder":"Благодаря́ тому́…","explain":"Whole-clause “thanks to” = <b>благодаря́ тому́ что</b> + clause."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · In order to — «для того́ что́бы»","title":"<em>Что́бы</em>, and its heavier twin <em>для того́ что́бы</em>","paras":["You already aim at a goal with <b>что́бы</b> — “in order to”: «Я учу́ ру́сский, <b>что́бы</b> рабо́тать в Москве́.» Same rule as B1: after что́бы use the <b>infinitive</b> when the subject stays the same, and the <b>past tense</b> when it changes (<b>что́бы он по́нял</b> — so that he'd understand).","In writing, that что́бы often grows a formal head: <b>для того́ что́бы</b> (“in order that / so as to”) and, more bookish still, <b>с тем что́бы</b>. Same grammar after them — infinitive or past — just a weightier, essay-ready connector: «<b>Для того́ что́бы</b> поня́ть текст, ну́жно чита́ть ме́жду строк.»","Careful with the spelling split from B1: purpose <b>что́бы</b> is one word; <b>что бы</b> (two words) is the different “whatever / what … would”. Purpose = one word, always."],"audio":"You already aim at a goal with chtoby — 'in order to': ya uchu russky, chtoby rabotat v Moskve. Same rule as B1: after chtoby use the infinitive when the subject stays the same, and the past tense when it changes — chtoby on ponyal, so that he'd understand. In writing, that chtoby often grows a formal head: dlya togo chtoby, 'in order that, so as to', and, more bookish still, s tem chtoby. Same grammar after them — infinitive or past — just a weightier, essay-ready connector: dlya togo chtoby ponyat tekst, nuzhno chitat mezhdu strok. Careful with the spelling split from B1: purpose chtoby is one word; chto by, two words, is the different 'whatever, what would'. Purpose is one word, always.","table":{"rows":[["что́бы рабо́тать (same subject → inf.) <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"чтобы работать\">🔊</button>","in order to work"],["что́бы он по́нял (new subject → past) <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"чтобы он понял\">🔊</button>","so that he'd understand"],["для того́ что́бы поня́ть текст <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"для того чтобы понять текст\">🔊</button>","in order to understand the text (formal)"],["с тем что́бы избежа́ть оши́бок <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"с тем чтобы избежать ошибок\">🔊</button>","so as to avoid mistakes (bookish)"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"In order to translate a novel, you have to feel the language.","en":"<b>Для того́ что́бы</b> перевести́ рома́н, на́до чу́вствовать язы́к.","say":"Для того чтобы перевести роман, надо чувствовать язык."},{"ru":"I explained everything twice so that they would understand.","en":"Я всё объясни́ла два́жды, <b>что́бы</b> они́ по́няли.","say":"Я всё объяснила дважды, чтобы они поняли."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"что́бы они́ понима́ют","right":"что́бы они́ по́няли","why":"After <b>что́бы</b> a verb is never present tense: same subject → infinitive, different subject → <b>past</b> (по́няли). Never «понима́ют»."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“I'm learning Russian in order to work in Moscow.” (same subject):","opts":["что́бы рабо́таю в Москве́","что́бы рабо́тать в Москве́","так что рабо́тать в Москве́"],"answer":1,"explain":"Same subject after <b>что́бы</b> → infinitive: <b>что́бы рабо́тать</b>. Never present tense; так что is consequence, not purpose."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the verb. Stress marks optional.","q":"New subject → past: «Я говорю́ гро́мко, что́бы все ___.» (so that everyone would hear — услы́шать)","accept":["услышали","услышали."],"placeholder":"все …","explain":"Different subject after что́бы → past tense: <b>услы́шали</b>."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“In order to understand the text, you must read slowly.”</b> (formal opener)","want":"Для того́ что́бы поня́ть текст, на́до чита́ть ме́дленно","answer":"Для того́ что́бы поня́ть текст, на́до чита́ть ме́дленно"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · So, therefore — naming the result","title":"<em>Так что</em>, <em>поэ́тому</em>, <em>сле́довательно</em> — and «так что» ≠ «что́бы»","paras":["Flip the arrow: instead of pointing back to a cause, point forward to a <b>result</b>. Everyday, that's <b>так что</b> — “so”: «Перево́д гото́в, <b>так что</b> я свобо́дна.» (The translation's done, so I'm free.) And <b>поэ́тому</b> — “therefore / that's why”, yours since B1.","For an essay, reach higher: <b>сле́довательно</b> — “consequently, it follows that”, the mark of a careful argument: «Фа́кты я́сны; <b>сле́довательно</b>, вы́вод очеви́ден.» (A cousin you'll meet in the register lesson is <b>таки́м о́бразом</b>, “thus”.)","Here's the trap that catches English speakers: <b>так что</b> = <b>consequence</b> (“so” = as a result), but <b>что́бы</b> = <b>purpose</b> (“so that” = in order to). English “so” hides both. «Я закры́ла дверь, <b>так что</b> ста́ло ти́хо» (result: so it went quiet) vs «Я закры́ла дверь, <b>что́бы</b> ста́ло ти́хо» (purpose: so that it would be quiet). Ask: a result that already follows, or an aim?"],"audio":"Flip the arrow: instead of pointing back to a cause, point forward to a result. Everyday, that's tak chto — 'so': perevod gotov, tak chto ya svobodna, the translation's done, so I'm free. And poetomu — 'therefore, that's why', yours since B1. For an essay, reach higher: sledovatelno — 'consequently, it follows that', the mark of a careful argument: fakty yasny, sledovatelno, vyvod ochividen. A cousin you'll meet in the register lesson is takim obrazom, 'thus'. Here's the trap that catches English speakers: tak chto is consequence, 'so' as a result, but chtoby is purpose, 'so that', in order to. English 'so' hides both. Ya zakryla dver, tak chto stalo tikho — result: so it went quiet. Ya zakryla dver, chtoby stalo tikho — purpose: so that it would be quiet. Ask: a result that already follows, or an aim?","table":{"rows":[["Дождь ко́нчился, так что мы пошли́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Дождь кончился, так что мы пошли\">🔊</button>","The rain stopped, so we set off. (result)"],["Бы́ло по́здно, поэ́тому я оста́лась. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Было поздно, поэтому я осталась\">🔊</button>","It was late, therefore I stayed."],["Фа́кты я́сны, сле́довательно, он прав. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Факты ясны, следовательно, он прав\">🔊</button>","The facts are clear, consequently he's right. (bookish)"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"The documents are signed, so the deal is closed.","en":"Докуме́нты подпи́саны, <b>так что</b> сде́лка закры́та.","say":"Документы подписаны, так что сделка закрыта."},{"ru":"He didn't answer; therefore, I wrote again.","en":"Он не отве́тил, <b>поэ́тому</b> я написа́ла ещё раз.","say":"Он не ответил, поэтому я написала ещё раз."},{"ru":"I closed the door so that it would be quiet (purpose).","en":"Я закры́ла дверь, <b>что́бы</b> ста́ло ти́хо.","say":"Я закрыла дверь, чтобы стало тихо."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я учу́ ру́сский, так что рабо́тать здесь.","right":"Я учу́ ру́сский, что́бы рабо́тать здесь.","why":"Here you mean the <b>aim</b> (in order to work) → <b>что́бы</b> + infinitive. <b>так что</b> would state a result that already follows, not a goal."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“The translation is done, ___ I'm free tonight.” (a result):","opts":["что́бы я свобо́дна ве́чером","так что я свобо́дна ве́чером","для того́ что́бы я свобо́дна ве́чером"],"answer":1,"explain":"A result that follows → <b>так что</b> (“so”). что́бы / для того́ что́бы mark a purpose, not a result."},{"type":"choice","q":"“I speak slowly ___ everyone understands me.” (aim = in order that):","opts":["так что все меня́ понима́ют","что́бы все меня́ понима́ли","поэ́тому все меня́ понима́ли"],"answer":1,"explain":"Your aim → <b>что́бы</b> + past (different subject): что́бы все меня́ <b>понима́ли</b>. так что / поэ́тому would state a result, not a goal."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Фа́кты я́сны, сле́довательно, вы́вод очеви́ден.","sub":"“The facts are clear, therefore the conclusion is obvious” — argue like an essay","want":"факты ясны следовательно вывод очевиден"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Emma has taken on a tough official translation. Ива́н wonders why she'd choose such a headache. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Поско́льку у тебя́ и так мно́го рабо́ты, заче́м ты взяла́ ещё оди́н тру́дный перево́д?","ru":"Since you already have so much work, why did you take on another difficult translation?"},{"who":"B","en":"Из-за де́нег? Нет. Я взяла́ его́ для того́ что́бы научи́ться но́вому.","ru":"Because of the money? No. I took it in order to learn something new."},{"who":"A","en":"Благодаря́ таки́м зака́зам ты растёшь как перево́дчик, э́то пра́вда.","ru":"Thanks to jobs like this you grow as a translator, that's true."},{"who":"B","en":"И́менно. Текст официа́льный, так что мне ну́жен то́чный, кни́жный стиль.","ru":"Exactly. The text is official, so I need a precise, bookish style."},{"who":"A","en":"Ввиду́ того́ что докуме́нт ва́жный, тебе́ помо́жет Макси́м?","ru":"In view of the fact that the document is important, will Maxim help you?"},{"who":"B","en":"Да, твой брат-инжене́р зна́ет те́хнику. Сле́довательно, терми́ны бу́дут пра́вильными.","ru":"Yes, your engineer brother knows the technical side. Consequently, the terms will be right."},{"who":"A","en":"Поэ́тому вы отли́чная кома́нда!","ru":"That's why you make a great team!"},{"who":"B","en":"Так как срок за́втра, я сажу́сь рабо́тать пря́мо сейча́с.","ru":"Since the deadline is tomorrow, I'm sitting down to work right now."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve connectors for cause, purpose and result — the joints of written Russian. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"поско́льку","ru":"since, because (bookish, leads)","ex":"Поско́льку уже́ по́здно, я остаю́сь до́ма."},{"en":"так как","ru":"as, since (neutral)","ex":"Так как я за́нят, я перезвоню́."},{"en":"благодаря́","ru":"thanks to (+ dat., welcome cause)","ex":"Благодаря́ по́мощи всё получи́лось."},{"en":"из-за","ru":"because of, due to (+ gen., bad cause)","ex":"Из-за дождя́ мы опозда́ли."},{"en":"ввиду́","ru":"in view of, owing to (+ gen., official)","ex":"Ввиду́ боле́зни он не пришёл."},{"en":"для того́ что́бы","ru":"in order to (formal purpose)","ex":"Для того́ что́бы успе́ть, встань ра́но."},{"en":"так что","ru":"so (result)","ex":"Всё гото́во, так что мо́жем идти́."},{"en":"поэ́тому","ru":"therefore, that's why","ex":"Бы́ло по́здно, поэ́тому я ушёл."},{"en":"сле́довательно","ru":"consequently, it follows that (bookish)","ex":"Он согла́сен, сле́довательно, начнём."},{"en":"причи́на","ru":"reason, cause","ex":"Назови́те причи́ну опозда́ния."},{"en":"цель","ru":"goal, aim, purpose","ex":"Кака́я цель ва́шего визи́та?"},{"en":"вы́вод","ru":"conclusion","ex":"Тако́й вы́вод сде́лал а́втор."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the connector phrase to its meaning.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["благодаря́ по́мощи","thanks to the help"],["из-за оши́бки","because of a mistake"],["для того́ что́бы поня́ть","in order to understand"],["так что я свобо́дна","so I'm free"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — cause, purpose, result. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"“Since the text is hard, I read it twice.” (bookish, front) —","opts":["Так что текст сло́жный, я чита́ю его́ два́жды.","Поско́льку текст сло́жный, я чита́ю его́ два́жды.","Что́бы текст сло́жный, я чита́ю его́ два́жды."],"answer":1},{"q":"Rain is an unwelcome cause →","opts":["для дождя́","благодаря́ дождю́","из-за дождя́"],"answer":2},{"q":"A welcome cause takes the dative with…","opts":["из-за","ввиду́","благодаря́"],"answer":2},{"q":"“in order to work here” —","opts":["что́бы рабо́тать здесь","так что рабо́тать здесь","поэ́тому рабо́тать здесь"],"answer":0},{"q":"“The deal is closed, ___ I'm free.” (a result) —","opts":["что́бы я свобо́дна","так что я свобо́дна","для того́ что́бы я свобо́дна"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's note to herself about the job she chose — every connector from today is hiding in it. Read it aloud.","title":"Заче́м я взяла́ э́тот перево́д","sentences":["Поско́льку рабо́та была́ тру́дной, мно́гие отказа́лись.","Но я согласи́лась, что́бы прове́рить свои́ си́лы.","Из-за коро́ткого сро́ка пришло́сь рабо́тать по ноча́м.","Благодаря́ по́мощи Макси́ма терми́ны ста́ли то́чными.","Текст официа́льный, так что стиль до́лжен быть кни́жным.","Сле́довательно, э́тот перево́д — шаг вперёд в мое́й профе́ссии."],"translation":"Since the work was difficult, many people refused. But I agreed, in order to test myself. Because of the short deadline, I had to work at night. Thanks to Maxim's help the terms became precise. The text is official, so the style must be bookish. Consequently, this translation is a step forward in my profession."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — argue a choice the way written Russian does.","prompt":"Write 3–4 sentences about a decision you made (a job, a trip, a purchase). Give a reason with поско́льку or так как, a purpose with что́бы or для того́ что́бы, and a result with так что or сле́довательно. Slip in благодаря́ or из-за for one cause.","hint":"Front-load a reason with «поско́льку…», aim with «что́бы + infinitive», and close with «так что…» or «сле́довательно, …» 🙂","example":"Поско́льку я хоте́ла понима́ть ру́сскую про́зу, я записа́лась на курс. Я учу́сь ка́ждый день, что́бы чита́ть без словаря́. Благодаря́ учи́телю я де́лаю успе́хи, так что ско́ро возьму́сь за пе́рвый рома́н."}},"19":{"day":19,"week":"03","themeRu":"Two, both, a half","themeEn":"Дво́е, о́ба, полтора́","intro":"How many people came to Emma's dinner on Saturday? “About ten”, she'd say — and Russian has a wonderfully sly way to say <em>about</em> ten: just flip the words. Numbers get richer at B2. Today you count people in a bunch (<b>дво́е, тро́е</b>), say “both” with a gender (<b>о́ба / о́бе</b>), handle halves and fractions (<b>полтора́</b>, <b>две тре́тьих</b>), and hedge a count three different ways. Let's do the maths, Russian-style.","introAudio":"How many people came to Emma's dinner on Saturday? 'About ten', she'd say — and Russian has a wonderfully sly way to say about ten: just flip the words. Numbers get richer at B2. Today you count people in a bunch, dvoye, troye; say 'both' with a gender, oba and obe; handle halves and fractions, poltora, dve tretikh; and hedge a count three different ways. Let's do the maths, Russian-style.","goals":["Count people in a group with the collectives <b>дво́е, тро́е, че́тверо</b> (+ genitive plural) — and know they skip women","Say “both” by gender: <b>о́ба</b> (m/n) vs <b>о́бе</b> (f), declined <b>обо́их / обе́их</b>","Handle “one and a half” — <b>полтора́</b> (m/n) / <b>полторы́</b> (f) — and fractions like <b>две тре́тьих</b>","Hedge a number three ways: inversion (<b>челове́к де́сять</b>), <b>о́коло</b> +gen, and <b>свы́ше / бо́лее / ме́нее</b>"],"learned":["Counted groups with <b>дво́е, тро́е, че́тверо</b> (+ genitive plural), not for women","Chose “both” by gender: <b>о́ба</b> (m/n) vs <b>о́бе</b> (f), declined обо́их / обе́их","Handled <b>полтора́ / полторы́</b> and fractions like <b>две тре́тьих</b>","Hedged numbers by inversion (<b>челове́к де́сять</b>), <b>о́коло</b>, and <b>свы́ше / бо́лее / ме́нее</b>"],"review":{"intro":"One minute back on B1 numbers before we widen them — a declined «два», a time on the clock, and a genitive after a count.","introAudio":"One minute back on B1 numbers before we widen them — a declined 'dva', a time on the clock, and a genitive after a count.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. This is B1 number declension.","q":"“with two Russian friends” (instrumental) —","opts":["с двумя́ ру́сскими друзья́ми","с два ру́сских друзе́й","с двух ру́сскими друзья́ми"],"answer":0,"explain":"«с» → instrumental: the number declines too — <b>с двумя́</b> ру́сскими друзья́ми."},{"type":"choice","q":"“by three o'clock” —","opts":["к трём часа́м","к три часа́","к трёх часо́в"],"answer":0,"explain":"к + dative: <b>к трём часа́м</b>. The number bends into the dative like the noun."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the noun form. Stress marks optional.","q":"Genitive plural after 5+: «пять ___» (books)","accept":["книг","книг."],"placeholder":"пять…","explain":"5 and up → genitive plural: пять <b>книг</b>."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Counting people in a bunch — «дво́е, тро́е»","title":"<em>Дво́е друзе́й, тро́е су́ток</em> — the collective numbers","paras":["Alongside два, три, четы́ре, Russian has a second little family for counting people <b>as a group</b>: <b>дво́е, тро́е, че́тверо, пя́теро, ше́стеро, се́меро</b> (2 through 7). They take the <b>genitive plural</b>: <b>дво́е друзе́й</b> (two friends), <b>тро́е студе́нтов</b> (three students), <b>пя́теро дете́й</b> (five children).","Use them for groups that are <b>male or mixed</b>, and especially for <b>дети, лю́ди, ребя́та</b>: <b>тро́е дете́й</b>, <b>че́тверо друзе́й</b>. The trap: <b>not for women</b> — “two (female) students” is <b>две студе́нтки</b>, never «дво́е студе́нток».","But there's one place a collective is the <b>only</b> option: nouns that live only in the plural — <b>су́тки</b> (a 24-hour day), <b>но́жницы</b> (scissors), <b>часы́</b> (a clock). You can't say «три су́тки», so you say <b>тро́е су́ток</b> (three days-and-nights), <b>дво́е но́жниц</b> (two pairs of scissors)."],"audio":"Alongside dva, tri, chetyre, Russian has a second little family for counting people as a group: dvoye, troye, chetvero, pyatero, shestero, semero, two through seven. They take the genitive plural: dvoye druzey, two friends; troye studentov, three students; pyatero detey, five children. Use them for groups that are male or mixed, and especially for deti, lyudi, rebyata: troye detey, chetvero druzey. The trap: not for women — 'two female students' is dve studentki, never dvoye studentok. But there's one place a collective is the only option: nouns that live only in the plural — sutki, a 24-hour day; nozhnitsy, scissors; chasy, a clock. You can't say 'tri sutki', so you say troye sutok, three days-and-nights; dvoye nozhnits, two pairs of scissors.","table":{"rows":[["дво́е друзе́й / тро́е студе́нтов <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"двое друзей, трое студентов\">🔊</button>","two friends / three students (male or mixed)"],["тро́е дете́й, че́тверо ребя́т <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"трое детей, четверо ребят\">🔊</button>","three children, four kids"],["тро́е су́ток (not «три су́тки») <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"трое суток\">🔊</button>","three days-and-nights (plural-only noun)"],["две студе́нтки (NOT дво́е) <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"две студентки\">🔊</button>","two (female) students → plain «две»"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"Three of my friends are translators too.","en":"<b>Тро́е</b> мои́х друзе́й то́же перево́дчики.","say":"Трое моих друзей тоже переводчики."},{"ru":"The job took three whole days (day and night).","en":"Рабо́та заняла́ <b>тро́е су́ток</b>.","say":"Работа заняла трое суток."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"дво́е сестёр","right":"две сестры́","why":"Collectives don't count women → <b>две сестры́</b> (plain feminine). Keep дво́е/тро́е for male or mixed groups and for дети / лю́ди."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"“three students (a mixed group)” —","opts":["три студе́нтов","тро́е студе́нтов","тро́е студе́нтки"],"answer":1,"explain":"A mixed group of people → collective + genitive plural: <b>тро́е студе́нтов</b>. три wants студе́нта; студе́нтки are women (→ три студе́нтки)."},{"type":"choice","q":"“two pairs of scissors” (plural-only noun) —","opts":["две но́жницы","два но́жниц","дво́е но́жниц"],"answer":2,"explain":"но́жницы has no singular, so a collective is required: <b>дво́е но́жниц</b>."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the collective. Stress marks optional.","q":"“The trip took ___ days-and-nights.” (three, with су́тки): «Пое́здка заняла́ ___ су́ток.»","accept":["трое","трое."],"placeholder":"…","explain":"Plural-only су́тки forces the collective: <b>тро́е су́ток</b>."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Both — «о́ба» or «о́бе»?","title":"<em>О́ба бра́та</em>, <em>о́бе сестры́</em> — “both” has a gender","paras":["“Both” splits by gender. <b>о́ба</b> for masculine and neuter, <b>о́бе</b> for feminine: <b>о́ба бра́та</b> (both brothers), <b>о́ба окна́</b> (both windows), <b>о́бе сестры́</b> (both sisters). Like два/две, it takes the <b>genitive singular</b> of the noun: о́ба бра́т<b>а</b>, о́бе сестр<b>ы́</b>.","And it declines. Masculine/neuter keeps an <b>-о-</b> stem: <b>обо́их, обо́им, обо́ими</b>. Feminine keeps an <b>-е-</b> stem: <b>обе́их, обе́им, обе́ими</b>. «Я говори́ла с <b>обо́ими</b> бра́тьями» (m), «Я говори́ла с <b>обе́ими</b> подру́гами» (f).","Let the vowel do the work: an <b>о</b> in обо́- points at men or things, an <b>е</b> in обе́- points at women. Get the gender first, then decline."],"audio":"'Both' splits by gender. Oba for masculine and neuter, obe for feminine: oba brata, both brothers; oba okna, both windows; obe sestry, both sisters. Like dva and dve, it takes the genitive singular of the noun: oba brata, obe sestry. And it declines. Masculine and neuter keep an -o- stem: oboikh, oboim, oboimi. Feminine keeps an -e- stem: obeikh, obeim, obeimi. Ya govorila s oboimi bratyami, masculine; ya govorila s obeimi podrugami, feminine. Let the vowel do the work: an o in obo- points at men or things, an e in obe- points at women. Get the gender first, then decline.","table":{"rows":[["о́ба бра́та (m) / о́бе сестры́ (f) <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"оба брата, обе сестры\">🔊</button>","both brothers / both sisters"],["с обо́ими друзья́ми (m, instr.) <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"с обоими друзьями\">🔊</button>","with both friends (masc.)"],["с обе́ими подру́гами (f, instr.) <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"с обеими подругами\">🔊</button>","with both friends (fem.)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Both brothers work in the same firm.","en":"<b>О́ба</b> бра́та рабо́тают в одно́й фи́рме.","say":"Оба брата работают в одной фирме."},{"ru":"I spoke with both sisters.","en":"Я поговори́ла с <b>обе́ими</b> сёстрами.","say":"Я поговорила с обеими сёстрами."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"о́ба сестры́","right":"о́бе сестры́","why":"Sisters are feminine → <b>о́бе</b> (е-stem). Keep <b>о́ба</b> for masculine/neuter (о́ба бра́та, о́ба окна́)."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“both sisters” —","opts":["о́бе сестры́","о́ба сестры́","о́бе сёстры"],"answer":0,"explain":"Feminine → <b>о́бе</b>, and the noun goes genitive singular: о́бе сестр<b>ы́</b>."},{"type":"choice","q":"“with both brothers” (instrumental) —","opts":["с обе́ими бра́тьями","с о́ба бра́тьями","с обо́ими бра́тьями"],"answer":2,"explain":"Masculine → о-stem, instrumental <b>обо́ими</b>: с обо́ими бра́тьями."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“Both windows are open.”</b> (окно́ is neuter)","want":"О́ба окна́ откры́ты","answer":"О́ба окна́ откры́ты"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · One and a half, and parts — «полтора́», «две тре́тьих»","title":"<em>Полтора́ часа́</em>, <em>полторы́ неде́ли</em>, <em>две тре́тьих</em>","paras":["“One and a half” also has a gender — just two forms. <b>полтора́</b> for masculine/neuter, <b>полторы́</b> for feminine, each taking the <b>genitive singular</b>: <b>полтора́ часа́</b> (an hour and a half), <b>полтора́ дня</b>, <b>полторы́ неде́ли</b> (a week and a half), <b>полторы́ ты́сячи</b>. (In the oblique cases both flatten to <b>полу́тора</b>.)","For other fractions, read them as “number + share”: the top is a plain number, the bottom an ordinal. <b>полови́на</b> = ½, <b>треть</b> = ⅓, <b>че́тверть</b> = ¼. Spelled out: <b>одна́ втора́я</b> (½), <b>одна́ тре́тья</b> (⅓), and with more than one on top, the bottom goes plural-genitive: <b>две тре́тьих</b> (⅔), <b>три четвёртых</b> (¾).","Whatever the fraction, the thing measured stays <b>genitive singular</b>: <b>две тре́тьих кни́ги</b> (two-thirds of the book), <b>полтора́ ли́тра</b> (a litre and a half)."],"audio":"'One and a half' also has a gender — just two forms. Poltora for masculine and neuter, poltory for feminine, each taking the genitive singular: poltora chasa, an hour and a half; poltora dnya; poltory nedeli, a week and a half; poltory tysyachi. In the oblique cases both flatten to polutora. For other fractions, read them as 'number plus share': the top is a plain number, the bottom an ordinal. Polovina is a half, tret a third, chetvert a quarter. Spelled out: odna vtoraya, one half; odna tretya, one third; and with more than one on top, the bottom goes plural-genitive: dve tretikh, two thirds; tri chetvyortykh, three quarters. Whatever the fraction, the thing measured stays genitive singular: dve tretikh knigi, two-thirds of the book; poltora litra, a litre and a half.","table":{"rows":[["полтора́ часа́ (m) / полторы́ неде́ли (f) <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"полтора часа, полторы недели\">🔊</button>","an hour and a half / a week and a half"],["полови́на, треть, че́тверть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"половина, треть, четверть\">🔊</button>","a half, a third, a quarter"],["две тре́тьих кни́ги <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"две третьих книги\">🔊</button>","two-thirds of the book"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"I've already translated half the book.","en":"Я уже́ перевела́ <b>полови́ну</b> кни́ги.","say":"Я уже перевела половину книги."},{"ru":"The meeting lasted an hour and a half.","en":"Встре́ча дли́лась <b>полтора́ часа́</b>.","say":"Встреча длилась полтора часа."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"полтора́ неде́ли","right":"полторы́ неде́ли","why":"неде́ля is feminine → <b>полторы́</b>. Keep полтора́ for masculine/neuter (полтора́ часа́, полтора́ дня)."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“a week and a half” (неде́ля is feminine) —","opts":["полторы́ неде́ли","полтора́ неде́ли","полторы́ неде́ль"],"answer":0,"explain":"Feminine → <b>полторы́</b> + genitive singular неде́ли."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the fraction. Stress marks optional.","q":"“___ of the work is already done.” (⅔): «___ рабо́ты уже́ гото́во.»","accept":["две третьих","две трети"],"placeholder":"… рабо́ты","explain":"⅔ = <b>две тре́тьих</b> (top number + plural-genitive ordinal); the noun stays genitive singular (рабо́ты). «две тре́ти» is the everyday spoken form."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Встре́ча дли́лась полтора́ часа́.","sub":"“The meeting lasted an hour and a half” — полтора́ + genitive singular","want":"встреча длилась полтора часа"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Roughly how many — the “about” tricks","title":"<em>Челове́к де́сять</em> — put the noun first and it means “about”","paras":["The neatest trick in Russian: to say “about ten”, just <b>swap the order</b>. <b>де́сять челове́к</b> = exactly ten people; <b>челове́к де́сять</b> = <b>about</b> ten. Noun before number = approximately: <b>часа́ два</b> (about two hours), <b>дней пять</b> (about five days), <b>рубле́й сто</b> (about a hundred roubles).","The word-tools: <b>о́коло</b> + genitive (“around”): <b>о́коло десяти́ челове́к</b>; <b>приме́рно</b> / <b>приблизи́тельно</b> + the normal count: <b>приме́рно де́сять челове́к</b>. And for “more/less than”: <b>бо́лее</b>, <b>ме́нее</b>, <b>свы́ше</b> — all + genitive: <b>свы́ше ста</b> челове́к (over a hundred), <b>ме́нее го́да</b> (less than a year).","So Emma has three ways to hedge a number: flip it (<b>челове́к де́сять</b>), reach for <b>о́коло</b> / <b>приме́рно</b>, or bound it with <b>свы́ше</b> / <b>бо́лее</b> / <b>ме́нее</b>. Written Russian loves the last set; speech loves the flip."],"audio":"The neatest trick in Russian: to say 'about ten', just swap the order. Desyat chelovek is exactly ten people; chelovek desyat is about ten. Noun before number means approximately: chasa dva, about two hours; dney pyat, about five days; rubley sto, about a hundred roubles. The word-tools: okolo plus genitive, 'around': okolo desyati chelovek; primerno or priblizitelno plus the normal count: primerno desyat chelovek. And for 'more or less than': boleye, meneye, svyshe — all plus genitive: svyshe sta chelovek, over a hundred; meneye goda, less than a year. So Emma has three ways to hedge a number: flip it, chelovek desyat; reach for okolo or primerno; or bound it with svyshe, boleye, meneye. Written Russian loves the last set; speech loves the flip.","table":{"rows":[["де́сять челове́к → челове́к де́сять <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"десять человек, человек десять\">🔊</button>","ten people → about ten (inversion)"],["о́коло десяти́ челове́к <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"около десяти человек\">🔊</button>","around ten people (о́коло + gen.)"],["свы́ше ста, ме́нее го́да <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"свыше ста, менее года\">🔊</button>","over a hundred, less than a year"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"There'll be about ten of us at the party.","en":"На пра́зднике нас бу́дет <b>челове́к де́сять</b>.","say":"На празднике нас будет человек десять."},{"ru":"Over a hundred people came to the lecture.","en":"На ле́кцию пришло́ <b>свы́ше ста</b> челове́к.","say":"На лекцию пришло свыше ста человек."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"о́коло де́сять челове́к","right":"о́коло десяти́ челове́к","why":"<b>о́коло</b> takes the <b>genitive</b>: о́коло десят<b>и́</b> челове́к. The number itself declines."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“There'll be about ten of us.” (approximate by inversion) —","opts":["Нас бу́дет де́сять челове́к.","Нас бу́дет о́коло де́сять челове́к.","Нас бу́дет челове́к де́сять."],"answer":2,"explain":"Noun before number = “about”: <b>челове́к де́сять</b>. Option 1 is exactly ten; option 2 needs the genitive (о́коло десяти́)."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which means “over a hundred people”?","opts":["свы́ше ста челове́к","свы́ше сто челове́к","о́коло сто челове́к"],"answer":0,"explain":"свы́ше + genitive: <b>свы́ше ста</b> челове́к. Both свы́ше and о́коло take the genitive (ста, not сто)."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the “around” word. Stress marks optional.","q":"Fill (+ genitive): «На встре́че бы́ло ___ двадцати́ челове́к.» (around)","accept":["около","около."],"placeholder":"…","explain":"“around” + genitive → <b>о́коло</b> двадцати́ челове́к."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Emma is planning a Saturday get-together and counts heads with Ле́на at the café. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Ско́лько госте́й бу́дет в суббо́ту? Я гото́влю сто́лик.","ru":"How many guests will there be on Saturday? I'm setting a table."},{"who":"B","en":"Нас бу́дет челове́к де́сять, то́чно не зна́ю.","ru":"There'll be about ten of us, I don't know exactly."},{"who":"A","en":"А из твои́х ста́рых друзе́й кто придёт?","ru":"And which of your old friends will come?"},{"who":"B","en":"Тро́е: Ива́н, Макси́м и Ка́тя. О́ба бра́та обеща́ли быть.","ru":"Three: Ivan, Maxim and Katya. Both brothers promised to be there."},{"who":"A","en":"Зна́чит, о́бе на́ши компа́нии встре́тятся!","ru":"So both our groups will meet!"},{"who":"B","en":"Да. Я гото́вила сала́т почти́ полтора́ часа́.","ru":"Yes. I made the salad for almost an hour and a half."},{"who":"A","en":"Полторы́ неде́ли наза́д ты не уме́ла гото́вить ру́сские блю́да!","ru":"A week and a half ago you couldn't cook Russian dishes!"},{"who":"B","en":"Учу́сь! Уже́ две тре́тьих реце́птов из кни́ги попро́бовала.","ru":"I'm learning! I've already tried two-thirds of the recipes in the book."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve number-words for real situations — groups, pairs, halves and hedges. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"дво́е","ru":"two (people, collective)","ex":"У меня́ дво́е дете́й."},{"en":"тро́е","ru":"three (people / plural-only)","ex":"Мы прожда́ли тро́е су́ток."},{"en":"че́тверо","ru":"four (people, collective)","ex":"В семье́ че́тверо дете́й."},{"en":"свы́ше","ru":"over, more than (+ gen.)","ex":"На ле́кции бы́ло свы́ше ста челове́к."},{"en":"о́ба","ru":"both (masculine / neuter)","ex":"О́ба сы́на живу́т в Москве́."},{"en":"о́бе","ru":"both (feminine)","ex":"О́бе до́чери учи́лись здесь."},{"en":"полтора́","ru":"one and a half (m / n)","ex":"Я жду уже́ полтора́ часа́."},{"en":"полторы́","ru":"one and a half (feminine)","ex":"Оста́лось полторы́ неде́ли."},{"en":"полови́на","ru":"a half","ex":"Полови́на кни́ги переведена́."},{"en":"треть","ru":"a third","ex":"Треть студе́нтов не пришла́."},{"en":"че́тверть","ru":"a quarter","ex":"Прошла́ че́тверть ве́ка."},{"en":"о́коло","ru":"about, around (+ gen.)","ex":"Ждём о́коло часа́."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the number phrase to its meaning.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["тро́е су́ток","three days and nights"],["о́ба бра́та","both brothers"],["полтора́ часа́","an hour and a half"],["челове́к де́сять","about ten people"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — collectives, both, halves and “about”. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"“three children” —","opts":["три дете́й","тро́е дете́й","тро́е де́ти"],"answer":1},{"q":"“two (female) students” —","opts":["две студе́нтки","дво́е студе́нток","дво́е студе́нтки"],"answer":0},{"q":"“both sisters” —","opts":["о́ба сестры́","о́бе сёстры","о́бе сестры́"],"answer":2},{"q":"“a week and a half” —","opts":["полторы́ неде́ли","полтора́ неде́ли","полторы́ неде́ль"],"answer":0},{"q":"“about ten people” (inversion) —","opts":["де́сять челове́к","челове́к де́сять","о́коло де́сять челове́к"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's note the morning after her gathering — the numbers tell the whole story. Read it aloud.","title":"На́ша встре́ча","sentences":["В суббо́ту у меня́ собрало́сь челове́к де́сять.","Тро́е ста́рых друзе́й прие́хали из Ло́ндона.","О́ба бра́та, Ива́н и Макси́м, то́же пришли́.","Я гото́вила полтора́ часа́ и о́чень уста́ла.","Но к ве́черу уже́ полови́на сала́тов была́ съе́дена.","Свы́ше двадцати́ блюд — и ни одного́ не оста́лось!"],"translation":"On Saturday about ten people gathered at my place. Three old friends came from London. Both brothers, Ivan and Maxim, came too. I cooked for an hour and a half and got very tired. But by evening half the salads had been eaten. Over twenty dishes — and not a single one was left!"},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — put real numbers into a real scene.","prompt":"Describe a gathering, a family, or a project in 3–4 sentences using numbers: a collective (дво́е / тро́е + genitive plural), о́ба or о́бе for a pair, полтора́ / полторы́ for a time, and one approximate (челове́к де́сять or о́коло …).","hint":"дво́е/тро́е for a male-or-mixed group, о́ба (m) / о́бе (f) for “both”, полтора́ (m) / полторы́ (f) for “one and a half”, and flip noun-before-number for “about” 🙂","example":"У нас в семье́ че́тверо: о́ба роди́теля и мы с бра́том. В суббо́ту собрало́сь челове́к де́сять друзе́й. Я гото́вила полтора́ часа́, и к ве́черу две тре́тьих еды́ уже́ съе́ли."}},"20":{"day":20,"week":"03","themeRu":"Which register?","themeEn":"Сти́ль: кни́жный и разгово́рный","intro":"«В связи́ с ва́шим запро́сом сообща́ю…» — Emma wrote that this morning to a ministry, then texted Ка́тя «зна́чит, я опа́здываю, не серди́сь!». Same person, two completely different Russians. That gap is <b>register</b>, and today you learn to cross it on purpose: the bookish connectors that build an essay (<b>таки́м о́бразом, тем не ме́нее</b>) and the tiny particles that colour speech (<b>же, ведь, ра́зве, неуже́ли</b>). Knowing which to reach for is one of the last, most native-sounding steps.","introAudio":"V svyazi s vashim zaprosom soobshchayu — Emma wrote that this morning to a ministry, then texted Katya 'znachit, ya opazdyvayu, ne serdis!'. Same person, two completely different Russians. That gap is register, and today you learn to cross it on purpose: the bookish connectors that build an essay, takim obrazom, tem ne meneye, and the tiny particles that colour speech, zhe, ved, razve, neuzheli. Knowing which to reach for is one of the last, most native-sounding steps.","goals":["Pick the register on purpose — <b>кни́жно-официа́льный</b>, <b>нейтра́льный</b>, <b>разгово́рный</b> — for essay, note or chat","Join written argument with <b>таки́м о́бразом, тем не ме́нее, что каса́ется, в связи́ с</b>","Colour speech with the nuance particles <b>же</b> (insistence), <b>ведь</b> (shared ground), <b>-то</b> (spotlight) — never «же = also»","Ask with attitude: <b>ра́зве / неуже́ли</b> for surprise, plain <b>ли</b> for a neutral or indirect yes/no"],"learned":["Chose the register on purpose across bookish, neutral and colloquial","Joined written argument with <b>таки́м о́бразом, тем не ме́нее, что каса́ется, в связи́ с</b>","Coloured speech with <b>же, ведь, -то</b> — keeping же apart from «also» (то́же)","Asked with <b>ра́зве / неуже́ли</b> for surprise and <b>ли</b> for a neutral/indirect yes/no"],"review":{"intro":"A minute back on this week's clause-builders — a reason, a concession, a purpose — before we dress them in a register.","introAudio":"A minute back on this week's clause-builders — a reason, a concession, a purpose — before we dress them in a register.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. This is yesterday-and-before.","q":"“Since it's late, I'm staying home.” (a front-loaded reason)","opts":["Поско́льку по́здно, я остаю́сь до́ма.","Так что по́здно, я остаю́сь до́ма.","Что́бы по́здно, я остаю́сь до́ма."],"answer":0,"explain":"A leading reason → <b>поско́льку</b>. так что states a result; что́бы marks a purpose."},{"type":"choice","q":"“Despite the rain, we went out.” (despite + noun)","opts":["Хотя́ дождь, мы вы́шли.","Несмотря́ на дождь, мы вы́шли.","Поско́льку дождь, мы вы́шли."],"answer":1,"explain":"“despite + noun” → <b>несмотря́ на</b> + accusative. хотя́ needs a whole clause; поско́льку means “since”."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“I'm learning Russian in order to read novels.”</b>","want":"Я учу́ ру́сский, что́бы чита́ть рома́ны","answer":"Я учу́ ру́сский, что́бы чита́ть рома́ны"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · One thought, three registers","title":"Кни́жный, нейтра́льный, разгово́рный — pick the register","paras":["Every language has levels, but Russian keeps them unusually distinct, and B2 is where you start choosing on purpose. Three bands: <b>кни́жно-официа́льный</b> (bookish/official — essays, documents, news), <b>нейтра́льный</b> (neutral — the safe default), and <b>разгово́рный</b> (colloquial — friends, chat).","The same idea dresses three ways. “Therefore”: bookish <b>сле́довательно</b> / <b>таки́м о́бразом</b>, neutral <b>поэ́тому</b>, colloquial <b>зна́чит</b>. “A lot”: bookish <b>значи́тельное коли́чество</b>, neutral <b>мно́го</b>, colloquial <b>ку́ча</b>. Wear the wrong one and you sound odd — a «ку́ча» in an essay, a «сле́довательно» over coffee.","Emma lives across all three: her document translations demand the bookish band, her notes sit in neutral, her texts to Ка́тя are pure colloquial. Today you learn the connectors that mark the bookish end and the particles that colour the colloquial one — and when to reach for each."],"audio":"Every language has levels, but Russian keeps them unusually distinct, and B2 is where you start choosing on purpose. Three bands: knizhno-ofitsialny, bookish and official — essays, documents, news; neitralny, neutral — the safe default; and razgovorny, colloquial — friends, chat. The same idea dresses three ways. 'Therefore': bookish sledovatelno or takim obrazom, neutral poetomu, colloquial znachit. 'A lot': bookish znachitelnoye kolichestvo, neutral mnogo, colloquial kucha. Wear the wrong one and you sound odd — a kucha in an essay, a sledovatelno over coffee. Emma lives across all three: her document translations demand the bookish band, her notes sit in neutral, her texts to Katya are pure colloquial. Today you learn the connectors that mark the bookish end and the particles that colour the colloquial one — and when to reach for each.","table":{"rows":[["сле́довательно / поэ́тому / зна́чит <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"следовательно, поэтому, значит\">🔊</button>","“therefore”: bookish · neutral · colloquial"],["тем не ме́нее / но всё-та́ки <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"тем не менее, но всё-таки\">🔊</button>","“nevertheless”: bookish vs colloquial"],["в связи́ с боле́знью / из-за боле́зни <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в связи с болезнью, из-за болезни\">🔊</button>","“due to illness”: official vs neutral"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"In a report: “Thus, the goal is achieved.”","en":"В отчёте: «<b>Таки́м о́бразом</b>, цель дости́гнута».","say":"В отчёте: таким образом, цель достигнута."},{"ru":"Over coffee: “So it worked out!”","en":"За ко́фе: «<b>Зна́чит</b>, всё получи́лось!»","say":"За кофе: значит, всё получилось!"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"В официа́льном письме́: «ку́ча дел».","right":"В официа́льном письме́: «мно́го дел».","why":"<b>ку́ча</b> (“a heap”) is colloquial — wrong in a formal letter. Neutral <b>мно́го</b> fits everywhere; bookish would be <b>значи́тельное коли́чество</b>."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"A formal report: “Therefore, the deadline changes.” Best fit:","opts":["Зна́чит, срок меня́ется.","Таки́м о́бразом, срок меня́ется.","Ну, срок меня́ется."],"answer":1,"explain":"Formal → bookish <b>таки́м о́бразом</b>. зна́чит and ну are colloquial."},{"type":"choice","q":"Texting a friend. Which sounds natural?","opts":["Ввиду́ вышеска́занного, я опозда́ю.","Сле́довательно, я опозда́ю.","Зна́чит, я опозда́ю."],"answer":2,"explain":"To a friend → colloquial <b>зна́чит</b>. The other two are official/bookish and sound stiff in a text."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks optional.","q":"The neutral “a lot” that fits any register: «У меня́ ___ рабо́ты.»","accept":["много","много."],"placeholder":"…","explain":"Neutral <b>мно́го</b> is safe everywhere — unlike colloquial ку́ча or bookish значи́тельное коли́чество."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The connectors of written argument","title":"<em>Таки́м о́бразом, тем не ме́нее, что каса́ется</em>","paras":["Written Russian argues with a set of fixed connectors — learn them as whole phrases. <b>таки́м о́бразом</b> = “thus / in this way” (drawing a conclusion). <b>тем не ме́нее</b> = “nevertheless / and yet” (conceding, then pushing on). <b>что каса́ется</b> + genitive = “as for / regarding”: <b>что каса́ется це́ны</b>… (as for the price…).","Two more carry Emma's bureaucracy: <b>в связи́ с</b> + instrumental = “in connection with / due to” (<b>в связи́ с боле́знью</b> — owing to illness), and the essay's balancing act <b>с одно́й стороны́… с друго́й стороны́</b> = “on the one hand… on the other”.","These are the joints of an essay or an official letter. They almost always sit at the <b>front</b> of their sentence, set off by a comma: «<b>Тем не ме́нее</b>, вы́вод я́сен.» Sprinkle them into speech and you'll sound like you're reading aloud — so keep them for the page."],"audio":"Written Russian argues with a set of fixed connectors — learn them as whole phrases. Takim obrazom is 'thus, in this way', drawing a conclusion. Tem ne meneye is 'nevertheless, and yet', conceding then pushing on. Chto kasayetsya plus genitive is 'as for, regarding': chto kasayetsya tseny, as for the price. Two more carry Emma's bureaucracy: v svyazi s plus instrumental, 'in connection with, due to' — v svyazi s boleznyu, owing to illness; and the essay's balancing act s odnoy storony, s drugoy storony, 'on the one hand, on the other'. These are the joints of an essay or an official letter. They almost always sit at the front of their sentence, set off by a comma: tem ne meneye, vyvod yasen. Sprinkle them into speech and you'll sound like you're reading aloud — so keep them for the page.","table":{"rows":[["Таки́м о́бразом, зада́ча решена́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Таким образом, задача решена\">🔊</button>","Thus, the task is solved."],["Тем не ме́нее, я согла́сна. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Тем не менее, я согласна\">🔊</button>","Nevertheless, I agree."],["Что каса́ется це́ны, она́ высока́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Что касается цены, она высока\">🔊</button>","As for the price, it's high."],["В связи́ с пого́дой рейс отмени́ли. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"В связи с погодой рейс отменили\">🔊</button>","Due to the weather, the flight was cancelled."]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"As for the deadline, we'll discuss it tomorrow.","en":"<b>Что каса́ется</b> сро́ка, обсу́дим его́ за́втра.","say":"Что касается срока, обсудим его завтра."},{"ru":"The text is hard; nevertheless, I'll finish on time.","en":"Текст тру́дный; <b>тем не ме́нее</b>, я зако́нчу в срок.","say":"Текст трудный, тем не менее, я закончу в срок."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Что каса́ется це́на…","right":"Что каса́ется це́ны…","why":"<b>что каса́ется</b> takes the <b>genitive</b>: что каса́ется це́ны. Think of it as “as for [of] the price”."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“As for the price, it's too high.” —","opts":["Что каса́ется це́ны, она́ сли́шком высока́.","Что каса́ется цена́, она́ сли́шком высока́.","Про це́ну, она́ сли́шком высока́."],"answer":0,"explain":"что каса́ется + <b>genitive</b>: <b>це́ны</b>. Option 3 (про + accusative) is colloquial, not the bookish connector."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“Thus, the goal is achieved.”</b> (bookish opener)","want":"Таки́м о́бразом, цель дости́гнута","answer":"Таки́м о́бразом, цель дости́гнута"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which is the official “due to illness”?","opts":["из-за боле́знь","по причи́не боле́знь","в связи́ с боле́знью"],"answer":2,"explain":"Official → <b>в связи́ с</b> + instrumental: в связи́ с боле́знью. (Neutral would be из-за боле́зни — genitive.)"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The little words that colour speech","title":"<em>Же, ведь, -то</em> — nuance, not translation","paras":["Colloquial Russian is full of tiny particles that carry <b>tone</b> rather than meaning — they're almost untranslatable, which is exactly why they mark a native. <b>же</b> adds insistence or mild contrast: «Ты <b>же</b> зна́ешь!» (But you <em>do</em> know! / You know that!), «Что <b>же</b> де́лать?» (So what on earth do we do?). Warning: <b>же</b> is <b>not</b> “also” — that's то́же/та́кже. Don't let the look-alike fool you.","<b>ведь</b> appeals to something you both already know — “after all, you see, isn't that so”: «<b>Ведь</b> э́то пра́вда.» (It's true, isn't it / after all.) It softens a claim into shared ground.","<b>-то</b> (a hyphenated particle) quietly spotlights the word it clings to: «Я-<b>то</b> зна́ю» (I, for my part, know), «Сего́дня-<b>то</b> он придёт» (Today, at least, he'll come). All three live in speech and warm, informal writing — never in an official letter."],"audio":"Colloquial Russian is full of tiny particles that carry tone rather than meaning — they're almost untranslatable, which is exactly why they mark a native. Zhe adds insistence or mild contrast: ty zhe znayesh, but you DO know; chto zhe delat, so what on earth do we do? Warning: zhe is not 'also' — that's tozhe or takzhe. Don't let the look-alike fool you. Ved appeals to something you both already know — 'after all, you see, isn't that so': ved eto pravda, it's true, isn't it. It softens a claim into shared ground. The hyphenated particle -to quietly spotlights the word it clings to: ya-to znayu, I for my part know; segodnya-to on pridyot, today at least he'll come. All three live in speech and warm, informal writing — never in an official letter.","table":{"rows":[["Ты же зна́ешь! <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Ты же знаешь!\">🔊</button>","But you DO know! (же = insistence)"],["Ведь э́то пра́вда. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Ведь это правда\">🔊</button>","It's true, isn't it. (ведь = shared ground)"],["Я-то зна́ю. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я-то знаю\">🔊</button>","I, for one, know. (-то = spotlight)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"You know Russian yourself, after all!","en":"Ты <b>же</b> сама́ зна́ешь ру́сский!","say":"Ты же сама знаешь русский!"},{"ru":"Come on, it's not far — you've been there.","en":"Да <b>ведь</b> э́то недалеко́, ты там был.","say":"Да ведь это недалеко, ты там был."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я же хочу́ ко́фе. (for “I also want coffee”)","right":"Я то́же хочу́ ко́фе.","why":"For “also / too” use <b>то́же</b>. <b>же</b> means insistence (“I DO want…”), not addition — a classic English-speaker mix-up."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“But you know that!” (insistence) —","opts":["Ты то́же зна́ешь э́то!","Ты же зна́ешь э́то!","Ты ли зна́ешь э́то!"],"answer":1,"explain":"Insistence → <b>же</b>: Ты же зна́ешь! то́же = “also” (different meaning); ли is a yes/no question particle."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which particle leans on shared knowledge (“after all”)?","opts":["ведь","же","-то"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>ведь</b> = “after all / you see”, resting on common ground. же = insistence; -то = a spotlight."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks optional.","q":"The particle for “also/too” (the false-friend trap for же): «Я ___ хочу́ пойти́.»","accept":["тоже","тоже."],"placeholder":"…","explain":"“also” = <b>то́же</b>, never же. же would mean “I DO want…”, not “I too”."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Questions with an attitude","title":"<em>Ра́зве, неуже́ли</em> (disbelief) vs plain «ли»","paras":["Not every question is neutral. <b>ра́зве</b> and <b>неуже́ли</b> load a question with <b>surprise or doubt</b> — roughly “really?! / you don't say / surely not”. «<b>Ра́зве</b> ты не зна́л?» = “You mean you didn't know?” «<b>Неуже́ли</b> уже́ по́здно?» = “Can it really be that late?!” They are <b>not</b> the way to ask a plain question — for “Did you know?” you just say «Ты знал?».","<b>ра́зве</b> leans toward “but I thought otherwise”; <b>неуже́ли</b> is stronger, almost incredulous. Both usually open the sentence.","The neutral yes/no particle is <b>ли</b> — a touch formal, and the one you already use for <b>indirect</b> questions: «Я не зна́ю, <b>придёт ли</b> он.» (I don't know whether he'll come.) In a direct question it sounds bookish-polite: «Не зна́ете <b>ли</b> вы, где вокза́л?» So: <b>ра́зве / неуже́ли</b> for astonishment, <b>ли</b> for a neutral or indirect yes/no."],"audio":"Not every question is neutral. Razve and neuzheli load a question with surprise or doubt — roughly 'really?! you don't say, surely not'. Razve ty ne znal? — 'You mean you didn't know?' Neuzheli uzhe pozdno? — 'Can it really be that late?!' They are not the way to ask a plain question — for 'Did you know?' you just say ty znal? Razve leans toward 'but I thought otherwise'; neuzheli is stronger, almost incredulous. Both usually open the sentence. The neutral yes-no particle is li — a touch formal, and the one you already use for indirect questions: ya ne znayu, pridyot li on, I don't know whether he'll come. In a direct question it sounds bookish-polite: ne znayete li vy, gde vokzal? So: razve or neuzheli for astonishment, li for a neutral or indirect yes-no.","table":{"rows":[["Ра́зве ты не зна́л? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Разве ты не знал?\">🔊</button>","You mean you didn't know? (doubt)"],["Неуже́ли э́то пра́вда? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Неужели это правда?\">🔊</button>","Can it really be true?! (disbelief)"],["Я не зна́ю, придёт ли он. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я не знаю, придёт ли он\">🔊</button>","I don't know whether he'll come. (indirect)"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"Can it really be that you translated the whole book?!","en":"<b>Неуже́ли</b> ты перевела́ всю кни́гу?!","say":"Неужели ты перевела всю книгу?!"},{"ru":"You mean this café is already closed?","en":"<b>Ра́зве</b> э́то кафе́ уже́ закры́то?","say":"Разве это кафе уже закрыто?"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Неуже́ли вы хоти́те ко́фе? (as a plain offer)","right":"Вы хоти́те ко́фе?","why":"A neutral offer is just «Вы хоти́те…?». <b>неуже́ли</b> would add shock (“You actually want coffee?!”) — save it for genuine surprise."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“Surely you're not tired already?!” (disbelief) —","opts":["Ты уста́л?","Неуже́ли ты уже́ уста́л?","Уста́л ли ты?"],"answer":1,"explain":"Disbelief → <b>неуже́ли</b>. Option 1 is a plain question; option 3 (ли) is a neutral/indirect yes-no."},{"type":"choice","q":"Indirect yes/no: “I don't know whether he read it.” —","opts":["Я не зна́ю, ра́зве он прочита́л.","Я не зна́ю, неуже́ли он прочита́л.","Я не зна́ю, прочита́л ли он."],"answer":2,"explain":"Indirect yes/no → <b>ли</b>: прочита́л ли он. ра́зве / неуже́ли carry surprise and can't build an indirect question."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Неуже́ли уже́ по́здно?","sub":"“Can it really be that late?!” — let неуже́ли carry the surprise","want":"неужели уже поздно"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Emma shows Ка́тя a formal letter she's just written, and they compare it with how people actually talk. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Неуже́ли ты сама́ написа́ла тако́е официа́льное письмо́?","ru":"Did you really write such a formal letter yourself?!"},{"who":"B","en":"Да! «В связи́ с ва́шим запро́сом сообща́ю…» — зву́чит соли́дно, пра́вда?","ru":"Yes! “In connection with your request, I hereby inform…” — sounds solid, doesn't it?"},{"who":"A","en":"Ра́зве так говоря́т лю́ди? В жи́зни ты бы сказа́ла про́ще.","ru":"Do people really talk like that? In real life you'd say it more simply."},{"who":"B","en":"Коне́чно. На письме́ — «таки́м о́бразом», а в разгово́ре — «зна́чит».","ru":"Of course. In writing it's “thus”, in conversation it's “so”."},{"who":"A","en":"Ведь оди́н и тот же смысл мо́жно сказа́ть по-ра́зному.","ru":"After all, the same meaning can be said in different ways."},{"who":"B","en":"И́менно. Что каса́ется э́того письма́, оно́ для нача́льника, так что стиль кни́жный.","ru":"Exactly. As for this letter, it's for the boss, so the style is bookish."},{"who":"A","en":"А мне-то ты напиши́ про́сто: «Приве́т, как дела́?»","ru":"And to me just write simply: “Hi, how are you?”"},{"who":"B","en":"Договори́лись! Тебе́ же я пишу́ как подру́ге.","ru":"Deal! To you I write as to a friend, you know."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve register-markers — the bookish connectors and the colloquial particles. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"таки́м о́бразом","ru":"thus, in this way (bookish)","ex":"Таки́м о́бразом, вопро́с решён."},{"en":"тем не ме́нее","ru":"nevertheless, and yet (bookish)","ex":"Уста́ла, тем не ме́нее рабо́таю."},{"en":"что каса́ется","ru":"as for, regarding (+ gen.)","ex":"Что каса́ется це́ны, она́ высока́."},{"en":"в связи́ с","ru":"in connection with, due to (+ instr.)","ex":"В связи́ с боле́знью он ушёл."},{"en":"же","ru":"particle: insistence, contrast","ex":"Ты же обеща́л прийти́!"},{"en":"ведь","ru":"particle: after all, you see","ex":"Ведь мы друзья́, пра́вда?"},{"en":"-то","ru":"particle: spotlights the word","ex":"Я-то всё по́нял сра́зу."},{"en":"ра́зве","ru":"really?, surely not? (doubt)","ex":"Ра́зве уже́ де́вять часо́в?"},{"en":"неуже́ли","ru":"can it really be?! (disbelief)","ex":"Неуже́ли ты не слы́шал?"},{"en":"ли","ru":"whether; yes/no particle","ex":"Спроси́, гото́в ли отчёт."},{"en":"кни́жный","ru":"bookish (register)","ex":"Стиль сли́шком кни́жный."},{"en":"разгово́рный","ru":"colloquial (register)","ex":"Э́то разгово́рное сло́во."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the register-marker to its meaning.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["таки́м о́бразом","thus, in this way"],["тем не ме́нее","nevertheless"],["что каса́ется","as for, regarding"],["неуже́ли?","can it really be?!"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — registers, connectors and particles. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"A formal essay's “therefore” —","opts":["зна́чит","таки́м о́бразом","ну"],"answer":1},{"q":"“As for the price…” takes the genitive —","opts":["что каса́ется це́ны","что каса́ется цена́","что каса́ется це́ну"],"answer":0},{"q":"«же» in “Ты же зна́ешь!” means —","opts":["whether","also / too","you DO know (insistence)"],"answer":2},{"q":"A question of disbelief uses —","opts":["ли","неуже́ли","-то"],"answer":1},{"q":"Indirect yes/no “whether he'll come” —","opts":["ра́зве он придёт","неуже́ли он придёт","придёт ли он"],"answer":2}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's note to herself about the two Russians she now speaks. Read it aloud.","title":"Два языка́ в одно́м","sentences":["Ру́сский язы́к у меня́ как бы двойно́й.","На письме́ я пишу́: «таки́м о́бразом», «в связи́ с».","А в кафе́ с Ка́тей — «зна́чит», «ну», «ведь».","Ра́зве э́то не удиви́тельно — оди́н язы́к, два ми́ра?","Тем не ме́нее, тепе́рь я чу́вствую, где како́й стиль.","И э́то ведь и есть настоя́щий B2."],"translation":"My Russian is sort of double. On paper I write “thus”, “in connection with”. But in a café with Katya it's “so”, “well”, “you see”. Isn't it amazing — one language, two worlds? Nevertheless, I now feel which style goes where. And that, after all, is what real B2 is."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — say one thing twice, in two registers.","prompt":"Take one simple message (you're running late, you liked a book, you can't come). Write it in 3–4 sentences: once in a bookish/official register with a connector (таки́м о́бразом, в связи́ с, тем не ме́нее), and once colloquially with a particle (же, ведь, -то, зна́чит). Show the contrast.","hint":"Bookish: «В связи́ с… таки́м о́бразом…». Colloquial: «Зна́чит, я… ты же зна́ешь…» — same idea, different clothes 🙂","example":"В связи́ с боле́знью я, к сожале́нию, не смогу́ прийти́; тем не ме́нее, наде́юсь уви́деться на сле́дующей неде́ле. А Ка́те напишу́ про́ще: «Приве́т! Я заболе́ла, зна́чит, не приду́. Ты же не серди́шься?»"}},"21":{"day":21,"week":"03","themeRu":"Review 3","themeEn":"Повторе́ние 3","intro":"Three weeks in, and look at the range you've picked up: you can hide a subject behind «кто-то», stack two negatives the Russian way, bend an argument through cause and concession, count a crowd, and switch register mid-sentence. Nothing new today — just a victory lap through Week 3, watching the pieces click together the way they do in real Russian.","introAudio":"Three weeks in, and look at the range you've picked up: you can hide a subject behind kto-to, stack two negatives the Russian way, bend an argument through cause and concession, count a crowd, and switch register mid-sentence. Nothing new today — just a victory lap through Week 3, watching the pieces click together the way they do in real Russian.","goals":["Choose the pronoun: <b>-то</b> (real but unknown) vs <b>-нибу́дь</b> (any/hypothetical) vs <b>ко́е-</b> (known, unnamed)","Keep double negation intact — <b>никто́ ничего́ не зна́ет</b> — and use <b>не́чего</b> + infinitive","Argue with the full clause set: concession (<b>хотя́, несмотря́ на то что</b>) and cause/purpose/result (<b>поско́льку, что́бы, так что</b>)","Count at B2 (<b>дво́е, о́ба, полтора́, челове́к де́сять</b>) and switch register with connectors and particles"],"learned":["Chose <b>-то / -нибу́дь / ко́е-</b> by certainty and time","Kept double negation and used <b>не́чего</b> + infinitive","Argued with concession and cause/purpose/result clauses","Counted at B2 and switched register on purpose"],"review":{"intro":"Straight into it — a quick pull from across the week. You built all of this; no hints needed.","introAudio":"Straight into it — a quick pull from across the week. You built all of this; no hints needed.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. It's all Week-3 material.","q":"“Someone phoned while you were out.” (a real, specific event)","opts":["Кто-нибу́дь звони́л.","Кто-то звони́л.","Никто́ не звони́л."],"answer":1,"explain":"A real event that happened → <b>-то</b>: Кто-то звони́л. -нибу́дь would be hypothetical/any; никто́ negates it."},{"type":"choice","q":"“No one said anything.” —","opts":["Никто́ сказа́л что-то.","Никто́ ничего́ не сказа́л.","Кто-то ничего́ сказа́л."],"answer":1,"explain":"Double negation stays: <b>никто́ ничего́ не сказа́л</b> — keep the «не» on the verb."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks optional.","q":"“Both” for feminine (о́ба / о́бе): «___ сестры́ здесь.»","accept":["обе","обе."],"placeholder":"…","explain":"Feminine “both” → <b>о́бе</b>: о́бе сестры́."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Somebody, anybody, nobody","title":"<em>-то / -нибу́дь / ко́е-</em> and <em>ни- / не́-</em>","paras":["Two mirror systems from the start of the week. For the <b>indefinite</b> “some-”, the ending tells the story: <b>-то</b> = real but unknown (it happened: «Кто-то звони́л»); <b>-нибу́дь</b> = any at all, still open (future, question, request: «Позвони́ кому́-нибу́дь»); <b>ко́е-</b> = the speaker knows but won't say («Я ко́е-что узна́ла»).","For the <b>negative</b>, Russian doubles down — literally. <b>ни-</b> words demand a second «не» on the verb: «<b>Никто́ ничего́ не</b> зна́ет» — nobody knows anything, three negatives and all required. Drop the «не» and the sentence breaks.","The stressed <b>не́-</b> series is different: <b>не́чего, не́кого, не́когда</b> + infinitive means “there's nothing / no one / no time to —”, and takes <b>no</b> second «не»: «Мне <b>не́чего</b> сказа́ть» (I've nothing to say). A preposition splits it: «<b>не́ с кем</b> говори́ть» (no one to talk to)."],"audio":"Two mirror systems from the start of the week. For the indefinite 'some-', the ending tells the story: -to is real but unknown — it happened: kto-to zvonil. -nibud is any at all, still open — future, question, request: pozvoni komu-nibud. Koe- means the speaker knows but won't say: ya koe-chto uznala. For the negative, Russian doubles down, literally. Ni- words demand a second 'ne' on the verb: nikto nichego ne znayet — nobody knows anything, three negatives and all required. Drop the 'ne' and the sentence breaks. The stressed ne- series is different: nechego, nekogo, nekogda plus an infinitive means 'there's nothing, no one, no time to —', and takes no second 'ne': mne nechego skazat, I've nothing to say. A preposition splits it: ne s kem govorit, no one to talk to.","table":{"rows":[["Кто-то звони́л / Позвони́ кому́-нибу́дь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Кто-то звонил, позвони кому-нибудь\">🔊</button>","someone did / call anyone"],["Никто́ ничего́ не зна́ет <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Никто ничего не знает\">🔊</button>","nobody knows anything (double negation)"],["Мне не́чего де́лать <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Мне нечего делать\">🔊</button>","I have nothing to do (не́- + infinitive)"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"I've something to tell you (I know what).","en":"Я хочу́ тебе́ <b>ко́е-что</b> сказа́ть.","say":"Я хочу тебе кое-что сказать."},{"ru":"There's no one here to help me.","en":"Здесь <b>не́кому</b> мне помо́чь.","say":"Здесь некому мне помочь."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Никто́ зна́ет об э́том.","right":"Никто́ не зна́ет об э́том.","why":"A <b>ни-</b> word still needs the verb's «не»: <b>никто́ не зна́ет</b>. Russian negation doubles — English's single negative is the trap."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"“Buy something (anything) for dinner.” (an open request) —","opts":["Купи́ что-то на у́жин.","Купи́ что-нибу́дь на у́жин.","Купи́ ничего́ на у́жин."],"answer":1,"explain":"An open request → <b>-нибу́дь</b>: что-нибу́дь. -то would point at one known thing."},{"type":"choice","q":"“I have no time to read.” (не́- + inf.) —","opts":["Мне не́когда чита́ть.","Мне никогда́ чита́ть.","Мне не́ время чита́ть."],"answer":0,"explain":"“no time to —” → <b>не́когда</b> + infinitive: Мне не́когда чита́ть. (никогда́ = “never”, a different word.)"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks optional.","q":"Fill the double-negative gap: «Я ___ не по́нял.» (nothing)","accept":["ничего","ничего."],"placeholder":"…","explain":"<b>ничего́</b> + «не» — the negation doubles: Я ничего́ не по́нял."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Concession, cause, purpose, result","title":"<em>Хотя́… поско́льку… что́бы… так что…</em>","paras":["Week 3 gave you a whole toolkit for joining ideas. <b>Concession</b> (“even though”): <b>хотя́</b> + clause, <b>несмотря́ на</b> + noun, <b>несмотря́ на то что</b> + clause, <b>как ни</b> стара́лся (however hard he tried). <b>Condition</b>: <b>при усло́вии что</b>, <b>в слу́чае е́сли</b>.","<b>Cause</b>: <b>поско́льку / так как</b> up front, <b>благодаря́</b> (+dat., welcome) vs <b>из-за</b> (+gen., unwelcome). <b>Purpose</b>: <b>что́бы</b> / <b>для того́ что́бы</b> + infinitive or past. <b>Result</b>: <b>так что</b>, <b>поэ́тому</b>, <b>сле́довательно</b>.","Two traps to keep straight: <b>хотя́</b> (although, + clause) vs <b>несмотря́ на</b> (despite, + noun); and <b>так что</b> (result: “so”) vs <b>что́бы</b> (purpose: “so that”). Ask what the link <em>does</em> — concede, cause, aim, or conclude — and the connector picks itself."],"audio":"Week 3 gave you a whole toolkit for joining ideas. Concession, 'even though': khotya plus a clause, nesmotrya na plus a noun, nesmotrya na to chto plus a clause, kak ni staralsya, however hard he tried. Condition: pri uslovii chto, v sluchaye yesli. Cause: poskolku or tak kak up front, blagodarya plus dative for a welcome cause, iz-za plus genitive for an unwelcome one. Purpose: chtoby or dlya togo chtoby plus infinitive or past. Result: tak chto, poetomu, sledovatelno. Two traps to keep straight: khotya, 'although' plus a clause, versus nesmotrya na, 'despite' plus a noun; and tak chto, result 'so', versus chtoby, purpose 'so that'. Ask what the link does — concede, cause, aim, or conclude — and the connector picks itself.","table":{"rows":[["Хотя́ бы́ло тру́дно, я не сдала́сь. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Хотя было трудно, я не сдалась\">🔊</button>","Although it was hard, I didn't give up. (concession)"],["Поско́льку срок бли́зко, тороплю́сь. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Поскольку срок близко, тороплюсь\">🔊</button>","Since the deadline is near, I'm hurrying. (cause)"],["Учу́сь, что́бы чита́ть про́зу. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Учусь, чтобы читать прозу\">🔊</button>","I study in order to read prose. (purpose)"],["Всё гото́во, так что иду́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Всё готово, так что иду\">🔊</button>","Everything's ready, so I'm going. (result)"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"Although the text was hard, I finished it thanks to Maxim.","en":"<b>Хотя́</b> текст был тру́дным, я зако́нчила его́ <b>благодаря́</b> Макси́му.","say":"Хотя текст был трудным, я закончила его благодаря Максиму."},{"ru":"The deadline was near, so I worked all night in order to finish.","en":"Срок был бли́зко, <b>так что</b> я рабо́тала всю ночь, <b>что́бы</b> зако́нчить.","say":"Срок был близко, так что я работала всю ночь, чтобы закончить."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Несмотря́ шёл дождь, мы гуля́ли.","right":"Несмотря́ на то что шёл дождь, мы гуля́ли.","why":"“Despite” + a whole clause needs the full <b>несмотря́ на то что</b>. Bare несмотря́ can't take a clause; хотя́ could («Хотя́ шёл дождь…»)."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“Although he was tired, he kept working.” —","opts":["Несмотря́ он уста́л, он рабо́тал.","Хотя́ он уста́л, он продолжа́л рабо́тать.","Поско́льку он уста́л, он продолжа́л рабо́тать."],"answer":1,"explain":"“Although + clause” → <b>хотя́</b>. несмотря́ needs «на (то что)»; поско́льку means “since”, flipping the logic."},{"type":"choice","q":"Welcome or unwelcome? “___ the delay, we missed the train.” —","opts":["Благодаря́ заде́ржке","Из-за заде́ржки","Что́бы заде́ржки"],"answer":1,"explain":"Missing a train is bad → <b>из-за</b> + genitive: из-за заде́ржки. благодаря́ (+dat.) would praise it."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“I closed the door so that it would be quiet.”</b> (purpose)","want":"Я закры́ла дверь, что́бы ста́ло ти́хо","answer":"Я закры́ла дверь, что́бы ста́ло ти́хо"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Numbers and register","title":"<em>Дво́е, о́ба, полтора́</em> — and <em>кни́жный vs разгово́рный</em>","paras":["Week 3's numbers: collectives <b>дво́е, тро́е, че́тверо</b> for male-or-mixed groups and plural-only nouns (<b>тро́е су́ток</b>) — but <b>две студе́нтки</b> for women; “both” by gender <b>о́ба</b> (m/n) / <b>о́бе</b> (f); “one and a half” <b>полтора́</b> (m/n) / <b>полторы́</b> (f); and “about” by inversion — <b>челове́к де́сять</b> ≈ roughly ten.","And register: the bookish connectors <b>таки́м о́бразом, тем не ме́нее, что каса́ется, в связи́ с</b> build an essay; the colloquial particles <b>же</b> (insistence), <b>ведь</b> (shared ground), <b>-то</b> (spotlight) warm up speech. Remember <b>же ≠ also</b> — that's то́же.","For questions with feeling, <b>ра́зве / неуже́ли</b> carry surprise, while plain <b>ли</b> asks a neutral or indirect yes/no. One idea, and you choose how to dress it — that flexibility is the heart of B2."],"audio":"Week 3's numbers: collectives dvoye, troye, chetvero for male-or-mixed groups and plural-only nouns, troye sutok — but dve studentki for women; 'both' by gender, oba for masculine and neuter, obe for feminine; 'one and a half', poltora for masculine and neuter, poltory for feminine; and 'about' by inversion — chelovek desyat, roughly ten. And register: the bookish connectors takim obrazom, tem ne meneye, chto kasayetsya, v svyazi s build an essay; the colloquial particles zhe, insistence; ved, shared ground; -to, spotlight, warm up speech. Remember zhe is not 'also' — that's tozhe. For questions with feeling, razve and neuzheli carry surprise, while plain li asks a neutral or indirect yes-no. One idea, and you choose how to dress it — that flexibility is the heart of B2.","table":{"rows":[["тро́е су́ток / о́ба бра́та / полторы́ неде́ли <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"трое суток, оба брата, полторы недели\">🔊</button>","three days / both brothers / a week and a half"],["челове́к де́сять (≈ about ten) <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"человек десять\">🔊</button>","about ten (inversion)"],["таки́м о́бразом (book.) / зна́чит (coll.) <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"таким образом, значит\">🔊</button>","“thus”: bookish vs colloquial"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Both brothers came; there were about ten of us in all.","en":"Пришли́ <b>о́ба</b> бра́та; всего́ нас бы́ло <b>челове́к де́сять</b>.","say":"Пришли оба брата; всего нас было человек десять."},{"ru":"As for the style — in an essay it's “thus”, in a chat it's “so”.","en":"<b>Что каса́ется</b> сти́ля: в эссе́ — «таки́м о́бразом», в ча́те — «зна́чит».","say":"Что касается стиля: в эссе — таким образом, в чате — значит."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"дво́е сестёр","right":"две сестры́","why":"Collectives skip women → <b>две сестры́</b>. Keep дво́е/тро́е for male, mixed, or plural-only nouns (тро́е су́ток)."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“both sisters” + “a week and a half” —","opts":["о́ба сестры́, полтора́ неде́ли","о́бе сестры́, полторы́ неде́ли","о́бе сёстры, полторы́ неде́ль"],"answer":1,"explain":"Feminine → <b>о́бе</b> and <b>полторы́</b>, each + genitive singular: о́бе сестры́, полторы́ неде́ли."},{"type":"choice","q":"A formal essay wants “nevertheless”. Choose:","opts":["всё равно́","тем не ме́нее","ну всё-та́ки"],"answer":1,"explain":"Bookish → <b>тем не ме́нее</b>. The others are colloquial."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks optional.","q":"“About ten” by inversion (people first): «Нас бы́ло ___ де́сять.»","accept":["человек","человек."],"placeholder":"…","explain":"Noun before number = “about”: <b>челове́к де́сять</b> ≈ around ten."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"End of a big week. Emma and Ива́н sit over coffee at Ле́на's café and look back — and half of Week 3 sneaks into their chat. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Неуже́ли уже́ коне́ц неде́ли? Как твои́ перево́ды?","ru":"Can it really be the end of the week already? How are your translations?"},{"who":"B","en":"Хорошо́! Хотя́ бы́ло тру́дно, я сдала́ о́ба докуме́нта во́время.","ru":"Good! Although it was hard, I handed in both documents on time."},{"who":"A","en":"Дво́е мои́х друзе́й то́же перево́дчики. Позва́ть кого́-нибу́дь из них?","ru":"Two of my friends are translators too. Shall I invite one of them?"},{"who":"B","en":"Обяза́тельно! Мне ведь всегда́ ну́жно с кем-то поговори́ть о рабо́те.","ru":"Definitely! After all, I always need someone to talk to about work."},{"who":"A","en":"Поско́льку у тебя́ тепе́рь мно́го зака́зов, ты, наве́рное, о́чень занята́?","ru":"Since you have a lot of orders now, you're probably very busy?"},{"who":"B","en":"Полторы́ неде́ли без о́тдыха! Но я не жа́луюсь — рабо́та лю́бимая.","ru":"A week and a half without rest! But I'm not complaining — the work is one I love."},{"who":"A","en":"Ты же зна́ешь, что все тобо́й горди́мся. Никто́ бо́льше так не мо́жет.","ru":"You know we're all proud of you. No one else can do it like that."},{"who":"B","en":"Спаси́бо. Что каса́ется о́тпуска — отдохну́, когда́ сдам после́дний перево́д.","ru":"Thanks. As for a holiday — I'll rest when I hand in the last translation."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve keystones of Week 3 — indefinites, negatives, connectors, numbers and particles. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"кто-то","ru":"someone (real, unknown)","ex":"Кто-то оста́вил запи́ску."},{"en":"кто-нибу́дь","ru":"someone / anyone (open)","ex":"Спроси́ кого́-нибу́дь."},{"en":"ко́е-кто","ru":"someone (I know who)","ex":"Ко́е-кто уже́ зна́ет."},{"en":"никто́","ru":"nobody","ex":"Никто́ не пришёл."},{"en":"ничего́","ru":"nothing (object)","ex":"Я ничего́ не ви́жу."},{"en":"не́чего","ru":"nothing to (+ infinitive)","ex":"Мне не́чего доба́вить."},{"en":"хотя́","ru":"although","ex":"Хотя́ уста́л, продолжа́ю."},{"en":"поско́льку","ru":"since, because (bookish)","ex":"Поско́льку по́здно, идём домо́й."},{"en":"о́ба","ru":"both (m / n)","ex":"О́ба сда́ли экза́мен."},{"en":"полтора́","ru":"one and a half (m / n)","ex":"Жду полтора́ часа́."},{"en":"неуже́ли","ru":"can it really be?! (disbelief)","ex":"Неуже́ли ты зако́нчил?"},{"en":"таки́м о́бразом","ru":"thus, in this way (bookish)","ex":"Таки́м о́бразом, всё гото́во."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Week-3 phrase to its meaning.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["никто́ ничего́ не зна́ет","nobody knows anything"],["хотя́ бы́ло тру́дно","although it was hard"],["о́ба бра́та","both brothers"],["неуже́ли?","can it really be?!"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Eight questions — one or two from every day this week. Pass this and Week 3 is yours.","items":[{"q":"“Someone phoned (it happened).” —","opts":["Кто-нибу́дь звони́л.","Никто́ звони́л.","Кто-то звони́л."],"answer":2},{"q":"“Nobody understood anything.” —","opts":["Никто́ по́нял всё.","Никто́ ничего́ не по́нял.","Кто-то ничего́ по́нял."],"answer":1},{"q":"“I have nothing to do.” —","opts":["Мне не́чего де́лать.","Мне ничего́ де́лать.","Мне не́ де́лать."],"answer":0},{"q":"“Although it was late, we stayed.” —","opts":["Хотя́ бы́ло по́здно, мы оста́лись.","Несмотря́ по́здно, мы оста́лись.","Поско́льку бы́ло по́здно, мы оста́лись."],"answer":0},{"q":"A welcome cause takes the dative with —","opts":["из-за","ввиду́","благодаря́"],"answer":2},{"q":"“The work is done, ___ I'm free.” (result) —","opts":["что́бы","так что","для того́ что́бы"],"answer":1},{"q":"“three days-and-nights” (plural-only noun) —","opts":["три су́ток","тро́е су́тки","тро́е су́ток"],"answer":2},{"q":"«неуже́ли» adds —","opts":["a neutral yes/no","surprise / disbelief","purpose"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's note closing out the week — the whole of Week 3 is folded into it. Read it aloud.","title":"Тре́тья неде́ля позади́","sentences":["Э́та неде́ля была́ насы́щенной, хотя́ и о́чень тру́дной.","Поско́льку зака́зов бы́ло мно́го, я почти́ не отдыха́ла.","Но никто́ не жа́ловался, и я ничего́ не бро́сила.","Дво́е друзе́й помогли́, и благодаря́ им я всё успе́ла.","Что каса́ется о́тдыха, отдохну́ на сле́дующей неде́ле.","Неуже́ли я уже́ говорю́ и пишу́ на двух ру́сских — кни́жном и живо́м?"],"translation":"This week was intense, although also very hard. Since there were many orders, I hardly rested. But no one complained, and I didn't drop anything. Two friends helped, and thanks to them I managed everything. As for rest — I'll rest next week. Can it really be that I now speak and write in two Russians — the bookish one and the living one?"},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — sum up a busy stretch using this week's whole toolkit.","prompt":"In 4–5 sentences, tell the story of a busy week (yours or Emma's). Use a concession (хотя́ / несмотря́ на то что), a cause (поско́льку / благодаря́ / из-за), a number (дво́е, о́ба, полтора́…), and one particle or register-marker (же, ведь, таки́м о́бразом).","hint":"Chain them: «Хотя́ бы́ло тру́дно… поско́льку… благодаря́… так что…», drop in о́ба / полтора́ for the count, and colour one line with же or ведь 🙂","example":"Э́та неде́ля была́ тру́дной, но интере́сной. Поско́льку зака́зов бы́ло мно́го, я рабо́тала почти́ по полтора́ дня без переры́ва. Хотя́ я уста́ла, я ничего́ не бро́сила — ведь э́ту рабо́ту я люблю́. Благодаря́ друзья́м я всё успе́ла, так что тепе́рь могу́ отдохну́ть."}},"22":{"day":22,"week":"04","themeRu":"Building and decoding words","themeEn":"Приста́вка, ко́рень, су́ффикс","intro":"A word Emma had never seen landed in her translation this morning: «переустро́йство». She didn't reach for the dictionary — she took it apart. <b>Пере-</b> (re-) + <b>устро́й</b> (arrange) + <b>-ство</b> (the -ing noun) = “reorganisation”. That's today's superpower. Russian builds words from parts, and once you know the parts you can <b>decode</b> a word you've never met and <b>build</b> the one you need. This is your potential vocabulary — thousands of words, for free.","introAudio":"A word Emma had never seen landed in her translation this morning: pereustroystvo. She didn't reach for the dictionary — she took it apart. Pere-, meaning re-, plus stroy, arrange, plus -stvo, the -ing noun: reorganisation. That's today's superpower. Russian builds words from parts, and once you know the parts you can decode a word you've never met and build the one you need. This is your potential vocabulary — thousands of words, for free.","goals":["Read a deverbal noun as its verb: <b>реше́ние</b> = “the deciding / a decision” (реши́ть + -ени(е))","Name the doer with <b>-тель / -ник / -щик</b>: писа́ть → <b>писа́тель</b>, переводи́ть → <b>перево́дчик</b>","Turn a quality into a noun with <b>-ость</b> (ва́жный → <b>ва́жность</b>) and read borrowed <b>-ирова-</b> verbs","Decode any long word by its <b>prefix + root + suffix</b> — your potential vocabulary toward 5000+ words"],"learned":["Read deverbal nouns as their verbs: <b>реше́ние</b> = the act of deciding","Named the doer with the suffixes -тель / -ник / -щик","Turned qualities into nouns with -ость and recognised -ирова- verbs","Decoded unfamiliar words by prefix + root + suffix"],"review":{"intro":"Week four is integration — you'll put the whole level to work. First, a minute back in week three: a concession, a collective numeral, and a participle folded into a case. They all live on this month.","introAudio":"Week four is integration — you'll put the whole level to work. First, a minute back in week three: a concession, a collective numeral, and a participle folded into a case. They all live on this month.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. An old friend from week three.","q":"“Despite the rain, we went for a walk.” (despite + a noun)","opts":["Несмотря́ на дождь, мы пошли́ гуля́ть.","Несмотря́ дождь, мы пошли́ гуля́ть.","Хотя́ дождь, мы пошли́ гуля́ть."],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>несмотря́ на</b> + a noun in the accusative: несмотря́ на дождь. Without «на» it's broken; «хотя́» needs a full clause (хотя́ шёл дождь), not a bare noun."},{"type":"choice","q":"“Both brothers are engineers.” (both — masculine)","opts":["О́бе бра́та — инжене́ры.","О́ба бра́та — инжене́ры.","Дво́е бра́та — инжене́ры."],"answer":1,"explain":"Masculine → <b>о́ба</b> (о́бе is feminine): о́ба бра́та. «дво́е» would need the genitive plural — дво́е бра́тьев."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“I'm reading a book written by a famous writer.”</b> (written = напи́санную)","want":"Я чита́ю кни́гу, напи́санную изве́стным писа́телем","answer":"Я чита́ю кни́гу, напи́санную изве́стным писа́телем"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The action, packed into a noun","title":"<em>реши́ть → реше́ние</em> — the verb becomes a thing","paras":["Russian loves to freeze a whole action into a noun. Add <b>-ени(е)</b> or <b>-ани(е)</b> to a verb stem and you get the <b>name of the action</b>: <b>реши́ть</b> (to decide) → <b>реше́ние</b> (a decision / the deciding), <b>обсуди́ть</b> (to discuss) → <b>обсужде́ние</b> (a discussion), <b>чита́ть</b> (to read) → <b>чте́ние</b> (reading).","These are the engine of written Russian. Where English says “when they built the house”, a Russian text writes <b>строи́тельство до́ма</b> — “the construction of the house”. One noun swallows a whole clause. That's exactly why real texts feel dense: they're packed with these deverbal nouns.","To <b>decode</b> one, find the verb hiding inside. <b>реше́ние</b> → реши́(ть) → “a decision”. <b>образова́ние</b> → образова́(ть) → “education, formation”. See the verb, and the noun tells you precisely what it means."],"audio":"Russian loves to freeze a whole action into a noun. Add -eniye or -aniye to a verb stem and you get the name of the action: reshit, to decide, becomes resheniye, a decision or the deciding; obsudit, to discuss, becomes obsuzhdeniye, a discussion; chitat, to read, becomes chteniye, reading. These are the engine of written Russian. Where English says 'when they built the house', a Russian text writes stroitelstvo doma, the construction of the house. One noun swallows a whole clause. To decode one, find the verb hiding inside: resheniye comes from reshit, obrazovaniye from obrazovat, education. See the verb, and the noun tells you what it means.","table":{"rows":[["реши́ть → реше́ние <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"решить, решение\">🔊</button>","to decide → a decision / the deciding"],["стро́ить → строи́тельство <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"строить, строительство\">🔊</button>","to build → construction"],["обсуди́ть → обсужде́ние <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"обсудить, обсуждение\">🔊</button>","to discuss → a discussion"],["изуча́ть → изуче́ние <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"изучать, изучение\">🔊</button>","to study → the study (of)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"The construction of the new building took two years.","en":"<b>Строи́тельство</b> но́вого зда́ния за́няло два го́да.","say":"Строительство нового здания заняло два года."},{"ru":"The discussion of the article lasted an hour.","en":"<b>Обсужде́ние</b> статьи́ дли́лось це́лый час.","say":"Обсуждение статьи длилось целый час."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"реши́ние","right":"реше́ние","why":"The suffix is <b>-ени(е)</b>: реши́(ть) → <b>реше́ние</b>. The и belongs to the verb; the noun's ending is -ение."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"<b>«обсужде́ние»</b> comes from which verb?","opts":["обсуди́ть (to discuss)","обеща́ть (to promise)","обслу́живать (to serve)"],"answer":0,"explain":"об- + суди́(ть) + -ени(е) → <b>обсужде́ние</b>. The д→жд shift is regular (суди́ть → сужде́ние)."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. The stress mark is just for you.","q":"Turn <b>предложи́ть</b> (to propose) into the action-noun “a proposal”:","accept":["предложение","предложение."],"placeholder":"предложе́…","explain":"предложи́(ть) + -ени(е) → <b>предложе́ние</b> (which also means “a sentence”!)."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The doer — <em>-тель, -ник, -щик</em>","title":"<em>писа́ть → писа́тель</em>, <em>переводи́ть → перево́дчик</em>","paras":["The same trick names the <b>person who does</b> the action. <b>-тель</b> is the workhorse: <b>писа́ть → писа́тель</b> (writer), <b>чита́ть → чита́тель</b> (reader), <b>преподава́ть → преподава́тель</b> (lecturer). It's Emma's own job too: переводи́ть → <b>перево́дчик / перево́дчица</b> (translator), built with the productive <b>-чик / -щик</b>.","Two more doer-suffixes complete the family. <b>-ник</b>: рабо́та → <b>рабо́тник</b> (worker), учи́ться → <b>уче́ник</b> (pupil). <b>-щик / -чик</b> often marks a manual or technical trade — <b>ка́менщик</b> (bricklayer), <b>лётчик</b> (pilot).","So an unfamiliar word turns readable: see <b>-тель</b> or <b>-ник</b> sitting on a verb-root, and you're looking at a person. <b>Слу́шатель</b>? “The one who listens” — a listener."],"audio":"The same trick names the person who does the action. -tel is the workhorse: pisat becomes pisatel, a writer; chitat becomes chitatel, a reader; prepodavat becomes prepodavatel, a lecturer. It's Emma's own job too: perevodit becomes perevodchik or perevodchitsa, a translator, built with the productive -chik and -shchik. Two more doer-suffixes complete the family. -nik: rabota becomes rabotnik, a worker; uchitsya becomes uchenik, a pupil. -shchik or -chik often marks a manual or technical trade: kamenshchik, a bricklayer; lyotchik, a pilot. So an unfamiliar word turns readable: see -tel or -nik on a verb-root, and you're looking at a person. Slushatel? The one who listens — a listener.","table":{"rows":[["писа́ть → писа́тель <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"писать, писатель\">🔊</button>","to write → a writer"],["чита́ть → чита́тель <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"читать, читатель\">🔊</button>","to read → a reader"],["переводи́ть → перево́дчик <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"переводить, переводчик\">🔊</button>","to translate → a translator"],["рабо́тать → рабо́тник <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"работать, работник\">🔊</button>","to work → a worker"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"My favourite writer lives in St Petersburg.","en":"Мой люби́мый <b>писа́тель</b> живёт в Петербу́рге.","say":"Мой любимый писатель живёт в Петербурге."},{"ru":"As a translator, Emma reads for days on end.","en":"Как <b>перево́дчик</b>, Э́мма чита́ет це́лыми дня́ми.","say":"Как переводчик, Эмма читает целыми днями."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"перево́дник","right":"перево́дчик","why":"The trade-suffix here is <b>-чик</b>: переводи́ть → <b>перево́дчик</b>. Not every verb takes -ник — notice the suffix per word."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which word means “a reader” (the person who reads)?","opts":["чита́ющий","чита́тель","чте́ние"],"answer":1,"explain":"-тель names the doer: чита́(ть) → <b>чита́тель</b>. чита́ющий is the participle (“reading”); чте́ние is the action-noun (“reading” as an activity)."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“My favourite writer wrote a new novel.”</b>","want":"Мой люби́мый писа́тель написа́л но́вый рома́н","answer":"Мой люби́мый писа́тель написа́л но́вый рома́н"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The quality — <em>-ость</em> — and borrowed <em>-ирова-</em> verbs","title":"<em>ва́жный → ва́жность</em>, <em>анализи́ровать</em>","paras":["Take an adjective, add <b>-ость</b>, and the quality becomes a noun: <b>ва́жный → ва́жность</b> (important → importance), <b>возмо́жный → возмо́жность</b> (possible → possibility, opportunity), <b>сло́жный → сло́жность</b> (complex → complexity). English does the very same with “-ness / -ity”, so these read almost for free.","One more productive pattern to recognise: verbs in <b>-ирова(ть)</b>. They're usually borrowed from Europe and name a modern action — <b>анализи́ровать</b> (analyse), <b>плани́ровать</b> (plan), <b>регистри́ровать</b> (register), <b>информи́ровать</b> (inform). If an English verb sounds technical, try <b>-ировать</b> — you'll often be right.","Careful with the borrowed ones — that's where <b>false friends</b> hide. <b>Актуа́льный</b> isn't “actual”, it's “relevant, topical”; <b>симпати́чный</b> isn't “sympathetic”, it's “nice-looking”. Decode by the root, but always check the sense."],"audio":"Take an adjective, add -ost, and the quality becomes a noun: vazhny becomes vazhnost, importance; vozmozhny becomes vozmozhnost, possibility or opportunity; slozhny becomes slozhnost, complexity. English does the same with -ness or -ity, so these read almost for free. One more pattern to recognise: verbs in -irovat. They're usually borrowed and name a modern action: analizirovat, analyse; planirovat, plan; registrirovat, register; informirovat, inform. If an English verb sounds technical, try -irovat. Careful with borrowed words — that's where false friends hide. Aktualny isn't 'actual', it's 'relevant, topical'; simpatichny isn't 'sympathetic', it's 'nice-looking'. Decode by the root, but always check the sense.","table":{"rows":[["ва́жный → ва́жность <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"важный, важность\">🔊</button>","important → importance"],["возмо́жный → возмо́жность <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"возможный, возможность\">🔊</button>","possible → possibility, opportunity"],["сло́жный → сло́жность <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"сложный, сложность\">🔊</button>","complex → complexity"],["анализи́ровать <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"анализировать\">🔊</button>","to analyse (borrowed -ирова-)"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"Everyone understands the importance of this decision.","en":"Все понима́ют <b>ва́жность</b> э́того реше́ния.","say":"Все понимают важность этого решения."},{"ru":"Emma is analysing a complicated text.","en":"Э́мма <b>анализи́рует</b> сло́жный текст.","say":"Эмма анализирует сложный текст."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"актуа́льный = actual","right":"актуа́льный = relevant / topical","why":"A classic false friend. Decode the root, then check the sense: <b>актуа́льный</b> means “relevant, current”, not “actual/real” (that's настоя́щий or реа́льный)."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"<b>«возмо́жность»</b> is the noun built from which adjective?","opts":["возмо́жный (possible)","мо́жно (one may)","мочь (to be able)"],"answer":0,"explain":"-ость builds a noun from an adjective: возмо́жный → <b>возмо́жность</b> (possibility, opportunity)."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the quality-noun. Plain letters are fine.","q":"Make the quality-noun “importance” from <b>ва́жный</b>:","accept":["важность","важность."],"placeholder":"ва́жн…","explain":"ва́жный → <b>ва́жность</b> (the -ость suffix, just like English -ness)."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Prefixes, and reading the whole word","title":"<em>не-, без-, анти-, сверх-</em> — a word by its parts","paras":["Prefixes flip or intensify meaning, and they stay the same across thousands of words. <b>не-</b> negates: возмо́жный → <b>невозмо́жный</b> (impossible). <b>без-/бес-</b> = “without”: <b>безопа́сный</b> (safe = without danger), <b>беспла́тный</b> (free = without payment). <b>анти-</b> = “anti-”: <b>антивое́нный</b> (anti-war). <b>сверх-</b> = “super/over-”: <b>сверхзвуково́й</b> (supersonic).","Now put it all together — the whole point of today. A long word you've never met is just <b>prefix + root + suffix</b>. Take <b>переустро́йство</b>: <b>пере-</b> (re-) + устро́й (arrange) + <b>-ство</b> (the -ing noun) = “reorganisation”. Take <b>безрабо́тица</b>: <b>без-</b> (without) + рабо́т (work) + <b>-ица</b> = “unemployment”.","This is your <b>potential vocabulary</b>: you don't need to have met a word to read it. The parts turn the B2 lexicon (~5100 words) into something far larger — every root grows a whole family. Emma decodes three new words a day this way, and rarely opens the dictionary."],"audio":"Prefixes flip or intensify meaning, and they stay the same across thousands of words. Ne- negates: vozmozhny becomes nevozmozhny, impossible. Bez- or bes- means 'without': bezopasny, safe, without danger; besplatny, free, without payment. Anti- means 'anti-': antivoyenny, anti-war. Sverkh- means 'super' or 'over': sverkhzvukovoy, supersonic. Now put it together — the whole point of today. A long word you've never met is just prefix plus root plus suffix. Take pereustroystvo: pere-, re-, plus stroy, arrange, plus -stvo, the -ing noun: reorganisation. Take bezrabotitsa: bez-, without, plus rabot, work, plus -itsa: unemployment. This is your potential vocabulary: you don't need to have met a word to read it. The parts turn the B2 lexicon into something far larger — every root grows a family. Emma decodes three new words a day this way, and rarely opens the dictionary.","table":{"rows":[["возмо́жный → невозмо́жный <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"возможный, невозможный\">🔊</button>","possible → impossible (не-)"],["опа́сный → безопа́сный <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"опасный, безопасный\">🔊</button>","dangerous → safe (без-)"],["вое́нный → антивое́нный <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"военный, антивоенный\">🔊</button>","military → anti-war (анти-)"],["звуково́й → сверхзвуково́й <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"звуковой, сверхзвуковой\">🔊</button>","sound (adj) → supersonic (сверх-)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"This translation is an almost impossible task.","en":"Э́тот перево́д — почти́ <b>невозмо́жная</b> зада́ча.","say":"Этот перевод — почти невозможная задача."},{"ru":"The lecture was free, and I understood almost everything.","en":"Ле́кция была́ <b>беспла́тной</b>, и я по́нял почти́ всё.","say":"Лекция была бесплатной, и я понял почти всё."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"безопа́сный = dangerous","right":"безопа́сный = safe","why":"<b>без-</b> means “without”, so безопа́сный = “without danger” = <b>safe</b>. Read the prefix and the meaning falls out."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"<b>безрабо́тица</b> breaks into без- + рабо́т- + -ица. It means…","opts":["overwork","unemployment","a workplace"],"answer":1,"explain":"без- (without) + рабо́т (work) + -ица (noun) → “without-work” = <b>unemployment</b>."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Э́то сло́во мо́жно поня́ть по частя́м.","sub":"“You can work this word out from its parts” — decode, don't memorise","want":"это слово можно понять по частям"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Emma shows Иван how she cracks a word she's never seen — while he discovers his new job runs on exactly these nouns. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Смотри́, Ива́н: в статье́ сло́во «переустро́йство». Я его́ не зна́ла, но поняла́ сра́зу.","ru":"Look, Ivan: in the article, the word «переустро́йство». I didn't know it, but I understood it at once."},{"who":"A","en":"Как? Ты же ви́дишь э́то сло́во впервы́е.","ru":"How? You're seeing this word for the first time."},{"who":"B","en":"По частя́м. Приста́вка, ко́рень, су́ффикс — ка́ждая часть несёт смысл. Получи́лось «reorganisation».","ru":"By its parts. Prefix, root, suffix — each part carries meaning. It came out as “reorganisation”."},{"who":"A","en":"У перево́дчика своя́ суперси́ла — чте́ние по ко́рню.","ru":"A translator has their own superpower — reading by the root."},{"who":"B","en":"Кста́ти, как твоя́ но́вая рабо́та? Ты тепе́рь настоя́щий рабо́тник.","ru":"By the way, how's your new job? You're a real worker now."},{"who":"A","en":"Почти́. Мно́го обсужде́ний, отчётов, плани́рования — це́лый мир отглаго́льных существи́тельных.","ru":"Almost. Lots of discussions, reports, planning — a whole world of deverbal nouns."},{"who":"B","en":"Ви́дишь? Ты уже́ говори́шь как а́втор докуме́нтов.","ru":"See? You already talk like the author of documents."},{"who":"A","en":"Зато́ тепе́рь я понима́ю ва́жность коро́тких предложе́ний.","ru":"On the other hand, now I understand the importance of short sentences."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words built from parts — the doers, the actions, the qualities. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"реше́ние","ru":"a decision, a solution","ex":"Э́то бы́ло тру́дное реше́ние."},{"en":"обсужде́ние","ru":"a discussion","ex":"Обсужде́ние дли́лось два часа́."},{"en":"строи́тельство","ru":"construction, building (the process)","ex":"Строи́тельство моста́ ещё идёт."},{"en":"образова́ние","ru":"education; formation","ex":"У неё прекра́сное образова́ние."},{"en":"писа́тель","ru":"a writer","ex":"Э́тот писа́тель о́чень изве́стен."},{"en":"чита́тель","ru":"a reader","ex":"Ка́ждый чита́тель поймёт по-сво́ему."},{"en":"перево́дчик","ru":"a translator","ex":"Хоро́ший перево́дчик — то́же а́втор."},{"en":"ва́жность","ru":"importance","ex":"Нельзя́ забыва́ть о ва́жности сна."},{"en":"возмо́жность","ru":"possibility, opportunity","ex":"У меня́ есть возмо́жность пое́хать."},{"en":"приста́вка","ru":"a prefix","ex":"Приста́вка «не-» меня́ет смысл."},{"en":"су́ффикс","ru":"a suffix","ex":"Су́ффикс «-тель» ука́зывает на челове́ка."},{"en":"анализи́ровать","ru":"to analyse","ex":"Она́ лю́бит анализи́ровать те́ксты."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each built word to its meaning.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["реше́ние","a decision"],["писа́тель","a writer"],["ва́жность","importance"],["невозмо́жный","impossible"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"<b>«реше́ние»</b> is built from which verb?","opts":["ре́зать (to cut)","реша́ющий (deciding)","реши́ть (to decide)"],"answer":2},{"q":"Which suffix names the <b>person</b> who does something?","opts":["-ость","-тель","-е́ние"],"answer":1},{"q":"<b>«ва́жность»</b> means…","opts":["important","importance","more important"],"answer":1},{"q":"The prefix <b>без-</b> means…","opts":["without","again","against"],"answer":0},{"q":"You meet <b>безрабо́тный</b> for the first time. It means…","opts":["hard-working","unemployed","retired"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's decoding log — read it aloud, and notice how every long word is just parts.","title":"Слова́ по частя́м","sentences":["Ка́ждое у́тро я нахожу́ в те́ксте но́вое сло́во.","Снача́ла оно́ ка́жется тру́дным и дли́нным.","Но я де́лю его́ на приста́вку, ко́рень и су́ффикс.","Тако́е сло́во легко́ прочита́ть, е́сли зна́ешь ча́сти.","Тепе́рь у меня́ есть потенциа́льный слова́рь — ты́сячи слов беспла́тно.","Перево́дчик — не то́лько знато́к языка́, но и де́тектив."],"translation":"Every morning I find a new word in a text. At first it seems hard and long. But I split it into a prefix, a root and a suffix. Such a word is simple to read if you know the parts. Now I have a potential vocabulary — thousands of words for free. A translator isn't only a language expert, but also a detective."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — build and decode.","prompt":"Take three long Russian words you've met (or invent them from parts): break each into prefix + root + suffix and say what it means. Then write two sentences that use a deverbal noun (like реше́ние, обсужде́ние, строи́тельство) instead of a full verb clause.","hint":"Doers end in -тель/-ник/-щик, qualities in -ость, actions in -ение/-ание; не-/без-/анти-/сверх- flip or intensify. 🙂","example":"Сло́во «переводи́ть» даёт «перево́дчик» — того́, кто перево́дит. По́сле до́лгого обсужде́ния мы при́няли ва́жное реше́ние. Строи́тельство но́вой шко́лы — большо́е собы́тие для го́рода."}},"23":{"day":23,"week":"04","themeRu":"Reading the real thing","themeEn":"Настоя́щий текст: газе́та и статья́","intro":"Open a real Russian newspaper and the first sentence can feel like a wall — long, and bristling with participles and passives. Emma faced that wall for two years; now she walks through it. The trick isn't more vocabulary, it's a way of <b>seeing</b>: under every terrifying sentence there's a small, plain skeleton, and everything you built in weeks 1–2 is the key to unpacking the rest. Today the reading passage takes centre stage — a real popular-science excerpt. Let's read it the way a translator does.","introAudio":"Open a real Russian newspaper and the first sentence can feel like a wall — long, and bristling with participles and passives. Emma faced that wall for two years; now she walks through it. The trick isn't more vocabulary, it's a way of seeing: under every terrifying sentence there's a small, plain skeleton, and everything you built in weeks one and two is the key to unpacking the rest. Today the reading passage takes centre stage — a real popular-science excerpt. Let's read it the way a translator does.","goals":["Find the <b>skeleton</b> of a long sentence first — кто? что де́лает? — then add the decoration","Unfold a participial phrase into a «кото́рый» clause: <b>при́нятые ме́ры → ме́ры, кото́рые при́няли</b>","Read a <b>passive</b> as a result and spot the press connectors (одна́ко, по слова́м, в результа́те)","Work a dense paragraph in two passes — gist first, detail second — without panicking at length"],"learned":["Found the subject-and-verb skeleton under a long, decorated sentence","Unfolded participial phrases into «кото́рый» clauses","Read passives as results and followed the connectors carrying the argument","Read a dense authentic paragraph in two passes, gist then detail"],"review":{"intro":"Today you meet weeks 1–2 in the wild, so let's wake them first: an active vs passive participle, a gerund with its one-subject rule, and yesterday's deverbal noun.","introAudio":"Today you meet weeks one and two in the wild, so let's wake them first: an active versus passive participle, a gerund with its one-subject rule, and yesterday's deverbal noun.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer.","q":"“the book being read” (right now — passive, present)","opts":["чита́ющая кни́га","чита́емая кни́га","чита́вшая кни́га"],"answer":1,"explain":"Passive present takes <b>-ем-</b>: <b>чита́емая</b> кни́га = “that is being read”. чита́ющая is active (“that reads”); чита́вшая is active past."},{"type":"choice","q":"“Having read the letter, she smiled.” (one subject — she)","opts":["Чита́я письмо́, она́ улыбну́лась.","Прочита́в письмо́, она́ улыбну́лась.","Прочита́в письмо́, зазвони́л телефо́н."],"answer":1,"explain":"“Having read” = completed, before → the perfective gerund <b>прочита́в</b>; and the gerund's subject must be the main subject (она́), so option 3 dangles (the phone didn't read)."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“The discussion of the article lasted an hour.”</b> (use the deverbal noun)","want":"Обсужде́ние статьи́ дли́лось час","answer":"Обсужде́ние статьи́ дли́лось час"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · What a real sentence hides","title":"Find the skeleton first: <em>кто? что де́лает?</em>","paras":["Open a Russian newspaper and the sentences look terrifying — long, and bristling with participles, deverbal nouns and passives. Here's the secret: under all that there's a plain little skeleton — a <b>subject and a verb</b>. Find it first, and the rest is just decoration hanging off it.","Take: «Гру́ппа учёных, рабо́тающих в университе́те, опубликова́ла иссле́дование.» Strip the middle and the skeleton is <b>гру́ппа опубликова́ла иссле́дование</b> — “a group published a study”. The phrase «рабо́тающих в университе́те» only tells you <b>which</b> group. On the first read, skip the decoration; on the second, add it back.","So the B2 reading move isn't “translate every word left to right”. It's: <b>find кто and что де́лает</b>, then unpack the modifiers one at a time. The grammar you built in weeks 1–2 is exactly what lets you do it."],"audio":"Open a Russian newspaper and the sentences look terrifying — long, and bristling with participles, deverbal nouns and passives. Here's the secret: under all that there's a plain little skeleton — a subject and a verb. Find it first, and the rest is decoration hanging off it. Take: Gruppa uchyonykh, rabotayushchikh v universitete, opublikovala issledovaniye. Strip the middle and the skeleton is gruppa opublikovala issledovaniye — a group published a study. The phrase rabotayushchikh v universitete only tells you which group. On the first read, skip the decoration; on the second, add it back. So the B2 reading move isn't translate every word left to right. It's: find kto and chto delayet, then unpack the modifiers one at a time. The grammar you built in weeks one and two is exactly what lets you do it.","table":{"rows":[["Гру́ппа учёных опубликова́ла иссле́дование <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Группа учёных опубликовала исследование\">🔊</button>","the skeleton: a group published a study"],["Гру́ппа учёных, рабо́тающих здесь, опубликова́ла иссле́дование <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Группа учёных, работающих здесь, опубликовала исследование\">🔊</button>","+ decoration: …(who are) working here…"],["по слова́м экспе́ртов <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"по словам экспертов\">🔊</button>","according to the experts (press cliché)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"The skeleton of the sentence is “scientists showed a result”.","en":"Скеле́т предложе́ния — «учёные показа́ли результа́т».","say":"Скелет предложения — учёные показали результат."},{"ru":"First find who and what they do, then add the details.","en":"Снача́ла найди́ кто и что де́лает, пото́м добавля́й дета́ли.","say":"Сначала найди кто и что делает, потом добавляй детали."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"гру́ппа рабо́тающих","right":"гру́ппа опубликова́ла","why":"The main verb is <b>опубликова́ла</b> (published) — that's the skeleton. «рабо́тающих» is a participle decorating «учёных», not the main action."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the main verb.","q":"In «Статья́, напи́санная профе́ссором, вы́звала спор», the main verb (the skeleton action) is…","opts":["напи́санная","вы́звала","профе́ссором"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>вы́звала</b> (caused) is the main verb: «статья́ … вы́звала спор». напи́санная is a participle decorating статья́."},{"type":"build","q":"Build the skeleton only: <b>“the scientists showed a result”</b>","want":"учёные показа́ли результа́т","answer":"учёные показа́ли результа́т"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Unpack the phrase, don't translate it","title":"<em>при́нятые ме́ры</em> = “the measures that were taken”","paras":["A participial phrase packs a whole «кото́рый» clause into a couple of words. Don't translate it word-by-word — <b>unfold it</b>. <b>при́нятые ме́ры</b> looks like “taken measures”, but read it as «ме́ры, кото́рые при́няли» — “the measures that were taken”. The ending tells you everything.","Read the participle's suffix like a label. <b>-ущ/-ющ/-ащ/-ящ</b> = active, now (рабо́тающий = “who is working”). <b>-вш/-ш</b> = active, before (написа́вший = “who wrote”). <b>-ем/-им</b> = passive, now (люби́мый = “who is loved”). <b>-нн/-енн/-т</b> = passive, done (напи́санный, при́нятый = “that was written / taken”).","So «учёные, проводя́щие иссле́дование» = «учёные, кото́рые прово́дят иссле́дование» (scientists conducting research), and «реше́ние, при́нятое на собра́нии» = «реше́ние, кото́рое при́няли на собра́нии» (a decision taken at the meeting). Unfold, and the fog lifts."],"audio":"A participial phrase packs a whole 'kotory' clause into a couple of words. Don't translate it word-by-word — unfold it. Prinyatye mery looks like 'taken measures', but read it as mery, kotorye prinyali — the measures that were taken. The ending tells you everything. Read the participle's suffix like a label. -ushch, -yushch, -ashch, -yashch is active, now: rabotayushchiy, who is working. -vsh or -sh is active, before: napisavshiy, who wrote. -em or -im is passive, now: lyubimy, who is loved. -nn, -enn or -t is passive, done: napisanny, prinyaty, that was written or taken. So uchyonye, provodyashchiye issledovaniye equals uchyonye, kotorye provodyat issledovaniye, scientists conducting research; and resheniye, prinyatoye na sobranii equals resheniye, kotoroye prinyali na sobranii, a decision taken at the meeting. Unfold, and the fog lifts.","table":{"rows":[["при́нятые ме́ры → ме́ры, кото́рые при́няли <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"принятые меры, меры, которые приняли\">🔊</button>","the measures taken → that were taken"],["напи́санная статья́ → статья́, кото́рую написа́ли <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"написанная статья, статья, которую написали\">🔊</button>","the written article → that was written"],["проводя́щие иссле́дование → кото́рые прово́дят <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"проводящие исследование, которые проводят\">🔊</button>","conducting research → who conduct"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"The measures taken did not help.","en":"<b>При́нятые</b> ме́ры не помогли́.","say":"Принятые меры не помогли."},{"ru":"The decision taken at the meeting changed everything.","en":"Реше́ние, <b>при́нятое</b> на собра́нии, всё измени́ло.","say":"Решение, принятое на собрании, всё изменило."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«при́нятые ме́ры» = “to take measures”","right":"«при́нятые ме́ры» = “the measures that were taken”","why":"<b>при́нятые</b> is a passive past participle, not a verb. Unfold it: ме́ры, кото́рые <b>при́няли</b>. The -т- marks “done to”."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Unfold «статья́, напи́санная в 1990 году́». It means…","opts":["the article that wrote something","the article that was written in 1990","the article writing something in 1990"],"answer":1,"explain":"напи́санная = passive past = «кото́рую написа́ли». So: “the article that was written in 1990”."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the one verb. Plain letters are fine.","q":"Unfold «при́нятое реше́ние» → «реше́ние, кото́рое …» (one verb, past):","accept":["приняли","приняли."],"placeholder":"кото́рое …","explain":"при́нятое = passive past → «реше́ние, кото́рое <b>при́няли</b>» (a decision that was taken)."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Passives and the press's glue-words","title":"<em>бы́ло при́нято реше́ние</em>, <em>одна́ко</em>, <em>по слова́м…</em>","paras":["News writing hides the doer with the <b>passive</b>. «Бы́ло при́нято реше́ние» = “a decision was taken” — by whom, it doesn't say. Read a passive as a <b>result</b>, not an action: «вопро́с ещё не решён» = “the question isn't settled yet”; «да́нные испо́льзуются» = “the data is being used”.","And real text is stitched together with <b>connectors</b> — the glue that carries the argument. Learn to spot them: <b>одна́ко</b> = however, <b>тем не ме́нее</b> = nevertheless, <b>в результа́те</b> = as a result, <b>по слова́м (+ gen.)</b> = according to, <b>как отмеча́ют</b> = as they note. They tell you where the writer is turning.","Put them together and a dense paragraph becomes a map: a passive states a result, a connector shows the turn. «Одна́ко, по слова́м экспе́ртов, вопро́с не решён» = “However, according to experts, the question isn't settled.” You're reading like a native now."],"audio":"News writing hides the doer with the passive. Bylo prinyato resheniye equals a decision was taken — by whom, it doesn't say. Read a passive as a result, not an action: vopros yeshchyo ne reshyon, the question isn't settled yet; dannye ispolzuyutsya, the data is being used. And real text is stitched together with connectors — the glue that carries the argument. Learn to spot them: odnako, however; tem ne meneye, nevertheless; v rezultate, as a result; po slovam plus the genitive, according to; kak otmechayut, as they note. They tell you where the writer is turning. Put them together and a dense paragraph becomes a map: a passive states a result, a connector shows the turn. Odnako, po slovam ekspertov, vopros ne reshyon equals however, according to experts, the question isn't settled. You're reading like a native now.","table":{"rows":[["бы́ло при́нято реше́ние <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"было принято решение\">🔊</button>","a decision was taken (passive)"],["одна́ко / тем не ме́нее <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"однако, тем не менее\">🔊</button>","however / nevertheless"],["по слова́м экспе́ртов <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"по словам экспертов\">🔊</button>","according to the experts"],["в результа́те <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в результате\">🔊</button>","as a result"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"According to the author, the question isn't settled yet.","en":"<b>По слова́м</b> а́втора, вопро́с ещё не решён.","say":"По словам автора, вопрос ещё не решён."},{"ru":"However, the data is already being used.","en":"<b>Одна́ко</b> да́нные уже́ испо́льзуются.","say":"Однако данные уже используются."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"по слова́ми экспе́ртов","right":"по слова́м экспе́ртов","why":"<b>по слова́м</b> takes the dative (“according to”), and экспе́ртов is genitive after it: “according to the experts”. It's a fixed press phrase."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Одна́ко» in a text signals…","opts":["a cause (because)","a result (so)","a contrast, a turn (however)"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>одна́ко</b> = “however” — the writer is turning against what came before."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"По слова́м учёных, вопро́с ещё не решён.","sub":"“According to the scientists, the question isn't settled yet” — a passive plus a press connector","want":"по словам учёных вопрос ещё не решён"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · A strategy for dense prose","title":"Gist first, guess by root, reread for detail","paras":["Here's Emma's method, and it's how every professional reads a hard text. <b>First pass — for the gist.</b> Read fast, find the skeleton of each sentence, and don't stop at unknown words: guess them by root (you decoded words by parts yesterday). Get the shape of the whole thing.","<b>Second pass — for the detail.</b> Now unfold the participles, read the passives as results, and follow the connectors. The words you guessed will mostly confirm themselves from context. Only now, if a key word still resists, reach for the dictionary.","Two honest reminders. Don't panic at length — a long sentence is usually one short skeleton plus decoration. And don't translate into English in your head: <b>read the Russian as Russian</b>. That's the difference between decoding a text and reading one."],"audio":"Here's Emma's method, and it's how every professional reads a hard text. First pass, for the gist. Read fast, find the skeleton of each sentence, and don't stop at unknown words: guess them by root — you decoded words by parts yesterday. Get the shape of the whole thing. Second pass, for the detail. Now unfold the participles, read the passives as results, and follow the connectors. The words you guessed will mostly confirm themselves from context. Only now, if a key word still resists, reach for the dictionary. Two honest reminders. Don't panic at length — a long sentence is usually one short skeleton plus decoration. And don't translate into English in your head: read the Russian as Russian. That's the difference between decoding a text and reading one.","examples":[{"ru":"On the first read I look for the gist, on the second — for the details.","en":"При пе́рвом чте́нии я ищу́ смысл, при второ́м — дета́ли.","say":"При первом чтении я ищу смысл, при втором — детали."},{"ru":"I don't translate; I read Russian as Russian.","en":"Я не перевожу́ — я чита́ю ру́сский как ру́сский.","say":"Я не перевожу — я читаю русский как русский."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"stopping at every unknown word","right":"guessing by root, then reading on","why":"On the first pass, a word you can decode from its parts (yesterday's skill) is usually close enough. Stop only for the key words that resist on the second pass."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"You hit an unknown long word on the first read of an article. Best move?","opts":["stop and look it up immediately","guess it by its root and read on","skip the whole sentence"],"answer":1,"explain":"The first pass is for the gist — decode the word by its parts and keep going. Save the dictionary for the key words that still resist on the second pass."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the connector. Plain letters are fine.","q":"Type the Russian connector that means “however”:","accept":["однако","однако."],"placeholder":"одна́…","explain":"<b>одна́ко</b> = “however” — it signals a turn in the argument."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Emma and Ка́тя wrestle with a dense science article over coffee — and Emma passes on her reading method. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Э́мма, ты чита́ла э́ту статью́ о па́мяти? Она́ ужа́сно сло́жная.","ru":"Emma, have you read this article about memory? It's terribly complicated."},{"who":"B","en":"Чита́ла. Снача́ла найди́ скеле́т: кто что сде́лал. Остально́е — украше́ния.","ru":"I have. First find the skeleton: who did what. The rest is decoration."},{"who":"A","en":"Вот: «При́нятые ра́нее тео́рии тре́буют уточне́ния». Что э́то зна́чит?","ru":"Here: “The theories accepted earlier require refinement.” What does that mean?"},{"who":"B","en":"«При́нятые» — э́то «кото́рые при́няли». Тео́рии, кото́рые ра́ньше при́няли, на́до уточни́ть.","ru":"“Accepted” means “that were accepted”. The theories that were accepted earlier need refining."},{"who":"A","en":"А «вопро́с ещё не решён»? Кто не реши́л?","ru":"And “the question isn't settled yet”? Who didn't settle it?"},{"who":"B","en":"Здесь пасси́в — не ва́жно кто. Про́сто вопро́с пока́ откры́т.","ru":"This is the passive — who doesn't matter. The question is simply still open."},{"who":"A","en":"И э́ти сло́ва — «одна́ко», «тем не ме́нее» — они́ везде́.","ru":"And these words — “however”, “nevertheless” — they're everywhere."},{"who":"B","en":"Э́то свя́зки. Они́ веду́т мысль. Найдёшь их — и текст стано́вится ка́ртой.","ru":"They're connectors. They carry the thought. Find them, and the text becomes a map."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for reading the real thing — the press, the study, the glue between sentences. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"статья́","ru":"an article","ex":"Э́та статья́ о нау́ке."},{"en":"газе́та","ru":"a newspaper","ex":"Я чита́ю газе́ту ка́ждое у́тро."},{"en":"иссле́дование","ru":"a study, research","ex":"Иссле́дование дли́лось три го́да."},{"en":"учёный","ru":"a scientist, scholar","ex":"Учёный опубликова́л статью́."},{"en":"результа́т","ru":"a result","ex":"Результа́т удиви́л всех."},{"en":"вопро́с","ru":"a question, an issue","ex":"Э́то сло́жный вопро́с."},{"en":"одна́ко","ru":"however","ex":"Одна́ко я не согла́сен."},{"en":"тем не ме́нее","ru":"nevertheless","ex":"Тем не ме́нее он прав."},{"en":"по слова́м","ru":"according to (someone)","ex":"По слова́м а́втора, э́то ва́жно."},{"en":"отмеча́ть","ru":"to note, to point out","ex":"Учёные отмеча́ют рост."},{"en":"свя́зка","ru":"a connective, a linking word","ex":"«Одна́ко» — э́то свя́зка."},{"en":"смысл","ru":"meaning, sense","ex":"Я по́нял о́бщий смысл."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each phrase to its meaning.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["напи́санная статья́","the written article"],["одна́ко","however"],["по слова́м экспе́ртов","according to the experts"],["иссле́дование","a study"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"In «студе́нт, чита́ющий кни́гу», the participle «чита́ющий» is…","opts":["passive, past","active, present","passive, present"],"answer":1},{"q":"«при́нятые реше́ния» unfolds to…","opts":["реше́ния, кото́рые при́няли","реше́ния, кото́рые реша́ют","реше́ния, кото́рые при́мут"],"answer":0},{"q":"«по слова́м мини́стра» means…","opts":["spoken to the minister","according to the minister","against the minister"],"answer":1},{"q":"Which word signals a contrast in an article?","opts":["поэ́тому","одна́ко","та́кже"],"answer":1},{"q":"On your first pass through a hard text you should…","opts":["look up every unknown word","read for the gist and guess by root","translate each sentence into English"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Today's centrepiece: a real popular-science excerpt from a paper. Read it twice — gist first, then unfold every participle and passive. This is what B2 reading actually looks like.","title":"Из газе́ты: учёные и па́мять","sentences":["Гру́ппа учёных, рабо́тающих в моско́вском университе́те, опубликова́ла но́вое иссле́дование.","В статье́, напи́санной для широ́кого чита́теля, объясня́ется, как рабо́тает па́мять.","При́нятые ра́нее тео́рии, по слова́м а́второв, тре́буют уточне́ния.","Иссле́дование, проведённое за три го́да, показа́ло неожи́данные результа́ты.","Одна́ко, как отмеча́ют специали́сты, вопро́с ещё не решён оконча́тельно.","Тем не ме́нее полу́ченные да́нные уже́ испо́льзуются в но́вых ку́рсах."],"translation":"A group of scientists working at a Moscow university has published a new study. In an article written for the general reader, it is explained how memory works. The theories accepted earlier, according to the authors, require refinement. The study, carried out over three years, showed unexpected results. However, as specialists note, the question has not yet been finally resolved. Nevertheless, the data obtained is already being used in new courses."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — read like a translator.","prompt":"Find (or invent) a short news sentence packed with a participle and a passive. Rewrite it two ways: (1) unfold the participle into a «кото́рый» clause, (2) name the connector that carries the argument. Then say, in one sentence, what the article is about (о чём статья́).","hint":"Skeleton first (кто? что де́лает?), then unfold: при́нятые ме́ры → ме́ры, кото́рые при́няли; watch for одна́ко / тем не ме́нее / по слова́м. 🙂","example":"Статья́ о па́мяти. «При́нятые тео́рии тре́буют прове́рки» зна́чит: тео́рии, кото́рые при́няли, на́до прове́рить. Одна́ко, по слова́м учёных, вопро́с ещё не решён."}},"24":{"day":24,"week":"04","themeRu":"Finer shades","themeEn":"Сравне́ние, сле́дует, наве́рное","intro":"Emma's translation is finished — but “finished” and “polished” are two different words. Today we sand the edges. Not new machinery, just the finer shades that make writing sound grown-up: the one-word superlative <b>важне́йший</b>, the impersonal <b>сле́дует</b> and <b>прихо́дится</b>, and the little parenthetical words — <b>коне́чно</b>, <b>наве́рное</b>, <b>по-мо́ему</b> — that a Russian threads through every careful sentence. And watch the commas: they're part of the shade.","introAudio":"Emma's translation is finished — but 'finished' and 'polished' are two different words. Today we sand the edges. Not new machinery, just the finer shades that make writing sound grown-up: the one-word superlative vazhneyshiy, the impersonal sleduyet and prikhoditsya, and the little parenthetical words — konechno, navernoye, po-moyemu — that a Russian threads through every careful sentence. And watch the commas: they're part of the shade.","goals":["Use the one-word superlative: <b>ва́жный → важне́йший</b> (“a most important…”), <b>ближа́йший</b> (the nearest)","Compare cleanly with <b>бо́лее / ме́нее</b> — and never stack a double comparative (бо́лее ста́рее ❌)","Say “one should / one has to” impersonally: <b>сле́дует прочита́ть</b>, <b>мне прихо́дится ждать</b>, <b>ей удаётся</b>","Drop in parenthetical words — <b>коне́чно, наве́рное, по-мо́ему, к сожале́нию</b> — set off with commas"],"learned":["Formed one-word superlatives with -ейш-/-айш- (важне́йший, ближа́йший)","Compared with бо́лее/ме́нее and avoided the double comparative","Used impersonal сле́дует / сто́ит / прихо́дится / удаётся with an infinitive","Set off parenthetical words (вводные слова) with commas"],"review":{"intro":"Polishing rests on foundations — so a quick warm-up: a plain B1 comparative, yesterday's word-building, and an always-on participle in a case.","introAudio":"Polishing rests on foundations — so a quick warm-up: a plain B1 comparative, yesterday's word-building, and an always-on participle in a case.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer.","q":"“My brother is older than me.” (simple comparative)","opts":["Мой брат ста́рше меня́.","Мой брат бо́лее ста́рше меня́.","Мой брат ста́ре чем я."],"answer":0,"explain":"The one-word comparative <b>ста́рше</b> + a genitive (меня́). «бо́лее ста́рше» stacks two comparatives (❌); «ста́ре» isn't the form."},{"type":"choice","q":"The noun «ва́жность» (importance) is built with -ость from…","opts":["the adjective ва́жный","the adverb ва́жно","the comparative важне́е"],"answer":0,"explain":"-ость turns an adjective into a quality-noun: <b>ва́жный → ва́жность</b>. (Yesterday's word-building.)"},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“a decision taken at the meeting”</b> (taken = при́нятое)","want":"реше́ние, при́нятое на собра́нии","answer":"реше́ние, при́нятое на собра́нии"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The one-word superlative — <em>важне́йший</em>","title":"<em>ва́жный → важне́йший</em>: “a most important…”","paras":["You already make superlatives with <b>са́мый</b>: <b>са́мый ва́жный</b> (the most important). Written Russian has a tighter, bookish alternative — one word, ending in <b>-ейший</b> or <b>-айший</b>: <b>важне́йший</b> (a most important / crucial), <b>сложне́йший</b> (a most complex), <b>велича́йший</b> (the greatest).","The <b>-айший</b> variant shows up after к/г/х, with the usual consonant shift: <b>бли́зкий → ближа́йший</b> (the nearest), <b>высо́кий → высоча́йший</b> (the highest), <b>глубо́кий → глубоча́йший</b> (the deepest). You'll meet these constantly in articles and formal prose.","A shade of meaning: this one-word form often means “extremely / one of the very…”, not literally “the single most”. <b>важне́йший вопро́с</b> = “a crucial question”. When you truly mean “the #1”, <b>са́мый</b> is still clearest. Both are correct — the one-word form just sounds more written."],"audio":"You already make superlatives with samy: samy vazhny, the most important. Written Russian has a tighter, bookish alternative — one word, ending in -eyshiy or -ayshiy: vazhneyshiy, a most important or crucial; slozhneyshiy, a most complex; velichayshiy, the greatest. The -ayshiy variant shows up after k, g, kh, with the usual consonant shift: blizkiy becomes blizhayshiy, the nearest; vysokiy becomes vysochayshiy, the highest; glubokiy becomes glubochayshiy, the deepest. You'll meet these constantly in articles and formal prose. A shade of meaning: this one-word form often means 'extremely' or 'one of the very', not literally 'the single most'. Vazhneyshiy vopros equals a crucial question. When you truly mean 'the number one', samy is still clearest. Both are correct — the one-word form just sounds more written.","table":{"rows":[["ва́жный → важне́йший <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"важный, важнейший\">🔊</button>","important → a most important, crucial"],["сло́жный → сложне́йший <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"сложный, сложнейший\">🔊</button>","complex → a most complex"],["бли́зкий → ближа́йший <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"близкий, ближайший\">🔊</button>","near → the nearest (-айший)"],["высо́кий → высоча́йший <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"высокий, высочайший\">🔊</button>","high → the highest (-айший)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"This is a most important question for the whole country.","en":"Э́то <b>важне́йший</b> вопро́с для всей страны́.","say":"Это важнейший вопрос для всей страны."},{"ru":"The nearest metro is five minutes away.","en":"<b>Ближа́йшее</b> метро́ в пяти́ мину́тах.","say":"Ближайшее метро в пяти минутах."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"са́мый важне́йший","right":"важне́йший (и́ли са́мый ва́жный)","why":"Don't combine them — <b>важне́йший</b> already contains the superlative. Use either the one word (важне́йший) or «са́мый ва́жный», never both."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"The one-word superlative of <b>бли́зкий</b> (near) is…","opts":["бли́зейший","ближа́йший","бо́лее бли́зкий"],"answer":1,"explain":"After -з-/к the form is <b>-айший</b> with a shift: бли́зкий → <b>ближа́йший</b> (the nearest). бо́лее бли́зкий is a comparative, not a superlative."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Plain letters are fine.","q":"Make the one-word superlative “a most important” from <b>ва́жный</b>:","accept":["важнейший","важнейший."],"placeholder":"важн…","explain":"ва́жный → <b>важне́йший</b> (the -ейший superlative)."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Compare cleanly — no double comparative","title":"<em>бо́лее сло́жный</em>, not <em>бо́лее сло́жнее</em>","paras":["Two ways to say “more X”, and you must pick <b>one</b>. Either the one-word comparative you know — <b>сложне́е</b> (more complex), <b>ста́рше</b> (older) — or the two-word <b>бо́лее + plain adjective</b> — <b>бо́лее сло́жный</b>, <b>бо́лее ста́рый</b>. Both are right. What's wrong is <b>combining</b> them.","This is the top B2 comparison error for English speakers: <b>бо́лее сло́жнее</b> ❌, <b>бо́лее ста́рее</b> ❌. English can't do it either (“more older”), and neither can Russian. Say <b>ста́рше</b> or <b>бо́лее ста́рый</b> — never <b>бо́лее ста́рше</b>. Same with <b>ме́нее</b> (less): <b>ме́нее ва́жный</b>, not <b>ме́нее важне́е</b>.","Two small helpers. After a comparative, “than” is either <b>чем</b> (with a comma: сложне́е, <b>чем</b> ра́ньше) or a bare <b>genitive</b> (сложне́е ста́рого те́кста). And for a simile — “as if / like” — use <b>сло́вно</b> or <b>бу́дто</b>: «Он говори́т, <b>сло́вно</b> чита́ет ле́кцию» (he talks as if he's giving a lecture)."],"audio":"Two ways to say 'more X', and you must pick one. Either the one-word comparative you know — slozhneye, more complex; starshe, older — or the two-word boleye plus a plain adjective — boleye slozhny, boleye stary. Both are right. What's wrong is combining them. This is the top B2 comparison error for English speakers: boleye slozhneye, no; boleye stareye, no. English can't do it either — 'more older' — and neither can Russian. Say starshe or boleye stary, never boleye starshe. Same with meneye, less: meneye vazhny, not meneye vazhneye. Two small helpers. After a comparative, 'than' is either chem, with a comma, or a bare genitive. And for a simile — 'as if' or 'like' — use slovno or budto: on govorit, slovno chitayet lektsiyu, he talks as if he's giving a lecture.","table":{"rows":[["бо́лее сло́жный <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"более сложный\">🔊</button>","more complex (two-word form)"],["сложне́е <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"сложнее\">🔊</button>","more complex (one-word form)"],["сло́вно / бу́дто <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"словно, будто\">🔊</button>","as if / like (a simile)"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"This text is more complex than the previous one.","en":"Э́тот текст <b>сложне́е</b> предыду́щего.","say":"Этот текст сложнее предыдущего."},{"ru":"He speaks as if he's giving a lecture.","en":"Он говори́т, <b>сло́вно</b> чита́ет ле́кцию.","say":"Он говорит, словно читает лекцию."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"бо́лее сло́жнее","right":"сложне́е (и́ли бо́лее сло́жный)","why":"Never stack two comparatives. Pick one: the one-word <b>сложне́е</b>, or <b>бо́лее</b> + the plain adjective (бо́лее сло́жный)."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which is correct for “more important”?","opts":["важне́е","бо́лее важне́е","са́мее ва́жный"],"answer":0,"explain":"One comparative only: <b>важне́е</b> (one word). «бо́лее важне́е» stacks two comparatives (❌); «са́мее» isn't a word."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“This book is more interesting than that one.”</b> (one-word comparative + genitive)","want":"Э́та кни́га интере́снее той","answer":"Э́та кни́га интере́снее той"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · “One should, one has to” — <em>сле́дует, прихо́дится</em>","title":"<em>сле́дует прочита́ть</em>, <em>мне прихо́дится ждать</em>","paras":["Careful writing leans on <b>impersonal</b> verbs — no “I/you/he” subject, just a bare form plus an infinitive. <b>сле́дует</b> + infinitive = “one should / ought to”: <b>Сле́дует прочита́ть догово́р внима́тельно</b> (one should read the contract carefully). <b>сто́ит</b> + infinitive = “it's worth”: <b>Сто́ит попро́бовать</b> (it's worth trying).","Two carry a <b>dative</b> person — the one it happens to. <b>прихо́дится</b> (+ dat.) = “has to, is forced to”: <b>Мне прихо́дится мно́го рабо́тать</b> (I have to work a lot). <b>удаётся</b> (+ dat.) = “manages, succeeds”: <b>Ей удаётся всё</b> (she manages everything). No «я/она́» — the person sits in the dative.","And <b>ка́жется</b> = “it seems”: <b>Ка́жется, он прав</b> (it seems he's right), or with a dative — <b>Мне ка́жется</b> (it seems to me). These impersonals are the quiet backbone of formal Russian: they state what should, must, or seems to be, without pointing a finger."],"audio":"Careful writing leans on impersonal verbs — no I, you or he subject, just a bare form plus an infinitive. Sleduyet plus an infinitive means 'one should' or 'ought to': sleduyet prochitat dogovor vnimatelno, one should read the contract carefully. Stoit plus an infinitive means 'it's worth': stoit poprobovat, it's worth trying. Two carry a dative person — the one it happens to. Prikhoditsya plus the dative means 'has to, is forced to': mne prikhoditsya mnogo rabotat, I have to work a lot. Udayotsya plus the dative means 'manages, succeeds': yey udayotsya vsyo, she manages everything. No ya or ona — the person sits in the dative. And kazhetsya means 'it seems': kazhetsya, on prav, it seems he's right, or with a dative, mne kazhetsya, it seems to me. These impersonals are the quiet backbone of formal Russian.","table":{"rows":[["сле́дует прочита́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"следует прочитать\">🔊</button>","one should read"],["сто́ит попро́бовать <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"стоит попробовать\">🔊</button>","it's worth trying"],["мне прихо́дится ждать <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"мне приходится ждать\">🔊</button>","I have to wait (dative)"],["ей удаётся всё <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ей удаётся всё\">🔊</button>","she manages everything (dative)"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"One should read this contract very carefully.","en":"Э́тот догово́р <b>сле́дует</b> прочита́ть о́чень внима́тельно.","say":"Этот договор следует прочитать очень внимательно."},{"ru":"Unfortunately, I often have to work at weekends.","en":"К сожале́нию, мне ча́сто <b>прихо́дится</b> рабо́тать по выходны́м.","say":"К сожалению, мне часто приходится работать по выходным."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"я сле́дую прочита́ть","right":"мне сле́дует прочита́ть / сле́дует прочита́ть","why":"<b>сле́дует</b> is impersonal — no “I” subject. Say <b>сле́дует прочита́ть</b> (one should read), optionally with a dative person (мне сле́дует)."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“I have to work a lot.” (impersonal, with a dative)","opts":["Я прихожу́ мно́го рабо́тать.","Мне прихо́дится мно́го рабо́тать.","Я прихо́дится мно́го рабо́тать."],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>прихо́дится</b> takes a dative person: <b>мне прихо́дится</b> (I have to). «я прихожу́» means “I arrive”; «я прихо́дится» mixes a nominative with an impersonal verb."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the one verb. Plain letters are fine.","q":"Complete impersonally: “One should read this.” — «Э́то … прочита́ть.»","accept":["следует","следует."],"placeholder":"сле́…","explain":"<b>сле́дует</b> + infinitive = “one should”: Э́то сле́дует прочита́ть."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Parenthetical words — <em>коне́чно, наве́рное</em> — and their commas","title":"<em>Он, коне́чно, прав</em> — set off вводные слова","paras":["Russians thread little <b>parenthetical words</b> (вводные слова) through their sentences to signal attitude — how sure, whose view, what a shame. The essentials: <b>коне́чно</b> (of course), <b>наве́рное</b> (probably), <b>по-мо́ему</b> (in my opinion), <b>к сожале́нию</b> (unfortunately), <b>ка́жется</b> (it seems), <b>во-пе́рвых / во-вторы́х</b> (firstly / secondly).","The rule that trips everyone: a parenthetical is <b>always set off by commas</b>, because it isn't part of the sentence's grammar — you could lift it out. <b>Он, коне́чно, прав.</b> <b>По-мо́ему, э́то ва́жно.</b> <b>Э́то, к сожале́нию, пра́вда.</b> The commas are what mark it as an aside.","One false-friend warning you met before: <b>коне́чно</b> here means “of course”, not “finally” — and in speech its «ч» sounds like [ш] ([кане́шна]). Sprinkle these words in and your Russian instantly sounds more native; forget the commas and it reads like a learner's."],"audio":"Russians thread little parenthetical words, vvodnye slova, through their sentences to signal attitude — how sure, whose view, what a shame. The essentials: konechno, of course; navernoye, probably; po-moyemu, in my opinion; k sozhaleniyu, unfortunately; kazhetsya, it seems; vo-pervykh and vo-vtorykh, firstly and secondly. The rule that trips everyone: a parenthetical is always set off by commas, because it isn't part of the sentence's grammar — you could lift it out. On, konechno, prav. Po-moyemu, eto vazhno. Eto, k sozhaleniyu, pravda. The commas mark it as an aside. One false-friend warning you met before: konechno here means 'of course', not 'finally' — and in speech its ch sounds like sh, kaneshna. Sprinkle these words in and your Russian instantly sounds more native; forget the commas and it reads like a learner's.","table":{"rows":[["Он, коне́чно, прав. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Он, конечно, прав\">🔊</button>","He is, of course, right."],["По-мо́ему, э́то ва́жно. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"По-моему, это важно\">🔊</button>","In my opinion, this is important."],["Э́то, к сожале́нию, пра́вда. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Это, к сожалению, правда\">🔊</button>","This is, unfortunately, true."]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Probably, we'll finish the work tomorrow.","en":"<b>Наве́рное</b>, мы зако́нчим рабо́ту за́втра.","say":"Наверное, мы закончим работу завтра."},{"ru":"In my opinion, this is the best translation.","en":"<b>По-мо́ему</b>, э́то лу́чший перево́д.","say":"По-моему, это лучший перевод."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Он коне́чно прав","right":"Он, коне́чно, прав","why":"A parenthetical needs commas on both sides: <b>Он, коне́чно, прав.</b> Without them, the aside runs into the sentence."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which is punctuated correctly?","opts":["По-мо́ему э́то ва́жно.","По-мо́ему, э́то ва́жно.","По, мо́ему, э́то ва́жно."],"answer":1,"explain":"The parenthetical «по-мо́ему» is set off by a comma: <b>По-мо́ему, э́то ва́жно.</b> It's one hyphenated word, not «по, мо́ему»."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Э́то, коне́чно, важне́йший вопро́с.","sub":"“This is, of course, a crucial question” — an aside in commas plus a one-word superlative","want":"это конечно важнейший вопрос"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Emma reads a line of her translation to Макси́м, and the two of them sand it into something sharper. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Макси́м, послу́шай фра́зу из перево́да. По-мо́ему, она́ звучи́т сли́шком про́сто.","ru":"Maxim, listen to a phrase from my translation. In my opinion, it sounds too plain."},{"who":"A","en":"Прочита́й. Как инжене́р, я люблю́ то́чность.","ru":"Read it out. As an engineer, I love precision."},{"who":"B","en":"«Э́то ва́жный вопро́с». Хочу́ сильне́е — «важне́йший»?","ru":"“This is an important question.” I want it stronger — “most important”?"},{"who":"A","en":"Да, «важне́йший вопро́с» звучи́т серьёзнее. Одно́ сло́во вме́сто «са́мый ва́жный».","ru":"Yes, “a crucial question” sounds more serious. One word instead of “the most important”."},{"who":"B","en":"А ещё: сле́дует ли писа́ть «сле́дует рассмотре́ть» вме́сто «на́до рассмотре́ть»?","ru":"And also: should I write “one should examine” instead of “need to examine”?"},{"who":"A","en":"В докуме́нте — да. «Сле́дует» — э́то стро́же, официа́льнее.","ru":"In a document — yes. “Сле́дует” is stricter, more formal."},{"who":"B","en":"И, коне́чно, запяты́е вокру́г «коне́чно». Без них — оши́бка.","ru":"And, of course, commas around “of course”. Without them — a mistake."},{"who":"A","en":"Ви́дишь, наве́рное, ты уже́ пи́шешь то́чнее меня́.","ru":"See, you probably already write more precisely than me."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the finer shades — superlatives, comparatives, impersonals, and the little asides. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"важне́йший","ru":"most important, crucial","ex":"Э́то важне́йший вопро́с."},{"en":"ближа́йший","ru":"the nearest, the closest","ex":"Ближа́йший по́езд в семь."},{"en":"сложне́е","ru":"more complex","ex":"Э́тот текст сложне́е того́."},{"en":"бо́лее","ru":"more (with an adjective)","ex":"Э́то бо́лее ва́жная зада́ча."},{"en":"сло́вно","ru":"as if, like","ex":"Он молчи́т, сло́вно спит."},{"en":"сле́дует","ru":"one should, ought to","ex":"Сле́дует прочита́ть внима́тельно."},{"en":"сто́ит","ru":"it's worth (doing)","ex":"Сто́ит попро́бовать ещё раз."},{"en":"прихо́дится","ru":"one has to, is forced to","ex":"Мне прихо́дится до́лго ждать."},{"en":"удаётся","ru":"one manages, succeeds","ex":"Ей всё удаётся легко́."},{"en":"коне́чно","ru":"of course","ex":"Ты, коне́чно, прав."},{"en":"наве́рное","ru":"probably","ex":"Наве́рное, бу́дет дождь."},{"en":"по-мо́ему","ru":"in my opinion","ex":"По-мо́ему, э́то отли́чно."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each phrase to its meaning.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["важне́йший","most important, crucial"],["сле́дует прочита́ть","one should read"],["по-мо́ему","in my opinion"],["бо́лее сло́жный","more complex"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"The one-word superlative of <b>ва́жный</b> is…","opts":["важне́е","важне́йший","са́мый важне́йший"],"answer":1},{"q":"Which is correct — “more difficult”?","opts":["бо́лее трудне́е","трудне́е","са́мый тру́дный"],"answer":1},{"q":"“One should read this.” →","opts":["Я сле́дую прочита́ть э́то.","Э́то сле́дует прочита́ю.","Э́то сле́дует прочита́ть."],"answer":2},{"q":"Which sentence sets off the aside correctly?","opts":["Он наве́рное прав.","Он, наве́рное, прав.","Он наве́рное, прав."],"answer":1},{"q":"«прихо́дится» takes the person in which case?","opts":["nominative (я)","dative (мне)","accusative (меня́)"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma on the art of the finish — read it aloud and catch every shade and every comma.","title":"Отте́нки смы́сла","sentences":["Перево́д гото́в, но его́ сле́дует отшлифова́ть.","«Ва́жный» стано́вится «важне́йшим», е́сли вопро́с действи́тельно серьёзный.","Иногда́ мне прихо́дится выбира́ть ме́жду то́чностью и красото́й.","По-мо́ему, хоро́ший текст — э́то текст без ли́шних слов.","И, коне́чно, я не забыва́ю о запяты́х вокру́г вво́дных слов.","Ка́жется, э́то и есть настоя́щее мастерство́ перево́дчика."],"translation":"The translation is ready, but it should be polished. «Important» becomes «most important» if the question is truly serious. Sometimes I have to choose between precision and beauty. In my opinion, a good text is a text without extra words. And, of course, I don't forget the commas around parenthetical words. It seems this is exactly what a translator's real mastery is."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — polish, don't just finish.","prompt":"Write four sentences about something you care about, using at least one from each shade: a one-word superlative (важне́йший, ближа́йший), an impersonal (сле́дует / прихо́дится / сто́ит), and a parenthetical word set off with commas (коне́чно, наве́рное, по-мо́ему). Check every comma.","hint":"One comparative only (сложне́е OR бо́лее сло́жный, never both); сле́дует/сто́ит + infinitive; commas both sides of the aside (Он, коне́чно, прав). 🙂","example":"По-мо́ему, э́то важне́йший вопро́с на́шего вре́мени. Его́ сле́дует обсужда́ть споко́йно. Иногда́, коне́чно, прихо́дится спо́рить. Но реше́ние на́до иска́ть вме́сте."}},"25":{"day":25,"week":"04","themeRu":"What comes first","themeEn":"Поря́док слов: те́ма и ре́ма","intro":"Two Russian sentences can have the exact same words and mean different things. «Кни́гу написа́л он» and «Он написа́л кни́гу» both say “he wrote the book” — but they answer different questions. Today's finale for your grammar is word order. English locks subject-verb-object and marks what's new with “a/the” and stress. Russian sets the words free — the endings keep track of who did what — and uses <b>position</b> itself to say what's known and what's news.","introAudio":"Two Russian sentences can have the exact same words and mean different things. Knigu napisal on, and on napisal knigu, both say 'he wrote the book' — but they answer different questions. Today's finale for your grammar is word order. English locks subject-verb-object and marks what's new with 'a' or 'the' and stress. Russian sets the words free — the endings keep track of who did what — and uses position itself to say what's known and what's news.","goals":["Put the <b>known</b> first and the <b>new</b> (the ре́ма) last: <b>Э́ту статью́ написа́л изве́стный учёный</b>","Reorder the very same words to change the message — «Кни́гу написа́л <b>он</b>» answers “who?”","Spotlight the new part with particles: <b>и́менно, то́лько, да́же, же</b>","Trust the <b>endings</b> to hold “who did what”, so word order is free to carry focus"],"learned":["Placed known info first and the new part (ре́ма) at the end","Reordered identical words to shift the focus and the answer","Used particles (и́менно, то́лько, да́же, же) to spotlight the new part","Let the case endings hold the roles while order carried emphasis"],"review":{"intro":"Word order rides on the cases — so let's wake them: the endings that mark who acts, a clean comparative, and yesterday's impersonal.","introAudio":"Word order rides on the cases — so let's wake them: the endings that mark who acts, a clean comparative, and yesterday's impersonal.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer.","q":"Which sentence means the DOG did the biting (the dog bit the man)?","opts":["Соба́ка укуси́ла челове́ка.","Соба́ку укуси́л челове́к.","Соба́ке укуси́л челове́к."],"answer":0,"explain":"The endings, not the order, mark who acts: <b>соба́ка</b> (nominative) bit <b>челове́ка</b> (accusative). Option 2 reverses the roles; option 3 puts «dog» in the dative."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which is correct — “more interesting”?","opts":["бо́лее интере́снее","интере́снее","са́мее интере́сный"],"answer":1,"explain":"One comparative only: <b>интере́снее</b> (or бо́лее интере́сный). «бо́лее интере́снее» stacks two (❌); «са́мее» isn't a word."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“One should read this article.”</b> (impersonal сле́дует)","want":"Э́ту статью́ сле́дует прочита́ть","answer":"Э́ту статью́ сле́дует прочита́ть"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Known first, new last","title":"<em>те́ма</em> (known) → <em>ре́ма</em> (new) at the end","paras":["Every Russian sentence has two parts by <b>information</b>, not by grammar. The <b>те́ма</b> — what we're already talking about, the old news — comes <b>first</b>. The <b>ре́ма</b> — the new information, the point — comes <b>last</b>, where the voice naturally stresses it. Neutral Russian order is: known → new.","Watch it work. «Э́ту статью́ написа́л изве́стный учёный» — we already know about the article (тема, first), and the news is <b>who wrote it</b>: изве́стный учёный (рема, last). Flip the focus and you flip the order: «Изве́стный учёный написа́л <b>э́ту статью́</b>» — now the scientist is old news and the article is the point.","English can't slide words like this — it keeps subject-verb-object and marks the new part with “a/the” and voice: “<b>a</b> famous scientist wrote the article” vs “the scientist wrote <b>an</b> article”. Russian has no articles, so it moves the words instead. Same information job, different tool."],"audio":"Every Russian sentence has two parts by information, not by grammar. The tema — what we're already talking about, the old news — comes first. The rema — the new information, the point — comes last, where the voice naturally stresses it. Neutral Russian order is: known, then new. Watch it work. Etu statyu napisal izvestny uchyony — we already know about the article, tema, first, and the news is who wrote it: izvestny uchyony, rema, last. Flip the focus and you flip the order: izvestny uchyony napisal etu statyu — now the scientist is old news and the article is the point. English can't slide words like this — it keeps subject-verb-object and marks the new part with 'a' or 'the' and voice: a famous scientist wrote the article, versus the scientist wrote an article. Russian has no articles, so it moves the words instead. Same job, different tool.","table":{"rows":[["Э́ту статью́ написа́л изве́стный учёный. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Эту статью написал известный учёный\">🔊</button>","(who wrote it?) — a famous scientist did"],["Изве́стный учёный написа́л э́ту статью́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Известный учёный написал эту статью\">🔊</button>","(what did he write?) — this article"],["ре́ма — в конце́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"рема в конце\">🔊</button>","the new info goes at the end"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"“Ivan phoned.” — answering “who phoned?” (Ivan is the news).","en":"Позвони́л <b>Ива́н</b>.","say":"Позвонил Иван."},{"ru":"“Ivan phoned.” — answering “what did Ivan do?” (phoned is the news).","en":"<b>Ива́н</b> позвони́л.","say":"Иван позвонил."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"always Subject-Verb-Object, like English","right":"known first, new (ре́ма) last","why":"Russian order follows <b>information</b>, not a fixed S-V-O. Put what's already known first and the new point at the end, where the stress lands."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"Someone asks «Кто написа́л статью́?» (Who wrote the article?). The most natural answer is…","opts":["Статью́ написа́л профе́ссор.","Профе́ссор написа́л статью́.","Написа́л профе́ссор статью́."],"answer":0,"explain":"The news is <b>who</b> — so the writer goes to the end (the ре́ма): «Статью́ написа́л <b>профе́ссор</b>». The known part (статью́) leads."},{"type":"build","q":"Answer «Что де́лает Э́мма?» — put the new part last: <b>“Emma is translating a book.”</b>","want":"Э́мма перево́дит кни́гу","answer":"Э́мма перево́дит кни́гу"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Same words, new message","title":"<em>Кни́гу написа́л он</em> vs <em>Он написа́л кни́гу</em>","paras":["Because the <b>endings</b> already say who's the subject and who's the object, Russian is free to shuffle the words — and it uses that freedom to change the <b>focus</b>. Same three words, different point: «<b>Он</b> написа́л кни́гу» (what did he do? — wrote a book) vs «Кни́гу написа́л <b>он</b>» (who wrote the book? — he did).","Notice nothing about “who did what” changes: <b>он</b> is nominative (the writer) and <b>кни́гу</b> is accusative (the thing written) in <b>both</b> sentences. The case tags travel with the words. Only the last slot — the ре́ма — moves, and with it the answer the sentence gives.","This is why a Russian sentence can feel “backwards” to an English ear: «Пода́рок мне подари́л Ива́н» = “It was Ivan who gave me the present.” The object leads, the giver lands last because <b>who</b> is the news. Read the endings, and it's perfectly clear."],"audio":"Because the endings already say who's the subject and who's the object, Russian is free to shuffle the words — and it uses that freedom to change the focus. Same three words, different point: on napisal knigu, what did he do, wrote a book, versus knigu napisal on, who wrote the book, he did. Notice nothing about who did what changes: on is nominative, the writer, and knigu is accusative, the thing written, in both sentences. The case tags travel with the words. Only the last slot — the rema — moves, and with it the answer the sentence gives. This is why a Russian sentence can feel backwards to an English ear: podarok mne podaril Ivan equals it was Ivan who gave me the present. The object leads, the giver lands last because who is the news. Read the endings, and it's perfectly clear.","table":{"rows":[["Он написа́л кни́гу. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Он написал книгу\">🔊</button>","(what did he do?) — wrote a book"],["Кни́гу написа́л он. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Книгу написал он\">🔊</button>","(who wrote it?) — he did"],["Пода́рок подари́л Ива́н. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Подарок подарил Иван\">🔊</button>","(who gave it?) — Ivan did"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"“It was Emma who translated this novel.” (who? — Emma, at the end)","en":"Э́тот рома́н перевела́ <b>Э́мма</b>.","say":"Этот роман перевела Эмма."},{"ru":"“Emma translated this novel.” (what did she do?)","en":"<b>Э́мма</b> перевела́ э́тот рома́н.","say":"Эмма перевела этот роман."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Кни́гу он написа́л» to mean “he wrote a BOOK”","right":"«Он написа́л кни́гу» — new part (кни́гу) last","why":"To make “a book” the news, it goes <b>last</b>: «Он написа́л <b>кни́гу</b>». Putting кни́гу first makes the book the known topic, not the point."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Э́ту статью́ перевела́ Э́мма» most naturally answers…","opts":["Что сде́лала Э́мма?","Кто перевёл статью́?","Каку́ю статью́ перевела́ Э́мма?"],"answer":1,"explain":"Э́мма is last → she's the ре́ма → the sentence answers <b>“who translated the article?”</b> — Emma did."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type one word. Plain letters are fine.","q":"Reorder to answer “WHO wrote the letter?” — put the doer last: «Письмо́ написа́л …» (the name “Ivan”):","accept":["иван","иван."],"placeholder":"…","explain":"The doer is the news, so it lands last: «Письмо́ написа́л <b>Ива́н</b>». (Ива́н is nominative — the writer.)"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Spotlight it — <em>и́менно, то́лько, да́же, же</em>","title":"Particles that point at the ре́ма","paras":["Sometimes you want to spotlight a word wherever it sits. Russian has little <b>particles</b> that clip onto a word and shout “this one!”. <b>и́менно</b> = “precisely, exactly this”: <b>И́менно он написа́л статью́</b> (it was precisely HE who wrote it). <b>то́лько</b> = “only”: <b>То́лько она́ зна́ет пра́вду</b> (only she knows the truth).","<b>да́же</b> = “even”: <b>Да́же Ива́н э́того не знал</b> (even Ivan didn't know). And the tiny <b>же</b> leans on the word before it for insistence: <b>Ты же зна́ешь!</b> (but you DO know!), <b>Что же де́лать?</b> (so what's to be done?). It's a nudge, not a word you translate.","These do in one particle what English does with heavy stress or “it is… who”: “it was <b>Ivan</b> who…”, “<b>only</b> she…”, “<b>even</b> he…”. Drop them onto the ре́ма and the focus is unmistakable — in speech and on the page."],"audio":"Sometimes you want to spotlight a word wherever it sits. Russian has little particles that clip onto a word and shout 'this one!'. Imenno means 'precisely, exactly this': imenno on napisal statyu, it was precisely he who wrote it. Tolko means 'only': tolko ona znayet pravdu, only she knows the truth. Dazhe means 'even': dazhe Ivan etogo ne znal, even Ivan didn't know. And the tiny zhe leans on the word before it for insistence: ty zhe znayesh, but you do know; chto zhe delat, so what's to be done? It's a nudge, not a word you translate. These do in one particle what English does with heavy stress or 'it is who': it was Ivan who, only she, even he. Drop them onto the rema and the focus is unmistakable — in speech and on the page.","table":{"rows":[["И́менно он написа́л статью́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Именно он написал статью\">🔊</button>","It was precisely HE who wrote it."],["То́лько она́ зна́ет пра́вду. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Только она знает правду\">🔊</button>","Only she knows the truth."],["Да́же Ива́н э́того не знал. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Даже Иван этого не знал\">🔊</button>","Even Ivan didn't know this."]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"It was precisely this book that Emma translated.","en":"<b>И́менно</b> э́ту кни́гу перевела́ Э́мма.","say":"Именно эту книгу перевела Эмма."},{"ru":"Only Maxim understood the problem.","en":"<b>То́лько</b> Макси́м по́нял пробле́му.","say":"Только Максим понял проблему."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«же» = “also”","right":"«же» = insistence / “but you know…”","why":"<b>же</b> isn't “also” (that's <b>то́же/та́кже</b>). It's an emphatic nudge on the word before it: <b>Ты же обеща́л!</b> — “but you promised!”"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“Only Emma knows this.” Which particle makes “only”?","opts":["да́же","то́лько","же"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>то́лько</b> = “only”: То́лько Э́мма э́то зна́ет. да́же = “even”; же is an emphatic nudge."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"И́менно э́ту статью́ написа́л Ива́н.","sub":"“It was precisely this article that Ivan wrote” — и́менно spotlights the focus","want":"именно эту статью написал иван"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · The endings do the bookkeeping","title":"Move the words — the cases keep the roles","paras":["Step back and see the whole design. English has almost no endings, so it <b>must</b> keep order fixed: “dog bites man” and “man bites dog” differ only by position. Russian marks the subject and object with <b>case endings</b>, so the roles are safe no matter where the words go. That's what buys the freedom you used all day.","So the two systems split the labour differently. English: order carries <b>grammar</b> (who's subject), articles and stress carry <b>focus</b> (what's new). Russian: endings carry <b>grammar</b>, and order carries <b>focus</b> — new to the end, or a particle to spotlight. Neither is freer; they just hand the same two jobs to different tools.","For your own writing, one habit pays off: after you've built a sentence, ask <b>“what's the news here?”</b> and slide that word to the end (or tag it with и́менно/то́лько). It's the last polish — and it's what makes your Russian read as arranged, not just assembled. That's the whole of B2 word order."],"audio":"Step back and see the whole design. English has almost no endings, so it must keep order fixed: 'dog bites man' and 'man bites dog' differ only by position. Russian marks the subject and object with case endings, so the roles are safe no matter where the words go. That's what buys the freedom you used all day. So the two systems split the labour differently. English: order carries grammar, who's the subject; articles and stress carry focus, what's new. Russian: endings carry grammar, and order carries focus — new to the end, or a particle to spotlight. Neither is freer; they just hand the same two jobs to different tools. For your own writing, one habit pays off: after you've built a sentence, ask 'what's the news here?' and slide that word to the end, or tag it with imenno or tolko. It's the last polish — and it's what makes your Russian read as arranged, not just assembled. That's the whole of B2 word order.","table":{"rows":[["Соба́ка укуси́ла челове́ка. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Собака укусила человека\">🔊</button>","the dog (nom) bit the man (acc)"],["Челове́ка укуси́ла соба́ка. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Человека укусила собака\">🔊</button>","same roles — endings unchanged, focus moved"],["Что здесь но́вое? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Что здесь новое?\">🔊</button>","ask: what's the news? → send it last"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"The endings show who acts; the order shows what's new.","en":"Оконча́ния пока́зывают, кто де́йствует; поря́док — что но́вое.","say":"Окончания показывают, кто действует; порядок — что новое."},{"ru":"Move the new word to the end — and the phrase comes alive.","en":"Поста́вь но́вое сло́во в коне́ц — и фра́за зазвучи́т.","say":"Поставь новое слово в конец — и фраза зазвучит."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"changing the endings when you reorder","right":"keep the endings, only move the words","why":"The case ending belongs to the word's <b>role</b>, not its position. «челове́ка» stays accusative wherever it stands. Move the word; never change its ending to fit a new slot."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Челове́ка укуси́ла соба́ка» — who did the biting?","opts":["the man","the dog","can't tell without the order"],"answer":1,"explain":"The endings decide, not the order: соба́ка is nominative (the biter), челове́ка is accusative (the bitten). <b>The dog</b> bit the man, even though “man” comes first."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the particle. Plain letters are fine.","q":"Type the Russian particle that means “precisely / exactly this”:","accept":["именно","именно."],"placeholder":"и́…","explain":"<b>и́менно</b> spotlights the focus: и́менно он — “precisely he”."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Over the counter at Ле́на's café, Emma untangles the great trick of Russian: same words, new message. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Э́мма, объясни́ мне одну́ вещь. Почему́ «Кни́гу написа́л он» и «Он написа́л кни́гу» — не одно́ и то же?","ru":"Emma, explain one thing to me. Why are «Кни́гу написа́л он» and «Он написа́л кни́гу» not the same?"},{"who":"B","en":"Слова́ те же, но нови́нка ра́зная. В конце́ стои́т са́мое ва́жное.","ru":"The words are the same, but the news is different. The most important thing stands at the end."},{"who":"A","en":"Зна́чит, «Кни́гу написа́л он» отвеча́ет на вопро́с «кто»?","ru":"So «Кни́гу написа́л он» answers the question “who”?"},{"who":"B","en":"И́менно. А «Он написа́л кни́гу» — на вопро́с «что он сде́лал».","ru":"Exactly. And «Он написа́л кни́гу» — the question “what did he do”."},{"who":"A","en":"А падежи́? Слова́ дви́гаются — и не пу́таются?","ru":"And the cases? The words move — and don't get muddled?"},{"who":"B","en":"Оконча́ния де́ржат ро́ли. «Кни́гу» — вини́тельный, куда́ его́ ни поста́вь.","ru":"The endings hold the roles. «Кни́гу» is accusative, wherever you put it."},{"who":"A","en":"Как удо́бно! Одни́ слова́ — и сто́лько отте́нков.","ru":"How convenient! The same words — and so many shades."},{"who":"B","en":"В англи́йском поря́док стро́гий, а здесь — свобо́дный. Мне э́то нра́вится бо́льше всего́.","ru":"In English the order is strict, but here it's free. That's what I like most of all."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for word order, focus, and the little particles that spotlight it. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"поря́док слов","ru":"word order","ex":"В ру́сском поря́док слов свобо́дный."},{"en":"те́ма","ru":"the theme (known info)","ex":"Те́ма — э́то то, что уже́ изве́стно."},{"en":"ре́ма","ru":"the rheme (new info)","ex":"Ре́ма стои́т в конце́."},{"en":"и́менно","ru":"precisely, exactly","ex":"И́менно он был прав."},{"en":"то́лько","ru":"only","ex":"То́лько ты меня́ понима́ешь."},{"en":"да́же","ru":"even","ex":"Да́же де́ти э́то зна́ют."},{"en":"оконча́ние","ru":"an ending (grammatical)","ex":"Оконча́ние пока́зывает паде́ж."},{"en":"вини́тельный","ru":"accusative (case)","ex":"«Кни́гу» — э́то вини́тельный паде́ж."},{"en":"свобо́дный","ru":"free","ex":"Здесь свобо́дный поря́док слов."},{"en":"стро́гий","ru":"strict","ex":"В англи́йском поря́док стро́гий."},{"en":"отте́нок","ru":"a shade, a nuance","ex":"Ка́ждый поря́док даёт свой отте́нок."},{"en":"подчёркивать","ru":"to emphasise, to underline","ex":"Части́ца подчёркивает сло́во."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each term or particle to its meaning.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["поря́док слов","word order"],["ре́ма","the new information"],["и́менно","precisely, exactly"],["то́лько","only"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"In a neutral Russian sentence, the <b>new</b> information (the ре́ма) usually comes…","opts":["first","last","in the middle"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Статью́ написа́л профе́ссор» best answers…","opts":["Что написа́л профе́ссор?","Где профе́ссор?","Кто написа́л статью́?"],"answer":2},{"q":"When Russian reorders «он» and «кни́гу», who's the subject?","opts":["whichever comes first","он — it's nominative either way","it becomes ambiguous"],"answer":1},{"q":"Which particle means “even”?","opts":["то́лько","да́же","и́менно"],"answer":1},{"q":"Why can Russian move words freely when English can't?","opts":["Russian has fixed stress","case endings mark the roles","Russian has more words"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's last note on grammar — read it aloud, and feel how much a single move of a word can do.","title":"Поря́док реша́ет","sentences":["В ру́сском поря́док слов свобо́дный, но не случа́йный.","Изве́стное я ста́влю в нача́ло, а но́вое — в коне́ц.","«Э́ту статью́ написа́л изве́стный учёный» — гла́вное здесь кто.","Оконча́ния де́ржат смысл, поэ́тому слова́ мо́гут дви́гаться.","А е́сли ну́жно вы́делить сло́во, помога́ют части́цы: и́менно, то́лько, да́же.","По-мо́ему, э́то са́мое краси́вое в ру́сском языке́."],"translation":"In Russian, word order is free, but not random. I put the known at the start, and the new at the end. In «This article was written by a famous scientist», the main thing here is who. The endings hold the meaning, so the words can move. And if you need to highlight a word, particles help: и́менно, то́лько, да́же. In my opinion, this is the most beautiful thing about Russian."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — arrange, don't just assemble.","prompt":"Take one simple sentence (subject–verb–object) and write it two ways, changing what's the news: once answering “who did it?” (doer last), once answering “what did they do?” (object last). Then add one sentence that uses и́менно or то́лько to spotlight a word.","hint":"Known first, new (ре́ма) last; the case endings keep the roles when you move words; и́менно / то́лько / да́же point at the focus. 🙂","example":"Э́ту кни́гу перевела́ Э́мма. Э́мма перевела́ э́ту кни́гу за ме́сяц. И́менно э́тот рома́н она́ люби́ла бо́льше всего́."}},"26":{"day":26,"week":"04","themeRu":"Writing it properly","themeEn":"Письмо́ и эссе́","intro":"Emma has two blank documents open: a letter to a publishing house that wants her translation, and an essay for a language forum. Both are in Russian — and both would be <b>wrong</b> in the voice she'd use with Ле́на over coffee. Today is about <b>register</b>: how a formal letter is built from fixed formulas («Уважа́емый…», «С уваже́нием»), and how an essay argues with bookish connectors («во-пе́рвых», «сле́довательно», «таки́м о́бразом»). Let's learn to write it properly.","introAudio":"Emma has two blank documents open: a letter to a publishing house that wants her translation, and an essay for a language forum. Both are in Russian — and both would be wrong in the voice she'd use with Lena over coffee. Today is about register: how a formal letter is built from fixed formulas, Uvazhaemyy and s uvazheniyem, and how an essay argues with bookish connectors, vo-pervykh, sledovatelno, takim obrazom. Let's learn to write it properly.","goals":["Open and close a formal letter with the right formulas: <b>Уважа́емый…!</b> … <b>С уваже́нием, …</b>","Build an essay in three moves: thesis → arguments → conclusion","Argue with bookish connectors: <b>во-пе́рвых, во-вторы́х, одна́ко, сле́довательно, таки́м о́бразом</b>","Match register to purpose — no «Приве́т» in a letter, no «сле́довательно» to a friend"],"learned":["Opened and closed a formal letter with the right formulas: <b>Уважа́емый…!</b> … <b>С уваже́нием</b>","Built an essay in three moves: thesis → arguments → conclusion","Argued with bookish connectors: <b>во-пе́рвых, одна́ко, сле́довательно, таки́м о́бразом</b>","Matched register to purpose — formal formulas for letters, bookish connectors for essays"],"review":{"intro":"One minute on the days just behind you — an impersonal «should», a two-sided connector, and a concession. All three feed straight into today's writing.","introAudio":"One minute on the days just behind you — an impersonal 'should', a two-sided connector, and a concession. All three feed straight into today's writing.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. From yesterday's finer shades.","q":"“One should read the contract carefully.” (impersonal “should”)","opts":["Сле́дует внима́тельно прочита́ть догово́р.","Сле́дую внима́тельно прочита́ть догово́р.","Сле́дует внима́тельно прочита́ю догово́р."],"answer":0,"explain":"Impersonal <b>сле́дует</b> + infinitive: Сле́дует прочита́ть. «Сле́дую» is “I follow”; «прочита́ю» is a personal future — neither fits the impersonal frame."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“On the one hand, it's difficult.”</b>","want":"С одно́й стороны́, э́то тру́дно","answer":"С одно́й стороны́, э́то тру́дно"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the missing connector.","q":"“Although it was late, she kept working.” Fill: …, бы́ло по́здно, она́ продолжа́ла рабо́тать.","accept":["хотя","хотя","хотя,"],"placeholder":"…","explain":"Concession → <b>хотя́</b> (although): Хотя́ бы́ло по́здно, она́ продолжа́ла рабо́тать."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · A letter is built from formulas","title":"<em>Уважа́емый Ива́н Петро́вич!</em> … <em>С уваже́нием, Э́мма</em>","paras":["A Russian formal letter has fixed bones. It opens with <b>Уважа́емый…</b> (“Dear / Respected…”, masculine) or <b>Уважа́емая…</b> (feminine) + the person's name, followed by an <b>exclamation mark</b> and a new line: «Уважа́емый Ива́н Петро́вич!». Never «Приве́т», never «Здра́вствуй» — those belong to friends.","It closes just as formulaically: <b>С уваже́нием,</b> (“Respectfully / Yours sincerely,”) and your name underneath. Between them, the body opens the business with a formula too: <b>Обраща́юсь к Вам…</b> (“I am writing to you…”), <b>В связи́ с…</b> (“In connection with…”), <b>Прошу́ Вас…</b> (“I request that you…”).","One more mark of respect: to a single addressee you write <b>Вы / Вас / Вам</b> with a <b>capital В</b>. The whole letter stays on formal «вы» — you're not chatting, you're conducting business in writing."],"audio":"A Russian formal letter has fixed bones. It opens with Uvazhaemyy, Dear or Respected, masculine, or Uvazhaemaya, feminine, plus the person's name, followed by an exclamation mark and a new line: Uvazhaemyy Ivan Petrovich. Never Privet, never Zdravstvuy — those belong to friends. It closes just as formulaically: s uvazheniyem, Respectfully or Yours sincerely, and your name underneath. Between them, the body opens the business with a formula too: Obrashchayus k Vam, I am writing to you; v svyazi s, in connection with; Proshu Vas, I request that you. One more mark of respect: to a single addressee you write Vy, Vas, Vam with a capital V. The whole letter stays on formal 'vy' — you're not chatting, you're conducting business in writing.","table":{"rows":[["Уважа́емый Ива́н Петро́вич! <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Уважаемый Иван Петрович\">🔊</button>","Dear Ivan Petrovich! (opening, masc.)"],["В связи́ с Ва́шим письмо́м… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"В связи с Вашим письмом\">🔊</button>","In connection with your letter… (opening the business)"],["Прошу́ Вас рассмотре́ть моё предложе́ние. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Прошу Вас рассмотреть моё предложение\">🔊</button>","I ask you to consider my proposal."],["С уваже́нием, Э́мма Кларк. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"С уважением, Эмма Кларк\">🔊</button>","Respectfully, Emma Clark. (closing)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Dear Ms. Ivanova! I am writing to you about the translation.","en":"<b>Уважа́емая</b> О́льга Ива́новна! <b>Обраща́юсь к Вам</b> по по́воду перево́да.","say":"Уважаемая Ольга Ивановна! Обращаюсь к Вам по поводу перевода."},{"ru":"In connection with your proposal, I request a meeting.","en":"<b>В связи́ с</b> Ва́шим предложе́нием <b>прошу́</b> о встре́че.","say":"В связи с Вашим предложением прошу о встрече."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Приве́т, Ива́н Петро́вич!","right":"Уважа́емый Ива́н Петро́вич!","why":"«Приве́т» is for friends. A formal letter opens with <b>Уважа́емый / Уважа́емая</b> + name and an exclamation mark — the register is set by the very first word."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"You're writing to a publisher you've never met. How do you open?","opts":["Приве́т! Как дела́?","Уважа́емый Серге́й Никола́евич!","Здра́вствуй, Серге́й!"],"answer":1,"explain":"Formal letter → <b>Уважа́емый</b> + full name + «!». The other two are for friends."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the fixed formula. Stress marks are optional.","q":"Close a formal letter: “Respectfully, …” — type the two-word sign-off (before your name):","accept":["с уважением","с уважением","С уважением"],"placeholder":"С … ,","explain":"<b>С уваже́нием,</b> + your name. It's the fixed close of every formal letter."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build the opening move: <b>“I am writing to you regarding the contract.”</b>","want":"Обраща́юсь к Вам по по́воду догово́ра","answer":"Обраща́юсь к Вам по по́воду догово́ра"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · An essay argues in three moves","title":"<em>Те́зис → аргуме́нты → вы́вод</em>","paras":["An analytical essay isn't a list — it's an argument with a shape. First a <b>thesis</b> (те́зис): the claim you'll defend, opened with <b>Я счита́ю, что…</b> (“I believe that…”) or <b>По мо́ему мне́нию…</b> (“In my opinion…”).","Then the <b>arguments</b>, stacked and numbered by connectors: <b>во-пе́рвых</b> (firstly), <b>во-вторы́х</b> (secondly), <b>кро́ме того́</b> (moreover). Each is a reason, not just another fact — you're building a case.","Finally the <b>conclusion</b>, signalled by <b>таки́м о́бразом</b> (“thus / in this way”) or <b>сле́довательно</b> (“therefore / consequently”): «Таки́м о́бразом, чте́ние на языке́ — лу́чший спо́соб вы́учить его́». Thesis, evidence, conclusion — that's the B2 essay."],"audio":"An analytical essay isn't a list — it's an argument with a shape. First a thesis, tezis: the claim you'll defend, opened with Ya schitayu chto, I believe that, or Po moyemu mneniyu, in my opinion. Then the arguments, stacked and numbered by connectors: vo-pervykh, firstly; vo-vtorykh, secondly; krome togo, moreover. Each is a reason, not just another fact — you're building a case. Finally the conclusion, signalled by takim obrazom, thus, or sledovatelno, therefore: Takim obrazom, chteniye na yazyke — luchshiy sposob vyuchit yego. Thesis, evidence, conclusion — that's the B2 essay.","table":{"rows":[["Я счита́ю, что… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я считаю, что\">🔊</button>","I believe that… (thesis)"],["Во-пе́рвых… во-вторы́х… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Во-первых, во-вторых\">🔊</button>","Firstly… secondly… (arguments)"],["Кро́ме того́,… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Кроме того\">🔊</button>","Moreover,… (adding an argument)"],["Таки́м о́бразом,… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Таким образом\">🔊</button>","Thus,… (conclusion)"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"In my opinion, reading changes a person.","en":"<b>По мо́ему мне́нию</b>, чте́ние меня́ет челове́ка.","say":"По моему мнению, чтение меняет человека."},{"ru":"Thus, one can conclude that the author is right.","en":"<b>Таки́м о́бразом</b>, мо́жно сде́лать вы́вод, что а́втор прав.","say":"Таким образом, можно сделать вывод, что автор прав."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я счита́ю, что кни́ги — э́то кла́ссно.","right":"Я счита́ю, что кни́ги обогаща́ют челове́ка.","why":"In an essay, keep the register even. <b>кла́ссно</b> (“cool”) is spoken slang; write the bookish <b>обогаща́ют</b> (“enrich”). Argue, don't chat."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which connector signals the <b>conclusion</b> of an essay?","opts":["во-пе́рвых","таки́м о́бразом","кро́ме того́"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>Таки́м о́бразом</b> (thus) and <b>сле́довательно</b> close the argument. «во-пе́рвых» opens it; «кро́ме того́» adds a point."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the connector.","q":"Fill the essay opener: “Firstly, …” — «…, ру́сский — сло́жный язы́к.»","accept":["во-первых","во-первых","во-первых,"],"placeholder":"…","explain":"<b>Во-пе́рвых</b> starts the numbered arguments; <b>во-вторы́х</b> continues them."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it in an even, bookish voice.","target":"Таки́м о́бразом, я счита́ю, что чте́ние необходи́мо.","sub":"the conclusion of an essay — thesis restated at the end","want":"таким образом я считаю что чтение необходимо"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Bookish words for bookish writing","title":"<em>одна́ко, сле́довательно, тем не ме́нее</em> — the written voice","paras":["Formal writing has its own connectors — the bookish twins of the little words you say out loud. In speech you say <b>но</b> (but); on the page you can write <b>одна́ко</b> (however). You say <b>поэ́тому</b> (so); you write <b>сле́довательно</b> (therefore) or <b>таки́м о́бразом</b> (thus).","Two more earn their keep in essays: <b>тем не ме́нее</b> (“nevertheless”) concedes a point and pushes on, and <b>что каса́ется…</b> (“as for… / regarding…”) opens a new angle: «Что каса́ется языка́, он о́чень бога́т».","You met these on day 20 as register; here you <b>use</b> them to write. Pick the bookish connector for the page, and the essay sounds like Russian prose, not a text message."],"audio":"Formal writing has its own connectors — the bookish twins of the little words you say out loud. In speech you say no, but; on the page you can write odnako, however. You say poetomu, so; you write sledovatelno, therefore, or takim obrazom, thus. Two more earn their keep in essays: tem ne meneye, nevertheless, concedes a point and pushes on, and chto kasayetsya, as for or regarding, opens a new angle: Chto kasayetsya yazyka, on ochen bogat. You met these on day 20 as register; here you use them to write. Pick the bookish connector for the page, and the essay sounds like Russian prose, not a text message.","table":{"rows":[["но → одна́ко <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"но, однако\">🔊</button>","but → however (bookish)"],["поэ́тому → сле́довательно <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"поэтому, следовательно\">🔊</button>","so → therefore (bookish)"],["тем не ме́нее… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"тем не менее\">🔊</button>","nevertheless… (concede and continue)"],["что каса́ется… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"что касается\">🔊</button>","as for… / regarding…"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"The task is hard; however, it is interesting.","en":"Зада́ча трудна́; <b>одна́ко</b> она́ интере́сна.","say":"Задача трудна; однако она интересна."},{"ru":"As for the translation, it took a month.","en":"<b>Что каса́ется</b> перево́да, он за́нял ме́сяц.","say":"Что касается перевода, он занял месяц."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Пробле́ма сло́жная, но, ну, реши́мая.","right":"Пробле́ма сло́жная, одна́ко реши́мая.","why":"In an essay, trade the spoken <b>но, ну</b> for the bookish <b>одна́ко</b>. Same meaning, right register."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"You're writing an essay. Replace the spoken «но» with the bookish word:","opts":["но","одна́ко","и"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>Одна́ко</b> is the written “however”. «но» is fine in speech; «и» means “and”."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“As for the book, it is worth reading.”</b>","want":"Что каса́ется кни́ги, её сто́ит прочита́ть","answer":"Что каса́ется кни́ги, её сто́ит прочита́ть"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the bookish connector.","q":"Fill the bookish “therefore”: «Фа́кты я́сны, …, вы́вод очеви́ден.»","accept":["следовательно","следовательно","следовательно,"],"placeholder":"…","explain":"<b>Сле́довательно</b> (therefore) draws the conclusion in writing — more formal than «поэ́тому»."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Right voice, right place","title":"No «Приве́т» in a letter, no «сле́довательно» to a friend","paras":["Here's the whole day in one rule: <b>register must match purpose</b>. A formal letter is built from formulas and «вы»; an essay argues with bookish connectors; a note to Ле́на is warm and short. Using the wrong one isn't a small slip — it changes what kind of person the reader thinks you are.","So two mistakes to dodge in opposite directions. Don't drop <b>«Приве́т, как дела́?»</b> into a letter to an official — it reads as disrespect. And don't write <b>«Сле́довательно, я приду́ на ко́фе»</b> to a friend — it reads as a robot. Each register has its home.","The good news: you already own all three voices. The letter reuses formulas, the essay reuses your connectors, the note reuses your everyday «ты». Today you just learned to <b>choose</b> — and choosing register is the last polish on real Russian writing."],"audio":"Here's the whole day in one rule: register must match purpose. A formal letter is built from formulas and 'vy'; an essay argues with bookish connectors; a note to Lena is warm and short. Using the wrong one isn't a small slip — it changes what kind of person the reader thinks you are. So two mistakes to dodge in opposite directions. Don't drop Privet, kak dela into a letter to an official — it reads as disrespect. And don't write Sledovatelno, ya pridu na kofe to a friend — it reads as a robot. Each register has its home. The good news: you already own all three voices. The letter reuses formulas, the essay reuses your connectors, the note reuses your everyday 'ty'. Today you just learned to choose — and choosing register is the last polish on real Russian writing.","examples":[{"ru":"To an official: “I request that you consider the matter.”","en":"Чино́внику: «<b>Прошу́ Вас</b> рассмотре́ть вопро́с».","say":"Чиновнику: Прошу Вас рассмотреть вопрос."},{"ru":"To a friend: “Hi! Coming for coffee?”","en":"Дру́гу: «<b>Приве́т!</b> Придёшь на ко́фе?»","say":"Другу: Привет! Придёшь на кофе?"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"В письме́ нача́льнику: «Приве́т!»","right":"В письме́ нача́льнику: «Уважа́емый Ива́н Петро́вич!»","why":"To a boss or an official, «Приве́т» breaks the register. Open with <b>Уважа́емый</b> + name. Save «Приве́т» for friends."},{"wrong":"Дру́гу: «Сле́довательно, встре́тимся в семь.»","right":"Дру́гу: «Зна́чит, встре́тимся в семь.»","why":"<b>Сле́довательно</b> is essay-bookish and sounds robotic to a friend. In speech use <b>зна́чит</b> (“so”). Register cuts both ways."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which line belongs in a <b>formal letter</b>, not a text to a friend?","opts":["Приве́т, как жизнь?","Прошу́ Вас отве́тить в тече́ние неде́ли.","Дава́й созвони́мся!"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>Прошу́ Вас…</b> + «вы» is the letter's voice. The other two are friendly speech."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which fits an <b>essay</b>, not a chat?","opts":["Кни́га — про́сто ого́нь!","Да́нный рома́н поднима́ет ва́жные вопро́сы.","Ну, кни́га кла́ссная."],"answer":1,"explain":"Bookish and even: <b>Да́нный рома́н поднима́ет ва́жные вопро́сы</b>. «ого́нь» and «кла́ссная» are slang."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the friendly greeting (one word).","q":"A note to Ле́на: “Hi! Coming tonight?” — the informal greeting is:","accept":["привет","привет","привет!"],"placeholder":"…!","explain":"To a friend, <b>Приве́т!</b> is exactly right — warm and informal. Just not in a formal letter."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Emma is at her desk with two documents open; Ива́н drops by and reads over her shoulder. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Над чем сиди́шь так серьёзно? Два докуме́нта сра́зу.","ru":"What are you working on so seriously? Two documents at once."},{"who":"B","en":"Письмо́ в изда́тельство и эссе́ для фо́рума. И в ка́ждом — свой го́лос.","ru":"A letter to a publishing house and an essay for a forum. And each has its own voice."},{"who":"A","en":"В письмо́ не напиши́ «Приве́т», наде́юсь?","ru":"You won't write «Hi» in the letter, I hope?"},{"who":"B","en":"Коне́чно нет. «Уважа́емый господи́н реда́ктор!» — и до конца́ на «вы».","ru":"Of course not. «Dear Mr. Editor!» — and formal «you» to the very end."},{"who":"A","en":"А эссе́? О чём спо́ришь на э́тот раз?","ru":"And the essay? What are you arguing this time?"},{"who":"B","en":"О том, что перево́д — э́то тво́рчество. Во-пе́рвых, во-вторы́х, сле́довательно — по всем пра́вилам.","ru":"That translation is a creative art. Firstly, secondly, therefore — by all the rules."},{"who":"A","en":"Ты уже́ пи́шешь, как ру́сский публици́ст. Да́же обо́роты кни́жные.","ru":"You already write like a Russian essayist. Even bookish turns of phrase."},{"who":"B","en":"Два го́да наза́д я не могла́ написа́ть и одного́ письма́. А тепе́рь — це́лых два.","ru":"Two years ago I couldn't write even one letter. And now — a whole two."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the page — the formulas of a letter and the connectors of an essay. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"уважа́емый","ru":"respected, dear (formal address)","ex":"Уважа́емый Ива́н Петро́вич, здра́вствуйте."},{"en":"С уваже́нием","ru":"respectfully (letter sign-off)","ex":"С уваже́нием, Э́мма Кларк."},{"en":"обраща́ться","ru":"to address, to appeal to","ex":"Я обраща́юсь к Вам с про́сьбой."},{"en":"в связи́ с","ru":"in connection with, regarding","ex":"В связи́ с боле́знью я не пришла́."},{"en":"те́зис","ru":"a thesis, a claim to defend","ex":"Свой те́зис ну́жно доказа́ть."},{"en":"аргуме́нт","ru":"an argument (a reasoned point, not a quarrel)","ex":"У меня́ есть си́льный аргуме́нт."},{"en":"вы́вод","ru":"a conclusion","ex":"Из э́того сле́дует ва́жный вы́вод."},{"en":"одна́ко","ru":"however (bookish)","ex":"Зада́ча трудна́, одна́ко возмо́жна."},{"en":"сле́довательно","ru":"therefore, consequently (bookish)","ex":"Он не пришёл, сле́довательно, забы́л."},{"en":"таки́м о́бразом","ru":"thus, in this way","ex":"Таки́м о́бразом, мы реши́ли зада́чу."},{"en":"эссе́","ru":"an essay","ex":"Я пишу́ эссе́ о литерату́ре."},{"en":"изда́тельство","ru":"a publishing house","ex":"Изда́тельство при́няло мой перево́д."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each writing formula to what it does.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Уважа́емый…!","Dear …! (formal opening)"],["С уваже́нием,","Respectfully, (sign-off)"],["во-пе́рвых","firstly (first argument)"],["таки́м о́бразом","thus (conclusion)"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions on register and writing. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"How does a formal Russian letter open?","opts":["Приве́т, друг!","Уважа́емый Ива́н Петро́вич!","Здо́рово, Ива́н!"],"answer":1},{"q":"Which is the bookish “however” for an essay?","opts":["одна́ко","но","а"],"answer":0},{"q":"Which connector closes an argument (“thus”)?","opts":["во-пе́рвых","кро́ме того́","таки́м о́бразом"],"answer":2},{"q":"The formal sign-off of a letter is…","opts":["Пока́!","С уваже́нием,","Целу́ю!"],"answer":1},{"q":"«As for the book…» in bookish Russian is…","opts":["Что каса́ется кни́ги…","Про кни́гу…","Насчёт кни́ги…"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's finished letter to the publisher — read it aloud and hear the formal register hold from the first word to the last.","title":"Письмо́ в изда́тельство","sentences":["Уважа́емый господи́н реда́ктор!","Обраща́юсь к Вам в связи́ с Ва́шим предложе́нием о перево́де рома́на.","Во-пе́рвых, э́та кни́га мне о́чень бли́зка; во-вторы́х, я уже́ перевела́ похо́жий текст.","Таки́м о́бразом, прошу́ Вас рассмотре́ть мою́ кандидату́ру.","С уваже́нием, Э́мма Кларк."],"translation":"Dear Mr. Editor! I am writing to you in connection with your proposal about translating the novel. Firstly, this book is very close to me; secondly, I have already translated a similar text. Thus, I ask you to consider my candidacy. Respectfully, Emma Clark."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — write something real, in the right register.","prompt":"Write a short formal letter (5–7 Russian sentences) OR a mini-essay. If a letter: open with «Уважа́емый…!», state your business with «В связи́ с…» or «Обраща́юсь к Вам…», and close with «С уваже́нием». If an essay: give a thesis («Я счита́ю, что…»), two arguments («во-пе́рвых… во-вторы́х…»), and a conclusion («таки́м о́бразом…»).","hint":"Keep the register even — bookish connectors for the essay, fixed formulas and «вы» for the letter. No «Приве́т» on official paper 🙂","example":"Я счита́ю, что чте́ние в оригина́ле — лу́чший спо́соб вы́учить язы́к. Во-пе́рвых, живо́й текст даёт слова́ в конте́ксте, а не спи́ском. Во-вторы́х, чита́я, мы привыка́ем к есте́ственному ри́тму ре́чи. Одна́ко без словаря́ снача́ла тру́дно, и э́то на́до призна́ть. Тем не ме́нее, ка́ждая страни́ца де́лает нас сильне́е. Таки́м о́бразом, я убеждена́: чита́ть ну́жно с пе́рвого дня."}},"27":{"day":27,"week":"04","themeRu":"Hearing and holding a debate","themeEn":"Но́вости, ле́кция, спор","intro":"Yesterday you wrote in register; today you <b>speak</b> in it. Emma went to a public lecture with Ива́н — machines, language, the future — and by the end it had turned into a lively argument. That's the skill now: catch fast, dense Russian by its signposts, then join in — open your view, weigh both sides, push back politely, and colour it all with the two little particles «ведь» and «же». Let's learn to hold a debate.","introAudio":"Yesterday you wrote in register; today you speak in it. Emma went to a public lecture with Ivan — machines, language, the future — and by the end it had turned into a lively argument. That's the skill now: catch fast, dense Russian by its signposts, then join in — open your view, weigh both sides, push back politely, and colour it all with the two little particles ved and zhe. Let's learn to hold a debate.","goals":["Open an opinion out loud: <b>на мой взгляд, мне ка́жется, ска́жем</b>","Weigh both sides and disagree politely: <b>с одно́й стороны́… с друго́й… — возмо́жно, но…</b>","Colour your speech with the particles <b>ведь</b> (after all) and <b>же</b> (emphasis) — not “also”","Ride the signposts of live speech: <b>речь идёт о…, как изве́стно, во-пе́рвых</b>"],"learned":["Opened an opinion out loud: <b>на мой взгляд, мне ка́жется, ска́жем</b>","Weighed both sides and disagreed politely: <b>с одно́й стороны́… с друго́й… — возмо́жно, но…</b>","Coloured speech with the particles <b>ведь</b> and <b>же</b> — nuance, not “also”","Rode the signposts of live speech: <b>речь идёт о…, как изве́стно, во-пе́рвых</b>"],"review":{"intro":"A minute on writing before we start speaking — a letter opener, an opinion frame, and a “since”. Then into the debate.","introAudio":"A minute on writing before we start speaking — a letter opener, an opinion frame, and a 'since'. Then into the debate.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. From yesterday's letters.","q":"A formal letter opens with…","opts":["Уважа́емый Ива́н Петро́вич!","Приве́т, Ива́н!","Здо́рово, дружи́ще!"],"answer":0,"explain":"Formal → <b>Уважа́емый</b> + full name + «!». The other two are for friends."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“In my opinion, he is right.”</b>","want":"По-мо́ему, он прав","answer":"По-мо́ему, он прав"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the missing connector.","q":"“Since it was raining, we stayed home.” Fill the bookish “since”: …, шёл дождь, мы оста́лись до́ма.","accept":["поскольку","поскольку","поскольку,"],"placeholder":"…","explain":"Cause → <b>поско́льку</b> (since / because): Поско́льку шёл дождь, мы оста́лись до́ма."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Opening an opinion","title":"<em>На мой взгляд, мне ка́жется, ска́жем</em>","paras":["In a discussion, Russians rarely blurt a bare claim — they frame it. The most natural openers for an opinion are <b>на мой взгляд</b> (“in my view”), <b>по-мо́ему</b> (“to my mind”), <b>мне ка́жется, что…</b> (“it seems to me that…”). They say “this is mine, not gospel”, and they make you sound like a native, not a textbook.","Then there are the little <b>hedges</b> that buy you a second and soften the edges: <b>ска́жем</b> (“let's say”), <b>возмо́жно</b> (“possibly”), <b>наве́рное</b> (“probably”), <b>в о́бщем</b> (“basically”). Drop them where English would hesitate — “well…”, “sort of…”, “I guess”.","These are <b>spoken</b> markers, the opposite of yesterday's bookish connectors. In an essay you write «сле́довательно»; in a debate you say «мне ка́жется, ска́жем, наве́рное». Same you — different register."],"audio":"In a discussion, Russians rarely blurt a bare claim — they frame it. The most natural openers for an opinion are na moy vzglyad, in my view; po-moyemu, to my mind; mne kazhetsya chto, it seems to me that. They say 'this is mine, not gospel', and they make you sound like a native, not a textbook. Then there are the little hedges that buy you a second and soften the edges: skazhem, let's say; vozmozhno, possibly; navernoye, probably; v obshchem, basically. Drop them where English would hesitate — well, sort of, I guess. These are spoken markers, the opposite of yesterday's bookish connectors. In an essay you write sledovatelno; in a debate you say mne kazhetsya, skazhem, navernoye. Same you — different register.","table":{"rows":[["На мой взгляд,… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"На мой взгляд\">🔊</button>","In my view,…"],["Мне ка́жется, что… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Мне кажется, что\">🔊</button>","It seems to me that…"],["Ска́жем,… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Скажем\">🔊</button>","Let's say,… (hedge)"],["Возмо́жно,… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Возможно\">🔊</button>","Possibly,… (hedge)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"In my view, this film is too long.","en":"<b>На мой взгляд</b>, э́тот фильм сли́шком дли́нный.","say":"На мой взгляд, этот фильм слишком длинный."},{"ru":"It seems to me he's right, let's say, in part.","en":"<b>Мне ка́жется</b>, он прав, <b>ска́жем</b>, отча́сти.","say":"Мне кажется, он прав, скажем, отчасти."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Э́то пло́хо. То́чка.","right":"На мой взгляд, э́то не совсе́м уда́чно.","why":"A bare verdict sounds blunt in Russian discussion. Frame it — <b>на мой взгляд</b> — and soften it. Opinions are offered, not dropped."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"Pick the natural way to <b>open an opinion</b> out loud:","opts":["Сле́довательно, фильм плох.","На мой взгляд, фильм сла́бый.","Да́нный фильм сла́бый."],"answer":1,"explain":"Spoken opinion → <b>на мой взгляд</b>. «сле́довательно» and «да́нный» are bookish/written."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“It seems to me that he's right.”</b>","want":"Мне ка́жется, что он прав","answer":"Мне ка́жется, что он прав"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the one-word hedge.","q":"Add a hedge — “Let's say, in a week.”: «…, че́рез неде́лю.»","accept":["скажем","скажем","скажем,"],"placeholder":"…","explain":"<b>Ска́жем</b> (“let's say”) hedges a number or a guess — buys a beat and softens the claim."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Holding an opinion in a debate","title":"<em>С одно́й стороны́… с друго́й… — возмо́жно, но…</em>","paras":["A real debate has two sides, and Russian weighs them out loud: <b>с одно́й стороны́…</b> (“on the one hand…”), <b>с друго́й стороны́…</b> (“on the other hand…”). Laying out both before you land shows you've thought it through.","To disagree without a fight, you <b>concede then turn</b>: <b>Возмо́жно, но…</b> (“Possibly, but…”), <b>Не совсе́м так</b> (“Not quite”), <b>Я бы поспо́рил(а)</b> (“I'd argue with that”), <b>Согла́сен, одна́ко…</b>. You grant the other person something, then make your point — the polite engine of any discussion.","And to defend your view when pushed: <b>де́ло в том, что…</b> (“the thing is…”), <b>и́менно поэ́тому…</b> (“that's exactly why…”). You're not just stating an opinion now — you're holding it under pressure."],"audio":"A real debate has two sides, and Russian weighs them out loud: s odnoy storony, on the one hand; s drugoy storony, on the other hand. Laying out both before you land shows you've thought it through. To disagree without a fight, you concede then turn: Vozmozhno, no, possibly but; Ne sovsem tak, not quite; Ya by posporil, I'd argue with that; Soglasen, odnako. You grant the other person something, then make your point — the polite engine of any discussion. And to defend your view when pushed: delo v tom chto, the thing is; imenno poetomu, that's exactly why. You're not just stating an opinion now — you're holding it under pressure.","table":{"rows":[["С одно́й стороны́… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"С одной стороны\">🔊</button>","On the one hand…"],["С друго́й стороны́… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"С другой стороны\">🔊</button>","On the other hand…"],["Возмо́жно, но… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Возможно, но\">🔊</button>","Possibly, but… (concede + turn)"],["Не совсе́м так. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Не совсем так\">🔊</button>","Not quite. (polite disagreement)"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"On the one hand it's expensive, on the other it's reliable.","en":"<b>С одно́й стороны́</b>, до́рого, <b>с друго́й</b> — надёжно.","say":"С одной стороны, дорого, с другой — надёжно."},{"ru":"Possibly, but I'd argue with that.","en":"<b>Возмо́жно, но</b> я бы поспо́рила.","say":"Возможно, но я бы поспорила."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Нет, ты не прав. Всё.","right":"Возмо́жно, но мне ка́жется, что э́то не так.","why":"Flat contradiction ends a discussion. <b>Возмо́жно, но…</b> concedes a little, then turns — the move that keeps a debate alive."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Someone disagrees with you. Which reply keeps the debate polite?","opts":["Ты соверше́нно не прав!","Возмо́жно, но я ви́жу э́то ина́че.","Всё, разгово́р око́нчен."],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>Возмо́жно, но…</b> concedes, then turns. The others shut the conversation down."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“On the one hand, it's interesting.”</b>","want":"С одно́й стороны́, э́то интере́сно","answer":"С одно́й стороны́, э́то интере́сно"},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and weigh both sides out loud.","target":"С одно́й стороны́, я согла́сна, но с друго́й — есть сомне́ния.","sub":"concede, then turn — the shape of polite disagreement","want":"с одной стороны я согласна но с другой есть сомнения"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The two particles that colour speech","title":"<em>ведь</em> (after all) and <em>же</em> (emphasis) — now you say them","paras":["Two tiny words carry huge amounts of nuance in spoken Russian, and now you <b>produce</b> them, not just recognise them. <b>Ведь</b> appeals to something you both know — “after all / you know / isn't it”: «Ты <b>ведь</b> уже́ чита́л э́то» = “you've read this already, haven't you”. It gently invites agreement.","<b>Же</b> adds emphasis, contrast or a touch of impatience: «Я <b>же</b> говори́л!» = “I <b>told</b> you!”; «Что <b>же</b> де́лать?» = “What are we to do, then?”; «Он <b>же</b> инжене́р» = “He's an engineer, after all.” It leans on the word right before it.","The trap: <b>же is not “also”</b>. “Also” is <b>то́же / та́кже</b>. Же and ведь don't map to a dictionary word — they add a shade of shared knowledge or insistence. Drop them in and your Russian suddenly sounds lived-in."],"audio":"Two tiny words carry huge amounts of nuance in spoken Russian, and now you produce them, not just recognise them. Ved appeals to something you both know — after all, you know, isn't it: Ty ved uzhe chital eto, you've read this already, haven't you. It gently invites agreement. Zhe adds emphasis, contrast or a touch of impatience: Ya zhe govoril, I told you; Chto zhe delat, what are we to do then; On zhe inzhener, he's an engineer, after all. It leans on the word right before it. The trap: zhe is not 'also'. 'Also' is tozhe or takzhe. Zhe and ved don't map to a dictionary word — they add a shade of shared knowledge or insistence. Drop them in and your Russian suddenly sounds lived-in.","table":{"rows":[["Ты ведь зна́ешь его́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Ты ведь знаешь его\">🔊</button>","You know him, after all. (shared knowledge)"],["Я же говори́л! <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я же говорил\">🔊</button>","I told you! (emphasis)"],["Что же де́лать? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Что же делать\">🔊</button>","What to do, then? (intensifier)"],["Он же инжене́р. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Он же инженер\">🔊</button>","He's an engineer, isn't he. (appeal)"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"You've been to Moscow, after all — tell us.","en":"Ты <b>ведь</b> был в Москве́ — расскажи́.","say":"Ты ведь был в Москве — расскажи."},{"ru":"But this is obvious!","en":"Э́то <b>же</b> очеви́дно!","say":"Это же очевидно!"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я же был там (meaning “I was there too”)","right":"Я то́же был там.","why":"<b>же</b> is not “also”. For “also / too” use <b>то́же</b> or <b>та́кже</b>. «Я же был там» means “But I WAS there!” — emphasis, not addition."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“You know Ива́н, after all.” Which particle fits?","opts":["Ты то́же зна́ешь Ива́на.","Ты ведь зна́ешь Ива́на.","Ты та́кже зна́ешь Ива́на."],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>ведь</b> appeals to shared knowledge (“after all”). то́же/та́кже mean “also” — a different idea."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Я же говори́л!» means…","opts":["I told you (I did say so)!","I also said it.","I will say it."],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>же</b> here is emphatic insistence: “I TOLD you.” Not “also” (то́же), not future."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the one-syllable particle.","q":"“But this is obvious!” — add the emphatic particle: «Э́то … очеви́дно!»","accept":["же"],"placeholder":"…","explain":"<b>же</b> leans on «э́то» and adds punch: “But this is obvious!” One syllable, big attitude."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · News, lecture, debate — catching it live","title":"<em>Речь идёт о…, как изве́стно</em> — holding the thread","paras":["Live Russian — the news, a lecture — comes fast and dense, packed with the participles and abstract nouns you now read. You can't catch every word, so catch the <b>signposts</b>: <b>речь идёт о…</b> (“this is about…”), <b>как изве́стно</b> (“as is known”), <b>во-пе́рвых… во-вторы́х…</b>, <b>в заключе́ние</b> (“in conclusion”).","These markers tell you where the speaker is in their argument even when the vocabulary slips past. Hear «во-пе́рвых» and you know a list has started; hear «таки́м о́бразом» and the conclusion is coming. Ride the connectors, not every word.","Then you join in. A lecture becomes a debate the moment someone says «а на мой взгляд…». Now you have all of it — open your view, weigh both sides, push back with «возмо́жно, но…», colour it with «ведь» and «же». That's holding a debate in Russian."],"audio":"Live Russian — the news, a lecture — comes fast and dense, packed with the participles and abstract nouns you now read. You can't catch every word, so catch the signposts: rech idyot o, this is about; kak izvestno, as is known; vo-pervykh, vo-vtorykh; v zaklyucheniye, in conclusion. These markers tell you where the speaker is in their argument even when the vocabulary slips past. Hear vo-pervykh and you know a list has started; hear takim obrazom and the conclusion is coming. Ride the connectors, not every word. Then you join in. A lecture becomes a debate the moment someone says a na moy vzglyad. Now you have all of it — open your view, weigh both sides, push back with vozmozhno no, colour it with ved and zhe. That's holding a debate in Russian.","table":{"rows":[["Речь идёт о… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Речь идёт о\">🔊</button>","This is about… (topic signpost)"],["Как изве́стно,… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Как известно\">🔊</button>","As is known,…"],["В заключе́ние… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"В заключение\">🔊</button>","In conclusion…"],["Что вы ду́маете об э́том? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Что вы думаете об этом\">🔊</button>","What do you think about this?"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"The news is about the new law.","en":"В но́востях <b>речь идёт о</b> но́вом зако́не.","say":"В новостях речь идёт о новом законе."},{"ru":"As is known, the lecture will be in Russian.","en":"<b>Как изве́стно</b>, ле́кция бу́дет на ру́сском.","say":"Как известно, лекция будет на русском."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Trying to catch every single word of the news.","right":"Ло́вим сигна́лы: «во-пе́рвых…», «таки́м о́бразом…».","why":"You don't need every word. Track the <b>connectors</b> — they map the argument, and the meaning fills in around them."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"In a lecture you hear «во-пе́рвых…». What does it tell you?","opts":["The lecture is ending.","A numbered list of arguments has begun.","The speaker disagrees."],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>во-пе́рвых</b> opens a list. Its partner «таки́м о́бразом» would signal the conclusion."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the topic signpost (two words).","q":"Signpost a topic: “This is about the economy.”: … об эконо́мике.","accept":["речь идёт","речь идет","речь идёт о"],"placeholder":"…","explain":"<b>Речь идёт о…</b> (“this is about…”) tells the listener the topic — a signpost to hold onto."},{"type":"choice","q":"Someone finishes with «а на мой взгляд, э́то не так». They are…","opts":["agreeing fully","opening their own opposing opinion","ending the discussion"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>на мой взгляд</b> + «не так» opens a counter-opinion — the lecture just became a debate."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Coming out of the lecture hall, Ива́н and Emma are still arguing about it on the way to the metro. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Ну как ле́кция? Ты ведь ре́дко хо́дишь на таки́е.","ru":"So how was the lecture? You rarely go to these, after all."},{"who":"B","en":"На мой взгляд, интере́сно. Речь шла о бу́дущем языка́.","ru":"In my view, interesting. It was about the future of language."},{"who":"A","en":"И что же он сказа́л? Что маши́ны нас заме́нят?","ru":"And what did he say, then? That machines will replace us?"},{"who":"B","en":"С одно́й стороны́, да. С друго́й — перево́дчик чу́вствует то, что маши́на не по́нимает.","ru":"On the one hand, yes. On the other — a translator feels what a machine doesn't understand."},{"who":"A","en":"Возмо́жно, но лю́ди ведь то́же ошиба́ются.","ru":"Possibly, but people make mistakes too, after all."},{"who":"B","en":"Не совсе́м так. Челове́к де́лает оши́бки, но и понима́ет отте́нки.","ru":"Not quite. A person makes mistakes, but also understands the nuances."},{"who":"A","en":"Ты же спо́ришь, как настоя́щий ле́ктор! Ка́тя была́ бы в восто́рге.","ru":"You argue like a real lecturer! Katya would be delighted."},{"who":"B","en":"Ра́ньше я то́лько слу́шала. Тепе́рь я спо́рю — и мне э́то нра́вится.","ru":"Before I only listened. Now I argue — and I like it."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for opinion, debate and listening — the spoken register. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"на мой взгляд","ru":"in my view","ex":"На мой взгляд, э́то ва́жно."},{"en":"мне ка́жется","ru":"it seems to me","ex":"Мне ка́жется, он прав."},{"en":"ска́жем","ru":"let's say (a hedge)","ex":"Прие́ду, ска́жем, в пять."},{"en":"возмо́жно","ru":"possibly, maybe","ex":"Возмо́жно, ты прав."},{"en":"с одно́й стороны́","ru":"on the one hand","ex":"С одно́й стороны́, э́то удо́бно."},{"en":"с друго́й стороны́","ru":"on the other hand","ex":"С друго́й стороны́, э́то до́рого."},{"en":"ведь","ru":"after all, you know (particle)","ex":"Ты ведь обеща́л."},{"en":"же","ru":"emphasis / but (particle, not “also”)","ex":"Я же говори́л тебе́!"},{"en":"спо́рить","ru":"to argue, to debate","ex":"Мы ча́сто спо́рим о поли́тике."},{"en":"мне́ние","ru":"an opinion","ex":"У ка́ждого своё мне́ние."},{"en":"ле́кция","ru":"a lecture","ex":"Ле́кция начала́сь в семь."},{"en":"но́вости","ru":"the news","ex":"Я слу́шаю но́вости ка́ждое у́тро."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each spoken marker to what it does.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["на мой взгляд","in my view"],["с друго́й стороны́","on the other hand"],["ведь","after all (particle)"],["возмо́жно, но…","possibly, but… (concede + turn)"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions on debate and particles. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"Natural way to open an opinion in speech?","opts":["Сле́довательно,…","На мой взгляд,…","В связи́ с…"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Ты ведь зна́ешь его́.» — ведь adds…","opts":["an appeal to shared knowledge (“after all”)","“also / too”","the future tense"],"answer":0},{"q":"To disagree politely, you say…","opts":["Ты соверше́нно не прав!","Всё, хва́тит.","Возмо́жно, но я ду́маю ина́че."],"answer":2},{"q":"«Я же говори́л!» — же here means…","opts":["also","emphatic “I did tell you!”","maybe"],"answer":1},{"q":"In a lecture, «во-пе́рвых…» signals…","opts":["the start of a numbered list","the conclusion","disagreement"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's note after the lecture — the spoken debate, written down. Read it aloud and hear the markers hold the argument together.","title":"По́сле ле́кции","sentences":["Вчера́ я была́ на ле́кции о языке́ и бу́дущем.","Речь шла о том, заме́нят ли маши́ны перево́дчиков.","На мой взгляд, ва́жно не сло́во, а его́ отте́нок.","С одно́й стороны́, маши́на бы́страя; с друго́й — она́ не чу́вствует.","Возмо́жно, я не права́, но я лю́блю э́ти спо́ры."],"translation":"Yesterday I was at a lecture about language and the future. It was about whether machines will replace translators. In my view, what matters is not the word but its nuance. On the one hand, a machine is fast; on the other, it doesn't feel. Maybe I'm wrong, but I love these debates."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — take a side and defend it, the way you would out loud.","prompt":"Pick a question you have an opinion on (technology, city vs. country, learning languages, books vs. films). In 5–7 Russian sentences, <b>state your view</b> («на мой взгляд…», «мне ка́жется…»), <b>weigh both sides</b> («с одно́й стороны́… с друго́й…»), and <b>defend it</b> against an objection («возмо́жно, но…»). Slip in a «ведь» or a «же».","hint":"This is spoken register — hedges and particles, not bookish connectors. «Ска́жем», «наве́рное», «ведь» make it sound alive 🙂","example":"На мой взгляд, чита́ть кни́ги важне́е, чем смотре́ть фи́льмы. С одно́й стороны́, фильм быстре́е и я́рче. Но, ска́жем, кни́га ведь оставля́ет ме́сто для воображе́ния. Мне ка́жется, и́менно поэ́тому по́сле кни́ги ду́маешь до́льше. Возмо́жно, кто-то не согла́сен, и э́то норма́льно. И всё же я выбира́ю кни́гу."}},"28":{"day":28,"week":"04","themeRu":"Turns of phrase","themeEn":"Фразеологи́змы и перено́сное движе́ние","intro":"You can argue in Russian now — but a native reaches for something you're still missing: the <b>turn of phrase</b>. Emma hit a wall in a translation this week, not on grammar but on idioms: «вы́йти из себя́» isn't “to step outside”, and you «приня́ть» a decision, never «сде́лать» it. Today is the idiomatic layer — fixed collocations, whole-phrase idioms, and verbs of motion that stopped moving and went figurative. This is what makes Russian sound Russian.","introAudio":"You can argue in Russian now — but a native reaches for something you're still missing: the turn of phrase. Emma hit a wall in a translation this week, not on grammar but on idioms: vyyti iz sebya isn't 'to step outside', and you prinyat a decision, never sdelat it. Today is the idiomatic layer — fixed collocations, whole-phrase idioms, and verbs of motion that stopped moving and went figurative. This is what makes Russian sound Russian.","goals":["Use fixed collocations with the right verb: <b>приня́ть реше́ние, обрати́ть внима́ние, име́ть значе́ние</b>","Read idioms as whole units, not word by word: <b>име́ть в виду́, на са́мом де́ле, в конце́ концо́в</b>","Decode figurative motion: <b>вы́йти из себя́, войти́ в положе́ние, довести́ до конца́</b>","Dodge the two traps: don't translate the pieces, don't swap the fixed verb"],"learned":["Used fixed collocations with the right verb: <b>приня́ть реше́ние, обрати́ть внима́ние, име́ть значе́ние</b>","Read idioms as whole units: <b>име́ть в виду́, на са́мом де́ле, в конце́ концо́в</b>","Decoded figurative motion: <b>вы́йти из себя́, войти́ в положе́ние, довести́ до конца́</b>","Dodged the two traps: don't translate the pieces, don't swap the fixed verb"],"review":{"intro":"A quick lap around the last few days — a particle, a figurative motion verb, and a bookish connector. Then the turns of phrase.","introAudio":"A quick lap around the last few days — a particle, a figurative motion verb, and a bookish connector. Then the turns of phrase.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. From yesterday's particles.","q":"“You know him, after all.” Which particle fits?","opts":["Ты то́же зна́ешь его́.","Ты ведь зна́ешь его́.","Ты та́кже зна́ешь его́."],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>ведь</b> appeals to shared knowledge (“after all”). то́же/та́кже mean “also” — a different idea."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“They postponed the meeting.”</b> (перенести́ = move / postpone)","want":"Они́ перенесли́ встре́чу","answer":"Они́ перенесли́ встре́чу"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the bookish connector (two words).","q":"Fill the essay “thus”: «…, чте́ние де́лает нас лу́чше.»","accept":["таким образом","таким образом","таким образом,"],"placeholder":"…","explain":"Conclusion → <b>таки́м о́бразом</b> (thus), the bookish close from day 26."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Words that travel in pairs","title":"<em>приня́ть реше́ние, обрати́ть внима́ние</em> — the verb is fixed","paras":["Some Russian ideas come welded to one particular verb — a <b>collocation</b>. You <b>приня́ть</b> (“take”) a decision, not «сде́лать»: <b>приня́ть реше́ние</b>. You <b>обрати́ть</b> (“turn”) attention, not «дать»: <b>обрати́ть внима́ние</b> (“to pay attention”). The noun picks its verb, and only that verb sounds right.","A few more you'll use daily: <b>име́ть значе́ние</b> (“to matter”, lit. “have meaning”), <b>оказа́ть по́мощь / подде́ржку</b> (“to provide help / support”), <b>задава́ть вопро́с</b> (“to ask a question”), <b>приноси́ть по́льзу</b> (“to be of benefit”).","English has these too — you “make” a decision but “pay” attention — you just never think about them. In Russian, learn the noun <b>with</b> its verb, as one chunk. Guessing the verb from English is exactly where it goes wrong."],"audio":"Some Russian ideas come welded to one particular verb — a collocation. You prinyat, take, a decision, not sdelat: prinyat resheniye. You obratit, turn, attention, not dat: obratit vnimaniye, to pay attention. The noun picks its verb, and only that verb sounds right. A few more you'll use daily: imet znacheniye, to matter, literally 'have meaning'; okazat pomoshch or podderzhku, to provide help or support; zadavat vopros, to ask a question; prinosit polzu, to be of benefit. English has these too — you 'make' a decision but 'pay' attention — you just never think about them. In Russian, learn the noun with its verb, as one chunk. Guessing the verb from English is exactly where it goes wrong.","table":{"rows":[["приня́ть реше́ние <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"принять решение\">🔊</button>","to take / make a decision"],["обрати́ть внима́ние <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"обратить внимание\">🔊</button>","to pay attention"],["име́ть значе́ние <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"иметь значение\">🔊</button>","to matter (have meaning)"],["оказа́ть по́мощь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"оказать помощь\">🔊</button>","to provide help"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"We took an important decision.","en":"Мы <b>при́няли</b> ва́жное реше́ние.","say":"Мы приняли важное решение."},{"ru":"Pay attention to this detail.","en":"<b>Обрати́ внима́ние</b> на э́ту дета́ль.","say":"Обрати внимание на эту деталь."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"сде́лать реше́ние","right":"приня́ть реше́ние","why":"A decision is <b>taken</b>, not “made”: <b>приня́ть реше́ние</b>. The verb is fixed — «сде́лать реше́ние» is a word-for-word calque from English."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"“To make a decision” in Russian is…","opts":["сде́лать реше́ние","приня́ть реше́ние","взять реше́ние"],"answer":1,"explain":"The fixed verb is <b>приня́ть</b>: приня́ть реше́ние. «сде́лать» and «взять» are calques."},{"type":"choice","q":"“Pay attention!” —","opts":["Дай внима́ние!","Обрати́ внима́ние!","Плати́ внима́ние!"],"answer":1,"explain":"You <b>turn</b> attention: <b>Обрати́ внима́ние</b>. Not “give” (дай) or “pay” (плати́) — those are English pictures."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the fixed verb.","q":"Fill the verb: “It doesn't matter.” — Э́то не … значе́ния.","accept":["имеет","имеет"],"placeholder":"…","explain":"<b>име́ть значе́ние</b> = “to matter”. Under negation the noun goes genitive: не име́ет значе́ния."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Meaning isn't the sum of the words","title":"<em>име́ть в виду́, на са́мом де́ле, в конце́ концо́в</em>","paras":["An <b>idiom</b> means something its words don't add up to. <b>Име́ть в виду́</b> looks like “to have in view”, but it means <b>“to mean / to have in mind”</b>: «Что ты <b>име́ешь в виду́</b>?» = “What do you mean?”. Translate the phrase, never the pieces.","A handful pay off constantly: <b>на са́мом де́ле</b> (“actually / in fact”), <b>в конце́ концо́в</b> (“in the end / after all”), <b>де́ло в том, что…</b> (“the thing is…”), <b>име́ть пра́во</b> (“to have the right”).","These are the phrases that make listening hard — you know every word and still miss the sense. So meet them as whole units, the way you'd learn a single word. Once they're yours, your Russian stops sounding translated."],"audio":"An idiom means something its words don't add up to. Imet v vidu looks like 'to have in view', but it means 'to mean' or 'to have in mind': Chto ty imeyesh v vidu, what do you mean. Translate the phrase, never the pieces. A handful pay off constantly: na samom dele, actually or in fact; v kontse kontsov, in the end or after all; delo v tom chto, the thing is; imet pravo, to have the right. These are the phrases that make listening hard — you know every word and still miss the sense. So meet them as whole units, the way you'd learn a single word. Once they're yours, your Russian stops sounding translated.","table":{"rows":[["име́ть в виду́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"иметь в виду\">🔊</button>","to mean, to have in mind"],["на са́мом де́ле <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"на самом деле\">🔊</button>","actually, in fact"],["в конце́ концо́в <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в конце концов\">🔊</button>","in the end, after all"],["име́ть пра́во <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"иметь право\">🔊</button>","to have the right"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"What do you mean?","en":"Что ты <b>име́ешь в виду́</b>?","say":"Что ты имеешь в виду?"},{"ru":"Actually, I agree with you.","en":"<b>На са́мом де́ле</b> я с тобо́й согла́сна.","say":"На самом деле я с тобой согласна."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Что ты име́ешь в ви́де? (word-for-word “have in view”)","right":"Что ты име́ешь в виду́?","why":"The idiom is fixed: <b>име́ть в виду́</b> (accusative виду́, stress on the end) = “to mean”. It isn't “have in view” — don't rebuild it from the parts."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Что ты име́ешь в виду́?» means…","opts":["“What do you have in view?”","“What do you mean?”","“What do you see?”"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>име́ть в виду́</b> is an idiom = “to mean / have in mind”. The literal “have in view” is a trap."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“Actually, I don't know.”</b>","want":"На са́мом де́ле я не зна́ю","answer":"На са́мом де́ле я не зна́ю"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the three-word idiom.","q":"“In the end, we agreed.”: «…, мы договори́лись.»","accept":["в конце концов","в конце концов","в конце концов,"],"placeholder":"…","explain":"<b>В конце́ концо́в</b> = “in the end / after all”. A fixed three-word phrase."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Verbs of motion that stopped moving","title":"<em>вы́йти из себя́, войти́ в положе́ние, довести́ до конца́</em>","paras":["Your verbs of motion have a second life: prefixed, they turn <b>figurative</b>. <b>Вы́йти</b> is “to go out” — but <b>вы́йти из себя́</b> (“to go out of oneself”) means <b>“to lose one's temper”</b>. Nobody goes anywhere; the motion is a metaphor.","The pattern is everywhere in real Russian: <b>войти́ в положе́ние</b> (“to understand someone's situation”, lit. “enter the position”), <b>довести́ до конца́</b> (“to see through / finish”, lit. “lead to the end”), <b>прийти́ к вы́воду</b> (“to reach a conclusion”, lit. “come to a conclusion”), <b>провести́ вре́мя</b> (“to spend time”).","So when a motion verb appears with no journey in sight, suspect an idiom. You already own the literal system from B1; now the same verbs carry feelings, decisions and time. Read them as pictures, not directions."],"audio":"Your verbs of motion have a second life: prefixed, they turn figurative. Vyyti is 'to go out' — but vyyti iz sebya, 'to go out of oneself', means 'to lose one's temper'. Nobody goes anywhere; the motion is a metaphor. The pattern is everywhere in real Russian: voyti v polozheniye, to understand someone's situation, literally 'enter the position'; dovesti do kontsa, to see through or finish, literally 'lead to the end'; priyti k vyvodu, to reach a conclusion; provesti vremya, to spend time. So when a motion verb appears with no journey in sight, suspect an idiom. You already own the literal system from B1; now the same verbs carry feelings, decisions and time. Read them as pictures, not directions.","table":{"rows":[["вы́йти из себя́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"выйти из себя\">🔊</button>","to lose one's temper"],["войти́ в положе́ние <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"войти в положение\">🔊</button>","to understand someone's situation"],["довести́ до конца́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"довести до конца\">🔊</button>","to see through, to finish"],["прийти́ к вы́воду <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"прийти к выводу\">🔊</button>","to reach a conclusion"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Don't lose your temper over trifles.","en":"Не <b>выходи́ из себя́</b> из-за пустяко́в.","say":"Не выходи из себя из-за пустяков."},{"ru":"I reached the conclusion that he was right.","en":"Я <b>пришла́ к вы́воду</b>, что он прав.","say":"Я пришла к выводу, что он прав."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Он вы́шел из себя́ на у́лицу. (taken literally)","right":"Он вы́шел из себя́ от зло́сти.","why":"<b>вы́йти из себя́</b> means “to lose one's temper”, not “step outside”. When a motion verb has no real destination, read it as an idiom."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Он вы́шел из себя́» means…","opts":["He stepped outside.","He lost his temper.","He left himself a note."],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>вы́йти из себя́</b> = to lose one's temper. The literal “went out” is the trap."},{"type":"choice","q":"“Please understand my situation.” —","opts":["Пожа́луйста, войди́те в моё положе́ние.","Пожа́луйста, зайди́те в мой дом.","Пожа́луйста, вы́йдите отсю́да."],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>войти́ в положе́ние</b> = to understand someone's situation. The others are literal motion."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the missing preposition.","q":"Fill the idiom: “to see the work through to the end” — довести́ рабо́ту … конца́.","accept":["до"],"placeholder":"…","explain":"<b>довести́ до конца́</b> = to finish / see through. «до» + genitive конца́."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Use them whole, keep the register","title":"Don't translate the pieces, don't swap the verb","paras":["Two traps, both fatal to idioms. <b>One:</b> translating the pieces — “have in view” for <b>име́ть в виду́</b>, “make a decision” for <b>приня́ть реше́ние</b>. <b>Two:</b> swapping the fixed verb — «<s>де́лать</s> внима́ние» instead of <b>обрати́ть внима́ние</b>. Learn each phrase as one unbreakable unit and both traps vanish.","Register matters here too. Collocations like <b>приня́ть реше́ние</b> and <b>име́ть значе́ние</b> are neutral — good everywhere. Some idioms lean casual (<b>вы́йти из себя́</b>), some lean bookish (<b>прийти́ к вы́воду</b>) — you'll feel it from where you meet them.","This is the layer that separates “correct” Russian from Russian that <b>sounds</b> Russian. You've built the grammar; idioms are the finish — the turns of phrase a native reaches for without thinking. Collect them like coins."],"audio":"Two traps, both fatal to idioms. One: translating the pieces — 'have in view' for imet v vidu, 'make a decision' for prinyat resheniye. Two: swapping the fixed verb — delat vnimaniye instead of obratit vnimaniye. Learn each phrase as one unbreakable unit and both traps vanish. Register matters here too. Collocations like prinyat resheniye and imet znacheniye are neutral — good everywhere. Some idioms lean casual, vyyti iz sebya; some lean bookish, priyti k vyvodu — you'll feel it from where you meet them. This is the layer that separates 'correct' Russian from Russian that sounds Russian. You've built the grammar; idioms are the finish — the turns of phrase a native reaches for without thinking. Collect them like coins.","examples":[{"ru":"She paid attention and made the right decision.","en":"Она́ <b>обрати́ла внима́ние</b> и <b>приняла́ ве́рное реше́ние</b>.","say":"Она обратила внимание и приняла верное решение."},{"ru":"The thing is, it doesn't matter now.","en":"<b>Де́ло в том</b>, что э́то тепе́рь не име́ет значе́ния.","say":"Дело в том, что это теперь не имеет значения."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я сде́лал внима́ние на оши́бку.","right":"Я обрати́л внима́ние на оши́бку.","why":"Attention is <b>turned</b>, not “made”: <b>обрати́ть внима́ние</b>. Keep the fixed verb — the collocation won't tolerate a substitute."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which sentence uses the idioms correctly?","opts":["Я сде́лал реше́ние и дал внима́ние.","Я при́нял реше́ние и обрати́л внима́ние.","Я взял реше́ние и плати́л внима́ние."],"answer":1,"explain":"Fixed verbs: <b>приня́ть</b> реше́ние, <b>обрати́ть</b> внима́ние. The others are English calques."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“I came to the conclusion that he was right.”</b>","want":"Я пришёл к вы́воду, что он прав","answer":"Я пришёл к вы́воду, что он прав"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the fixed accusative form.","q":"Fill the idiom: “What do you mean?” — «Что ты име́ешь в …?»","accept":["виду","виду"],"placeholder":"в …?","explain":"<b>име́ть в виду́</b> = “to mean”. The fixed accusative is <b>виду́</b>, stressed on the end."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Emma is at Ле́на's café between chapters of a hard translation; Ле́на brings coffee and notices she's frazzled. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Ты сего́дня кака́я-то напряжённая. Что случи́лось?","ru":"You seem tense today. What happened?"},{"who":"B","en":"Тру́дный перево́д. Ника́к не могу́ довести́ его́ до конца́.","ru":"A hard translation. I just can't see it through to the end."},{"who":"A","en":"Понима́ю. Я вхожу́ в твоё положе́ние — сама́ так рабо́таю.","ru":"I understand. I get where you're coming from — I work the same way myself."},{"who":"B","en":"Са́мое тру́дное — идио́мы. Ка́ждая зна́чит не то, что ка́жется.","ru":"The hardest part is the idioms. Each means not what it seems."},{"who":"A","en":"Ты про «вы́йти из себя́»? Я в пе́рвый раз то́же не поняла́.","ru":"You mean “to lose your temper”? I didn't get it the first time either."},{"who":"B","en":"И́менно. На са́мом де́ле, э́то и есть са́мое интере́сное в языке́.","ru":"Exactly. Actually, that's the most interesting thing about the language."},{"who":"A","en":"Ты обрати́ла внима́ние, как ты говори́шь? Уже́ идио́мами!","ru":"Have you noticed how you talk? Already in idioms!"},{"who":"B","en":"В конце́ концо́в, два го́да в Москве́ что-то зна́чат.","ru":"In the end, two years in Moscow count for something."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve turns of phrase — collocations, idioms, and motion gone figurative. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"приня́ть реше́ние","ru":"to take / make a decision","ex":"Мы при́няли ва́жное реше́ние."},{"en":"обрати́ть внима́ние","ru":"to pay attention","ex":"Обрати́ внима́ние на дета́ли."},{"en":"име́ть в виду́","ru":"to mean, to have in mind","ex":"Что ты име́ешь в виду́?"},{"en":"име́ть значе́ние","ru":"to matter","ex":"Э́то не име́ет значе́ния."},{"en":"на са́мом де́ле","ru":"actually, in fact","ex":"На са́мом де́ле всё про́сто."},{"en":"в конце́ концо́в","ru":"in the end, after all","ex":"В конце́ концо́в мы согласи́лись."},{"en":"вы́йти из себя́","ru":"to lose one's temper","ex":"Он вы́шел из себя́ от зло́сти."},{"en":"войти́ в положе́ние","ru":"to understand someone's situation","ex":"Войди́ в моё положе́ние."},{"en":"довести́ до конца́","ru":"to see through, to finish","ex":"Ну́жно довести́ де́ло до конца́."},{"en":"прийти́ к вы́воду","ru":"to reach a conclusion","ex":"Я пришёл к тако́му вы́воду."},{"en":"провести́ вре́мя","ru":"to spend time","ex":"Мы хорошо́ провели́ вре́мя."},{"en":"идио́ма","ru":"an idiom","ex":"Э́та идио́ма о́чень ча́стая."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each turn of phrase to its meaning.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["приня́ть реше́ние","to make a decision"],["обрати́ть внима́ние","to pay attention"],["вы́йти из себя́","to lose one's temper"],["на са́мом де́ле","actually, in fact"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions on the idiomatic layer. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"“To make a decision” is…","opts":["сде́лать реше́ние","приня́ть реше́ние","взять реше́ние"],"answer":1},{"q":"«име́ть в виду́» means…","opts":["to mean / have in mind","to have in view","to keep in sight"],"answer":0},{"q":"«вы́йти из себя́» means…","opts":["to step outside","to leave a note","to lose one's temper"],"answer":2},{"q":"“Pay attention” uses which verb?","opts":["обрати́ть","дать","сде́лать"],"answer":0},{"q":"«на са́мом де́ле» means…","opts":["on one's own body","actually / in fact","at the same place"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's note to herself about idioms — read it aloud, and notice how the phrases mean more than their words.","title":"Ру́сские идио́мы","sentences":["Ру́сский язы́к по́лон удиви́тельных выраже́ний.","Снача́ла я понима́ла ка́ждое сло́во, но не смы́сл.","«Вы́йти из себя́» не зна́чит вы́йти на у́лицу.","Тепе́рь я понима́ю, что име́ет в виду́ ру́сский.","На са́мом де́ле, идио́мы — э́то ключ к языку́."],"translation":"The Russian language is full of surprising expressions. At first I understood every word, but not the meaning. “To lose one's temper” doesn't mean to step out onto the street. Now I understand what a Russian means. Actually, idioms are the key to the language."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — put the new turns of phrase to work.","prompt":"Write 5–7 Russian sentences about a real situation (a hard project, a disagreement, a decision) using at least <b>four</b> of today's phrases: приня́ть реше́ние, обрати́ть внима́ние, име́ть в виду́, вы́йти из себя́, войти́ в положе́ние, довести́ до конца́, на са́мом де́ле, в конце́ концо́в.","hint":"Keep each phrase whole — don't translate the pieces and don't swap the verb. приня́ть (not «сде́лать») реше́ние 🙂","example":"На про́шлой неде́ле я приняла́ тру́дное реше́ние: взять о́чень большо́й перево́д. Снача́ла я не обрати́ла внима́ния на сро́ки и чуть не вы́шла из себя́. Но реда́ктор вошёл в моё положе́ние и дал мне ещё три дня. На са́мом де́ле, э́то меня́ спасло́. Я реши́ла довести́ рабо́ту до конца́, чего́ бы э́то ни сто́ило. В конце́ концо́в, я пришла́ к вы́воду, что тру́дные те́ксты — лу́чшие учителя́."}},"29":{"day":29,"week":"04","themeRu":"Putting it together","themeEn":"Свя́зный те́кст уровня́ B2","intro":"No new grammar today — instead, the moment it all clicks into one. Emma is finishing that hard translation, and watching her own Russian do something new: a thesis, a reason with «поско́льку», a concession with «хотя́», a folded participle, a gerund, a quiet passive — all in one even paragraph. That's a connected B2 text. Today you assemble the level: every tool you own, working together in a single controlled voice.","introAudio":"No new grammar today — instead, the moment it all clicks into one. Emma is finishing that hard translation, and watching her own Russian do something new: a thesis, a reason with poskolku, a concession with khotya, a folded participle, a gerund, a quiet passive — all in one even paragraph. That's a connected B2 text. Today you assemble the level: every tool you own, working together in a single controlled voice.","goals":["Frame an argument with connectors: <b>поско́льку</b> (since), <b>хотя́</b> (although), <b>сле́довательно</b> (therefore)","Compress with participles and gerunds — and keep the commas honest","Stay precise: the passive for objectivity, the case each verb governs","Assemble it all into one connected paragraph, register even, from thesis to conclusion"],"learned":["Framed an argument with connectors: <b>поско́льку, хотя́, сле́довательно</b>","Compressed with participles and gerunds — commas kept honest","Stayed precise: the passive for objectivity, the case each verb governs","Assembled it all into one connected paragraph, register even, thesis to conclusion"],"review":{"intro":"One lap around the core of B2 — a participle, a gerund, and a verb's government. Then you put them all in one text.","introAudio":"One lap around the core of B2 — a participle, a gerund, and a verb's government. Then you put them all in one text.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. A participle from week 1.","q":"“the article written by the professor” —","opts":["статья́, напи́санная профе́ссором","статья́, писа́вшая профе́ссором","статья́, пи́шущая профе́ссора"],"answer":0,"explain":"Passive past participle: <b>напи́санная</b> (which was written) + instrumental «профе́ссором». The others are active (who was writing / who is writing)."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“Having read the letter, she smiled.”</b>","want":"Прочита́в письмо́, она́ улыбну́лась","answer":"Прочита́в письмо́, она́ улыбну́лась"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the case the verb wants.","q":"“I'm interested in history.” Fill: Я интересу́юсь … .","accept":["историей","историей"],"placeholder":"…","explain":"<b>интересова́ться</b> + instrumental → <b>исто́рией</b>. The verb chooses the case."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Build the frame first","title":"<em>Поско́льку… хотя́… сле́довательно…</em>","paras":["A connected B2 text isn't longer sentences — it's <b>linked</b> ones. The frame is your connectors, each doing one job: state a reason with <b>поско́льку</b> (“since”), grant a point with <b>хотя́</b> (“although”), draw a conclusion with <b>сле́довательно</b> (“therefore”).","Lay the frame before the detail. A thesis, a reason, a concession, a conclusion: «<b>Поско́льку</b> язы́к — э́то культу́ра, его́ на́до чу́вствовать. <b>Хотя́</b> э́то тру́дно, результа́т сто́ит уси́лий. <b>Сле́довательно</b>, чте́ние необходи́мо.»","You met each of these in weeks 3–4; here you <b>chain</b> them. The connectors are the spine — get them in order and the paragraph already thinks like Russian prose."],"audio":"A connected B2 text isn't longer sentences — it's linked ones. The frame is your connectors, each doing one job: state a reason with poskolku, since; grant a point with khotya, although; draw a conclusion with sledovatelno, therefore. Lay the frame before the detail. A thesis, a reason, a concession, a conclusion: Poskolku yazyk eto kultura, yego nado chuvstvovat. Khotya eto trudno, rezultat stoit usiliy. Sledovatelno, chteniye neobkhodimo. You met each of these in weeks three and four; here you chain them. The connectors are the spine — get them in order and the paragraph already thinks like Russian prose.","table":{"rows":[["Поско́льку язы́к сло́жен,… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Поскольку язык сложен\">🔊</button>","Since the language is complex,… (reason)"],["Хотя́ э́то тру́дно,… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Хотя это трудно\">🔊</button>","Although it's hard,… (concession)"],["…, сле́довательно, вы́вод я́сен. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"следовательно, вывод ясен\">🔊</button>","…, therefore the conclusion is clear."]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"Since he was late, we started without him.","en":"<b>Поско́льку</b> он опозда́л, мы на́чали без него́.","say":"Поскольку он опоздал, мы начали без него."},{"ru":"Although the text is hard, it's worth reading.","en":"<b>Хотя́</b> текст сло́жный, его́ сто́ит прочита́ть.","say":"Хотя текст сложный, его стоит прочитать."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Несмотря́ он опозда́л, мы на́чали.","right":"Несмотря́ на то что он опозда́л, мы на́чали.","why":"«Несмотря́» needs its full frame <b>несмотря́ на то что</b> before a clause. For a bare “although” + clause, use <b>хотя́</b>. Don't leave the connector half-built."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"Pick the reason connector (“since / because”) for a bookish text:","opts":["поско́льку","хотя́","заче́м"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>поско́льку</b> gives a reason. «хотя́» concedes; «заче́м» asks “what for”."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“Although it's difficult, I like it.”</b>","want":"Хотя́ э́то тру́дно, мне нра́вится","answer":"Хотя́ э́то тру́дно, мне нра́вится"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the reason connector.","q":"Fill the reason connector (“since”): «…, я живу́ в Москве́, я чита́ю по-ру́сски.»","accept":["поскольку","поскольку","поскольку,"],"placeholder":"…","explain":"<b>поско́льку</b> = since (a reason). It opens the cause before the result."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Fold clauses into phrases","title":"<em>напи́санный, чита́я</em> — the compression layer","paras":["Inside the frame, B2 prose is <b>dense</b>. Where speech says «кни́га, кото́рую написа́л а́втор», the page folds it into a participle: «кни́га, <b>напи́санная</b> а́втором». Where speech says «когда́ я чита́л, я ду́мал», the page uses a gerund: «<b>чита́я</b>, я ду́мал».","Two rules keep it clean. A participial phrase <b>after</b> its noun takes commas on both sides: «статья́, <b>напи́санная им</b>, интере́сна». And a gerund must share the main subject — «<b>Прочита́в письмо́</b>, она́ улыбну́лась» (she read, she smiled — same “she”).","This is the difference between “learner Russian” and the real thing. You built these forms in weeks 1–2; assembling them into a paragraph is what today is for. Compress, but keep the commas honest."],"audio":"Inside the frame, B2 prose is dense. Where speech says kniga, kotoruyu napisal avtor, the page folds it into a participle: kniga, napisannaya avtorom. Where speech says kogda ya chital, ya dumal, the page uses a gerund: chitaya, ya dumal. Two rules keep it clean. A participial phrase after its noun takes commas on both sides: statya, napisannaya im, interesna. And a gerund must share the main subject — Prochitav pismo, ona ulybnulas: she read, she smiled — same 'she'. This is the difference between learner Russian and the real thing. You built these forms in weeks one and two; assembling them into a paragraph is what today is for. Compress, but keep the commas honest.","table":{"rows":[["кни́га, напи́санная а́втором <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"книга, написанная автором\">🔊</button>","the book written by the author (participle)"],["статья́, напи́санная им, интере́сна <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"статья, написанная им, интересна\">🔊</button>","the article written by him is interesting (commas both sides)"],["Чита́я письмо́, она́ пла́кала. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Читая письмо, она плакала\">🔊</button>","Reading the letter, she cried. (gerund, same subject)"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"The letter, written in Russian, lay on the table.","en":"Письмо́, <b>напи́санное по-ру́сски</b>, лежа́ло на столе́.","say":"Письмо, написанное по-русски, лежало на столе."},{"ru":"Having finished the work, he went home.","en":"<b>Зако́нчив рабо́ту</b>, он пошёл домо́й.","say":"Закончив работу, он пошёл домой."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Чита́я письмо́, зазвони́л телефо́н.","right":"Когда́ я чита́л письмо́, зазвони́л телефо́н.","why":"The gerund's subject must match the main clause's. A phone can't “read”, so «чита́я» can't attach to «телефо́н» — unfold it to a «когда́» clause. This dangling gerund is the classic B2 slip."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Fold into a participle: «кни́га, кото́рую написа́л он» →","opts":["кни́га, пи́шущая им","кни́га, напи́санная им","кни́га, написа́вшая его́"],"answer":1,"explain":"Passive past participle: <b>напи́санная</b> (which was written) + instrumental «им». «пи́шущая» is active present; «написа́вшая» is active past (“who wrote”)."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which gerund sentence is correct (one subject)?","opts":["Вы́йдя из до́ма, пошёл дождь.","Вы́йдя из до́ма, она́ уви́дела дождь.","Вы́йдя из до́ма, дождь начался́."],"answer":1,"explain":"The gerund «вы́йдя» must share the subject: <b>она́</b> went out and <b>она́</b> saw. Rain can't “go out”."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the participle (neuter).","q":"Fold «письмо́, кото́рое написа́ли» into one word: «письмо́, …» (passive past participle):","accept":["написанное","написанное"],"placeholder":"…","explain":"<b>напи́санное</b> — neuter passive past participle, agreeing with письмо́."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Objectivity and the right case","title":"<em>Догово́р подпи́сан; зави́сит от…</em>","paras":["B2 writing is precise in two more ways. The <b>passive</b> makes it objective — a process with <b>-ся</b> (<b>дом стро́ится</b>, “the house is being built”), a result with a short participle (<b>догово́р подпи́сан</b>, “the contract is signed”). It states facts without pointing at who did them.","And <b>government</b> keeps every case right. A verb demands its case, not the “logical” one: <b>зави́сеть от</b> + genitive (<b>зави́сит от пого́ды</b>), <b>интересова́ться</b> + instrumental (<b>интересу́юсь исто́рией</b>), <b>уча́ствовать в</b> + prepositional. Get the verb, and the case follows.","Together they're the mark of a controlled writer: the passive when the doer doesn't matter, the exact case every time. This is precisely where English habits leak in — so this is where a B2 text proves itself."],"audio":"B2 writing is precise in two more ways. The passive makes it objective — a process with -sya, dom stroitsya, the house is being built; a result with a short participle, dogovor podpisan, the contract is signed. It states facts without pointing at who did them. And government keeps every case right. A verb demands its case, not the 'logical' one: zaviset ot plus genitive, zavisit ot pogody; interesovatsya plus instrumental, interesuyus istoriyey; uchastvovat v plus prepositional. Get the verb, and the case follows. Together they're the mark of a controlled writer: the passive when the doer doesn't matter, the exact case every time. This is precisely where English habits leak in — so this is where a B2 text proves itself.","table":{"rows":[["Дом стро́ится. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Дом строится\">🔊</button>","The house is being built. (-ся process)"],["Догово́р подпи́сан. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Договор подписан\">🔊</button>","The contract is signed. (result)"],["Всё зави́сит от пого́ды. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Всё зависит от погоды\">🔊</button>","It all depends on the weather. (от + genitive)"],["Я интересу́юсь исто́рией. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я интересуюсь историей\">🔊</button>","I'm interested in history. (instrumental)"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"The work has been done, the report is written.","en":"Рабо́та <b>вы́полнена</b>, отчёт <b>напи́сан</b>.","say":"Работа выполнена, отчёт написан."},{"ru":"The result depends on many factors.","en":"Результа́т <b>зави́сит от</b> мно́гих фа́кторов.","say":"Результат зависит от многих факторов."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я интересу́юсь исто́рию.","right":"Я интересу́юсь исто́рией.","why":"<b>интересова́ться</b> governs the <b>instrumental</b>: исто́рией, not «исто́рию» (accusative). The verb chooses the case — learn them as a pair."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“It all depends on the weather.” — which case after «зави́сит от»?","opts":["зави́сит от пого́ду","зави́сит от пого́ды","зави́сит от пого́де"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>зави́сеть от</b> + genitive: от пого́ды. The preposition «от» always takes the genitive."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“The house is being built quickly.”</b>","want":"Дом стро́ится бы́стро","answer":"Дом стро́ится бы́стро"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the short participle.","q":"Passive result: “The contract is signed.” — Догово́р … .","accept":["подписан","подписан"],"placeholder":"…","explain":"<b>подпи́сан</b> — short passive participle (masc.), a signed-and-done result."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · The whole thing, flowing","title":"Frame + compression + precision + <em>е́сли бы</em>","paras":["Now put the layers together into one paragraph. The <b>frame</b> connects (поско́льку, хотя́, сле́довательно); the <b>participles and gerunds</b> compress; the <b>passive and government</b> keep it precise; and one <b>hypothesis</b> with <b>е́сли бы</b> + past adds depth: «<b>Е́сли бы</b> я не учи́ла ру́сский, я бы не поняла́ э́того».","The last discipline is <b>even register</b>. Don't mix «сле́довательно» with «кла́ссно» in one breath — pick bookish for an essay, or spoken for a talk, and hold it. Flexible word order is fine: the case endings hold each word's role even when you move the rema to the end.","That's a real B2 text — not “correct exercises” strung together, but Russian that argues, concedes, supposes and concludes in one even voice. You already own every piece. Today you just proved you can write the whole."],"audio":"Now put the layers together into one paragraph. The frame connects: poskolku, khotya, sledovatelno; the participles and gerunds compress; the passive and government keep it precise; and one hypothesis with esli by plus past adds depth: Esli by ya ne uchila russkiy, ya by ne ponyala etogo. The last discipline is even register. Don't mix sledovatelno with klassno in one breath — pick bookish for an essay, or spoken for a talk, and hold it. Flexible word order is fine: the case endings hold each word's role even when you move the rema to the end. That's a real B2 text — not correct exercises strung together, but Russian that argues, concedes, supposes and concludes in one even voice. You already own every piece. Today you just proved you can write the whole.","examples":[{"ru":"If I hadn't read this, I wouldn't have understood.","en":"<b>Е́сли бы</b> я не прочита́ла э́того, я бы не поняла́.","say":"Если бы я не прочитала этого, я бы не поняла."},{"ru":"Although the topic is complex, it can, of course, be understood.","en":"<b>Хотя́</b> те́ма сложна́, её, коне́чно, мо́жно поня́ть.","say":"Хотя тема сложна, её, конечно, можно понять."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Э́то ва́жный вопро́с, и он про́сто ого́нь.","right":"Э́то ва́жный вопро́с, заслу́живающий внима́ния.","why":"Keep the register even. In an analytical text, don't drop slang «ого́нь» among bookish phrasing — replace it with a participle like <b>заслу́живающий внима́ния</b> (“deserving attention”)."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Add a hypothesis. “If I had time, I'd read more.” (unreal)","opts":["Е́сли бу́дет вре́мя, я почита́ю.","Е́сли бы бы́ло вре́мя, я бы чита́ла бо́льше.","Е́сли бы есть вре́мя, я чита́ю."],"answer":1,"explain":"Unreal → <b>е́сли бы</b> + past + бы in both halves. Option 1 is a real future; option 3 mixes бы with the present."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which paragraph keeps <b>one even register</b>?","opts":["Поско́льку те́ма важна́, сле́довательно, её ого́нь изучи́ть.","Поско́льку те́ма важна́, её, безусло́вно, сто́ит изучи́ть.","Те́ма кла́ссная, поэ́тому сле́довательно чита́ем."],"answer":1,"explain":"Option 2 stays bookish throughout. The others mix slang («ого́нь», «кла́ссная») into a formal frame."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it in one even voice.","target":"Хотя́ ру́сский сло́жен, я довела́ де́ло до конца́.","sub":"concession + a figurative-motion idiom, held in one register","want":"хотя русский сложен я довела дело до конца"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Late evening; Emma has just sent off the finished translation and calls Ива́н to read him the last paragraph. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Ну что, зако́нчила? Го́лос у тебя́ дово́льный.","ru":"So, did you finish? You sound pleased."},{"who":"B","en":"Зако́нчила. Хо́чешь, прочита́ю после́дний абза́ц? Он весь из оборо́тов.","ru":"I did. Want me to read you the last paragraph? It's all built from phrases."},{"who":"A","en":"Дава́й. Интере́сно, как ты э́то собрала́.","ru":"Go on. I'm curious how you put it together."},{"who":"B","en":"«Поско́льку кни́га, напи́санная в тяжёлые го́ды, полна́ наде́жды, её сто́ит чита́ть да́же сейча́с.»","ru":"“Since the book, written in hard years, is full of hope, it's worth reading even now.”"},{"who":"A","en":"Кра́сиво. Тут и прича́стие, и поско́льку, и всё ро́вно, в одно́м реги́стре.","ru":"Beautiful. There's a participle, and «since», and it's all even, in one register."},{"who":"B","en":"Хотя́ бы́ло тру́дно, я довела́ перево́д до конца́. Е́сли бы не ру́сский, я бы э́того не смогла́.","ru":"Although it was hard, I saw the translation through to the end. If not for Russian, I couldn't have done it."},{"who":"A","en":"Два го́да наза́д ты проси́ла помо́чь с одни́м предложе́нием.","ru":"Two years ago you were asking for help with a single sentence."},{"who":"B","en":"А тепе́рь я пишу́ це́лые страни́цы — и, ка́жется, ду́маю на э́том языке́.","ru":"And now I write whole pages — and, it seems, I think in this language."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for connected, controlled prose — connectors, compression, precision. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"поско́льку","ru":"since, because (bookish)","ex":"Поско́льку он за́нят, подождём."},{"en":"хотя́","ru":"although","ex":"Хотя́ по́здно, я не сплю."},{"en":"сле́довательно","ru":"therefore, consequently","ex":"Дождь, сле́довательно, зо́нтик ну́жен."},{"en":"свя́зный","ru":"connected, coherent (of a text)","ex":"Э́то свя́зный, я́сный текст."},{"en":"оборо́т","ru":"a phrase, a turn (participial/gerund)","ex":"В те́ксте мно́го оборо́тов."},{"en":"зави́сеть от","ru":"to depend on (+ genitive)","ex":"Всё зави́сит от тебя́."},{"en":"интересова́ться","ru":"to be interested in (+ instrumental)","ex":"Я интересу́юсь нау́кой."},{"en":"вы́полнить","ru":"to carry out, to complete","ex":"Рабо́та вы́полнена в срок."},{"en":"результа́т","ru":"a result, an outcome","ex":"Результа́т меня́ пора́довал."},{"en":"уси́лие","ru":"an effort","ex":"Э́то потре́бовало уси́лий."},{"en":"необходи́мо","ru":"it is necessary","ex":"Необходи́мо всё прове́рить."},{"en":"реги́стр","ru":"register (level of style)","ex":"Держи́ оди́н реги́стр в те́ксте."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each tool of connected prose to its job.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["поско́льку","since (a reason)"],["хотя́","although (a concession)"],["зави́сеть от","to depend on (+ genitive)"],["напи́санный","written (passive participle)"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five questions — the level, assembled. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"“the article written by the professor” —","opts":["статья́, напи́санная профе́ссором","статья́, писа́вшая профе́ссором","статья́, пи́шущая профе́ссора"],"answer":0},{"q":"Which gerund sentence is correct (one subject)?","opts":["Прочита́в письмо́, она́ запла́кала.","Прочита́в письмо́, зазвони́л телефо́н.","Прочита́в письмо́, стол упа́л."],"answer":0},{"q":"“I depend on my friends.” — case after «от»?","opts":["от друзья́х","от друзья́ми","от друзе́й"],"answer":2},{"q":"Unreal wish: “If I could, I'd stay.”","opts":["Е́сли смогу́, оста́нусь.","Е́сли бы я могла́, я бы оста́лась.","Е́сли бы смогу́, остаю́сь."],"answer":1},{"q":"«Догово́р подпи́сан» is…","opts":["a question","a passive result (“is signed”)","a future plan"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"A paragraph from Emma's finished translation preface — a truly connected B2 text. Read it aloud and spot each tool at work.","title":"Заче́м чита́ть в оригина́ле","sentences":["Поско́льку язы́к нельзя́ отдели́ть от культу́ры, его́ на́до чу́вствовать, а не то́лько учи́ть.","Кни́га, напи́санная носи́телем, звучи́т ина́че, чем перево́д.","Чита́я в оригина́ле, я замеча́ю отте́нки, кото́рые тру́дно переда́ть.","Хотя́ снача́ла э́то сло́жно, со вре́менем текст открыва́ется.","Смысл зави́сит от ка́ждого сло́ва, и ничего́ нельзя́ пропусти́ть.","Сле́довательно, для перево́дчика ва́жно чита́ть так, как чита́ет а́втор."],"translation":"Since language can't be separated from culture, you have to feel it, not just study it. A book written by a native sounds different from a translation. Reading in the original, I notice nuances that are hard to convey. Although it's difficult at first, over time the text opens up. The meaning depends on every word, and nothing can be skipped. Therefore, for a translator it's important to read the way the author reads."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — write one connected paragraph, the whole level in a single voice.","prompt":"Write a connected B2 paragraph (6–8 Russian sentences) on a topic you care about. Include: a <b>thesis</b>, a reason with <b>поско́льку</b>, a concession with <b>хотя́</b>, a hypothesis with <b>е́сли бы</b>, at least one <b>participle or gerund phrase</b>, one <b>passive</b>, and a conclusion with <b>сле́довательно / таки́м о́бразом</b>. Keep the register even.","hint":"Lay the connectors first, then compress with a participle/gerund and add one passive. Watch the government (зави́сит <b>от</b> + gen., интересу́юсь + instr.) 🙂","example":"Поско́льку мир стано́вится всё бо́лее свя́занным, зна́ние языко́в важне́е, чем ра́ньше. Челове́к, говоря́щий на двух языка́х, ви́дит бо́льше отте́нков. Хотя́ учёба тре́бует вре́мени, результа́т того́ сто́ит. Изуча́я язы́к, мы лу́чше понима́ем и свою́ культу́ру. Мно́гое, коне́чно, зави́сит от пра́ктики, и без неё ничего́ не бу́дет вы́учено. Е́сли бы я не начала́ учи́ть ру́сский, я бы не откры́ла для себя́ це́лый мир. Сле́довательно, вре́мя, потра́ченное на язы́к, никогда́ не быва́ет поте́ряно."}},"30":{"day":30,"week":"04","themeRu":"About me (B2 capstone)","themeEn":"О себе́: взгляд наза́д и вперёд","intro":"Thirty days ago the participle looked like a wall; now you build them without thinking, argue with «поско́льку» and «хотя́», and read a novel written for Russians. So here's the last one — the biggest — and it's about you. Not the beginner's «О себе́» from a year ago, but a B2 self-portrait: who you've become, how you think and argue in Russian now, and where the road leads next. No new grammar. Just you, assembling the whole summit into one voice.","introAudio":"Thirty days ago the participle looked like a wall; now you build them without thinking, argue with poskolku and khotya, and read a novel written for Russians. So here's the last one — the biggest — and it's about you. Not the beginner's about-me from a year ago, but a B2 self-portrait: who you've become, how you think and argue in Russian now, and where the road leads next. No new grammar. Just you, assembling the whole summit into one voice.","goals":["Describe the whole you at B2: participles, passive, the case each verb governs","Argue and reflect: connect with <b>поско́льку, хотя́, сле́довательно</b> and your own opinion","Look back and forward with <b>е́сли бы</b> and a gerund — how far you've come, where you're heading","Write a full B2 <b>«О себе́»</b> — an analytical self-portrait, plus a spoken monologue"],"learned":["Described the whole you at B2: participles, passive, the case each verb governs","Argued and reflected: connected with <b>поско́льку, хотя́, сле́довательно</b> and your own opinion","Looked back and forward with <b>е́сли бы</b> and a gerund","Wrote a full B2 <b>«О себе́»</b> — an analytical self-portrait and a spoken monologue"],"review":{"intro":"One last warm-up — a lap around the whole summit. A participle, a gerund, a verb's government, a concession, and one hypothesis. Then your capstone.","introAudio":"One last warm-up — a lap around the whole summit. A participle, a gerund, a verb's government, a concession, and one hypothesis. Then your capstone.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. A tour of everything you've built.","q":"“a person who reads a lot”, in one word →","opts":["мно́го чита́ющий челове́к","мно́го чита́вший челове́к","мно́го чи́танный челове́к"],"answer":0,"explain":"Active present participle: <b>чита́ющий</b> (the one reading). «чита́вший» is past; the third isn't right here."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which gerund sentence keeps one subject?","opts":["Живя́ в Москве́, я по́нял мно́гое.","Живя́ в Москве́, вре́мя прошло́ бы́стро.","Живя́ в Москве́, го́род измени́лся."],"answer":0,"explain":"The gerund «живя́» must share the subject: <b>я</b> live and <b>я</b> understood. Time and the city can't “live”."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“I'm interested in Russian literature.”</b>","want":"Я интересу́юсь ру́сской литерату́рой","answer":"Я интересу́юсь ру́сской литерату́рой"},{"type":"choice","q":"“Although it was hard, I finished.”","opts":["Хотя́ бы́ло тру́дно, я зако́нчила.","Потому́ что бы́ло тру́дно, я зако́нчила.","Что́бы бы́ло тру́дно, я зако́нчила."],"answer":0,"explain":"Concession → <b>хотя́</b>. «потому́ что» gives a cause; «что́бы» a purpose — both change the meaning."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the two-word opener.","q":"Unreal condition: “If someone had told me this…, I wouldn't have believed.” Fill: «…, мне сказа́ли об э́том, я бы не пове́рила.»","accept":["если бы","если бы"],"placeholder":"…","explain":"Unreal → <b>е́сли бы</b> + past, with «бы» again in the main clause."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Who I've become","title":"<em>О себе́</em> at B2 — participles, passive, government","paras":["Start with the portrait, but at full B2 resolution. A year ago you said «Я перево́дчик». Now you can say it with a participle: «Я — <b>перево́дчик, рабо́тающий</b> с литерату́рой и докуме́нтами». You describe yourself the way a Russian text would.","The precision tools come with you. The <b>passive</b> for what's been done — «мно́гое <b>сде́лано</b>, мно́гое ещё впереди́». And <b>government</b> for every case — «я <b>интересу́юсь</b> перево́дом (instr.), <b>зави́шу от</b> пра́ктики (gen.), <b>отношу́сь к</b> языку́ (dat.) с любо́вью».","This is the whole grammar of the level, turned on yourself. Say who you are, what you do, what you've built — in participles, in the passive, in the exact cases the verbs demand. The self-portrait proves the level."],"audio":"Start with the portrait, but at full B2 resolution. A year ago you said Ya perevodchik. Now you can say it with a participle: Ya perevodchik, rabotayushchiy s literaturoy i dokumentami. You describe yourself the way a Russian text would. The precision tools come with you. The passive for what's been done — mnogoye sdelano, mnogoye yeshchyo vperedi. And government for every case — ya interesuyus perevodom, instrumental; zavishu ot praktiki, genitive; otnoshus k yazyku, dative, s lyubovyu. This is the whole grammar of the level, turned on yourself. Say who you are, what you do, what you've built — in participles, in the passive, in the exact cases the verbs demand. The self-portrait proves the level.","table":{"rows":[["Я — перево́дчик, рабо́тающий с те́кстом. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я переводчик, работающий с текстом\">🔊</button>","I'm a translator working with text. (participle)"],["Мно́гое сде́лано, мно́гое впереди́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Многое сделано, многое впереди\">🔊</button>","Much has been done, much lies ahead. (passive)"],["Я интересу́юсь перево́дом. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я интересуюсь переводом\">🔊</button>","I'm interested in translation. (instrumental)"],["Я отношу́сь к языку́ с любо́вью. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я отношусь к языку с любовью\">🔊</button>","I feel about the language with love. (к + dative)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"I'm a translator who has been living in Moscow for over two years.","en":"Я — перево́дчик, <b>живу́щий</b> в Москве́ уже́ бо́льше двух лет.","say":"Я переводчик, живущий в Москве уже больше двух лет."},{"ru":"The decision to stay was taken long ago.","en":"Реше́ние оста́ться бы́ло <b>при́нято</b> давно́.","say":"Решение остаться было принято давно."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я интересу́юсь ру́сскую литерату́ру.","right":"Я интересу́юсь ру́сской литерату́рой.","why":"<b>интересова́ться</b> governs the instrumental: <b>ру́сской литерату́рой</b>. Even here, in your own portrait, the verb chooses the case."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"Describe yourself with a participle: “a translator working with literature” —","opts":["перево́дчик, рабо́тавший с литерату́рой","перево́дчик, рабо́тающий с литерату́рой","перево́дчик, рабо́танный литерату́рой"],"answer":1,"explain":"Active present participle: <b>рабо́тающий</b> (the one working). «рабо́тавший» is past; the third isn't a real form here."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“Much has been done.”</b> (passive)","want":"Мно́гое сде́лано","answer":"Мно́гое сде́лано"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the case after «от».","q":"Government: “I depend on practice.” — Я зави́шу от … .","accept":["практики","практики"],"placeholder":"…","explain":"<b>зави́сеть от</b> + genitive → <b>пра́ктики</b>."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · How I think and argue now","title":"<em>Поско́льку… на мой взгляд… таки́м о́бразом</em>","paras":["A B2 «О себе́» doesn't just list facts — it <b>reflects</b>. You give reasons with <b>поско́льку</b>, weigh with <b>с одно́й стороны́… с друго́й…</b>, offer your view with <b>на мой взгляд</b>, and conclude with <b>таки́м о́бразом</b>. Your self-portrait becomes a small argument about your own life.","And the idioms are yours now. You <b>приняли реше́ние</b> (took a decision) to stay; you <b>довели́ де́ло до конца́</b> (saw it through); on <b>са́мом де́ле</b> (in fact) the language changed you. These turns of phrase are what make it sound like a Russian talking, not a form being filled in.","So reflect out loud: why you stayed, what's hard, what you believe. This is the intellectual life B2 opened — reading real prose, holding a debate, writing an essay — and now you narrate it in your own even voice."],"audio":"A B2 about-me doesn't just list facts — it reflects. You give reasons with poskolku, weigh with s odnoy storony, s drugoy; offer your view with na moy vzglyad, and conclude with takim obrazom. Your self-portrait becomes a small argument about your own life. And the idioms are yours now. You prinyali resheniye, took a decision, to stay; you doveli delo do kontsa, saw it through; na samom dele, in fact, the language changed you. These turns of phrase are what make it sound like a Russian talking, not a form being filled in. So reflect out loud: why you stayed, what's hard, what you believe. This is the intellectual life B2 opened — reading real prose, holding a debate, writing an essay — and now you narrate it in your own even voice.","table":{"rows":[["Поско́льку я люблю́ язы́к,… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Поскольку я люблю язык\">🔊</button>","Since I love the language,…"],["На мой взгляд, э́то измени́ло меня́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"На мой взгляд, это изменило меня\">🔊</button>","In my view, it changed me."],["Я приняла́ реше́ние оста́ться. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я приняла решение остаться\">🔊</button>","I took the decision to stay."],["Таки́м о́бразом, ру́сский стал мои́м. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Таким образом, русский стал моим\">🔊</button>","Thus, Russian became mine."]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"Since I read a lot, I began to think in Russian.","en":"<b>Поско́льку</b> я мно́го чита́ю, я начала́ ду́мать по-ру́сски.","say":"Поскольку я много читаю, я начала думать по-русски."},{"ru":"In fact, I took this decision long ago.","en":"<b>На са́мом де́ле</b>, э́то реше́ние я приняла́ давно́.","say":"На самом деле, это решение я приняла давно."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я сде́лала реше́ние оста́ться.","right":"Я приняла́ реше́ние оста́ться.","why":"The fixed verb is <b>приня́ть</b>: <b>приняла́ реше́ние</b>. «сде́лать реше́ние» is the English calque — even now, keep the collocation whole."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Reflect with a reason: “Since I love the language, I stayed.”","opts":["Поско́льку я люблю́ язы́к, я оста́лась.","Что́бы я люблю́ язы́к, я оста́лась.","Хотя́ я люблю́ язы́к, я оста́лась."],"answer":0,"explain":"Reason → <b>поско́льку</b>. «что́бы» is purpose; «хотя́» would concede (“although I love it”), the opposite sense."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“In my view, Russian changed me.”</b>","want":"На мой взгляд, ру́сский измени́л меня́","answer":"На мой взгляд, ру́сский измени́л меня́"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the missing preposition.","q":"Fixed idiom: “I saw it through to the end.” — Я довела́ де́ло … конца́.","accept":["до"],"placeholder":"…","explain":"<b>довести́ до конца́</b> — the figurative-motion idiom, «до» + genitive."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The road behind and ahead","title":"<em>Е́сли бы…</em>, a gerund, and the look forward","paras":["Finish by turning to face both ways. Look back with a gerund folding two years into one breath: «<b>Огля́дываясь наза́д</b>, я не узнаю́ себя́ пре́жнюю». And measure the distance with <b>е́сли бы</b> + past: «<b>Е́сли бы</b> мне сказа́ли об э́том два го́да наза́д, я бы не пове́рила».","Then look forward. Real plans use <b>е́сли + future</b> (Е́сли бу́дет вре́мя, я возьму́сь за рома́н); dreams use the conditional <b>бы</b>; and hope keeps its own verb — <b>наде́юсь, что…</b>. The «О себе́» that opened as a list now closes as a life with a direction.","That's the summit. Not perfect Russian — living Russian: you read it, write it, argue in it, and now you think in it. Two years ago you couldn't say three words; today you can say all of this. So say it — one last «О себе́», the whole of you."],"audio":"Finish by turning to face both ways. Look back with a gerund folding two years into one breath: Oglyadyvayas nazad, ya ne uznayu sebya prezhnyuyu. And measure the distance with esli by plus past: Esli by mne skazali ob etom dva goda nazad, ya by ne poverila. Then look forward. Real plans use esli plus future: Esli budet vremya, ya vozmus za roman; dreams use the conditional by; and hope keeps its own verb — nadeyus chto. The about-me that opened as a list now closes as a life with a direction. That's the summit. Not perfect Russian — living Russian: you read it, write it, argue in it, and now you think in it. Two years ago you couldn't say three words; today you can say all of this. So say it — one last about-me, the whole of you.","table":{"rows":[["Огля́дываясь наза́д, я всё по́мню. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Оглядываясь назад, я всё помню\">🔊</button>","Looking back, I remember it all. (gerund)"],["Е́сли бы мне сказа́ли, я бы не пове́рила. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Если бы мне сказали, я бы не поверила\">🔊</button>","If I'd been told, I wouldn't have believed it. (unreal)"],["Е́сли бу́дет вре́мя, я возьму́сь за рома́н. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Если будет время, я возьмусь за роман\">🔊</button>","If I have time, I'll take on a novel. (real plan)"],["Я наде́юсь, что впереди́ ещё мно́гое. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я надеюсь, что впереди ещё многое\">🔊</button>","I hope much still lies ahead."]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"Looking back, I don't recognize my former self.","en":"<b>Огля́дываясь наза́д</b>, я не узнаю́ себя́ пре́жнюю.","say":"Оглядываясь назад, я не узнаю себя прежнюю."},{"ru":"I hope there are still many Russian roads ahead.","en":"Я <b>наде́юсь</b>, что впереди́ ещё мно́го ру́сских доро́г.","say":"Я надеюсь, что впереди ещё много русских дорог."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Е́сли бы бу́дет вре́мя, я бы взяла́сь.","right":"Е́сли бы бы́ло вре́мя, я бы взяла́сь.","why":"The conditional <b>бы</b> leans on the <b>past</b>, never the future: <b>Е́сли бы бы́ло…</b>. It's the last trap of the whole course — and now you step right over it."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Measure the distance: “If they'd told me two years ago, I wouldn't have believed it.” (unreal)","opts":["Е́сли мне ска́жут, я не пове́рю.","Е́сли бы мне сказа́ли, я бы не пове́рила.","Е́сли бы мне ска́жут, я бы не пове́рила."],"answer":1,"explain":"Unreal → <b>е́сли бы</b> + past in both halves. Option 1 is a real future; option 3 forces a future after «бы»."},{"type":"choice","q":"Look back with a gerund: “Looking back, I remember everything.”","opts":["Огля́дываясь наза́д, я всё по́мню.","Огля́дываюсь наза́д, я всё по́мню.","Огля́дываясь наза́д, всё по́мнится."],"answer":0,"explain":"The gerund «огля́дываясь» needs the same subject «я» doing the remembering. Option 2 isn't a gerund; option 3 dangles (no one to look back)."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it with quiet pride.","target":"Огля́дываясь наза́д, я ви́жу, как далеко́ я зашла́.","sub":"look back on the whole road — a gerund, in your own voice","want":"оглядываясь назад я вижу как далеко я зашла"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"The last evening of the level. Emma and Ива́н sit by the window, and she reads him the B2 «О себе́» she's just finished. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Ну что, после́днее «О себе́»? Прочита́ешь мне?","ru":"So, the final “About me”? Will you read it to me?"},{"who":"B","en":"Прочита́ю. То́лько э́то уже́ не то «О себе́», что год наза́д.","ru":"I will. Only it's not the “About me” from a year ago anymore."},{"who":"A","en":"Ве́рю. Тогда́ ты проси́ла помо́чь с ка́ждым сло́вом.","ru":"I believe it. Back then you asked for help with every word."},{"who":"B","en":"«Поско́льку язы́к стал ча́стью меня́, я ду́маю на нём да́же во сне.»","ru":"“Since the language has become part of me, I even think in it in my dreams.”"},{"who":"A","en":"Краси́во. И, кста́ти, гра́мотно — ни одно́й оши́бки.","ru":"Beautiful. And, by the way, correct — not a single mistake."},{"who":"B","en":"«Е́сли бы два го́да наза́д мне показа́ли э́тот текст, я бы не пове́рила, что напишу́ его́ сама́.»","ru":"“If two years ago I'd been shown this text, I wouldn't have believed I'd write it myself.”"},{"who":"A","en":"А что впереди́? Ты же ве́чно смо́тришь вперёд.","ru":"And what's ahead? You're always looking forward, after all."},{"who":"B","en":"Наде́юсь перевести́ це́лый рома́н. А пока́ — спаси́бо, что был ря́дом весь э́тот путь.","ru":"I hope to translate a whole novel. And for now — thank you for being there this whole journey."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for looking back with pride and forward with hope — the B2 close. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"огля́дываться наза́д","ru":"to look back","ex":"Огля́дываясь наза́д, я ра́да."},{"en":"измени́ться","ru":"to change (oneself)","ex":"Я си́льно измени́лась за два го́да."},{"en":"взгляд","ru":"a view, a gaze, a look","ex":"Мой взгляд на мир стал ши́ре."},{"en":"мышле́ние","ru":"thinking, mindset","ex":"Язы́к меня́ет мышле́ние."},{"en":"убежде́ние","ru":"a conviction, a belief","ex":"У меня́ есть свои́ убежде́ния."},{"en":"досто́инство","ru":"dignity, merit","ex":"Она́ де́ржится с досто́инством."},{"en":"свобо́дно","ru":"fluently, freely","ex":"Тепе́рь я говорю́ свобо́дно."},{"en":"зре́лость","ru":"maturity","ex":"Э́то знак языково́й зре́лости."},{"en":"перево́д","ru":"a translation","ex":"Я сдала́ большо́й перево́д."},{"en":"путь","ru":"a path, a journey","ex":"Э́то был до́лгий путь."},{"en":"го́рдость","ru":"pride","ex":"Ру́сский — моя́ ти́хая го́рдость."},{"en":"благода́рность","ru":"gratitude","ex":"Я чу́вствую благода́рность к друзья́м."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["огля́дываться наза́д","to look back"],["мышле́ние","thinking, mindset"],["е́сли бы","if (unreal condition)"],["таки́м о́бразом","thus, in conclusion"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Eight questions — the whole summit in miniature. Take your time; you've got all of this.","items":[{"q":"«a translator working with texts» —","opts":["перево́дчик, рабо́тающий с те́кстами","перево́дчик, рабо́тавший с те́кстами","перево́дчик, рабо́танный с те́кстами"],"answer":0},{"q":"Which gerund keeps one subject?","opts":["Огля́дываясь наза́д, вре́мя лети́т.","Огля́дываясь наза́д, я всё по́мню.","Огля́дываясь наза́д, всё забы́лось."],"answer":1},{"q":"“I'm interested in literature.” — which case?","opts":["интересу́юсь литерату́ру","интересу́юсь литерату́рой","интересу́юсь литерату́ры"],"answer":1},{"q":"“Although it was hard, I finished.”","opts":["Хотя́ бы́ло тру́дно, я зако́нчила.","Поэ́тому бы́ло тру́дно, я зако́нчила.","Что́бы бы́ло тру́дно, я зако́нчила."],"answer":0},{"q":"Unreal: “If I could, I'd translate a novel.”","opts":["Е́сли смогу́, переведу́ рома́н.","Е́сли бы я могла́, я бы перевела́ рома́н.","Е́сли бы я смогу́, переведу́ рома́н."],"answer":1},{"q":"«Реше́ние при́нято» is…","opts":["a question","a command","a passive result (“is taken/made”)"],"answer":2},{"q":"“to make a decision” — the fixed verb is…","opts":["сде́лать","приня́ть","дать"],"answer":1},{"q":"The bookish “therefore” to close an argument is…","opts":["по́тому","сле́довательно","заче́м"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's B2 «О себе́» — the one she read to Ива́н. Everything in it is yours now. Read it aloud, and hear how far the road has come.","title":"О себе́","sentences":["Два го́да наза́д я учи́ла падежи́; тепе́рь я ду́маю на ру́сском, не замеча́я э́того.","Я перево́дчик, чита́ющий ру́сскую про́зу в оригина́ле и спо́рящий о ней с друзья́ми.","Поско́льку язы́к стал ча́стью меня́, вме́сте с ним пришло́ и но́вое мышле́ние.","Хотя́ оши́бки ещё случа́ются, они́ меня́ не пуга́ют.","Е́сли бы кто-то показа́л мне э́ту страни́цу два го́да наза́д, я бы то́лько улыбну́лась.","Таки́м о́бразом, э́то не коне́ц пути́, а то́лько по́лпути."],"translation":"Two years ago I was learning cases; now I think in Russian without noticing it. I'm a translator who reads Russian prose in the original and argues about it with friends. Since the language has become part of me, a new way of thinking came with it. Although mistakes still happen, they don't scare me. If someone had shown me this page two years ago, I'd only have smiled. Thus, this isn't the end of the road — only the halfway point."},"essay":{"intro":"The last one — and the whole summit in a single text. Take your time; you've earned every word of it.","prompt":"Write your B2 «О себе́» in two parts. <b>Part 1 — a written self-portrait</b> (6–8 Russian sentences): who you are now, described with a participle and a passive; why you stayed with this, framed with «поско́льку» and «хотя́»; a hypothesis with «е́сли бы»; and where you're heading. <b>Part 2 — a spoken monologue</b>: say two or three of those sentences out loud, in an even, natural voice. Keep the register even, watch the government, and let it sound like you.","hint":"Move through the level: a participle («перево́дчик, рабо́тающий…»), a passive («реше́ние при́нято»), connectors («поско́льку, хотя́, сле́довательно»), a gerund («огля́дываясь наза́д»), and «е́сли бы». Quiet pride suits it 🙂","example":"Меня́ зову́т Э́мма, и уже́ бо́льше двух лет я живу́ в Москве́, рабо́тая перево́дчиком. Поско́льку я перевожу́ литерату́ру и докуме́нты, ру́сский стал для меня́ не предме́том, а о́бразом мы́сли. Кни́ги, напи́санные для носи́телей, я тепе́рь чита́ю в оригина́ле и спо́рю о них на ле́кциях. Хотя́ оши́бки ещё случа́ются, они́ меня́ не пуга́ют: реше́ние идти́ до конца́ давно́ при́нято. На мой взгляд, язы́к измени́л не то́лько мою́ рабо́ту, но и моё мышле́ние. Огля́дываясь наза́д, я понима́ю, как далеко́ я зашла́. Е́сли бы два го́да наза́д мне сказа́ли, что я напишу́ э́тот текст сама́, я бы не пове́рила. Сле́довательно, впереди́ ещё мно́го доро́г, и я наде́юсь пройти́ их до конца́."}}};
